  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="Basal Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal Cell Skin Cancer is a common form of skin cancer, characterized by its tendency to recur and spread to nearby lymph nodes or distant parts of the body if left untreated.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal Cell Skin Cancer is a type of skin cancer that may recur or metastasize, requiring careful diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal Cell Skin Cancer, also known as Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC), is the most common type of skin cancer, diagnosed in about 3 million cases annually in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="United States">
      <data key="d0">United States</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The United States is a country where a vaccine for HPV, aimed at preventing cervical cancer, is available and recommended.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States is a country where many clinical trials are conducted, often offering a variety of cancer treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States is the country where about 3 million cases of Basal Cell Skin Cancer are diagnosed each year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UV Radiation">
      <data key="d0">UV Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">UV Radiation is a significant risk factor contributing to the development of Basal Cell Carcinoma, damaging the DNA within skin cells.&lt;SEP&gt;UV Radiation is a significant risk factor for developing Basal Cell Skin Cancer, as it can damage the DNA in basal skin cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin Cancer is a broad term for cancers that develop in the skin, with Squamous Cell Carcinoma being one of the types.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin cancer is a type of cancer that occurs when skin cells begin to grow uncontrollably, often requiring regular examinations to monitor its development.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin cancer occurs when abnormal cell growth develops in the skin, of which Basal Cell Skin Cancer is the most frequent form.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin cancer refers to abnormal cell growth on the skin's surface, primarily caused by UV radiation exposure, particularly from the sun.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal Cell Carcinoma is the most common type of skin cancer, which arises from basal cells in the epidermis, generally due to UV exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal Cell Carcinoma, also known as Basal Cell Skin Cancer (BCC), is the most prevalent form of skin cancer, noted for its high treatability if detected early.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3 Million Cases">
      <data key="d0">3 Million Cases</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Each year, approximately 3 million cases of Basal Cell Carcinoma are diagnosed in the United States, indicating its widespread occurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regular Skin Exams">
      <data key="d0">Regular Skin Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regular Skin Exams are crucial for patients who have undergone treatment for Basal Cell Carcinoma, helping monitor for recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Layers">
      <data key="d0">Skin Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin comprises three layers: the epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis, each playing vital roles in protection and sensation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Skin Layers, consisting of the Epidermis, Dermis, and Hypodermis, play a crucial role in the development of skin cancer types, including Basal Cell Carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fair Skin">
      <data key="d0">Fair Skin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Individuals with Fair Skin are at a higher risk of developing Basal Cell Carcinoma due to reduced melanin levels that protect against UV radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Age">
      <data key="d0">Age</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Age is a risk factor for Basal Cell Carcinoma, as the likelihood of developing skin cancer increases over time due to cumulative sun exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Age is a significant risk factor for prostate cancer, as the likelihood of developing the disease increases as a person gets older.&lt;SEP&gt;Age is a significant risk factor for prostate cancer, with higher incidence rates in men aged 65 and above.&lt;SEP&gt;Age is a significant risk factor for prostate cancer, with the likelihood of diagnosis increasing markedly in males aged 65 years and older.&lt;SEP&gt;Age is identified as the biggest risk factor for prostate cancer, with increasing risk as one grows older.&lt;SEP&gt;Age is the primary risk factor for prostate cancer, with the likelihood of developing the disease increasing as individuals get older.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Suppression">
      <data key="d0">Immune Suppression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune Suppression can elevate the risk of developing Basal Cell Carcinoma, particularly in individuals with weakened immune systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnosis of Basal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Diagnosis of Basal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnosis of Basal Cell Carcinoma involves recognizing atypical growths and conducting medical examinations to confirm skin cancer presence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Glands">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal glands are small glands located on top of each kidney, crucial for hormone production and regulating various bodily functions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Adrenal Glands are small, triangular-shaped glands located on top of each kidney, responsible for producing essential hormones that regulate metabolism, stress response, and immune function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal Tumors are growths that can form in one or both adrenal glands; they may be benign or malignant and can impact hormone production.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenal tumors are abnormal growths on the adrenal glands, which can produce excess hormones and may be identified through various imaging and tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenal tumors can be benign or malignant growths in the adrenal glands, with various types affecting hormone levels and health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenocortical Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adrenocortical Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenocortical Carcinoma is a rare but serious form of cancer that arises in the adrenal cortex, often leading to various health complications due to hormonal imbalances.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenocortical Carcinoma is a rare type of cancer that originates in the adrenal cortex, impacting hormone production and overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenocortical carcinoma (ACC) is a type of adrenal cancer that begins in the adrenal cortex and primarily affects children ages 5 and under, and adults in their 40s and 50s.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cushing Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Cushing Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cushing Syndrome is a condition caused by excessive cortisol production, often associated with adrenal adenomas or tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Cushing Syndrome is caused by high cortisol levels, which can result from adrenal tumors, leading to various symptoms including weight gain, high blood pressure, and skin changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroblastoma">
      <data key="d0">Neuroblastoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuroblastoma is a cancerous tumor found in the adrenal glands that primarily affects children under the age of 5, commonly found in infants.&lt;SEP&gt;Neuroblastoma is a cancerous tumor found in the adrenal glands that primarily affects children under the age of 5, often presenting in infants.&lt;SEP&gt;Neuroblastoma is a rare cancer most commonly found in infants and young children, originating in immature nerve cells in the adrenal gland and sometimes elsewhere in the abdomen.&lt;SEP&gt;Neuroblastoma is a rare cancer that primarily affects infants and young children, often originating in the adrenal glands.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 3: Types of Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 3: Types of Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">This chapter outlines different treatment methods available for managing Basal Cell Skin Cancer, emphasizing the importance of personalized treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 4: Treatment by Risk and Recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 4: Treatment by Risk and Recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">This chapter discusses treatment strategies based on individual risk factors and the likelihood of cancer recurrence, essential in personalizing patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Gland Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Gland Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal gland cancer is a rare type of cancer affecting approximately 1 to 2 people per million, and it begins in the adrenal cortex.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pheochromocytoma">
      <data key="d0">Pheochromocytoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pheochromocytoma begins in the adrenal medulla and is often benign but can be cancerous, presenting symptoms like high blood pressure and rapid heartbeat.&lt;SEP&gt;Pheochromocytoma is a tumor that starts in the adrenal medulla, characterized by symptoms such as high blood pressure and rapid heartbeat, and while most cases are benign, some can be cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenoma is a benign tumor that represents the most common type of polyp found in the colon, which can eventually become colon cancer if not monitored.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenoma is a benign type of polyp that, over many years, can develop into invasive rectal cancer if not monitored.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenoma is a noncancerous tumor of the adrenal gland, which can be functioning or nonfunctioning and is the most common type of adrenal gland tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenoma is the most common type of adrenal gland tumor, typically noncancerous, and can be functioning or nonfunctioning.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenoma is the most common type of polyp that can develop in the colon and may eventually become colon cancer if left untreated.&lt;SEP&gt;An adenoma is a benign tumor of glandular origin, which can develop into colorectal cancer over time if not monitored.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is a genetic disorder that can increase the risk of developing adrenal tumors among other types of tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is a hereditary condition that may increase the risk for developing adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carney Complex">
      <data key="d0">Carney Complex</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carney complex is a genetic condition related to increased tumor formation, including those in the adrenal glands, leading to a predisposition for adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Carney complex is a genetic condition that can increase the risk of adrenal tumors as part of its broader spectrum of effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP)">
      <data key="d0">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP) is a rare genetic condition leading to the development of hundreds to thousands of polyps in the colon, which can become cancerous.&lt;SEP&gt;Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is a hereditary condition linked to a higher risk of developing adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is a hereditary disorder characterized by the development of numerous polyps in the colon, leading to an increased cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an inherited condition that significantly heightens the risk of developing various types of tumors, including adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Li-Fraumeni Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Li-Fraumeni Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a genetic disorder associated with a heightened risk of several cancers, including pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a genetic disorder that predisposes individuals to several types of cancer, including pancreatic cancer, due to mutations affecting tumor suppressor genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a hereditary condition that predisposes individuals to a range of cancers, including adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a hereditary condition that predisposes individuals to multiple cancers, including adrenal malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1 (MEN 1)">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1 (MEN 1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN 1) is a hereditary syndrome associated with an elevated risk for developing adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN 1) is an inherited disorder linked to increased risk of endocrinal tumors, including adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2 (MEN 2)">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2 (MEN 2)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 (MEN 2) is a genetic condition that can lead to a variety of tumors, including adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 (MEN 2) is a hereditary condition that may increase the risk of various endocrine tumors, including adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (NF1)">
      <data key="d0">Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (NF1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) is a genetic disorder that can lead to an increased risk of adrenal tumors among other complications.&lt;SEP&gt;Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) is a hereditary disorder characterized by skin and nerve tumors, which can include an elevated risk for adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome (VHL)">
      <data key="d0">Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome (VHL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome (VHL) is a genetic condition associated with a predisposition to multiple tumors, including those affecting the adrenal glands.&lt;SEP&gt;Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome (VHL) is a hereditary condition associated with several tumors, including the risk for adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors, 2022">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors, 2022</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors is a document providing essential information about adrenal tumors, diagnostic processes, and medical history preparation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT scan">
      <data key="d0">CT scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A CT scan is a medical imaging procedure that uses x-rays to create detailed images of internal body structures, commonly used for cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;A CT scan is an imaging test that combines multiple X-ray images to create a detailed three-dimensional picture of the inside of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The computed tomography (CT) scan is an imaging test that uses x-rays to create detailed pictures of the body's structures, including the brain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI scan">
      <data key="d0">MRI scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI scan is a medical imaging technique that uses strong magnetic fields and radio waves to generate detailed images of organs and tissues without radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a medical imaging technique using radio waves and powerful magnets to create detailed images of organs and tissues without using X-rays.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET scan">
      <data key="d0">PET scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET (Positron Emission Tomography) scan is a imaging test that uses a radioactive drug to show metabolism and activity of cells, particularly to detect cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan (Positron Emission Tomography) is a diagnostic imaging test that uses radioactive tracers to visualize metabolic processes in the body, particularly useful for detecting cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is an imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to visualize cancer cells in the body, often in combination with CT or MRI scans for accuracy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood tests are laboratory tests performed on blood samples to assess the health status and detect various medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical History">
      <data key="d0">Medical History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical history is a comprehensive record documenting a patient’s health issues, treatments, and medications throughout their life.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical history records all health issues a patient has experienced which aids in determining the appropriate treatment for liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical history records all health issues a patient has experienced, aiding in determining the appropriate treatment for liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history involves documenting a patient's past health information, which can impact future treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a detailed record documenting an individual's health issues and treatments over time, essential for understanding risks and planning treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a documented record of a patient's past health issues and treatments that guides healthcare providers in determining appropriate treatment for AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is the record of a patient’s previous health issues and conditions, crucial for diagnosing new illnesses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Provider">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A health care provider is a professional responsible for delivering medical care and advice, including physicians and specialists.&lt;SEP&gt;A health care provider is a professional who evaluates patients' health and administers necessary medical procedures and tests.&lt;SEP&gt;A health care provider is a professional who works with patients to determine health statuses, recommend tests, and suggest treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A health care provider is a trained professional who gives health care services and guidance to patients regarding their diseases and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care provider refers to medical professionals involved in diagnosing and treating patients, including consultations related to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers are professionals involved in the assessment, diagnosis, and treatment of diseases, including breast cancer, and they play a crucial role in patient care.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers are professionals who deliver medical services and support to patients, discussing treatment results and monitoring health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic diseases are long-lasting conditions that can be controlled but are rarely cured, affecting normal functioning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contact Information">
      <data key="d0">Contact Information</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contact Information refers to the details of communication for individuals within a patient's care team, essential for emergencies and regular communication.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Physician">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Physician</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary care physician is a healthcare provider responsible for general health maintenance and coordinating overall care for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary Care Physicians (PCPs) are healthcare providers who manage overall patient care and coordinate treatments, including cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary care physicians (PCPs) are often the first point of contact for patients with symptoms of anal cancer and refer them to specialists.&lt;SEP&gt;The Primary Care Physician is the main healthcare provider who oversees a patient's overall health and is informed of any changes in care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Binder">
      <data key="d0">Medical Binder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Medical Binder is an organized folder or notebook used to compile and manage important medical records, tests, and contact information.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical binder is an organizational tool for patients to keep track of their medical records, test results, and treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical binder is an organizational tool used by patients to keep medical records, test results, and treatment information easily accessible during healthcare appointments.&lt;SEP&gt;A personal organizational tool used by patients to compile and keep track of their medical records, test results, and treatment information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Results">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging Results are findings from various diagnostic tests like CT and MRI scans that provide information on patients' health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second Opinion">
      <data key="d0">Second Opinion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Second Opinion refers to obtaining an additional evaluation and consultation from another healthcare professional regarding a diagnosis or treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Second Opinion refers to obtaining an additional medical perspective on treatment options, important for cancer patients seeking personalized care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Scans">
      <data key="d0">MRI Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI Scans are advanced imaging techniques that use magnetic fields and radio waves to visualize internal body structures without ionizing radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI scans use magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of organs and tissues, often used in diagnosing prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET (Positron Emission Tomography) scan uses radioactive tracers to provide images of metabolic processes in the body, particularly useful for detecting cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET Scan employs radioactive substances to visualize metabolic processes in tissues, particularly useful in detecting cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET Scan is a functional imaging technique that detects cancer activity using a radioactive tracer injected into the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan (Positron Emission Tomography) is a nuclear imaging technique used to observe metabolic processes in the body, particularly in cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan (positron emission tomography) uses radioactive tracers to detect areas of biochemical activity in the body, often related to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan employs a radioactive tracer to identify cancer cells in the body and evaluate their metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is a diagnostic imaging technique that detects cancerous cells in the body by highlighting their activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is a type of imaging test that detects cancerous cells by using a radioactive tracer injected into the body, highlighting areas of metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is an imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to highlight cancerous cells and assess the extent of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan uses radioactive tracers to visualize metabolic processes in the body, particularly for detecting cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan utilizes a radiotracer to show how cells use sugar, which helps in identifying cancerous cells. It's particularly useful for detecting small groups of cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan, or positron emission tomography scan, is a type of imaging test that detects metabolic activity in tissues and can identify areas of cancer growth.&lt;SEP&gt;A positron emission tomography (PET) scan involves a radioactive tracer to identify cancer cells in the body, often showing up as bright spots.&lt;SEP&gt;A positron emission tomography (PET) scan uses a radioactive tracer injected into the body to visualize areas of cancer cells through imaging technology.&lt;SEP&gt;A scanning method that uses a radioactive tracer to visualize how tissues and organs are functioning, particularly for detecting cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;PET Scan is a type of imaging test that detects cancer cells in the body by using a radioactive glucose injection.&lt;SEP&gt;The PET Scan is an imaging procedure that uses radioactive substances to visualize metabolic processes in the body, particularly for cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast">
      <data key="d0">Contrast</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast is a substance used in imaging tests to enhance the visibility of internal structures during examinations like CT and MRI scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast refers to substances used in medical imaging to enhance the visibility of structures within the body during scans, which may or may not be used in certain MRI tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Portals">
      <data key="d0">Patient Portals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Online patient portals offer a way for patients to access medical records, test results, and communicate with healthcare teams.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient Portals are online platforms that allow patients to access their medical information, including test results and appointment scheduling.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient portals are online platforms that allow individuals to access and manage their health information and test results easily.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient portals are online platforms that provide individuals access to their medical records and test results, enhancing communication with the care team.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient portals are secure online platforms that allow patients to access their health information, communicate with healthcare providers, and manage appointments and test results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family Members">
      <data key="d0">Family Members</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Family Members are relatives whose health history can impact a patient's risk for certain diseases and conditions, particularly genetic ones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health History">
      <data key="d0">Health History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Comprehensive documentation of a patient's past medical treatments, conditions, and family medical history.&lt;SEP&gt;Health History is a comprehensive account of an individual's medical background, treatments, and family health information that can influence diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Health history encompasses an individual's past medical conditions and health events that can influence cancer risk assessment and testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Health history is a comprehensive account of an individual's medical issues and family health history used to inform treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Health history is a record of a patient's past medical issues and treatments that informs the current care and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Health history is an assessment of an individual's past medical issues, treatments, and family health conditions important for diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms are clinical signs experienced by patients that may indicate underlying health issues, like hormone overproduction from tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms are physical or mental indications of a condition or disease, important for early detection and diagnosis of illnesses, including cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms are physical or mental indicators that suggest a patient's health condition and inform healthcare providers about the patient's current state.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms are physical or psychological signs that may indicate the presence of disease, often manifesting in various ways depending on the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of prostate cancer include blood in urine, pain during urination, unexplained weight loss, and erectile dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms refer to physical or mental features indicating a condition, such as frequent urination or sexual problems, which are critical to diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG-PET Scan is a type of positron emission tomography scan that uses a radioactive sugar tracer to highlight active cancer cells, making them appear brighter in medical images.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SSTR-PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">SSTR-PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SSTR-PET Scan is a medical imaging technique that uses tracers that bind to somatostatin receptors on cells, useful for identifying neuroendocrine tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC) is a common type of skin cancer that originates in squamous cells, often caused by prolonged sun exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) is a type of skin cancer arising from squamous cells, often found in various tissues, notably the mouth.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) is the most common type of throat cancer and arises from the flat cells found in the mucous membranes.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is the most common type of nasopharyngeal cancer, arising from squamous cells that line the surface of the nasopharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a common type of cervical cancer arising from squamous cells, often treated with surgery or chemoradiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a malignant tumor arising from squamous cells, commonly found in the skin and tissues lining the anal canal.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a subtype of lung cancer that accounts for a significant portion of lung cancer cases, specifically within NSCLC.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a type of NSCLC that originates from cells lining the bronchi.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a type of cancer that arises from the squamous cells, often found in the nasopharynx and linked to various risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is another form of esophageal cancer that typically starts in the thin, flat cells of the esophagus, more prevalent in Eastern Europe, Asia, and Africa.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is one of the types of bladder cancer that arises from squamous cells, which can occur in the bladder lining.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of anal cancer, starting in squamous cells that form the skin's surface.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of cervical cancer that arises from squamous cells in the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma refers to cancer that begins in the squamous cells of the nasopharynx and is a major type of carcinoma affecting this area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biochemical Tests">
      <data key="d0">Biochemical Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biochemical tests measure substances in blood, urine, or saliva to detect hormone levels related to adrenal tumors and assess treatment responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melanocytes">
      <data key="d0">Melanocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Melanocytes are skin cells that produce melanin, the pigment responsible for skin color and protection from UV radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Major Risk Factors">
      <data key="d0">Major Risk Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Major risk factors for squamous cell skin cancer include excessive sun exposure, indoor tanning, and environmental toxins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET/MRI">
      <data key="d0">PET/MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PET/MRI is a combined imaging technique that merges positron emission tomography and magnetic resonance imaging for enhanced diagnostic accuracy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroendocrine Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Neuroendocrine Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuroendocrine tumors are a type of tumor that arises from hormone-producing cells in the neuroendocrine system, which can be assessed using specific imaging techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="18-Fluorodeoxyglucose">
      <data key="d0">18-Fluorodeoxyglucose</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">18-Fluorodeoxyglucose is a radioactive glucose compound used as a tracer in PET scans to visualize metabolically active cells, primarily in cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Cells">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Cells are abnormal cells that grow uncontrollably, leading to tumors, and can exhibit specific traits based on hormone receptor status or other markers.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that are characterized by uncontrolled cell division, leading to tumors and can spread through the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably and can invade surrounding tissues, leading to the formation of tumors and the spread of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably and may spread to other parts of the body, often detected through PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably, often forming tumors, and can be visualized through diagnostic tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably, potentially forming tumors and spreading to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that grow and divide uncontrollably, leading to various types of cancer including CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that multiply uncontrollably and can spread throughout the body, leading to various forms of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells that divide uncontrollably and can invade other tissues, often leading to tumors and various types of cancer, including CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells that grow uncontrollably and can invade nearby tissues, leading to the formation of tumors and various forms of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epidermis">
      <data key="d0">Epidermis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The epidermis is the outermost layer of the skin, providing a waterproof barrier and housing squamous cells that can become cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dermis">
      <data key="d0">Dermis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The dermis is the layer of skin beneath the epidermis that contains connective tissue, hair follicles, and sweat glands.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypodermis">
      <data key="d0">Hypodermis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The hypodermis, or subcutis, is the deepest skin layer composed mainly of fat and connective tissues, providing insulation and cushioning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melanoma">
      <data key="d0">Melanoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Melanoma is a more aggressive form of skin cancer that develops from melanocytes, the pigment cells in the skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Factors Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Risk Factors Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk factors analysis involves identifying elements that increase the likelihood of developing certain diseases, particularly cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Toxins">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Toxins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Environmental Toxins refer to harmful substances in the environment, including tobacco smoke and vehicle exhaust that increase the risk of skin damage and cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental toxins include pollutants that can increase the risk of developing skin cancers, such as tobacco smoke and chemical exposure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Wounds">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Wounds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Wounds are long-lasting wounds that can lead to complications like Marjolin’s ulcer, which is associated with the development of Squamous Cell Carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic wounds are long-lasting sores that can increase the risk of skin cancer development, notably in the form of CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC)">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that may develop from certain risk factors such as environmental toxins, scars, and chronic wounds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actinic Keratosis">
      <data key="d0">Actinic Keratosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A rough or scaly patch of skin that may develop into squamous cell skin cancer, often resulting from prolonged sun exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinic Keratosis refers to scaly or rough skin patches caused by sun exposure, increasing the risk of developing squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Xeroderma Pigmentosum (XP)">
      <data key="d0">Xeroderma Pigmentosum (XP)</data>
      <data key="d1">genetic syndrome</data>
      <data key="d2">Xeroderma Pigmentosum is a genetic condition that hinders the body's ability to repair DNA damage caused by sunlight, elevating the risk for skin cancers including CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recessive Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa (RDEB)">
      <data key="d0">Recessive Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa (RDEB)</data>
      <data key="d1">genetic syndrome</data>
      <data key="d2">Recessive Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa is a rare inherited skin disorder that causes fragile skin and is associated with a higher risk of squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunosuppressants">
      <data key="d0">Immunosuppressants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunosuppressants are drugs used to prevent the body from rejecting transplanted organs, which can lower the immune system's ability to fight off diseases, including skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunosuppressants are medications that can weaken the immune system, increasing the risk of persistent HPV infection and cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Genetics Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Genetics Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Genetics Counseling involves providing guidance to individuals with higher risks of cancers, including skin cancers, often requiring specialized medical support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marjolin’s Ulcer">
      <data key="d0">Marjolin’s Ulcer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Marjolin’s Ulcer is a type of skin cancer that forms in previously scar tissue or chronic wounds, indicating the potential severe outcomes of such conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Marjolin’s ulcer is a specific form of squamous cell skin cancer that develops in old wounds or scars, known for being more challenging to treat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Skin Biopsy is a medical procedure used to diagnose skin abnormalities and conditions, including confirming skin cancer diagnoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologist">
      <data key="d0">Pathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pathologist diagnoses cancer through analysis of biopsy samples, crucial for AML diagnosis and staging.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pathologist is a medical doctor who specializes in diagnosing diseases by examining tissues, including the analysis of cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pathologist is a medical expert responsible for examining tissue samples to diagnose diseases, including blood cancers like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pathologist is a medical professional responsible for diagnosing diseases by studying tissue samples, vital in AML diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pathologist is a medical professional who examines biopsied tissue samples to diagnose diseases, including various forms of skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist analyzes biopsy samples to diagnose cancer and other diseases affecting breast tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist analyzes tissues and cells from biopsies to provide diagnosis and staging of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor specializing in diagnosing diseases by examining tissues and cells under a microscope.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor specializing in diagnosing diseases through microscopic examination of tissue samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor specializing in diagnosing diseases through the examination of tissues, important in confirming cancer diagnoses.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor who examines tissue samples from breast biopsies to diagnose cancers and other diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical expert who examines tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose diseases, including the type and grade of ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical expert who studies tissue samples to diagnose diseases such as cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical professional who examines biopsy samples under a microscope to determine the presence and characteristics of cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical professional who examines tissues and cells to diagnose diseases, including determining the presence of cancer from surgical samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a specialist who examines tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a specialist who examines tissue samples under a microscope to identify cancer types and grades after biopsies are performed.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a specialized doctor who examines tissue and cell samples to diagnose diseases, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoma is a type of cancer that affects the immune system, which can escalate the risk of developing other cancers such as Squamous Cell Carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoma is a type of cancer that originates in the lymphatic system, particularly affecting lymphocytes, a subtype of white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoma refers to a type of cancer that affects the lymphatic system, related to lymph nodes and lymphatic tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer characterized by the overproduction of lymphocytes and is evaluated through blood tests and other diagnostics.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia is a slow-growing form of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, potentially weakening the immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)">
      <data key="d0">Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system, making individuals more susceptible to infections and cancers, including skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Cancer Center specializes in the treatment and prevention of cancer, providing expertise in managing and treating conditions like Squamous Cell Carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A facility specializing in the diagnosis, treatment, and management of cancer, providing various imaging and screening tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counselor">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counselor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Genetic Counselor provides individuals with information and support regarding genetic disorders and their implications, including cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor is a healthcare professional who helps individuals understand their genetic risks for diseases like cancer and assists in making informed health decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor is a healthcare professional who provides guidance on genetic testing and inherited cancer syndromes to patients and their families.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor is a specialist who provides advice and support on genetic risks, testing options, and implications for patients and families.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor is a trained professional who provides information and support regarding genetic conditions and testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor is a trained professional who provides information and support regarding genetic testing and hereditary cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor provides guidance to patients on genetic tests and hereditary cancer risks, helping them understand their health.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor provides information and support regarding genetic tests and implications for cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Punch Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Punch Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Skin Punch Biopsy is a procedure to obtain skin samples for diagnostic purposes, particularly in identifying hereditary conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical procedure performed to remove a small piece of skin and surrounding tissue for diagnostic examination, often done with a specialized tool.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure where a small piece of skin and connective tissue is removed for testing, typically done in a doctor's office or hospital.&lt;SEP&gt;A skin punch biopsy is a medical procedure that removes a small piece of skin to evaluate for genetic predisposition to leukemia and other conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A skin punch biopsy is a medical procedure used to obtain a sample of skin for testing genetic predisposition to leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excision Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Excision Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure that involves the removal of all or part of a lesion to check for cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical procedure where all or part of a lesion is removed to assess for cancerous cells, typically performed in a clinical setting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI">
      <data key="d0">MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical imaging technique that uses powerful magnets and radio waves to produce detailed images of organs and tissues inside the body.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) scan is a diagnostic procedure that uses magnetic fields to create detailed images of internal body structures.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) is a medical imaging technique that uses strong magnets and radio waves to create detailed images of organs and tissues inside the body.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI is a diagnostic imaging technique using magnetic fields and radio waves to visualize internal structures, helpful in staging anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI is a medical imaging technique that produces detailed images of the organs and tissues in the body, often combined with PET for better diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI is a medical imaging technique used for staging rectal cancer and assessing its extent within the rectum and surrounding lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI is a non-invasive imaging technique using magnets and radio waves to create detailed images of the body's internal structures, particularly useful for visualizing cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI is a non-radiation imaging technique that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of soft tissues inside the body.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI is an imaging technique that uses magnetic fields to create detailed images of the thyroid and neck.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI utilizes magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of abdominal and pelvic areas, useful for evaluating ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI utilizes magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of the abdominal and pelvic areas, useful for evaluating ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is a medical imaging technique used to visualize detailed internal structures of the body, useful for various diagnoses.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is a medical imaging technique used to visualize detailed internal structures of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a medical imaging technique used to assess organs, including for indications of colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a medical imaging technique used to visualize detailed internal structures, particularly useful for assessing rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a scanning technique primarily used to view soft tissues like the brain, useful in detecting tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC)">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A common form of skin cancer that arises mainly on sun-exposed areas of the skin, typically treatable if caught early.&lt;SEP&gt;A common type of skin cancer that occurs more frequently in light-skinned individuals, primarily in sun-exposed areas, and is treatable if detected early.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computed Tomography (CT) Scan">
      <data key="d0">Computed Tomography (CT) Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A CT scan is a detailed imaging procedure that helps determine the size and extent of cancer within the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A CT scan is an imaging technique that uses multiple X-ray images to create cross-sectional images of the body, typically performed more quickly than an MRI scan.&lt;SEP&gt;An imaging procedure that utilizes x-rays and computer algorithms to create detailed cross-sectional images of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A non-invasive imaging technique that uses strong magnetic fields and radio waves to generate detailed images of organs and tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI is an imaging method that uses strong magnets and radio waves to generate detailed images of organs and tissues inside the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is a medical imaging technique that utilizes strong magnetic fields and radio waves to generate detailed images of organs and tissues within the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses powerful magnets to generate detailed images of the inside of the body without using radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Positron Emission Tomography (PET) Scan">
      <data key="d0">Positron Emission Tomography (PET) Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET scan is an imaging test that uses radioactive substances to diagnose cancer based on cellular sugar metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan utilizes a radioactive tracer to visualize metabolic activity of cells in the body, especially useful for detecting cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A Positron Emission Tomography (PET) Scan is an imaging test that uses a radioactive substance to visualize metabolic processes in the body, particularly for cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;An imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to highlight areas of metabolic activity in the body, useful for cancer detection and monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Dye">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Dye</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A substance injected into a patient's bloodstream to enhance the visibility of internal organs during imaging studies like CT scans and MRIs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nodal Disease">
      <data key="d0">Nodal Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A condition where cancer cells have spread to lymph nodes, indicating a more advanced stage of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Exam">
      <data key="d0">Skin Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A physical examination conducted by a healthcare provider to check for new or changing skin lesions indicative of skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A minimally invasive procedure where a needle is used to extract a small sample of tissue for pathological examination.&lt;SEP&gt;A needle biopsy is a procedure to extract a small sample of tissue from a lymph node or bone marrow to check for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Local Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to squamous cell carcinoma that has not spread to lymph nodes and is considered in the early stages of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to squamous cell carcinoma that has spread to nearby lymph nodes, indicating a more serious stage of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that can arise from certain genetic syndromes, old wounds, or scars, and may be harder to treat when it presents as Marjolin’s ulcer.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that can be treated with various methods, including surgery and radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Skin Cancer is a type of skin cancer that can be classified into local, regional, and metastatic forms, with varying treatment options based on risk assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The health care team consists of medical professionals involved in diagnosing and treating patients with follicular lymphoma, including doctors, nurses, and specialists.&lt;SEP&gt;The health care team consists of various medical professionals who collaborate to provide comprehensive care and treatment for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The health care team consists of various medical professionals, including oncologists, surgeons, and nurses, who provide comprehensive care for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The health care team involves various medical professionals such as oncologists, dermatologists, and nurses who provide comprehensive care for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dermatologists">
      <data key="d0">Dermatologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dermatologists are doctors specialized in diagnosing and treating skin conditions, including skin cancer, and perform skin exams.&lt;SEP&gt;Dermatologists are doctors specialized in diagnosing and treating skin conditions, including skin cancers, and performing skin exams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Mohs Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohs surgeons are dermatologists with advanced training in Mohs surgery, removing skin cancer tumors while ensuring complete excision with microscope assistance.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs surgeons are dermatologists with advanced training in Mohs surgery, specialized in removing cancerous skin tumors and reconstructing the affected area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical oncologists specialize in diagnosing and surgically treating cancerous tumors, including those that have metastasized beyond the skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical oncologists specialize in diagnosing and surgically treating cancerous tumors, including those that have spread beyond the skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Medical Oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical oncologists are specialists in prescribing chemotherapy and other medications to treat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical oncologists specialize in prescribing chemotherapy and other medications for cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologists">
      <data key="d0">Pathologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologists are medical experts who study tissue and cell samples obtained from biopsies to determine the characteristics and stage of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologists study tissue and cells obtained during biopsies to determine the stage and characteristics of cancerous tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plastic Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Plastic Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Plastic surgeons are involved in both removing cancerous tumors and reconstructing the affected areas post-surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic surgeons perform reconstructive surgery post-cancer removal, restoring function and appearance to the affected areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck surgeons deal with surgical interventions related to cancers of the head and neck region, including the ears, nose, and throat.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck surgeons treat both cancerous and noncancerous conditions in the head and neck region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation oncologists are specialists trained in administering radiation therapy as a treatment for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation oncologists use various types of radiation therapy to treat cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharmacists">
      <data key="d0">Pharmacists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Pharmacists are healthcare professionals trained to prepare and dispense medications, providing information about their proper use and side effects, particularly in cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Pharmacists prepare and dispense medication and provide information on proper use and potential side effects, particularly for cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geneticists">
      <data key="d0">Geneticists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Geneticists study inherited traits and advise on genetic disorders related to cancer risk, counseling families at risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Geneticists study inherited traits and assist in diagnosing genetic disorders related to cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Nurses">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Nurses</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncology nurses provide hands-on care to cancer patients, assisting with therapies and managing patient well-being.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncology nurses provide hands-on care, managing treatment side effects, administering therapies, and supporting patients through their cancer journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncology nurses provide hands-on patient care, including administering therapies, managing care, and supporting patients throughout the treatment process.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncology nurses provide patient care throughout cancer treatment, including managing therapies and supporting patient needs during recovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Photodynamic Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Photodynamic Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Photodynamic therapy is a treatment method using a light-sensitive drug that kills cancer cells when activated by specific light exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Photodynamic therapy is an innovative treatment that uses light-sensitive drugs to kill cancer cells upon exposure to specific wavelengths of light.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cryotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Cryotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Cryotherapy involves freezing cancerous cells to destroy them and is effective in treating precancerous and cancerous skin conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryotherapy is a procedure that freezes and destroys precancerous and cancerous cells, often using liquid nitrogen.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryotherapy is a treatment method that freezes and destroys cancerous or abnormal skin cells using cold agents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topical Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Topical Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Topical therapies are medications applied directly to the skin to treat skin cancers, including creams like 5-fluorouracil and imiquimod.&lt;SEP&gt;Topical therapies are medications applied directly to the skin, such as creams used to treat early squamous cell skin cancer or actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An oncologist is a doctor who specializes in diagnosing and treating cancer, coordinating the care for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical doctor who specializes in diagnosing and treating cancer patients, advising them on their treatment and care options.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical doctor who specializes in diagnosing and treating cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical professional specialized in diagnosing and treating cancer, responsible for performing physical exams and managing patient follow-up care.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a physician specializing in the diagnosis and treatment of cancer, working closely with a care team to stage cancer and plan treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-Fluorouracil">
      <data key="d0">5-Fluorouracil</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">5-Fluorouracil is a chemotherapy cream applied directly to the skin for treating early-stage squamous cell skin cancer and actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imiquimod">
      <data key="d0">Imiquimod</data>
      <data key="d1">chemical</data>
      <data key="d2">Imiquimod is a topical medication that acts as an immune response modifier to treat superficial skin cancers like squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Imiquimod is an immune response modifier cream used for treating abnormal skin growths by stimulating the immune system to damage these abnormal cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-fluorouracil">
      <data key="d0">5-fluorouracil</data>
      <data key="d1">chemical</data>
      <data key="d2">5-fluorouracil, commonly known as 5-FU, is a chemotherapy agent applied directly to the skin for treating actinic keratoses, distinct from traditional chemotherapy administered via the bloodstream.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Calcipotriene">
      <data key="d0">Calcipotriene</data>
      <data key="d1">chemical</data>
      <data key="d2">Calcipotriene, marketed as Sorilux and Dovonex, is a vitamin D derivative used in conjunction with 5-fluorouracil to treat actinic keratosis by activating the skin’s immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tirbanibulin">
      <data key="d0">Tirbanibulin</data>
      <data key="d1">chemical</data>
      <data key="d2">Tirbanibulin, known as Klisyri, is a microtubule inhibitor used topically to kill fast-growing abnormal skin cells associated with actinic keratosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperkeratosis">
      <data key="d0">Hyperkeratosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperkeratosis refers to the thickening of the outer layer of skin, often associated with certain skin conditions, including actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curettage">
      <data key="d0">Curettage</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Curettage is a surgical technique that involves scraping away abnormal skin using a spoon-shaped tool.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrodessication">
      <data key="d0">Electrodessication</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrodessication involves scraping off the tumor and then cauterizing the area with an electric needle to kill any remaining cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrodessication involves scraping the visible tumor and using an electric needle to cauterize the base of the wound to kill remaining cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Mohs Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohs Surgery is a specialized surgical procedure recommended for high-risk or very high-risk cases of Squamous Cell Skin Cancer to ensure complete removal of the cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs surgery is a precise surgical technique for excising skin cancer while minimizing damage to surrounding healthy tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs surgery is a precise surgical technique used to excise skin cancer, ensuring the complete removal of cancerous tissues while preserving healthy skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs surgery is a specialized surgical technique used to treat skin cancer by removing cancerous tissue layer by layer and examining it microscopically.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB) is a surgical procedure to remove and examine lymph nodes to determine cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;A sentinel lymph node biopsy is a surgical procedure that examines lymph nodes to check for cancer spread from primary tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB or SNB) is a surgical procedure performed to remove sentinel lymph nodes and assess if cancer has metastasized.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB or SNB) is a surgical procedure to remove sentinel lymph nodes to check for cancer spread during surgeries like mastectomy or lumpectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel lymph node biopsy is a focused surgical technique to identify the first lymph nodes that are likely to be affected by cancer for testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel lymph node biopsy is a procedure that removes and examines the first lymph nodes that cancer may spread to from a primary tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d0">Standard Surgical Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard surgical excision involves the removal of a tumor along with surrounding normal tissue, often performed in a medical office.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node dissection is a surgery intended to remove lymph nodes that may contain cancer cells, frequently done in conjunction with treatments for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node dissection is a surgical procedure performed during cancer surgeries to remove and test lymph nodes for the presence of cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node dissection is a surgical procedure that involves removing lymph nodes to check for cancer spread, which is a common practice in cervical cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node dissection is a surgical procedure that involves removing lymph nodes to treat cancer spread, notable for its role when APR is not performed.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node dissection is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of lymph nodes to examine for cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node dissection is an extensive surgical procedure to remove lymph nodes to evaluate for cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topical Keratolytics">
      <data key="d0">Topical Keratolytics</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Topical keratolytics are solutions used to soften and remove thickened skin, often utilized in managing actinic keratosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cisplatin">
      <data key="d0">Cisplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cisplatin is a chemotherapy drug commonly used in cancer treatment that can cause various side effects including kidney damage and hearing issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a chemotherapy drug that belongs to the platinum-based drugs used in the treatment of various cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a chemotherapy drug used to kill fast-growing cancer cells, often employed in treating squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum chemotherapy drug used for treating squamous cell skin cancer and other types of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug commonly used for treating bladder cancer, known for its effectiveness and potential kidney damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug commonly used for various cancers, including cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug commonly used in the treatment of cervical cancer and is administered in cycles during chemoradiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug commonly used to treat various cancers and can cause side effects such as kidney damage and hearing loss.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug commonly used to treat various cancers, including cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug frequently used in treating cervical cancer, known for its effectiveness in cancer cell disruption.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is an intravenous chemotherapy drug used in combination with 5-FU for treating anal cancer, working to damage the DNA of cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carboplatin">
      <data key="d0">Carboplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carboplatin is a chemotherapy agent used as a standard treatment for ovarian cancer, often combined with paclitaxel.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is a chemotherapy medication used in combination with other drugs to treat ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is a platinum-based drug used in chemotherapy to treat various cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is an alternative chemotherapy drug used in cases where cisplatin is unsuitable, utilized in cervical cancer treatment protocols.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is an alternative platinum-based chemotherapy drug used when cisplatin is too harsh or contraindicated in cervical cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is another chemotherapy drug that is part of the treatment regimen for squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is another platinum-based chemotherapy drug used in combination treatments for various types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="General Anesthesia">
      <data key="d0">General Anesthesia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">General Anesthesia is a medical procedure used during surgeries to render the patient unconscious and pain-free.&lt;SEP&gt;General anesthesia is a medically induced state of unconsciousness used during surgical procedures to prevent pain and awareness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External Beam Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">External Beam Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) directs a beam of radiation at the tumor from a machine for effective treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;External beam radiation therapy (EBRT) is the most common form of radiation used to treat bladder cancer, targeting tumor areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brachytherapy is a form of internal radiation therapy where radioactive material is placed directly inside or near the tumor, often used in cervical cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of radiation therapy used to treat cancer, often recommended in conjunction with surgery for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of radiation therapy where a radiation source is placed close to the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of radiation therapy where a radioactive source is placed inside or close to the cancerous area.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a localized form of radiation therapy used after surgical procedures to target cervical cancer, particularly effective for treating certain stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a localized form of radiation treatment that involves placing radioactive material directly inside or next to the tumor in the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a localized radiation treatment that involves placing radioactive material directly inside or near a tumor to maximize the dose to the cancer while minimizing damage to surrounding tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a localized radiation treatment where radioactive material is placed inside the body, specifically targeting cervical tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a radiation therapy that involves placing radioactive material directly at or near the tumor site, effective for treating localized cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is an internal radiation treatment that involves placing radioactive material inside the body near the tumor, used for treating cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Radiation Oncologist is a doctor specialized in using radiation treatment to manage cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A Radiation Oncologist specializes in treating cancer with radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A Radiation Oncologist uses radiation therapy to treat cancer, often implementing it in cases of lung cancer as part of a comprehensive treatment plan.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a medical doctor who specializes in treating cancer patients using radiation therapy and managing its side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a medical doctor who specializes in using radiation therapy as a treatment for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a medical doctor who specializes in using radiation therapy to treat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a medical professional who devises treatment plans involving radiation therapy for cancer patients, including those with anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a physician who specializes in prescribing and planning radiation therapy as a treatment for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist plans and prescribes radiation therapy for cancer patients, particularly those undergoing treatment for bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist specializes in administering radiation therapy to treat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist specializes in treating cancer with radiation therapy, often part of the multidisciplinary care team for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist specializes in using radiation therapy as a treatment for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist specializes in using radiation therapy to treat cancer, particularly for patients with cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anesthesia">
      <data key="d0">Anesthesia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anesthesia is a medical intervention that renders a patient unconscious or numb to pain during surgical procedures, such as biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Anesthesia is a medical practice used to prevent pain during surgery, allowing patients to undergo procedures without conscious awareness.&lt;SEP&gt;Anesthesia refers to medications given to prevent pain during surgical procedures, including biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Anesthesia refers to the medical practice of providing pain relief during surgical procedures, important in cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Anesthesia refers to the medications used to numb pain or induce sleep during medical procedures, such as a prostate biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;Anesthesia refers to the use of medications to induce a loss of sensation or consciousness during medical procedures, making patients more comfortable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wound Healing">
      <data key="d0">Wound Healing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Wound Healing refers to the process by which the skin and underlying tissues recover after surgical procedures or injuries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation Treatment is a cancer therapy utilizing high-energy radiation to kill cancer cells or shrink tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation treatment is a common therapy for cancer patients, which can have side effects such as weakening bones in the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cell Nevus Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Nevus Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal Cell Nevus Syndrome is a genetic condition that increases the risk of developing skin cancer, particularly basal cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Xeroderma Pigmentosum">
      <data key="d0">Xeroderma Pigmentosum</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Xeroderma Pigmentosum is a rare genetic disorder that increases sensitivity to ultraviolet (UV) light, raising the risk of skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Oncology">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Oncology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation Oncology is a medical specialty focused on treating cancer with radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adjuvant Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Adjuvant Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adjuvant Therapy is treatment given after the primary treatment to reduce the risk of cancer recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Adjuvant therapy is a treatment given after surgery to eliminate any remaining cancer cells and reduce the risk of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation Side Effects refer to the adverse effects experienced during or after radiation treatment, affecting skin and surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Targeted Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Targeted therapies are systemic treatments that attack specific cancer cell receptors to interfere with cancer growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapies directly attack specific types of receptors on cancer cells, with EGFR inhibitors being notable examples for squamous cell skin cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy aims at specific genetic markers in bladder cancer cells to treat the disease more effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy involves using medications that specifically target cancer cell mutations or features to treat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a cancer treatment approach that specifically targets cancer cell pathways or proteins, minimizing damage to healthy cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a cancer treatment that uses drugs or other substances to precisely identify and attack cancer cells, minimizing damage to normal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a form of systemic therapy that focuses on specific characteristics of cancer cells, potentially used for cervical cancer with distinct biomarkers.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a newer form of systemic therapy designed to attack specific features of cancer cells, potentially an option for advanced cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a specialized form of cancer treatment that targets specific features of cancer cells to inhibit their growth and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a treatment option for cancer that aims at specific genetic markers in cancer cells, potentially leading to better treatment outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a treatment that uses drugs or other substances to precisely identify and attack cancer cells, relevant for CLL based on specific biomarkers.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a type of cancer treatment that uses drugs or other substances to precisely identify and attack cancer cells, minimizing damage to normal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy utilizes specific drugs that attack cancer cells while minimizing damage to healthy cells, reducing side effects compared to traditional chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EGFR Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">EGFR Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">EGFR inhibitors are targeted therapies that block the epidermal growth factor receptor, preventing cancer cell growth.&lt;SEP&gt;EGFR inhibitors target the epidermal growth factor receptor on cancer cells, blocking their growth and proliferation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cetuximab">
      <data key="d0">Cetuximab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cetuximab, also known as Erbitux, is an EGFR inhibitor used to treat squamous cell skin cancer among other cancer types.&lt;SEP&gt;Cetuximab, known as Erbitux, is an EGFR inhibitor used to treat squamous cell skin cancer and various other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapy boosts the immune system’s ability to fight cancer cells and includes treatments like PD-1 inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy enhances the immune system's ability to detect and destroy cancer cells, with PD-1 inhibitors being a significant treatment option for squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy harnesses the body's immune system to fight cancer, using drugs such as rituximab to target cancer cells specifically.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy involves using immune checkpoint inhibitors to treat certain anal cancer patients, potentially avoiding the need for radical surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a cancer treatment that leverages the body's immune system to fight cancer cells, including treatments utilizing immune checkpoint inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment approach that utilizes the body's immune system to fight cancer cells and is a potential option for advanced prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment designed to enhance the body's natural defenses against cancer, utilizing substances to stimulate or suppress the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment method that utilizes the body's immune system to fight cancer, often involving immune checkpoint inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment that leverages the body's immune system to fight bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment that uses the body's immune system to fight cancer, potentially producing various side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment that utilizes the body's immune system to fight cancer, often by enhancing or restoring immune responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment that utilizes the body’s immune system to fight cancer, which can include antibodies or immune checkpoint inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a type of cancer treatment that harnesses the body's immune system to fight cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a type of cancer treatment that helps the immune system fight cancer more effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is an emerging treatment that harnesses the immune system to fight cancer and may be used for treating recurrent or metastatic cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is an innovative cancer treatment that leverages the immune system to combat cancer cells, applicable for recurrent or advanced cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy utilizes the body's immune system to combat cancer cells, employing strategies like checkpoint inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-1 Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">PD-1 Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-1 inhibitors are a class of immunotherapy drugs that block the programmed cell death protein 1, helping the immune system fight cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;PD-1 inhibitors are a type of immunotherapy drug that block the PD-1 protein on immune cells, enhancing the immune system's response to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cemiplimab">
      <data key="d0">Cemiplimab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cemiplimab (Libtayo) is an immunotherapy treatment recommended for cancers with specific biomarkers, representing a targeted approach in cancer therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cemiplimab, branded as Libtayo, is a PD-1 inhibitor used for treating squamous cell skin cancer and other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Cemiplimab, branded as Libtayo, is a PD-1 inhibitor used in the treatment of squamous cell skin cancer and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pembrolizumab">
      <data key="d0">Pembrolizumab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is an immune checkpoint inhibitor used in the treatment of cancers with specific biomarkers, often as part of a combination therapy regime.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab is a checkpoint inhibitor used in immunotherapy, particularly for dMMR/MSI-H colorectal cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab, known as Keytruda, is a PD-1 inhibitor that is used to treat various cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab, known as Keytruda, is another PD-1 inhibitor used in cancer immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. Food and Drug Administration">
      <data key="d0">U.S. Food and Drug Administration</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The FDA is a federal agency responsible for protecting public health by ensuring the safety and efficacy of drugs, including those tested through clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a federal agency responsible for the approval and regulation of drugs, devices, and treatment approaches based on the results of clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a regulatory body responsible for approving drugs, devices, and treatment approaches that have passed clinical trial evaluations.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is responsible for the approval of new drugs and treatments post-evaluation in clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer is a resource provided by NCCN to offer patients essential information and guidance throughout their cancer treatment journey.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer are informational resources intended to help patients and caregivers understand cancer and navigate their care.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer are resources aimed at educating and assisting patients in understanding their cancer treatment options and decision-making processes.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer are resources designed to help patients understand their cancer treatment options and the medical tests they may undergo.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer are resources that provide comprehensive information on cancer care and support for patients and their families.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer offer resources and guidelines to support patients diagnosed with various cancers, including gastric cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer provide information aimed at patients and young adults seeking treatment for acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) and other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer provide information and resources for patients about cancer types, treatments, and supportive care.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer provide resources and information for patients undergoing cancer treatments and managing side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer provide resources and information to patients about cancer treatment options and supportive care.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer provide valuable resources and information for patients facing cancer treatments and decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer provides resources and guidelines for cancer patients, focusing on various cancer types and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer provides resources and treatment guidelines for cancer patients, particularly those diagnosed with gastric adenocarcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Side effects are adverse symptoms or reactions experienced by patients undergoing cancer treatment, such as nausea and vomiting.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are secondary, typically undesirable effects of a medical treatment that can impact a patient's quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are secondary, typically undesirable effects of medical treatment that occur alongside the intended effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are undesirable effects caused by cancer treatment, which can vary depending on the treatment type and patient response.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are unwanted effects resulting from treatment, including symptoms like neck pain, nausea, or changes in taste during thyroid therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are unwanted symptoms or reactions resulting from medical treatment, such as those caused by chemotherapy or radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects refer to the unintended and often adverse effects experienced by patients undergoing treatments such as intravesical therapy or chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects refer to unwanted symptoms or reactions experienced as a result of treatment for conditions such as Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects refer to various adverse reactions that patients may experience as a result of cancer treatments, including chemotherapy and immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Cancer Centers">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Cancer Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Cancer Centers are accredited institutions in the U.S. that specialize in providing comprehensive cancer care, including access to clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Cancer Centers are designated facilities in the U.S. that provide comprehensive cancer care and may conduct clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Cancer Centers are established centers providing clinical trials for cancer treatment options, with a focus on quality care.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Cancer Centers are part of a network that provides information and access to cancer treatment and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Cancer Centers refers to the network of leading cancer treatment facilities that abide by the recommendations of the National Comprehensive Cancer Network.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Cancer Centers are institutions in the United States specializing in cancer treatment and research, providing access to clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Cancer Centers are specialized facilities in the United States that focus on cancer treatment, research, and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Cancer Institute">
      <data key="d0">National Cancer Institute</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCI is an organization that leads efforts in cancer research and provides resources and information regarding cancer treatment and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) conducts and supports cancer research and provides information about clinical trials and treatment options to patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a federal agency that conducts and supports research on cancer treatment and provides information about clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a part of the National Institutes of Health and conducts and supports cancer research, including clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute is a government agency in the U.S. that conducts research and provides information on various cancers, including follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. National Library of Medicine">
      <data key="d0">U.S. National Library of Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) provides resources and databases related to clinical trials and medical research.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine oversees clinicaltrials.gov, a resource for finding clinical trials worldwide.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Information Service">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Information Service</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cancer Information Service provides information and support regarding cancer and clinical trials to patients and families.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="squamous cell skin cancer">
      <data key="d0">squamous cell skin cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell skin cancer (CSCC) is a common type of skin cancer characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the squamous cells, which compose most of the skin's upper layers.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer arising from squamous cells, typically associated with sun exposure and having various risk categories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="photodynamic therapy">
      <data key="d0">photodynamic therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Photodynamic therapy is a treatment option that uses light energy to activate a drug that targets and destroys cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cryotherapy">
      <data key="d0">cryotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cryotherapy is a medical treatment that involves the application of extreme cold to destroy abnormal tissues, often used for treating skin lesions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="topical therapy">
      <data key="d0">topical therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Topical therapy involves the application of medication directly to the skin, often used in treating skin cancers like actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Risk Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk assessment involves evaluating the characteristics of Squamous Cell Skin Cancer to categorize it as low, high, or very high risk, influencing treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk assessment is a process used to evaluate the likelihood of squamous cell skin cancer recurrence or progression based on specific tumor characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Placebo">
      <data key="d0">Placebo</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A placebo is an inactive substance or treatment that is often used in clinical trials as a control to compare the effects of a real treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A placebo is an inactive substance used in clinical trials as a control to test the efficacy of a new treatment, often to determine its effect compared to no active treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Standard Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard treatment refers to established or widely accepted therapies used for cancer care before considering experimental treatments or clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemical Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Chemical Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy is a type of cancer treatment that uses drugs to kill or slow the growth of cancer cells, often used when cancer has spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Overall Risk Category">
      <data key="d0">Overall Risk Category</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Overall risk category is a classification system to indicate the likelihood of cancer recurrence based on tumor characteristics and location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Disease">
      <data key="d0">Local Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local disease refers to cancer that has not spread to nearby lymph nodes, typically treated with surgical options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Risk Disease">
      <data key="d0">High-Risk Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-risk disease indicates a classification of cancer that has a higher likelihood of return or spread after treatment, guiding treatment choices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Very High-Risk Disease">
      <data key="d0">Very High-Risk Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Very high-risk disease indicates an even greater risk of recurrence and metastasis, requiring more aggressive treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Features">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Features</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor features include characteristics like size, shape, and edge definition that help determine the cancer type and risk level.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANCER (6237)">
      <data key="d0">CANCER (6237)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">CANCER (6237) is a phone service provided by the National Cancer Institute to help individuals find information and resources related to cancer treatment and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs surgery">
      <data key="d0">Mohs surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohs surgery is a precise surgical technique used to treat skin cancer by removing cancerous tissue layer by layer and is particularly effective for high-risk or very high-risk CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PDEMA">
      <data key="d0">PDEMA</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">PDEMA (Peripheral Dermal Excision and Mohs Surgery) is a surgical treatment option recommended for patients with high-risk squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curettage and electrodesiccation">
      <data key="d0">Curettage and electrodesiccation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Curettage and electrodesiccation (C&amp;E) is a treatment option for low-risk CSCC involving scraping away cancerous tissue and applying electricity to destroy remaining cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="skin biopsy">
      <data key="d0">skin biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A skin biopsy is a medical procedure where a small sample of skin tissue is removed for analysis to diagnose conditions such as squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)">
      <data key="d0">human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system, making individuals more susceptible to certain cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AIDS">
      <data key="d0">AIDS</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">AIDS (Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome) is a condition that significantly weakens the immune system and increases susceptibility to various infections, including HPV.&lt;SEP&gt;AIDS is the advanced stage of HIV infection when the immune system is severely damaged, increasing the risk of developing cancers like squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDC.gov">
      <data key="d0">CDC.gov</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">CDC.gov is the official website of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, offering public health information, including tobacco use resources.&lt;SEP&gt;The CDC.gov provides resources and information on tobacco use and cessation support for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SmokeFree.gov">
      <data key="d0">SmokeFree.gov</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">SmokeFree.gov is a website that offers support and resources for individuals looking to quit smoking, particularly important for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;SmokeFree.gov is a website that provides resources and support for individuals looking to quit smoking or vaping.&lt;SEP&gt;SmokeFree.gov is a website that provides resources for individuals seeking support to quit smoking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BeTobaccoFree.gov">
      <data key="d0">BeTobaccoFree.gov</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">BeTobaccoFree.gov is a government resource aimed at helping individuals understand the risks of tobacco use and how to quit.&lt;SEP&gt;BeTobaccoFree.gov is a government website dedicated to helping people quit tobacco use and promoting tobacco-free living.&lt;SEP&gt;BeTobaccoFree.gov is an initiative aimed at providing educational resources and support for quitting tobacco, which is crucial for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Grade">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor grade is a classification describing how abnormal the cancer cells appear compared to normal cells, affecting the risk of tumor spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Type">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Type</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor type refers to the specific classification of cancer, which is determined through histological analysis of tissue samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Connective Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Connective Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Connective tissue in tumors is an indicator signifying the aggressiveness of cancer and potential risk of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphovascular Invasion">
      <data key="d0">Lymphovascular Invasion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphovascular invasion indicates the presence of cancer cells in lymphatics or blood vessels, pointing to a higher likelihood of cancer spreading.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weakened Immune System">
      <data key="d0">Weakened Immune System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A weakened immune system significantly increases the risk of developing various cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Long-term Corticosteroid Use">
      <data key="d0">Long-term Corticosteroid Use</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Long-term corticosteroid use is associated with immunosuppression and increased susceptibility to cancer recurrences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Organ Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Organ Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Receiving an organ transplant often involves immunosuppressive therapy, which can elevate cancer risk such as for squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor">
      <data key="d0">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune checkpoint inhibitors are a form of immunotherapy designed to help the immune system recognize and attack cancer cells more effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-based Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-based Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-based chemotherapy includes agents like carboplatin, cisplatin, and oxaliplatin, which can damage kidneys and have other side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is a form of systemic treatment for cancer that uses platinum compounds to disrupt cancer cell growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is a systemic therapy option for patients with Squamous Cell Skin Cancer, particularly effective when combined with radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is a type of systemic therapy that utilizes platinum-containing compounds to treat various cancers, particularly effective when combined with radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cemiplimab-rwlc (Libtayo)">
      <data key="d0">Cemiplimab-rwlc (Libtayo)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Cemiplimab-rwlc, marketed as Libtayo, is an immunotherapy option recommended for certain patients with Squamous Cell Skin Cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trial</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A clinical trial is a research study conducted to evaluate new treatments or therapies in patients with various conditions, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study that tests how well new medical approaches work in people, including new drugs, therapies, or procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study that tests new treatments for Squamous Cell Skin Cancer, which can include chemotherapy or immunotherapy options.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study that tests new treatments or therapies in patients to determine their efficacy and safety.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study that tests new treatments or therapies in patients to determine their safety and effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that test how well new medical approaches work in people, specifically for cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that test new treatments, offering patients access to experimental therapies for conditions like anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials offer new treatment options and therapies for glioma patients, providing access to cutting-edge research.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d0">High-Risk CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-risk Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC) refers to a classification of skin cancer that has aggressive features and may require advanced treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Very High-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Very High-Risk CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Very high-risk Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC) is an advanced classification of skin cancer with significant features indicating a likelihood of aggressive behavior and poor prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trials for Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trials for Systemic Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical trials for systemic therapy include experimental treatment regimens that aim to test the effectiveness of new cancer therapies including chemotherapy and immunotherapy for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Factors">
      <data key="d0">Risk Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk factors are conditions or behaviors that increase the likelihood of developing a disease, such as smoking, exposure to pollutants, and family history for lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors are conditions or traits that increase an individual's likelihood of developing cancer, such as smoking, exposure to secondhand smoke, and family history.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors for Squamous Cell Skin Cancer include characteristics that may influence the likelihood of developing or the aggressiveness of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curettage and Electrodesiccation (C&amp;E)">
      <data key="d0">Curettage and Electrodesiccation (C&amp;E)</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Curettage and Electrodesiccation (C&amp;E) is a less invasive treatment option for low-risk Squamous Cell Skin Cancer, involving scraping and drying out the cancerous tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excision">
      <data key="d0">Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Excision is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the cancerous lesion and surrounding healthy tissue, typically used for local CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC)">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer arising from squamous cells, which can metastasize and affect nearby lymph nodes or other body parts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy (SBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SBRT is a specialized form of radiation therapy that delivers precise, high doses of radiation to small tumor regions, typically administered over a few sessions.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic body radiotherapy is an advanced radiation technique that delivers high doses of radiation precisely to small tumor areas, typically given over a few sessions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph nodes are small structures that are part of the lymphatic system, involved in filtering harmful substances and are often sites for cancer metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer surgery refers to medical procedures performed to remove tumors and affected lymph nodes as a primary treatment for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC)">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic squamous cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer that can spread to other parts of the body through blood and lymphatic systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Therapy Options">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Therapy Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic therapy options provide various treatment strategies for cancer, including chemotherapy and targeted therapy when surgery or radiation isn't viable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a type of blood cancer caused by a specific abnormal gene, the BCR::ABL1, resulting from chromosomal changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a type of blood cancer characterized by the presence of the Philadelphia chromosome and the BCR::ABL1 gene.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Myeloid Leukemia is a type of cancer that arises from blood stem cells and is characterized by an increased number of myeloid progenitor cells. It can progress to a more advanced phase known as blast phase CML (BP-CML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia Chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia Chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">The Philadelphia chromosome is a shortened chromosome 22 associated with Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML), resulting from a genetic abnormality.&lt;SEP&gt;The Philadelphia chromosome is a shortened chromosome 22 that contains the BCR::ABL1 gene and is a hallmark of CML.&lt;SEP&gt;The Philadelphia chromosome is an abnormal chromosome associated with Chronic Myeloid Leukemia, resulting from a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood">
      <data key="d0">Blood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood is a bodily fluid that can be sampled for karyotype analysis and contains various types of cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a connective tissue comprising plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, playing a crucial role in overall health and immune function.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a tissue composed of various cells that transport oxygen and nutrients while playing a crucial role in immune response and preventing bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a tissue comprising various cells that transport oxygen and nutrients in the body while playing a key role in the immune system and bleeding prevention.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a tissue that transports oxygen and nutrients throughout the body and is composed of plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a vital fluid in the human body that carries oxygen and nutrients to cells and removes waste products. It plays a significant role in the immune system and in preventing bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a vital tissue made of plasma and various cell types that carries oxygen and nutrients while playing a major role in immune function and bleeding control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood stem cells are immature cells in the bone marrow that can develop into various types of blood cells, including red blood cells and lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood stem cells are precursor cells in the bone marrow responsible for producing all types of blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood stem cells are the precursors to all types of blood cells and undergo multiple stages to differentiate into red blood cells, white blood cells, or platelets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow is a soft tissue found within bones where hematopoietic stem cells produce red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is a soft, spongy tissue inside bones where blood cells are formed, including the abnormal lymphoblasts seen in ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is a spongy tissue in bones responsible for producing blood cells, including the lymphocytes that are involved in ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the flexible tissue found in the interior of bones, critical for producing blood cells, including lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the organ where blood cells are produced. It contains hematopoietic stem cells that differentiate into various types of blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the soft tissue found in the center of bones where blood cells are produced, and it is critical in diagnosing and treating AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the soft tissue inside bones responsible for producing blood cells, including lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the soft tissue inside bones where blood cells are produced and can be tested for diseases like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the soft, spongy tissue inside bones where new blood cells are produced, including lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the soft, spongy tissue inside bones where new blood cells, including lymphocytes, are produced.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the sponge-like tissue in the center of most bones where blood cells are produced.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones that produces blood cells and is used for karyotype sampling.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones that produces blood cells, and where cancer can occasionally be detected in lymphoma patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones where blood cells are produced, critical for hematopoietic stem cell functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones where blood cells are produced, playing a crucial role in stem cell transplantation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones where blood cells, including lymphocytes, are produced and matured.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones where blood stem cells and various types of blood cells are produced.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones where blood stem cells mature into various blood cells through differentiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the tissue inside bones responsible for blood cell production; it is often affected in leukemia, including ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CSCC">
      <data key="d0">CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CSCC (Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma) is a common form of skin cancer that may require frequent medical follow-ups based on the risk level of the patient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Levels">
      <data key="d0">Risk Levels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk levels refer to classifications of patients based on their likelihood of developing further skin cancer, influencing the frequency of medical checks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Experts">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Experts</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN experts are healthcare professionals who provide guidelines and recommendations for best practices in cancer treatment and follow-up care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood cells include various types such as red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, each performing essential functions in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blood Cells (RBCs)">
      <data key="d0">Red Blood Cells (RBCs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Red blood cells (RBCs) transport oxygen throughout the body and are vital for maintaining metabolic functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cells (WBCs)">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cells (WBCs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">White blood cells (WBCs) are crucial for the immune response, helping the body fight infections and diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platelets (PLTs)">
      <data key="d0">Platelets (PLTs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platelets (PLTs) are cell fragments involved in blood clotting and preventing excessive bleeding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Differentiation">
      <data key="d0">Differentiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Differentiation is the process by which blood stem cells develop into various types of blood cells, essential to hematopoiesis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Decisions">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Decisions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment decisions in cancer care involve assessing various options based on guidelines to improve patient outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment decisions refer to the options available to manage and treat cancer based on its stage and characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CML">
      <data key="d0">CML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a type of cancer that affects blood and bone marrow, characterized by the presence of the BCR::ABL1 gene which causes uncontrolled blood cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1 gene">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1 gene</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">The BCR::ABL1 gene is a fusion gene resulting from the chromosomal translocation, crucial for the diagnosis and treatment of CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Philadelphia chromosome is the abnormal chromosome 22 that is a hallmark of CML, resulting from the fusion of parts of chromosome 9 and 22.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NSCLC, or Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer, is a type of lung cancer that is more common than SCLC and is characterized by various subtypes.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC) is a category of lung cancer that can be classified into several types based on the originating cell type.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) includes various subtypes of lung cancers and is more common than SCLC.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) is the most common type of lung cancer, characterized by uncontrolled cell growth in the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung cancer">
      <data key="d0">Lung cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung cancer refers to uncontrolled cell growth in lung tissue, which can lead to tumors and spreading to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloid progenitor cells are early-stage blood cells that can differentiate into various types of non-lymphoid blood cells, playing a crucial role in the development of blood components.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells are the precursor cells that develop into various types of blood cells and are affected in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloblasts">
      <data key="d0">Myeloblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloblasts are immature myeloid cells that are precursors to other blood cells; they are commonly referenced in the context of CML as they represent the uncontrolled growth characteristic of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature myeloid cells that are prominently involved in the pathology of CML and contribute to the disease's clinical characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that can develop into granulocytes and are often present in patients diagnosed with AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoblasts">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoblasts are immature lymphocytes that are often present in higher numbers during cases of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, indicating abnormal cell growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoblasts are immature lymphocytes that are produced excessively in ALL, leading to crowding out normal blood cell production.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoblasts are immature lymphocytes that proliferate in cases of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), impacting the normal function of blood production.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoblasts are immature lymphoid cells that can increase in number during advanced forms of CML, particularly in blast phase CML (BP-CML).&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoblasts are immature lymphoid cells that can increase in number during the blast phase of CML, indicating a severe progression of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blast Phase CML (BP-CML)">
      <data key="d0">Blast Phase CML (BP-CML)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blast phase CML is an advanced form of chronic myeloid leukemia characterized by a significant increase in blast cells, leading to more complex treatment requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CML Treatment">
      <data key="d0">CML Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CML treatment focuses on inhibiting the activity of the BCR::ABL1 protein to manage the disease effectively and may include targeted therapies and supportive care strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Test Results">
      <data key="d0">Test Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Test results are critical for determining the appropriate treatment plan for CML, including the results of blood tests and possible biopsies needed for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Physician (PCP)">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Physician (PCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">PCPs are typically the first point of contact for patients, playing a crucial role in coordinating overall health care, especially for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary Care Physicians serve as the first point of contact in medical care, coordinating with specialists and managing general health for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary care physicians are often the first healthcare providers patients consult when experiencing symptoms related to anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician is a healthcare provider who plays a crucial role in coordinating care and keeping the patient informed about their health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician plays a key role in coordinating patient care, providing referrals, and assisting with general health management.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician plays a vital role in coordinating patient care and addressing overall health concerns during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN.org">
      <data key="d0">NCCN.org</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN.org is a resource for patient guidelines about different types of cancers including mouth and head/neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a website providing guidelines and information related to cancer patient care and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a website providing information and resources for cancer patients, including treatment guidelines and a feedback survey for improvement.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a website providing patient guidelines for cancer, including information on NSCLC and related treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a website that provides patient guidelines and information on cancer care, including lung cancer screening and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a website that provides patient guidelines for cancer, offering support and information regarding distress and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization providing helpful information on cancer treatment and patient care guidelines.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization providing patient guidelines and resources focused on cancer treatment and management.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization that provides guidelines and information on cancer treatment options and patient care.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is the official website providing access to guidelines and resources for cancer patients and healthcare professionals.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org offers guidelines and information for cancer patients, including resources on fertility preservation and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org provides guidelines and resources for cancer patients, emphasizing fertility preservation and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenocarcinoma is a less common type of bladder cancer that originates in the glandular cells of the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a malignant tumor that originates from adenomas and is a leading type of colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a subtype of cervical cancer that originates from glandular cells and has specific treatment protocols based on its stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of NSCLC that often forms from cells lining the alveoli and producing mucus, and it is the most common form of NSCLC.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer that begins in an adenoma and is characterized by malignant tumor growth in the colon.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer that originates in glandular tissue, with gastric adenocarcinomas being specific to the stomach lining.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer that originates in the glandular cells of the rectum, often associated with polyps.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cervical cancer that develops in glandular cells of the cervix, second to squamous cell carcinoma in prevalence.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of esophageal cancer that arises from mucus-secreting cells, commonly found in the lower esophagus, and its occurrence is increasing in North America and Western Europe.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of non-small cell lung cancer that begins in the glandular cells lining the lungs and is one of the most common forms of lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is the most common type of rectal cancer that arises from adenomatous polyps.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma of the stomach is a common form of cancer that arises from the glandular cells lining the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Large Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Large Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Large cell carcinoma is a type of NSCLC that forms from large cells found throughout the airways.&lt;SEP&gt;Large cell carcinoma is an undifferentiated type of lung cancer that can grow anywhere in the lungs, contributing to the NSCLC category.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment for NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Treatment for NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment for NSCLC includes various methods such as surgery, radiation therapy, and chemoradiation, tailored to the patient's condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pulmonologist">
      <data key="d0">Pulmonologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pulmonologist is a doctor specialized in lung diseases, responsible for diagnosing and treating patients with lung conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pulmonologist is a medical doctor specializing in respiratory system diseases, who may be involved in the care of lung cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pulmonologist is a medical specialist who manages diseases involving the lungs and respiratory tract, including lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pulmonologist is a medical doctor specialized in lung diseases and is integral to managing cases of lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pulmonologist is a medical doctor who specializes in diagnosing and treating diseases related to the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thoracic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Thoracic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Thoracic Radiologist is a specialist focusing on imaging of the thoracic region to diagnose diseases like lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A Thoracic Radiologist specializes in chest imaging, crucial for diagnosing lung-related diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A Thoracic Radiologist specializes in imaging techniques for diagnosing conditions related to the chest, including lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic radiologist is a medical expert specializing in imaging techniques to evaluate chest conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic radiologist specializes in imaging of the chest and plays a key role in diagnosing lung nodules and diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thoracic Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Thoracic Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Thoracic Surgeon is a medical specialist who performs surgeries on organs within the chest, including lung cancer operations.&lt;SEP&gt;A Thoracic Surgeon specializes in surgical procedures on the thorax, including surgeries to remove lung tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A Thoracic Surgeon specializes in surgical procedures related to the chest, including lung cancer surgeries.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic surgeon is a medical doctor who specializes in surgical procedures within the chest, including operations for lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic surgeon is a specialist who performs surgical procedures within the chest, particularly regarding lung conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Airways of the Lungs">
      <data key="d0">Airways of the Lungs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Airways of the lungs refer to the network of passages through which air travels from the trachea to the alveoli, playing a critical role in respiration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Throat">
      <data key="d0">Throat</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The throat is a crucial passageway for air leading to the windpipe, and it serves as a conduit for both air and food.&lt;SEP&gt;The throat is a muscular tube in the neck that plays a key role in breathing and swallowing, and is associated with various cancers, particularly throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Windpipe">
      <data key="d0">Windpipe</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The windpipe, or trachea, is the tube that carries air from the throat to the bronchi and is essential for breathing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchi">
      <data key="d0">Bronchi</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchi are the two primary air passages that branch off from the trachea into the lungs, facilitating airflow.&lt;SEP&gt;The bronchi are the two large air passages that branch from the trachea to the lungs, allowing airflow to each lung and facilitating gas exchange.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchioli">
      <data key="d0">Bronchioli</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchioli are smaller airways that branch from the bronchi and lead to the alveoli, playing a critical role in gas exchange.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alveoli">
      <data key="d0">Alveoli</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Alveoli are tiny air sacs at the end of bronchioli where gas exchange occurs, allowing oxygen to pass into the blood.&lt;SEP&gt;Alveoli are tiny air sacs in the lungs where gas exchange occurs, allowing oxygen to enter the bloodstream and carbon dioxide to be expelled.&lt;SEP&gt;Alveoli are tiny air sacs in the lungs where oxygen and carbon dioxide exchange takes place, and they can be impacted by lung cancer and its progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Types of NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Types of NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Types of NSCLC refer to various forms of Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer, each characterized by different cellular origins and behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stages of Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stages of Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages of Cancer describe the extent of cancer progression within the body, categorized from early to advanced stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic Cancer refers to cancer that has spread from its original site to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer is cancer that has spread from its original site to other areas of the body, often complicating treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer is cancer that has spread from its original site to other parts of the body, indicating advanced disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer is cancer that has spread from the original site to other parts of the body, indicating a more severe progression of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer is cancer that has spread to other parts of the body, making it more complex to treat.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread from its original site to other parts of the body and behaves differently than primary cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread from its original site to other parts of the body, including pancreatic cancer that has metastasized.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to the progression of esophageal cancer to distant organs such as the liver, lung, or bone, complicating treatment and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgery for Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Surgery for Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgery for Cancer involves the removal of cancerous tissues and is often the primary treatment for localized cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemoradiation">
      <data key="d0">Chemoradiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy to enhance treatment effectiveness against cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy, typically used for locally advanced cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy and radiation to enhance treatment efficacy for non-metastatic anal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy to enhance treatment effectiveness for bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy, commonly used for cervical cancer treatment in multiple stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy, commonly utilized for treating locally advanced cancers to maximize treatment efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation involves concurrent treatment with radiation therapy and chemotherapy for advanced cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy, often used concurrently to enhance the treatment of advanced cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combination treatment approach that involves the use of both chemotherapy and radiation therapy to treat anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combination treatment involving EBRT and chemotherapy, recommended for most locally advanced cervical cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combined treatment approach incorporating EBRT and chemotherapy for effectively targeting cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combined treatment of chemotherapy and radiation therapy utilized for managing cervical cancer, particularly in locally advanced cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a recommended treatment for nonmetastatic perianal cancers that do not meet criteria for local excision, involving both chemotherapy and radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a treatment strategy that combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy, often used for locally advanced cervical cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation refers to the combined use of chemotherapy and radiation therapy to treat cancer, particularly anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supportive Care">
      <data key="d0">Supportive Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supportive Care refers to services and treatments that improve the quality of life for cancer patients, addressing symptoms and needs.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care aims to relieve symptoms of cancer or the side effects of treatment, enhancing overall quality of life for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care encompasses treatments that improve the quality of life for patients, including pain management and psychological support throughout cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care encompasses various services aimed at improving the comfort and quality of life for patients with serious illness, including pain management and emotional support.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care focuses on relieving symptoms and side effects of cancer therapies, improving the quality of life for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care includes various resources aimed at improving comfort and quality of life for patients with metastatic cancer, encompassing pain management and emotional support.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care includes various services and support provided to patients with cancer, focused on alleviating symptoms and improving quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care is aimed at relieving symptoms and side effects of cancer or its treatment, improving the quality of life for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care refers to health care that aims to improve the quality of life for cancer patients by relieving symptoms and providing emotional and practical support.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care refers to treatments and interventions that aid in the management of symptoms and side effects associated with cancer treatment, enhancing overall patient quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Nodules">
      <data key="d0">Lung Nodules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung Nodules are small masses of tissue in the lungs that may require evaluation for potential cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Nodule">
      <data key="d0">Lung Nodule</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A lung nodule is a small mass of tissue in the lung, commonly found through imaging; most nodules are not cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Lung Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung cancer screening is recommended for people at high risk for lung cancer, aimed at early detection and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) is the most common type of lung cancer, with various subtypes including adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell lung cancer (SCLC) is a fast-growing cancer that arises in the neuroendocrine cells of the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pulmonary Disease">
      <data key="d0">Pulmonary Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pulmonary disease refers to conditions that affect the lungs, which may include various ailments such as lung cancer, asthma, and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) among others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the cells lining the lungs, categorized primarily into non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) and small cell lung cancer (SCLC).&lt;SEP&gt;Lung cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the lungs, most commonly occurring due to smoking, and can be categorized into types such as non-small cell lung cancer and small cell lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Images">
      <data key="d0">Lung Images</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung images are diagnostic images obtained through various imaging methods to evaluate lung conditions and identify potential issues such as nodules or cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco Smoke">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco Smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tobacco smoke is a major risk factor for lung cancer, containing over 50 known carcinogens that significantly increase the likelihood of developing lung cancer and other health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second-Hand Smoke">
      <data key="d0">Second-Hand Smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Second-hand smoke is the smoke exhaled by smokers or emitted from burning tobacco products, increasing the risk of lung cancer for non-smokers.&lt;SEP&gt;Second-hand smoke is the smoke inhaled from burning tobacco products or exhaled by smokers, contributing to increased health risks, including lung cancer for non-smokers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemopreventive Agents">
      <data key="d0">Chemopreventive Agents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemopreventive agents are substances aimed at reducing the risk of cancer, including potential drugs or dietary components that may help prevent lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radon">
      <data key="d0">Radon</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radon is a naturally occurring radioactive gas that can accumulate in homes and is known to increase the risk of lung cancer when inhaled consistently over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asbestos">
      <data key="d0">Asbestos</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Asbestos is a group of minerals used in building materials that poses serious health risks, including a significant increase in the risk of lung cancer upon exposure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Pollution">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Pollution</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Environmental pollution encompasses various contaminants in the air, such as fine particulates and gases, which can increase the risk of developing lung diseases including lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobes">
      <data key="d0">Lobes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobes are sections of the lungs; the right lung has three lobes and the left lung has two, and they play a crucial role in respiratory function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell lung cancer is a fast-growing cancer of the neuroendocrine cells in the lungs and is noted for its aggressive nature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatments">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer treatments encompass various therapies such as chemotherapy, radiation, and surgery that can affect fertility and overall reproductive health.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatments have advanced and now offer multiple options for patients, aiming to address aggressive cancers like SCLC effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatments include a variety of medical procedures aimed at treating cancer, potentially impacting fertility and overall health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lungs">
      <data key="d0">Lungs</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Lungs are another potential site for metastasis in advanced-stage prostate cancer, affecting respiratory health and management of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;The lungs are a pair of vital organs in the respiratory system responsible for gas exchange, allowing oxygen to enter the bloodstream and carbon dioxide to be expelled.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinomas are a category of cancer originating in the epithelial cells, with subtypes including squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma, which are common forms of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchus">
      <data key="d0">Bronchus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The bronchus is a major air passage that branches off the trachea, leading into the lungs, and is lined with cells that can give rise to different types of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchioles">
      <data key="d0">Bronchioles</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchioles are smaller branches of the bronchi that lead to alveoli in the lungs, where gas exchange occurs, and can also be affected by cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthcare Provider">
      <data key="d0">Healthcare Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A healthcare provider is a professional or organization that offers medical services, including diagnosis, treatment, and patient care, particularly related to head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;A healthcare provider offers medical services, diagnoses, and treatment plans for patients; they play a crucial role in cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;Healthcare providers (HCPs) are medical professionals who diagnose and manage patients' health conditions, including cancer screenings and treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondhand Smoke">
      <data key="d0">Secondhand Smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondhand smoke is the smoke that is exhaled by smokers or from burning tobacco products, and it is a significant risk factor for developing lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer screening includes tests and exams to detect cancer early when it is most treatable, particularly important for liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer screening involves tests and examinations to detect cancer at an early stage, before symptoms appear, potentially leading to more successful treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Traits">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Traits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic traits refer to inherited characteristics passed from parents to children, which can influence an individual's susceptibility to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tumor is a mass of tissue formed by an abnormal collection of cells that grow uncontrollably, common in cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;A tumor is an abnormal growth of tissue that can be benign or malignant and is key in determining cancer stage.&lt;SEP&gt;A tumor is an abnormal growth of tissue which can be benign or malignant (cancerous) and is often a central concern in oncology.&lt;SEP&gt;A tumor is an abnormal mass of tissue that can be benign or malignant; in this context, it refers to anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A tumor, in this context, is an abnormal mass of tissue that forms when cells grow uncontrollably, which can be benign or malignant (cancerous).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCLC">
      <data key="d0">SCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SCLC, or Small Cell Lung Cancer, is a form of lung cancer primarily caused by smoking cigarettes and characterized by rapid growth and difficulty in early detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interventional Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Interventional Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An Interventional Radiologist performs minimally invasive procedures guided by imaging to treat conditions like lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;An Interventional Radiologist specializes in using imaging guidance for minimally invasive treatments, including interventions for lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;An interventional radiologist is a physician who performs minimally invasive procedures guided by imaging, often used in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Medical Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Medical Oncologist is a doctor specializing in the treatment of cancer using chemotherapy and other therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;A Medical Oncologist is a physician who treats cancer, including AML, using systemic therapies such as chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A Medical Oncologist treats cancer patients with systemic therapies such as chemotherapy, often involved in managing lung cancer cases.&lt;SEP&gt;A Medical Oncologist treats cancer with systemic therapy and is integral to the AML treatment regimen.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is a physician focused on treating cancer with chemotherapy, hormonal therapies, and targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is a physician specializing in the treatment of cancer with chemotherapy and other medications.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is a physician specializing in treating cancer, using chemotherapy and other medicines.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is a physician who specializes in the treatment of cancer using chemotherapy and other medications.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is a specialist who treats cancer using systemic therapies such as chemotherapy and is a key member of the cancer treatment team.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is an expert in treating cancer through chemotherapy and other medical interventions.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is an expert in treating cancer with chemotherapy and other medical interventions, crucial for patient management.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist treats cancer using systemic therapies such as chemotherapy and immunotherapy, coordinating patient care with other team members.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist treats cancer using systemic therapy, including chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Esophagus is a muscular tube that connects the throat to the stomach, playing an essential role in transporting food.&lt;SEP&gt;The Esophagus is a muscular tube through which food passes from the throat to the stomach, crucial in the digestive system and affected in esophageal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagus is a muscular tube connecting the throat to the stomach, playing a crucial role in transporting food.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagus is a muscular tube that carries food and liquids from the mouth to the stomach, situated near areas prone to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagus is a muscular tube that connects the throat to the stomach, playing a crucial role in the digestive process by transporting food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digestive System">
      <data key="d0">Digestive System</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Digestive System is a complex system responsible for transporting and breaking down food and absorbing nutrients, including the esophagus, stomach, and intestines.&lt;SEP&gt;The Digestive System is responsible for nutrient absorption and waste elimination, comprising various organs including the esophagus.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system is a complex network of organs that takes in food, breaks it down, absorbs nutrients, and eliminates waste, involving various parts such as the esophagus, stomach, and intestines.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system is responsible for taking in and breaking down food, absorbing nutrients, and removing waste from the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking Cigarettes">
      <data key="d0">Smoking Cigarettes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking cigarettes is identified as the main cause of Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC), with most affected individuals having a history of heavy smoking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paraneoplastic Syndromes">
      <data key="d0">Paraneoplastic Syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Paraneoplastic syndromes are a group of rare disorders triggered by an immune system response to a neoplasm, leading to symptoms distinct from the tumor itself.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Airways">
      <data key="d0">Airways</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Airways are the passages that air travels through to reach the lungs, critical for respiration and impacted by lung diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chest">
      <data key="d0">Chest</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The chest houses the lungs and heart and is a critical area in the context of respiratory diseases and cancer diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophageal Cancer begins in the esophagus, leading to abnormal growth of cells within this muscular tube connecting the throat to the stomach.&lt;SEP&gt;Esophageal cancer is a type of cancer starting in the cells lining the esophagus, often categorized into two types: squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma, depending on the cell type involved.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer-Causing Chemicals">
      <data key="d0">Cancer-Causing Chemicals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer-causing chemicals, or carcinogens, include substances like radon and asbestos that significantly increase lung cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barrett Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Barrett Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Barrett Esophagus is a condition where the normal squamous cells of the lower esophagus are replaced with abnormal cells due to chronic reflux, resulting in an increased risk of developing adenocarcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagogastric Junction">
      <data key="d0">Esophagogastric Junction</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The esophagogastric junction is the anatomical area where the esophagus and stomach connect.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagogastric junction is the anatomical area where the esophagus connects to the stomach, playing a crucial role in the digestive system and relevant in esophageal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver">
      <data key="d0">Liver</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver is one of the organs where metastatic prostate cancer can spread, impacting overall health and treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a vital organ involved in various functions including detoxification, protein synthesis, and digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a vital organ located on the right side of the body, responsible for filtering blood, producing bile, and processing nutrients.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is an essential organ that processes chemicals in the body, and its functionality is vital for overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is an organ that can be affected by metastatic esophageal cancer, being one of the common sites where the cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreas">
      <data key="d0">Pancreas</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The pancreas is a gland located in the abdomen, crucial for digestion and hormone production, and is involved in the pathology of pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The pancreas is a vital organ situated near the esophagus that can also be impacted by the spread of esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Upper Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Upper Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The upper esophagus is the initial segment of the esophagus, made of striated muscle, and under voluntary control, playing a role in the swallowing process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Middle Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Middle Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The middle section of the esophagus contains both striated and smooth muscle, allowing for a mixed type of control during the swallowing process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lower Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Lower Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The lower part of the esophagus consists solely of smooth muscle and operates involuntarily, being crucial for the transport of food to the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tunica Adventitia">
      <data key="d0">Tunica Adventitia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The tunica adventitia is the outermost layer of the esophagus, consisting of connective tissue that provides structural support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Squamous Cell Carcinoma (ESCC)">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Squamous Cell Carcinoma (ESCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma is a type of esophageal cancer that arises from the thin, flat cells of the esophagus, more prevalent in certain regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Adenocarcinoma (EAC)">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Adenocarcinoma (EAC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophageal adenocarcinoma is another type of esophageal cancer that develops in the mucus-making cells of the esophagus, often related to chronic gastroesophageal reflux.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Reflux">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Reflux</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic reflux is a gastrointestinal condition that leads to the backward flow of stomach contents into the esophagus, causing potential damage and increasing cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer staging is a medical process that assesses the extent of cancer in a patient to guide treatment decisions and predict prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is a medical process that determines the extent and severity of cancer, guiding treatment protocols.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is a medical process used to determine the extent of cancer spread in the body, often using systems such as TNM to classify the severity of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is the process through which the spread and extent of cancer in a patient's body is determined and described.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging refers to the classification of cancer based on the extent of disease spread, which influences treatment decisions and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of determining the extent of cancer in the body, which influences treatment options and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Blood Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood vessels are conduits that carry blood throughout the body and may be involved in the metastatic spread of esophageal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood vessels are tubular structures that carry blood throughout the body, playing a critical role in delivering nutrients and oxygen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Status">
      <data key="d0">Performance Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance Status (PS) is a measure of a patient's general health and ability to perform daily tasks, critical for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance Status measures an individual's fitness and ability to perform daily activities, affecting treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status assesses a patient's fitness level and ability to carry out daily activities, influencing treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is a measure of a patient's general health and ability to perform daily activities, influencing treatment decisions in cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is a measure of a patient's general well-being and ability to carry out daily activities, influencing treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is a measure used to assess a patient’s ability to perform ordinary tasks, often used in determining treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is an assessment of a patient's ability to perform daily activities, influencing treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is an assessment of a patient's general well-being and ability to carry out daily activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is an assessment used to determine a patient's capability to carry out daily activities, influencing treatment decisions for conditions like CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a nonprofit organization that provides guidelines and resources for cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a not-for-profit alliance of leading cancer centers that aims to improve the quality of cancer care through clinical practice guidelines and patient resources.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an alliance of leading cancer centers across the United States that works to improve cancer care through evidence-based guidelines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin Lymphoma (HL) is a type of cancer that originates from lymphocytes and is identifiable by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hodgkin Lymphoma is a type of cancer that typically starts in the lymph nodes and is characterized by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hodgkin lymphoma is a type of cancer that originates in the lymphatic system, primarily affecting lymphocytes and leading to the formation of tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Hodgkin lymphoma is a type of cancer that originates in the lymphatic system, primarily affecting lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Foundation">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Foundation supports cancer patients and advances cancer treatment by funding innovative doctors and distributing patient and caregiver resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MyChart">
      <data key="d0">MyChart</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">MyChart is a health record account system that allows patients to track appointments, results, and communicate with their care team.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is a specific online patient portal that enables patients to manage health information and communicate with healthcare professionals.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is a virtual health record platform that helps patients manage appointments, medical records, and communicate with their healthcare team.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online health management tool that allows patients to access test results, appointments, and communicate with their health care team.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online health record system that allows patients to track their medical appointments and communicate with their care teams.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online patient portal allowing patients to access their medical records and communicate with their health care providers.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online patient portal that allows patients to access their test results, track appointments, and communicate with their care team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A cancer care team involves various healthcare professionals working together to manage cancer treatment and support for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Care Team includes healthcare providers who support and guide patients through their cancer diagnosis and treatment journey.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Care Team is comprised of healthcare professionals who work together to support a patient's diagnosis, treatment, and follow-up care.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Care Team provides support, counseling, and various services to patients dealing with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team consists of various health care providers, including specialists and support staff, working together to treat cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgkin Lymphoma in Adults">
      <data key="d0">Hodgkin Lymphoma in Adults</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin Lymphoma in Adults is a specific category in cancer care that provides guidelines and recommendations tailored for adults diagnosed with Hodgkin Lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reed-Sternberg Cells">
      <data key="d0">Reed-Sternberg Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Reed-Sternberg Cells are abnormally large lymphocytes that are characteristic of Hodgkin Lymphoma, helping differentiate it from other types of lymphomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Reed-Sternberg cells are large, abnormal cells found in classic Hodgkin lymphoma that play a key role in the disease's identification.&lt;SEP&gt;Reed-Sternberg cells are large, abnormal lymphocytes seen in Hodgkin lymphoma, characterized by their multi-nucleated appearance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology are regularly updated recommendations used by healthcare providers to guide cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insurance Forms">
      <data key="d0">Insurance Forms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Insurance Forms are documents required by healthcare providers for billing and reimbursement procedures related to a patient's medical care.&lt;SEP&gt;Insurance forms are documents needed for processing claims related to medical treatment and services, necessary for managing healthcare costs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Research">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Research</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Research encompasses various scientific studies aimed at understanding cancer and developing effective treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic System">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The lymphatic system is a crucial part of the immune system, comprised of organs and tissues that help defend the body against infections and disease.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a major component of the immune system, comprising a network of tissues and organs that help the body combat infections and diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a network of vessels that helps in transporting lymph, crucial for immune function and fluid balance.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a network that plays a crucial role in immune function and fluid balance in the body, and is involved in cancer metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a part of the immune system that helps the body fight infection and disease, consisting of lymph nodes, vessels, and organs.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is an integral part of the immune system, comprising lymph vessels, lymph nodes, and organs that facilitate immune responses.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is part of the immune system that helps the body fight infection and disease, with structures including lymphatic vessels, lymph nodes, and organs.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system plays a crucial role in the immune system, helping the body fight infections and diseases, including cancers like CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thymus">
      <data key="d0">Thymus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The thymus is a gland that plays a critical role in the immune system by maturing T-cells, which can be enlarged in cases of T-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;The thymus is a small organ located in front of the windpipe, which is involved in the development of T-cells and can become enlarged in cases of T-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;The thymus is an organ located in the upper chest where lymphocytes develop into T cells, pivotal for immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;The thymus is an organ where lymphocytes develop into T cells, playing a crucial role in the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spleen">
      <data key="d0">Spleen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The spleen is an organ that helps filter blood and plays a role in immune response, often checked for signs of diseases such as CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;The spleen is the largest organ of the lymphatic system, involved in filtering blood and producing lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;The spleen is the largest organ of the lymphatic system; it filters blood and produces lymphocytes to combat infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classic Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Classic Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Classic Hodgkin lymphoma (CHL) is the most common subtype of Hodgkin lymphoma, characterized by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Classic Hodgkin lymphoma is the most common type of Hodgkin lymphoma, characterized by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NLPHL is a rare form of Hodgkin lymphoma known for popcorn-shaped cells, which can grow slowly or transform into more aggressive cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma (NLPHL) is a rare subtype characterized by popcorn-shaped cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Care encompasses all forms of treatment, support, and research focused on preventing and treating cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer care encompasses various treatment approaches, diagnosis methods, and patient guidance for cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer care involves a range of treatments and decision-making processes tailored to the individual's specific condition and preferences, as emphasized in the guidelines.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer care involves various treatment options and guidelines to manage and treat different forms of cancer, including Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer care involves various treatment options and protocols designed to manage cancer and improve patient outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer care refers to the various forms of medical treatment and supportive services provided to individuals diagnosed with cancer, addressing physical, emotional, and psychological needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excisional Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Excisional Lymph Node Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An excisional lymph node biopsy is a surgical procedure where whole lymph nodes are removed for testing to diagnose Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Excisional lymph node biopsy is a surgical procedure to remove whole lymph nodes for testing to diagnose Hodgkin lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph">
      <data key="d0">Lymph</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph is a clear fluid that circulates through the lymphatic system and contains infection-fighting white blood cells called lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph is a clear fluid within the body that helps transport white blood cells, and it plays a crucial role in the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of Hodgkin lymphoma may include swollen lymph nodes, fever, night sweats, and unexplained weight loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnosing Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Diagnosing Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnosing Hodgkin lymphoma involves various tests including blood tests, imaging tests, and typically an excisional lymph node biopsy to confirm the presence of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="serosa">
      <data key="d0">serosa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The serosa, also known as the serous membrane or visceral peritoneum, is a thin membrane that covers internal organs and lines body cavities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bile Duct Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bile Duct Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bile duct cancer refers to tumors that develop in the bile ducts, with various stages of progression, including intraductal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular lymphoma (FL) is a slow-growing non-Hodgkin lymphoma that develops from abnormal B lymphocytes, forming clusters in lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Follicular lymphoma (FL) is a type of slow-growing non-Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by the proliferation of B lymphocytes, often forming tumors in the lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germinal Centers">
      <data key="d0">Germinal Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Germinal centers are temporary structures in lymph nodes that form during immune responses and are sites for B cell growth and differentiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World Health Organization (WHO)">
      <data key="d0">World Health Organization (WHO)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The World Health Organization (WHO) provides classification systems for diseases, including different grades of follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Consensus Classification (ICC)">
      <data key="d0">International Consensus Classification (ICC)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The International Consensus Classification (ICC) groups follicular lymphoma grades for treatment purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Stages">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer stages describe the extent and severity of cancer spread, affecting treatment decisions and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma (NHL)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma (NHL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NHL is a common type of blood cancer affecting lymphocytes, stemming from uncontrolled growth of these white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;NHL is a diverse group of blood cancers that originate from lymphocytes and account for a significant portion of hematologic malignancies.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-Hodgkin lymphoma is a diverse group of blood cancers that include various subtypes, including follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">B Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B lymphocytes are a subtype of lymphocytes that produce antibodies to help fight infections and are often affected in conditions like ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;B lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell involved in the immune response, which underlies the development of follicular lymphoma when they proliferate abnormally.&lt;SEP&gt;B lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that plays a crucial role in the immune response, capable of producing antibodies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World Health Organization">
      <data key="d0">World Health Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The World Health Organization (WHO) is specialized agency of the United Nations responsible for international public health, including classifications of diseases such as follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is an aggressive form of lymphoma that can develop from transformed follicular lymphoma and presents with rapidly growing tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric-type FL">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric-type FL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric-type FL (PTFL) is a subtype of follicular lymphoma that primarily affects children but can also occur in adults, usually characterized by specific symptoms and lymph node involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transformed FL">
      <data key="d0">Transformed FL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transformed FL refers to cases where follicular lymphoma evolves into a more aggressive form known as diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome Translocation">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome Translocation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosome translocation is a genetic alteration where parts of chromosomes are exchanged, often associated with various cancers, including follicular lymphoma and its variants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Testing">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer testing provides critical information regarding the presence and type of cancer, guiding treatment decisions for patients with follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Organization Classification">
      <data key="d0">Health Organization Classification</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health Organization classification refers to the categorization system established by health authorities, including definitions of various types of diseases, such as follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swollen Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Swollen Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Swollen lymph nodes are among the symptoms of follicular lymphoma, often indicating the presence of lymphoma in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspirate">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspirate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow aspirate is a medical procedure where liquid bone marrow is extracted for examination to diagnose diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone Marrow Aspirate is a procedure that involves extracting liquid bone marrow for diagnostic testing, particularly for leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate involves obtaining bone marrow samples to assess for blood cancers like AML and to perform necessary cytogenetic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate is a procedure that involves taking a sample of bone marrow to diagnose blood disorders such as Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate is a procedure that removes liquid and cellular components from bone marrow for examination, typically used in conjunction with a biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate is a procedure used to extract and analyze bone marrow, helping in the diagnosis of lymphomas including follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Changes">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic changes refer to mutations or alterations in DNA that can contribute to the development of follicular lymphoma and influence treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">B Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B symptoms include fever, night sweats, and weight loss, which are systemic symptoms associated with follicular lymphoma and other lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support System">
      <data key="d0">Support System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A support system comprises friends and family who provide emotional and practical support to the patient during diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A support system refers to friends, family, and peers that provide emotional and logistical support to patients diagnosed with follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mouth Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Mouth Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mouth cancer, also known as oral cancer, affects various parts of the mouth and is a type of head and neck cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mouth cancer, also referred to as oral cancer, includes various malignancies that can develop in any part of the oral cavity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck cancer encompasses a diverse group of cancers occurring in the head or neck area, with mouth cancer being one of them.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer encompasses a variety of cancer types that affect the head and neck region, often requiring specific treatment considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer encompasses various malignancies that affect the tissues in the head and neck region.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer includes a variety of cancers that affect the oral cavity, pharynx, larynx, and surrounding areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer is a term for cancers that develop in the oral cavity, pharynx, larynx, sinuses, and nasal cavity.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer refers to a group of cancers originating in the head or neck area, diagnosed and treated based on tumor location.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer refers to a group of cancers that start in the tissues of the head and neck, including the throat, mouth, and nasal cavity.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer refers to a range of cancers located in the anatomical area of the head and neck, and it may affect various functionality including speech, swallow, and overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers comprise a group of cancers that occur in the head and neck region, including the mouth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC)">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SCC is the most common type of mouth cancer, originating in the squamous cells lining the mouth.&lt;SEP&gt;SCC is the most common type of nasopharyngeal cancer, developing in the flat cells of the mucus membranes.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma is the most prevalent type of mouth cancer, affecting the flat cells in the mucous membranes of the mouth.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma is the predominant type of cancer found in the nasopharynx, originating from the flat cells of the mucous membranes, and has specific treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Cavity">
      <data key="d0">Oral Cavity</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The oral cavity refers to the inside of the mouth, which includes several anatomical structures that can develop cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The oral cavity, commonly known as the mouth, is a critical part of both the digestive and respiratory systems where numerous vital functions occur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mucosal Lip">
      <data key="d0">Mucosal Lip</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Mucosal lip refers to the part of the lips that is in contact with the oral cavity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mouth">
      <data key="d0">Mouth</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Mouth is the oral cavity that serves multiple functions including eating, speaking, and breathing, and is divided into various structural components.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tongue">
      <data key="d0">Tongue</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The tongue is a muscular organ in the mouth that aids in chewing, tasting, and speech. It plays a crucial role in the digestive process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gums">
      <data key="d0">Gums</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Gums are part of oral health that can be affected by treatment for head and neck cancer, making regular evaluations essential.&lt;SEP&gt;Gums, or gingiva, are the soft tissues that surround the teeth and play a critical role in oral health by keeping teeth anchored in place.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Buccal Mucosa">
      <data key="d0">Buccal Mucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Buccal mucosa is the lining inside the cheeks, an important part of the oral cavity that contains the mucous membrane.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alveolar Ridge">
      <data key="d0">Alveolar Ridge</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Alveolar ridge is the bony structure that contains the sockets for the teeth in the upper and lower jaws, essential for dental health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hard Palate">
      <data key="d0">Hard Palate</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The hard palate is the bony front part of the roof of the mouth that separates the oral cavity from the nasal cavity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Retromolar Trigone">
      <data key="d0">Retromolar Trigone</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Retromolar trigone refers to the small area of tissue behind the wisdom teeth, significant in dental contexts and oral health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Depth of Invasion (DOI)">
      <data key="d0">Depth of Invasion (DOI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DOI is a medical term that describes how deeply a tumor has penetrated into the oral mucosa, which is vital for treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Mucosa">
      <data key="d0">Oral Mucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Oral mucosa is the moist tissue lining the inside of the mouth and includes both epithelial and connective tissue layers important for oral function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment encompasses various medical interventions aimed at eradicating cancer and managing its symptoms, which can impact fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Treatment encompasses various medical procedures and therapies to combat cancer, affecting a patient's mental and physical wellbeing.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses a range of medical therapies, such as chemotherapy and radiation, aimed at eradicating cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses a variety of therapies designed to eliminate cancer or control its growth, including surgery, radiation, and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various approaches such as surgery, radiation therapy, and targeted therapies aimed at combating cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various methods and strategies used to combat cancer, including surgery, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, and supportive care.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various methods and therapies aimed at fighting cancer, including conventional and complementary approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various therapies and medications aimed at eliminating cancer cells and managing symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment involves various medical interventions aimed at combating cancer, including diagnosis, therapy type, dosage, and duration of treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment refers to the medical interventions aimed at managing cancer, including surgery, chemotherapy, radiation, and targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment refers to the medical interventions employed to manage cancer, including procedures, therapies, and medications.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment refers to the medical procedures undertaken to manage or eradicate cancer, which varies according to the type and stage of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Volume Center">
      <data key="d0">High-Volume Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">High-Volume Centers are medical facilities known for performing a large number of specific procedures or treatments, often leading to better patient outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;High-volume centers are hospitals or medical facilities specializing in specific types of cancer treatments, providing expertise and improved outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="General Health Tests">
      <data key="d0">General Health Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">General health tests are evaluations that help in understanding a patient's health status and guide treatment planning for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;General health tests include a variety of evaluations, such as measuring vital signs and assessing organ function, to gauge overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;General health tests refer to various assessments conducted to evaluate a patient's overall health before initiating treatment for conditions such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco and Alcohol Use History">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco and Alcohol Use History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tobacco and alcohol use history involves documenting past and current use, as they are known risk factors for head and neck cancers including NPC.&lt;SEP&gt;Tobacco and alcohol use history refers to an individual's consumption of these substances, which are risk factors for head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Location">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Location</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor location is a key factor that influences cancer treatment plans, as different locations may require different approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Stage">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer stage indicates the extent of cancer in the body and is crucial for determining treatment strategies and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer stage refers to how advanced the cancer is, influencing treatment decisions and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emotional Support">
      <data key="d0">Emotional Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Emotional support involves assistance and understanding provided to patients by family, friends, and peer groups during their cancer journey to help cope with stress.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nutrition evaluation assesses dietary needs and restrictions for patients, especially those undergoing treatment for head and neck cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrition evaluation assesses the dietary needs of cancer patients, which is crucial for maintaining strength during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family Health History">
      <data key="d0">Family Health History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family health history encompasses the record of health conditions among family members and is essential in assessing risk factors for diseases like rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history involves documenting health-related information about family members to assess risk factors for diseases, including certain cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history is important for understanding genetic predispositions to certain cancers and influences patient treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history pertains to the records of health conditions and diseases that affect family members, used in assessing genetic risks for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition Expert">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition Expert</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nutrition expert or dietitian provides guidance on nutritional needs and dietary strategies to support patients before and during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nutrition Expert advises cancer patients on dietary choices to ensure adequate nutrition before, during, and after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speech Therapist">
      <data key="d0">Speech Therapist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A speech or swallowing therapist evaluates and assists patients with communication and swallowing difficulties, particularly after treatment for head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Psychological Support Team">
      <data key="d0">Psychological Support Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A psychological support team offers emotional and mental health support for individuals diagnosed with cancer, addressing issues like distress and anxiety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOG Performance Status Scale">
      <data key="d0">ECOG Performance Status Scale</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ECOG Performance Status is a scale used to assess a patient's general health and ability to perform daily activities, which informs treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Fertility Specialist provides guidance to cancer patients on preserving fertility options before undergoing treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist assists cancer patients in understanding and managing their fertility options before, during, and after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is a healthcare provider who assists individuals or couples seeking to understand and address fertility issues, especially before cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is a healthcare provider who helps patients understand the impacts of cancer treatment on their fertility and offers preservation options.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is a medical expert who helps individuals and couples understand their options for having children, especially during or after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is a medical professional who provides guidance and treatment options for individuals concerned about fertility issues, particularly before cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist provides guidance to individuals on preserving their fertility before cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Height and Weight Measurements">
      <data key="d0">Height and Weight Measurements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Height and weight measurements are essential components of a physical examination, allowing healthcare providers to assess an individual's overall fitness and health status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Exam">
      <data key="d0">Physical Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A physical exam involves a thorough assessment of a patient's health through various tests, including checking organs and lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is a clinical assessment of a patient's body to check for signs of disease, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is a clinical assessment performed by healthcare providers to evaluate a patient's health, particularly after surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is a thorough assessment conducted by healthcare providers to check the overall health of a patient and identify potential issues.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is a thorough evaluation by a healthcare provider to assess a patient's general health, including an examination for cancer symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is an assessment performed by healthcare providers to evaluate a patient's overall health status, crucial for diagnosing AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distress Screening">
      <data key="d0">Distress Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress screening evaluates patients' emotional, mental, and social wellbeing after a cancer diagnosis and can adjust support services accordingly.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress screening is a psychological assessment to evaluate emotional well-being in cancer patients and provide support options.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress screening is a tool used to identify emotional distress in cancer patients, allowing for timely psychological support and intervention.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress screening is an assessment tool used to identify the emotional and psychological well-being of cancer patients, often leading to support services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine Tests">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocrine tests are performed to evaluate hormone levels, especially in patients undergoing radiation therapy affecting glands in the head and neck.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine tests evaluate hormone levels, particularly important for patients who undergo radiation therapy impacting the head and neck area.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine tests evaluate hormone levels, particularly the thyroid and pituitary glands, which may be affected by cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hearing Test">
      <data key="d0">Hearing Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A hearing test assesses auditory function and is significant for patients undergoing treatment for head and neck tumors, as those can affect hearing.&lt;SEP&gt;A hearing test measures hearing ability and identifies any potential hearing loss resulting from cancer or its treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Hearing tests evaluate auditory function, which may be affected by tumors or treatments in the head and neck area.&lt;SEP&gt;The Hearing Test is a procedure to evaluate an individual's hearing ability and detect any hearing loss, especially relevant for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A nutrition assessment involves evaluating dietary needs and guidance for cancer patients to maintain adequate nutrition during treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrition Assessment is a process where cancer patients meet with nutrition experts to evaluate their dietary needs during treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrition assessment involves evaluating a patient's dietary requirements and advising on nutrition status before treatment begins.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrition assessments involve evaluating dietary needs and recommendations, especially important before cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Preservation">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Preservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility Preservation encompasses medical techniques to safeguard reproductive potential prior to cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility Preservation involves medical strategies for individuals undergoing cancer treatment to maintain their reproductive options.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility Preservation involves techniques to help individuals retain the ability to have children following cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility Preservation refers to various methods and treatments designed to protect the fertility of individuals before undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation encompasses medical procedures that help individuals retain their ability to have children after treatments that may compromise fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation encompasses techniques and strategies to protect reproductive capabilities before cancer treatments that may affect fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation includes methods to safeguard reproductive options for patients undergoing cancer treatment that may affect fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation involves medical methods to save a person's ability to have children after treatments that may affect fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation involves strategies to protect or maintain fertility options for individuals undergoing medical treatments that may affect their reproductive capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation refers to medical procedures that assist individuals in preserving their fertility prior to undergoing cancer treatments that might compromise their reproductive capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation refers to the medical procedures and options available to help patients maintain their ability to conceive in the future, especially after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation strategies are methods used to protect a patient's ability to have children following cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation techniques are methods used to maintain reproductive options for individuals diagnosed with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation techniques are methods utilized to maintain reproductive options for individuals diagnosed with cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck cancer treatment involves medical interventions specifically targeting cancers located in the head and neck area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Material">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Material</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast Material is a substance used to enhance the quality of images taken during various imaging tests, improving clarity for diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material enhances the quality of images produced during imaging tests, allowing for clearer visualization of structures within the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a substance used during imaging tests to enhance the quality of the images, assisting in better visualization of leukemia spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a substance used in imaging tests to enhance image quality and visibility of structures in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a substance used in imaging tests to enhance the visibility of internal structures, improving the clarity of the images.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is used to enhance the clarity of internal images during medical imaging procedures, such as CT and MRI scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is used to enhance the quality of images produced by various imaging tests of the body, helping to clarify the images of organs and structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast materials are substances utilized in medical imaging to enhance the visibility of internal structures, aiding in clearer and more accurate diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Scan">
      <data key="d0">MRI Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan employs powerful magnets and radio waves to generate precise images of internal structures, often using contrast to improve clarity.&lt;SEP&gt;A magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan uses powerful magnets and radio waves to generate images of internal structures, also often employing contrast material for enhanced detail.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI Scan is a diagnostic imaging technique that employs radio waves and powerful magnets to produce detailed images of the body's internal structures without using x-rays.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI Scan uses magnetic fields and radio waves to create high-resolution images of the body without radiation, used for cancer assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan (magnetic resonance imaging) uses magnetic fields and radio waves to create images of organs and tissues without radiation exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a diagnostic imaging technique used to visualize the brain and assess for signs of lymphoma or other abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a diagnostic imaging technique utilized for identifying primary CNS lymphoma by providing detailed images of the brain and spinal cord.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a medical imaging technique that uses strong magnets and radio waves to create images of the body's internal structures without radiation exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to generate detailed internal images, useful for assessing leukemia effects on the brain.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is an imaging test that uses radio waves and magnets to create detailed images of the inside of the body, particularly useful for examining the prostate and surrounding areas.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan utilizes magnetic fields and radio waves to create images of organs and tissues, helpful in detecting complications of leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan utilizes magnetic fields and radio waves to generate images of internal organs without radiation exposure, useful for analyzing tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan, or magnetic resonance imaging scan, uses strong magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of organs and tissues in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI scans are diagnostic procedures using magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of the body's internal structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An FDG-PET/CT combines PET scan and CT technologies using a specific radiotracer to detect cancer effectively at various sites in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;An FDG-PET/CT scan combines the technologies of both PET and CT scans, utilizing fluorodeoxyglucose as a tracer to detect cancerous cells in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT combines positron emission tomography with computed tomography, using a radiotracer to provide detailed images that help identify cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging test that uses a radiotracer to assess metabolic activity in tissues, particularly useful in identifying cancerous growths.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT is a hybrid imaging technique combining PET and CT scans to provide detailed images of metabolic activity along with anatomical structures.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT is a hybrid imaging technique combining PET and CT scans, utilizing fluorodeoxyglucose as a radiotracer to provide insights into cancerous regions in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT scan is a hybrid imaging method that combines PET and CT scans using a fluorodeoxyglucose tracer to examine the metabolic activity of tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An ultrasound is a diagnostic imaging technique that uses high-frequency sound waves to create images of the inside of the body, employed here specifically for breast cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound imaging uses sound waves to produce images of internal organs, often used to investigate potential tumors and guidance for biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a diagnostic imaging technique used to visualize thyroid nodules and provides critical information regarding their size and structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging method that employs high-frequency sound waves to create images of internal structures, commonly utilized in various medical evaluations.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging method that utilizes high-energy sound waves to create images of internal organs, often used in medical diagnostics.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses high-frequency sound waves to create images of internal organs and structures, useful for detecting small areas of concern.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging test used to visualize the ovaries and potentially identify tumors or abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound uses high-energy sound waves to create images of organs, notably helpful in visualizing small cancer areas, typically without the use of x-rays.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panoramic Dental X-ray">
      <data key="d0">Panoramic Dental X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Panoramic Dental X-ray captures comprehensive images of the entire mouth, including teeth and jaws, often used for planning dental treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A panoramic dental x-ray captures a wide view of the mouth in a single image and is commonly used in dentistry to aid in treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A panoramic dental x-ray captures the entire mouth in one image and is often used by dentists for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A panoramic dental x-ray is a dental imaging technique that captures a wide view of the mouth and surrounding structures, assisting in dental treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A panoramic dental x-ray is an imaging technique that captures a wide view of the jaw and teeth, often used in planning dental procedures for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Provider (HCP)">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Provider (HCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">HCPs are professionals responsible for the diagnosis and treatment of cancer patients, guiding them throughout their care.&lt;SEP&gt;Health Care Providers (HCPs) are medical professionals responsible for diagnosing and treating health conditions, including interpreting medical test results.&lt;SEP&gt;Health Care Providers (HCPs) are professionals who provide medical care and guidance, essential for discussing and interpreting test results in cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Health Care Providers are medical professionals involved in diagnosing, treating, and supporting cancer patients, including managing their overall health care strategy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contraception">
      <data key="d0">Contraception</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contraception involves methods used to prevent pregnancy, especially important for individuals undergoing cancer treatment, as certain treatments may affect fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testicles">
      <data key="d0">Testicles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testicles are male reproductive organs that can be affected by cancer and its treatments, potentially leading to fertility issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Testicles are male reproductive organs whose health can be influenced by cancer treatment, potentially affecting fertility opportunities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetics">
      <data key="d0">Genetics</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetics refers to the study of genes and heredity, which can influence an individual's risk for cancer and response to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Reports">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Reports</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging reports are documents generated by radiologists that provide interpretations and findings from imaging tests, essential for patient diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dentists">
      <data key="d0">Dentists</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Dentists are healthcare professionals who specialize in the diagnosis, treatment, and prevention of oral diseases and conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Oral Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Oral surgeons are specialized dentists who perform surgical procedures on the mouth, jaws, and facial structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking and Vaping">
      <data key="d0">Smoking and Vaping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking and Vaping are habits that increase the risk of developing various cancers and can negatively affect treatment efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking and vaping refer to the inhalation of substances, typically tobacco and nicotine, which can significantly increase cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nicotine">
      <data key="d0">Nicotine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nicotine is a chemical compound found in tobacco that causes addiction and urges individuals to continue smoking.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is a chemical found in tobacco that is highly addictive and complicates the cessation of smoking and vaping.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is a chemical in tobacco that can affect cancer treatment outcomes and is a significant focus for patients looking to quit smoking.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is a potent addictive substance found in tobacco products, which can complicate cancer treatment and healing processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is the addictive chemical found in tobacco that complicates quitting smoking, especially for individuals diagnosed with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is the addictive chemical in tobacco, significantly affecting cancer treatment and healing processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 is an immune protein that can inhibit the immune response against cancer, affecting treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Programmed death-ligand 1 (PD-L1) is a protein that can be expressed by cancer cells and can impact immune evasion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutation testing is a diagnostic process used to identify specific genetic mutations in cancer cells to guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutation testing is a laboratory analysis used to identify mutations in cancer cells to guide treatment choices.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutation testing is used to identify specific mutations in tumors that may influence treatment options for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENT Doctor">
      <data key="d0">ENT Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An ear, nose, and throat (ENT) doctor specializes in diagnosing and treating conditions related to the ear, nose, throat, and related structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Teeth">
      <data key="d0">Teeth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Teeth are hard structures in the mouth used for biting and chewing food, playing a crucial role in dental health.&lt;SEP&gt;Teeth are structures that can be impacted by head and neck cancer treatments, necessitating the importance of regular dental checkups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Upper and Lower Jaws">
      <data key="d0">Upper and Lower Jaws</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Upper and lower jaws are the bone structures in the mouth that hold the teeth and facilitate chewing and speaking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surrounding Structures and Tissues">
      <data key="d0">Surrounding Structures and Tissues</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surrounding structures and tissues include the gums, muscles, and connective tissues related to the mouth and jaws.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dentists and Oral Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Dentists and Oral Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Dentists and oral surgeons are healthcare providers specializing in oral health and surgical procedures for oral and dental conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cannabis">
      <data key="d0">Cannabis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cannabis use may influence the effectiveness of anesthesia and recovery during cancer treatment due to its effects on the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking Cessation Support Websites">
      <data key="d0">Smoking Cessation Support Websites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Websites such as SmokeFree.gov and BeTobaccoFree.gov provide resources and support for individuals attempting to quit smoking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy Procedures">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy Procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsy procedures involve sampling tissue to diagnose conditions like cancer and may vary based on the suspected site of malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">FNA is a medical procedure used to remove tissue or cells from a lymph node for testing, often applied when lymph nodes are suspected to be affected by cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;FNA is a type of biopsy that uses a thin needle to remove a small sample of tissue from a tumor, often utilized when diagnosing ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA) is a type of biopsy that uses a thin needle to remove tissue for examination, often used to assess thyroid nodules.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration is a minimally invasive biopsy technique that uses a thin needle to obtain cells from a tumor or lymph node.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-needle aspiration is a minimally invasive procedure used to collect tissue samples from thyroid nodules for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Histology">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Histology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor histology is the study of tumor tissue's microscopic characteristics to identify cancer types and characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor histology refers to the microscopic study of tumor tissues to determine the type and characteristics of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Instability (MSI)">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Instability (MSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite instability (MSI) is a condition of genetic hypermutability that is often evaluated in the context of cancer diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite instability (MSI) is a condition where DNA repair mechanisms fail, leading to an accumulation of mutations in microsatellite regions found in genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite instability is a genetic condition in which repetitive sequences in DNA become unstable, often associated with certain types of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite instability refers to the condition where DNA replication errors are not corrected, often associated with certain cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Programmed Death-Ligand 1 (PD-L1)">
      <data key="d0">Programmed Death-Ligand 1 (PD-L1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 is a protein that can suppress the immune response, impacting cancer treatment approaches and effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutational burden (TMB) is a measure of the number of mutations within a tumor's DNA, which can influence treatment responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden (TMB) quantifies the number of mutations within a tumor's DNA and can provide insights into a malignancy's response to immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden refers to the number of mutations within a tumor’s DNA, influencing treatment options, especially immunotherapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENT (Ear, Nose, and Throat) Specialists">
      <data key="d0">ENT (Ear, Nose, and Throat) Specialists</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">ENT specialists are medical doctors focused on diagnosing and treating conditions related to the ear, nose, throat, and neck.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d0">Oral Cancer Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Oral cancer staging refers to the process of determining the extent of mouth cancer, including tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis, as per the TNM system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AJCC">
      <data key="d0">AJCC</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) creates standards for cancer staging, including the TNM system for mouth cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) developed a staging system for cancer to guide treatment decisions and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Node Metastasis (TNM)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Node Metastasis (TNM)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tumor Node Metastasis (TNM) system is a classification system used to describe the extent of cancer spread, including tumor size, lymph node involvement, and presence of metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The Tumor Node Metastasis (TNM) system is a classification system used to stage cancers based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis to distant sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Outcomes">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Outcomes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment outcomes refer to the expected results following cancer treatments and how various factors, such as lifestyle choices, can affect them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jaw">
      <data key="d0">Jaw</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The jaw is a critical part of the anatomy that can be affected by head and neck cancer and its treatment, influencing dental health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility">
      <data key="d0">Fertility</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility is the ability to conceive children, which can be affected by certain cancer treatments, making it an important consideration for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility refers to the ability to conceive, which may be impacted by cancer treatments, necessitating discussions prior to planning a pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA Mutations">
      <data key="d0">DNA Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA mutations are changes in genetic material that may influence cancer treatment options, as some therapies target specific mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking">
      <data key="d0">Smoking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking increases the risk of various cancers, including anal cancer, by damaging tissues and impairing immune function.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a harmful habit that significantly increases the risk of developing bladder cancer and other health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a lifestyle risk factor that has been linked with a higher risk of prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a risk factor for cancer, and counseling can assist individuals in quitting this habit, significantly impacting health outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a risk factor that can adversely affect treatment outcomes and increase toxicity during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is identified as one of the risk factors that can increase the likelihood of developing cervical cancer by undermining the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is identified as the greatest risk factor contributing to the development of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaping">
      <data key="d0">Vaping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaping, like smoking, is linked to negative effects on health and can impact cancer treatment efficacy and safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Myeloid Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Acute Myeloid Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of blood cancer originating from stem cells in the bone marrow, characterized by a buildup of immature white blood cells known as myeloid blasts or myeloblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer characterized by an accumulation of myeloblasts in the blood and bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects blood-forming cells in the bone marrow and leads to the production of abnormal blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stem cells are early-stage blood-forming cells found in the bone marrow that can develop into various blood cells including red blood cells and white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cells are undifferentiated cells capable of dividing and developing into various cell types, used in medical treatments such as stem cell rescue.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cells are undifferentiated cells that have the potential to develop into different cell types, including blood cells in the context of AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid Blasts">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid Blasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloid blasts are very immature white blood cells that accumulate in AML, preventing the formation of mature blood cells and causing health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANCER">
      <data key="d0">CANCER</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer refers to a group of diseases involving abnormal cell growth with the potential to invade or spread to other parts of the body. It is characterized by various types, including localized and metastatic forms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4 Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4 Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 cancer is considered very advanced disease, often characterized by metastasis where cancer cells spread to other organs, making treatment complex and outcomes less favorable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines are established recommendations provided by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network that help in navigating cancer treatment choices for various cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines are evidence-based recommendations for cancer treatment, including approaches for primary CNS lymphoma management.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines provide evidence-based recommendations for cancer treatment and patient management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regional Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Regional Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regional lymph nodes are lymph nodes located near a tumor that help filter harmful substances and can be involved in cancer spread, particularly in the head and neck.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extranodal Extension">
      <data key="d0">Extranodal Extension</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extranodal extension refers to the spread of cancer beyond lymph nodes into surrounding tissues, which can indicate a more aggressive disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML is a type of blood cancer that starts in the stem cells of bone marrow, characterized by the accumulation of immature white blood cells called myeloblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a fast-progressing type of blood cancer that affects the bone marrow and blood, often requiring intensive treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a form of blood cancer that begins in the bone marrow's stem cells, leading to a build-up of immature white blood cells that interfere with the production of healthy blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of blood cancer characterized by the rapid production of abnormal white blood cells, requiring specific treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of blood cancer that requires a multidisciplinary approach for treatment, involving various specialists and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer arising from the bone marrow that affects the blood and is characterized by the rapid increase in abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects blood and bone marrow, characterized by the presence of immature white blood cells called myeloblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of leukemia characterized by rapid growth of abnormal blood cells and requires advanced genetic testing to guide treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is another type of blood cancer that originates in the stem cells of bone marrow, featuring various subtypes found primarily in adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute myeloid leukemia is a fast-growing type of blood cancer characterized by the rapid increase in immature blood cells, which can occur as a rare side effect of chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blasts">
      <data key="d0">Blasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blasts are immature blood cells that can indicate the presence of leukemia when detected in high numbers in circulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts are immature blood cells, specifically myeloid blasts in AML, that accumulate due to the disease and prevent normal blood cell function.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts are immature white blood cells that are committed to becoming a type of blood cell and are found in increased numbers in patients with AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Preferences">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Preferences</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer preferences refer to the treatment choices and options that patients may consider based on their values and desires regarding their cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carotid Artery">
      <data key="d0">Carotid Artery</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The carotid artery is a major blood vessel in the neck that supplies blood to the brain, and can be involved in tumors affecting the neck and head region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Depth of Invasion">
      <data key="d0">Depth of Invasion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Depth of invasion (DOI) is a clinical measure used to assess how deeply a tumor has penetrated into surrounding tissues, which can impact treatment decisions and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Red Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Red blood cells (RBCs or erythrocytes) are responsible for carrying oxygen throughout the body, essential for cellular function.&lt;SEP&gt;Red blood cells (erythrocytes) are responsible for carrying oxygen throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Red blood cells are responsible for transporting oxygen in the blood and can also be affected due to the production of abnormal cells in ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Red blood cells are responsible for transporting oxygen in the body, and their counts may be affected in patients with CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;Red blood cells are responsible for transporting oxygen throughout the body and are produced in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">White blood cells (WBCs or leukocytes) are part of the immune system and help fight infections. They include various types such as granulocytes, monocytes, and lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;White blood cells (leukocytes) are essential for the immune system, fighting infections and foreign invaders.&lt;SEP&gt;White blood cells are crucial components of the immune system, responsible for fighting infections and diseases; their counts can be indicative of health conditions such as CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;White blood cells are part of the immune system that fight infections and are produced in the bone marrow; they include granulocytes, monocytes, and lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;White blood cells, which include lymphocytes, play a key role in the immune response and are primarily impacted by ALL, leading to an excess of immature cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platelets">
      <data key="d0">Platelets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platelets (PLTs or thrombocytes) are blood components that help control bleeding by facilitating clotting processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets (thrombocytes) are involved in blood clotting, helping to control bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets are cell fragments in the blood that help with clotting and controlling bleeding, produced in the bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets are cell fragments that play a critical role in blood clotting; their levels can also be affected in CLL patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets are small cell fragments in the blood that play a crucial role in clotting and are one of the components affected in leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopoietic stem cells are blood-forming stem cells crucial for producing all types of blood cells and are involved in stem cell rescues post-chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematopoietic stem cells are primitive cells in the bone marrow that have the ability to differentiate into all types of blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML">
      <data key="d0">AML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML, or Acute Myeloid Leukemia, is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a specific type of leukemia that requires genetic mutation testing to determine appropriate treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects blood-forming cells in the bone marrow and leads to the production of abnormal blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal white blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Granulocytes">
      <data key="d0">Granulocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Granulocytes are a type of white blood cell that play a key role in the body's immune response, especially in fighting infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Cells">
      <data key="d0">Immune Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune cells are cells in the body designed to protect against diseases, including various types of white blood cells such as granulocytes, monocytes, and lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Blood Count">
      <data key="d0">Complete Blood Count</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">A Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a key test that measures the levels of red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, providing insights into overall health and potential diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy are procedures used to extract and analyze bone marrow for diagnosing conditions like acute myeloid leukemia (AML).&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy is a procedure used to collect bone marrow samples for the diagnosis of diseases such as leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain CT">
      <data key="d0">Brain CT</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain CT is a type of imaging test that helps in diagnosing conditions related to the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain MRI">
      <data key="d0">Brain MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain MRI is an imaging test that uses magnetic fields to visualize brain structures, often used to detect abnormalities associated with conditions like leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lumbar Puncture">
      <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A lumbar puncture (LP) is a medical procedure that involves removing spinal fluid for testing, often used to diagnose conditions like leukemia spreading to the spinal fluid.&lt;SEP&gt;A lumbar puncture (spinal tap) is a procedure to remove spinal fluid for testing, particularly for leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A lumbar puncture, also known as a spinal tap, is a medical procedure that involves removing cerebrospinal fluid for testing to confirm conditions such as primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure that involves removing cerebrospinal fluid from the spine for testing, commonly used in the diagnosis of central nervous system cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumbar puncture, or spinal tap, is a procedure to collect cerebrospinal fluid, often used in the diagnosis of leukemia-related complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uric Acid">
      <data key="d0">Uric Acid</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Uric Acid is a substance measured in blood tests, important for assessing kidney function and other metabolic disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lactate Dehydrogenase">
      <data key="d0">Lactate Dehydrogenase</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme released during cell death, indicating tissue damage or high cell turnover.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme that can indicate tissue damage when elevated in the blood.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme that is often assessed in blood tests to evaluate cell damage and disease, including various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitamin B12">
      <data key="d0">Vitamin B12</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitamin B12 is a crucial nutrient necessary for the formation of red and white blood cells and overall body function.&lt;SEP&gt;Vitamin B12 is a nutrient measured in blood tests, essential for red blood cell formation and neurological function.&lt;SEP&gt;Vitamin B12 is essential for forming red blood cells and is monitored for its levels as part of blood health assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Folic Acid">
      <data key="d0">Folic Acid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Folic Acid (folate) is crucial for cell division and red blood cell formation, often monitored in blood tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Folic Acid is a B vitamin that is important for DNA synthesis and is checked in blood tests, especially in cases of potential leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Folic acid is a vitamin that helps the body in producing new proteins and is essential for red and white blood cell formation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Clotting Tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood Clotting Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood clotting tests are medical evaluations that assess the body's ability to stop bleeding and the efficiency of the coagulation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood clotting tests are performed to evaluate the blood's ability to clot, crucial for patients at risk of bleeding complications in conditions like AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Tests">
      <data key="d0">Heart Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Heart tests are evaluations conducted to assess cardiac function and health status, particularly in patients undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Heart tests assess the condition and functionality of the heart, often necessary during the treatment of leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrolytes">
      <data key="d0">Electrolytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrolytes are ions in the blood that are crucial for maintaining neurological and muscular functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrolytes are minerals in the blood that help regulate various bodily functions, measured in tests to monitor organ health, especially during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrolytes refer to ions in the blood necessary for physiological functions, including nerve signal transmission and muscle contraction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Metabolic Panel">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Metabolic Panel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) evaluates various substances in the blood to monitor organ function, especially the kidneys and liver.&lt;SEP&gt;A Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) measures substances in the blood, providing vital information on organ function, particularly the kidneys and liver.&lt;SEP&gt;The Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) is a blood test that assesses the health of major organs and metabolism, providing critical data in the treatment of AML.&lt;SEP&gt;The Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) is a blood test that measures various chemicals, proteins, and substances to assess overall health, particularly for the kidneys and liver.&lt;SEP&gt;The Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) measures important chemicals, proteins, and other substances in the blood to assess overall health and organ function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Blood Count and Differential">
      <data key="d0">Complete Blood Count and Differential</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">The Complete Blood Count and Differential is a detailed blood test used extensively to diagnose and monitor blood disorders, including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron">
      <data key="d0">Iron</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron is an essential mineral involved in maintaining bodily functions, primarily in hemoglobin production for oxygen transport.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron levels are critical for hemoglobin production, and deficiencies or overloads can lead to significant health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HLA Typing">
      <data key="d0">HLA Typing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HLA Typing is a test that identifies the unique proteins on cells that are vital for determining compatibility for organ transplants.&lt;SEP&gt;HLA typing is a blood test that identifies a person's unique human leukocyte antigen proteins, crucial for organ transplants matching.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Status (PS)">
      <data key="d0">Performance Status (PS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance Status refers to a scale used to measure the fitness and daily functioning ability of cancer patients, influencing treatment decisions and care plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status assesses a person's fitness level and ability to carry out daily tasks, guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Tests">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Marrow Tests are crucial in diagnosing conditions like leukemia by analyzing samples to assess cellular abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone Marrow Tests involve analyzing samples to diagnose diseases like leukemia by examining cellular composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are medical procedures used to diagnose and monitor blood disorders, including ALL, by examining bone marrow for abnormal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are procedures to diagnose conditions like leukemia by examining the bone marrow and its cellular components.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are procedures to examine the bone marrow and blood cells, sometimes done to determine causes for low blood cell counts or diagnose conditions like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests assess the health and function of bone marrow through aspiration and biopsy procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests, including aspirates and biopsies, are performed to examine the bone marrow and its cells, key in diagnosing AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bile Ducts">
      <data key="d0">Bile Ducts</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Bile ducts are tubes that carry bile from the liver to the gallbladder and the small intestine, and their blockage can indicate health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hemoglobin">
      <data key="d0">Hemoglobin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hemoglobin is a protein in red blood cells responsible for oxygen transport throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blood Cells (RBC)">
      <data key="d0">Red Blood Cells (RBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Red Blood Cells are crucial for transporting oxygen from the lungs to body tissues and returning carbon dioxide for exhalation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cells (WBC)">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cells (WBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">White Blood Cells are part of the immune system and help the body fight infections and other diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravenous (IV) Iron Supplement">
      <data key="d0">Intravenous (IV) Iron Supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IV iron supplements are administered to patients to treat iron deficiency and maintain proper bodily functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coagulation Panel">
      <data key="d0">Coagulation Panel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A coagulation panel is a series of blood tests that evaluate the blood's ability to clot and screen for clotting disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;The Coagulation Panel assesses the blood's ability to clot, which is crucial for preventing excessive bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;The Coagulation Panel tests the blood's ability to clot, essential for evaluating bleeding disorders or thrombosis risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) Panel">
      <data key="d0">Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) Panel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The DIC panel tests for a serious disorder resulting in both clotting and bleeding complications in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia is a group of cancers that affects blood cells and can be influenced by genetic predisposition, requiring specific treatment considerations based on individual genetics.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a type of blood cancer characterized by the overproduction of abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a type of cancer affecting blood cells, characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a type of cancer that affects blood and bone marrow, leading to the overproduction of abnormal blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a type of cancer that affects blood cells and can require specific genetic testing to assess predisposition and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coagulopathy">
      <data key="d0">Coagulopathy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Coagulopathy refers to a condition in which the blood's ability to clot is impaired, leading to excessive bleeding or clotting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA)">
      <data key="d0">Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HLA are proteins that are critical for the immune system to recognize self and non-self cells, impacting transplant compatibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Cell Transplant (HCT)">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Cell Transplant (HCT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">HCT is a medical procedure used to treat certain cancers and blood disorders by replacing damaged bone marrow with healthy cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) Score">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) Score</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ECOG score is a measure of a patient's performance status, evaluating their functional status and ability to perform daily activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS)">
      <data key="d0">Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The KPS is another system to assess a patient's functional impairment, used in treatment considerations for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The KPS scale assesses a patient’s ability to carry out daily activities and is used in oncology to evaluate treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;The Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is a scoring system used to assess a patient's ability to perform ordinary tasks, important in treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow biopsy involves taking a small sample of bone and marrow to identify conditions affecting the blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone marrow biopsy is a procedure used to extract a sample of bone marrow for examination to confirm diagnoses such as AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical procedure used to extract a sample of bone marrow for testing, particularly to check for cancer cells in the case of lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone Marrow Biopsy is a procedure that involves taking a sample of bone tissue to diagnose diseases like leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow biopsy involves extracting a small core of bone marrow tissue to assess its condition and diagnose various illnesses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN)">
      <data key="d0">Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BPDCN is a rare and aggressive form of leukemia that arises from dendritic cells in the bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;BPDCN is a rare type of leukemia that may be diagnosed through specialized tests, often associated with AML.&lt;SEP&gt;BPDCN is an aggressive subtype of AML that can be found in blood, bone marrow, lymph nodes, and skin.&lt;SEP&gt;BPDCN is an aggressive subtype of AML that can be found in various tissues including blood, bone marrow, lymph nodes, and skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A lymph node biopsy is a procedure used to remove and examine lymph nodes, often to check for cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Lesion Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Lesion Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A skin lesion biopsy is a procedure where a skin sample is removed for examination and diagnosis of skin conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flow Cytometry">
      <data key="d0">Flow Cytometry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Flow Cytometry is a laboratory technique used to analyze the characteristics of cells, especially in diagnosing leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory method that analyzes the physical and chemical characteristics of cells or particles.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique used to detect and analyze physical and chemical characteristics of cells, commonly applied in identifying white blood cell types and blood cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a method used to detect specific proteins on the surface of cells, aiding in the diagnosis of conditions such as CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunophenotyping">
      <data key="d0">Immunophenotyping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunophenotyping is a diagnostic process that uses antibodies to identify and classify various types of blood cells, crucial in leukemia diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is a laboratory procedure that uses antibodies to identify specific cell types, particularly in assessing leukemias and lymphomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is a process involving flow cytometry to identify the types of cells based on the presence of specific markers.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is a process involving the use of antibodies to identify specific white blood cell antigens that indicate various blood cancers, particularly Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Treatment">
      <data key="d0">AML Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML treatment encompasses methods targeting specific biomarkers and genetic alterations associated with Acute Myeloid Leukemia, often involving personalized approaches based on genetic findings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunohistochemistry (IHC)">
      <data key="d0">Immunohistochemistry (IHC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunohistochemistry is a staining technique used to visualize specific antigens in cells, aiding in the diagnosis and understanding of blood cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunohistochemistry is a technique used to visualize the expression of specific antigens in cells, aiding in the diagnosis of cancers such as AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunohistochemistry is a type of staining process that allows researchers and clinicians to visualize the presence of specific proteins in cells within tissue sections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic Testing in AML involves analyzing leukemia cells for specific mutations that influence treatment options and patient prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing assesses the risk of inherited conditions, including breast cancer, and provides information relevant for treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing encompasses tests to analyze DNA sequences for hereditary mutations and their implications for cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing in the context of AML refers to testing leukemia cells for specific genetic mutations that affect treatment approaches and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing involves analyzing DNA to identify genetic predispositions for diseases, including stomach cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing involves analyzing DNA to identify genetic predispositions to certain diseases, including some forms of cancer, influencing management and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is a diagnostic tool used to identify genetic mutations or biomarkers associated with various types of cancer, including ALL and pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is a laboratory analysis used to identify genetic mutations that may contribute to cancer risk or influence treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is a medical test that analyzes DNA to identify abnormalities or mutations that can lead to diseases such as cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is a medical test that identifies changes in chromosomes, genes, or proteins to assess the risk of inherited diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is a method used to identify inherited risks for cancer by detecting mutations in genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is performed to identify mutations in genes such as BRCA1 and BRCA2, which can influence ovarian cancer risk and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is the process of analyzing a person's DNA to identify genetic mutations that may increase cancer risk, particularly for hereditary prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing refers to tests that help identify the presence of genetic mutations that may increase the risk of developing gliomas and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosomes">
      <data key="d0">Chromosomes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosomes are structures within cells that contain DNA and genetic information, which can undergo mutations in leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes are structures within cells that contain DNA, organized into pairs, which carry genetic information.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes are structures within cells that contain DNA; abnormalities in chromosomes are often associated with certain types of cancers, including leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes are structures within cells that contain genes, and humans have 23 pairs that carry genetic information essential for cell function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutation testing involves analyzing genetic changes in cancer cells to guide treatment plans, particularly in AML cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization (FISH)">
      <data key="d0">Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization (FISH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FISH is a genetic testing methodology used to detect specific DNA changes in leukemia cells.&lt;SEP&gt;FISH is a genetic testing technique used to identify specific DNA changes associated with leukemia and helps guide treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;FISH is a technique used to detect and localize specific DNA sequences on chromosomes, valuable in identifying chromosomal abnormalities in cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Cell Donors">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Cell Donors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biological family members who may provide hematopoietic (blood-forming) cells for transplantation are tested for leukemia predisposition syndromes.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematopoietic cell donors are individuals whose stem cells can be used for transplantation in patients with blood cancers, including leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes are inherited genetic conditions that increase the risk of developing leukemia in individuals.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia predisposition syndromes are hereditary conditions that increase the risk of developing leukemia, often requiring genetic testing for assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS)">
      <data key="d0">Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NGS is a high-throughput technique that analyzes DNA sequences to identify mutations relevant to leukemia treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) is an advanced sequencing technology that analyzes genetic variations, detecting mutations that impact AML treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) refers to advanced methods that analyze DNA sequences to identify mutations that may affect gene function.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-Generation Sequencing is a revolutionary method to determine the DNA sequence of genes, useful for identifying mutations related to AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-generation sequencing (NGS) is a high-throughput approach to analyze DNA changes in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karyotype">
      <data key="d0">Karyotype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A karyotype is a visual representation of an individual's chromosomes used to identify chromosomal abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A karyotype is the number and appearance of chromosomes in the nucleus of a eukaryotic cell, often used in diagnosis and risk assessment in leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A karyotype test analyzes the number and appearance of chromosomes to detect genetic abnormalities relevant to conditions like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;Karyotyping is a laboratory test that examines the size, shape, and number of chromosomes in a sample and helps identify genetic disorders, including those linked to leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Karyotyping is a test that provides a visual profile of chromosomes in a sample, helping to identify abnormalities linked to leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gene Expression Profiling">
      <data key="d0">Gene Expression Profiling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene expression profiling analyzes the patterns of gene activity to understand the biology of cancer cells, aiding in targeted treatment selection in AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene expression profiling tests the activity of genes that control cell function and helps determine cancer characteristics and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antibodies">
      <data key="d0">Antibodies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Antibodies are immune system proteins that identify and neutralize foreign objects like bacteria and viruses; in AML, they are used for specific diagnostic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)">
      <data key="d0">Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PCR is a molecular biology technique used to amplify segments of DNA, allowing for detection of genetic mutations relevant to leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;PCR is a technique for amplifying DNA, making it useful for identifying genetic alterations in leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is a sensitive technique used to amplify DNA or RNA and identify genetic abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is a widely used technique in molecular biology to amplify DNA for analysis, particularly useful in genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting imaging studies and diagnosing conditions based on the results.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting imaging tests, such as MRI and PET scans, to diagnose conditions like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting medical images and conveying results to oncologists for cancer treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting medical images to diagnose conditions, including assessing MRI and CT scans for CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting medical images, playing a crucial role in diagnosing conditions like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specializing in interpreting imaging tests and providing analysis to help with patient care decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specializing in interpreting medical images and diagnosing diseases based on those images.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor trained to interpret medical imaging and assist in the diagnosis of diseases, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor who interprets medical images, such as CT scans and MRIs, to diagnose health conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical expert who interprets imaging tests and prepares reports for physicians.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical professional specialized in interpreting medical images such as CT and MRI scans to support diagnostic processes.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical professional who analyzes imaging results to identify issues such as brain tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a specialist who uses imaging techniques to diagnose and treat diseases, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG)">
      <data key="d0">Electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An electrocardiogram (ECG) measures the electrical activity of the heart and provides information on heart rate and rhythm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Echocardiogram">
      <data key="d0">Echocardiogram</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An echocardiogram is an ultrasound test that visualizes the heart's structure and function through sound waves.&lt;SEP&gt;An echocardiogram uses ultrasound to produce images of the heart, showing its structure and function.&lt;SEP&gt;An echocardiogram, or echo, uses sound waves to create images of the heart, showing its structure and function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cardiologist">
      <data key="d0">Cardiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A cardiologist is a medical specialist focused on diagnosing and treating heart-related conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Cells">
      <data key="d0">Human Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Human cells typically contain 23 pairs of chromosomes, totaling 46 chromosomes, essential for genetic information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Targeted Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Targeted Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Targeted therapies are treatment strategies aimed at specific genetic mutations or abnormalities in cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Tests for Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Tests for Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging tests specific to leukemia diagnosis involve visualizing internal organs to identify disease spread or complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Portal">
      <data key="d0">Patient Portal</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A patient portal is an online application that allows patients to access their medical records and communicate with healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Agents">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Agents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast agents are substances used in imaging testing procedures to enhance the clarity of the resulting images.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT and MRI Technologies">
      <data key="d0">CT and MRI Technologies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CT and MRI technologies are advanced imaging modalities that use different principles to visualize internal body structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiology">
      <data key="d0">Radiology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiology involves imaging techniques used to visualize the internal structures of the body, assisting in the diagnosis and evaluation of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiology is a medical specialty that utilizes imaging techniques to diagnose and treat diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematologist">
      <data key="d0">Hematologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Hematologist is a doctor specializing in blood diseases, crucial in diagnosing and treating conditions like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A Hematologist is a medical specialist focused on blood diseases and blood cancers, playing a key role in AML treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A hematologist is a medical expert specializing in blood diseases and cancers, providing treatment for conditions like AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QTc">
      <data key="d0">QTc</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">QTc refers to the time it takes for the heart's electrical system to recharge between beats, and its prolongation can lead to dangerous heart rhythms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ejection Fraction">
      <data key="d0">Ejection Fraction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ejection fraction is a measurement of the percentage of blood that is pumped out of the left ventricle of the heart each time it beats, indicating heart function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Cell Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Cell Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopoietic cell transplant is a procedure that infuses healthy blood-forming stem cells into a patient to replace bone marrow damaged by disease or treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Team (MDT)">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Team (MDT)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Multidisciplinary Team (MDT) is a collaborative group of healthcare professionals who work together to plan and implement treatment strategies for patients with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Multidisciplinary Team (MDT) is a group of specialists from various medical fields working together to coordinate treatment for AML patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The Multidisciplinary Team is a collective of healthcare professionals with expertise in AML, involved in treatment planning and implementation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic and Biomarker Tests">
      <data key="d0">Genetic and Biomarker Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic and biomarker tests are employed to understand the specific subtype of AML and to help target treatment strategies for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic and biomarker tests are used to identify specific characteristics of cancer, which can guide treatment decisions and provide prognosis information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A doctor plays a crucial role in discussing treatment options, preventive measures, and managing patient health, especially concerning cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;Doctors are healthcare professionals who diagnose and treat diseases, including managing care for patients with AML.&lt;SEP&gt;The doctor is a healthcare professional who receives imaging results from the radiologist to plan subsequent steps in patient care.&lt;SEP&gt;The doctor, as part of the care team, plays a crucial role in diagnosing and treating AML, as well as guiding patients through their care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cladribine">
      <data key="d0">Cladribine</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Cladribine, marketed as Mavenclad, is an antimetabolite drug used in the treatment of specific cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clofarabine">
      <data key="d0">Clofarabine</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Clofarabine, known as Clolar, is an antimetabolite used in treating certain leukemias.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cytarabine">
      <data key="d0">Cytarabine</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Cytarabine is a chemotherapeutic agent used primarily in the treatment of leukemia and is often used in combination with other drugs.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine is a chemotherapy agent used in the treatment of various forms of leukemia, often in combination therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine is an anthracycline chemotherapy drug frequently used in AML treatment regimens.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine, also called Ara-C, is an anthracycline used in many treatment regimens for AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine, also known as Ara-C, is an effective chemotherapy agent widely used in AML treatment regimens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fludarabine">
      <data key="d0">Fludarabine</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Fludarabine is a chemotherapy drug used specifically in treating certain blood cancers, including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Methotrexate">
      <data key="d0">Methotrexate</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Methotrexate is a chemotherapy agent involved in the treatment of various cancers, including AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug that is an antimetabolite, used to treat several types of cancer, including breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug used primarily to treat primary CNS lymphoma and other cancers by interfering with the metabolism of folate to inhibit cancer cell division.&lt;SEP&gt;Methotrexate is the main chemotherapy drug used for treating primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Daunorubicin">
      <data key="d0">Daunorubicin</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Daunorubicin is a chemotherapy drug used in combination with other drugs to treat Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML), especially in cases with genetic abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Daunorubicin is a chemotherapy medication used to treat leukemia, often combined with other drugs to enhance efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Daunorubicin is an anthracycline used to treat cancer, particularly for AML therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Idarubicin">
      <data key="d0">Idarubicin</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Idarubicin, sold as Idamycin PFS, is an anthracycline used to combat AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mitoxantrone">
      <data key="d0">Mitoxantrone</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Mitoxantrone, branded as Novantrone, is a medication used in treating various cancers, including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gemtuzumab ozogamicin">
      <data key="d0">Gemtuzumab ozogamicin</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Gemtuzumab ozogamicin (GO) is a targeted therapy for AML that combines an antibody with chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Azacitidine">
      <data key="d0">Azacitidine</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Azacitidine, branded as Vidaza, is a hypomethylating agent used to treat AML by restoring gene function.&lt;SEP&gt;Azacitidine, marketed as Vidaza, is a hypomethylating agent used in cancer treatment to reactivate silenced genes in leukemia cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Decitabine">
      <data key="d0">Decitabine</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Decitabine, known as Dacogen, is another hypomethylating agent utilized in the treatment of certain leukemias.&lt;SEP&gt;Decitabine, sold as Dacogen, is a hypomethylating agent used in the treatment of certain types of leukemia by blocking excessive methylation in DNA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Binding Factor">
      <data key="d0">Core Binding Factor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Core Binding Factor (CBF) is involved in genetic abnormalities associated with AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Core Binding Factor (CBF) relates to genetic abnormalities affecting AML progression and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLT3">
      <data key="d0">FLT3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FLT3 denotes a mutation related to AML, with specific therapies targeting these mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;FLT3 is a gene that, when mutated, can contribute to the progression of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) and is targeted by certain therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;FLT3 refers to mutations associated with AML, for which specific targeted therapies are developed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypomethylating Agents">
      <data key="d0">Hypomethylating Agents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hypomethylating Agents are therapies that modify DNA methylation to reactivate silenced genes in leukemia cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypomethylating Agents are treatments that help reactivate silenced genes in leukemia cells, showing efficacy in AML cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypomethylating agents are drugs that block methyl groups from binding to DNA, helping to reactivate silenced genes in leukemia cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypomethylating agents help revert the silencing of genes in leukemia cells, promoting their maturation into normal cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dual-Drug Liposome">
      <data key="d0">Dual-Drug Liposome</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Dual-drug liposome therapy combines cytarabine and daunorubicin to effectively treat AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard of Care">
      <data key="d0">Standard of Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard of Care refers to the best-known methods for treating specific diseases based on clinical trial results and established practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard of care refers to the best-known treatment protocols based on clinical trials for a specific disease, including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Guide">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Guide</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ovarian Cancer Guide provides comprehensive treatment recommendations for various types of ovarian cancers and aims to educate individuals about the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovaries">
      <data key="d0">Ovaries</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovaries are female reproductive organs where eggs are produced and can be affected by ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovaries are reproductive organs in females responsible for producing estrogen and progesterone, critical hormones during the menstrual cycle and pregnancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovaries are the female reproductive organs that can be affected by cancer treatments, impacting fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;The ovaries are part of the female reproductive system, vital for sexual reproduction and hormone production, and are located in the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fallopian Tubes">
      <data key="d0">Fallopian Tubes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Fallopian tubes are reproductive organs that connect the ovaries to the uterus and play a crucial role in the fertilization process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uterus">
      <data key="d0">Uterus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The uterus is a key organ in the female reproductive system where a fertilized egg can develop into a fetus during pregnancy.&lt;SEP&gt;The uterus is a reproductive organ in women that can be visualized using different imaging techniques to assess for any abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;The uterus is a reproductive organ in women, which can be visualized using MRI or other imaging techniques to check for cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;The uterus is the female reproductive organ where cervical cancer can originate, specifically from the cervical canal or ectocervix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervix">
      <data key="d0">Cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The cervix is a cylindrical structure at the lower part of the uterus that plays a crucial role in female reproductive health and is the site of cervical cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is a narrow, lower end of the uterus that connects to the vagina and is prone to developing cancer from abnormal cell changes.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower part of the uterus that opens into the vagina and is a focal point in the imaging process during cancer evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower part of the uterus that opens into the vagina and is a key site for cervical cancer diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower part of the uterus that opens into the vagina and is notably examined for signs of cancer through imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower part of the uterus that opens into the vagina and plays an important role in menstruation and childbirth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vagina">
      <data key="d0">Vagina</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The vagina is a muscular tube leading from the external genitals to the cervix of the uterus in females.&lt;SEP&gt;The vagina is the canal leading from the external genitals to the cervix of the uterus, and is involved in the development of cervical changes and cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;The vagina is the canal leading from the external genitals to the uterus and may be assessed for health conditions through imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;The vagina is the canal leading from the external genitals to the uterus, which may be examined in imaging tests for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Serous Carcinoma (HGSC)">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Serous Carcinoma (HGSC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-Grade Serous Carcinoma is one of the most common and aggressive forms of ovarian cancer, primarily arising from the epithelium.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Endometrioid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Endometrioid Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-Grade Endometrioid Carcinoma is another type of ovarian cancer that is characterized by its aggressive nature and originates in the epithelial tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;High-grade endometrioid carcinoma is another type of ovarian cancer that is treated similarly to high-grade serous carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologic Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologic Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A gynecologic oncologist is a medical expert specializing in surgeries and treatments for cancers of the female reproductive system.&lt;SEP&gt;A gynecologic oncologist is a surgeon specializing in cancers of the female reproductive system, particularly involved in treating ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A gynecologic oncologist specializes in treating female reproductive cancers through surgery, chemotherapy, and patient care.&lt;SEP&gt;A gynecologic oncologist specializes in treating female reproductive cancers, primarily through surgery and chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian cancers are a group of diseases that affect the ovaries and can start in various tissues, including the epithelium, supporting tissues, or reproductive cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Peritoneal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Primary Peritoneal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary peritoneal cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the tissue lining the abdominal wall and abdominal organs, often associated with ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormones">
      <data key="d0">Hormones</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormones produced by the ovaries play crucial roles in regulating aspects of female health, including menstruation, pregnancy, and secondary sexual characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Examination">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Examination</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A pelvic examination is a procedure where a healthcare provider checks the female reproductive organs for signs of disease or abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Ovarian Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms associated with ovarian cancer include bloating, pelvic pain, and difficulty eating, which can impact a patient's nutrition and overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of ovarian cancer can include bloating, heartburn, indigestion, pelvic pain, trouble eating, and frequent urination.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of ovarian cancer may include bloating, pain in the abdomen, and changes in digestion, which can often be mistaken for other conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Plan">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment plan is a comprehensive strategy developed by healthcare professionals to address a patient's specific medical condition, particularly cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A treatment plan is a detailed outline developed by healthcare providers to manage cancer treatment based on the patient's specific diagnosis and circumstances.&lt;SEP&gt;A treatment plan is a structured guide created by healthcare providers to manage and treat a patient's cancer effectively based on its stage.&lt;SEP&gt;A treatment plan outlines the strategy and methods to be employed in treating a patient’s cancer, including goals and side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that arises from the ovaries, often presenting symptoms only in advanced stages, making early detection challenging.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries and can be influenced by inherited genetic mutations such as those found in the BRCA genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries and can spread to other parts of the body. It has various stages and typically involves treatments such as surgery and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries and may require treatments such as chemotherapy and maintenance therapies post-surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that originates from the ovaries and can be diagnosed through various imaging and testing methods mentioned.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that originates from the ovaries and is commonly diagnosed through biopsies and imaging tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and can be characterized by its relapse rates and responses to chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and is classified into stages based on its progression and extent of spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and may require treatments such as chemotherapy and maintenance therapies for management and improvement of outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Exam">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A pelvic exam is a physical examination where a healthcare provider checks the reproductive organs for abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pap Test">
      <data key="d0">Pap Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pap test is a procedure used primarily to detect cervical cancer, but is included in the broader evaluation for ovarian health.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pap test is a procedure used primarily to detect cervical cancer, not ovarian cancer, included as part of a broader evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pap test is a screening procedure for cervical cancer that detects abnormal cells in the cervix, typically conducted annually after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pap test is a screening procedure that collects cells from the cervix to identify abnormalities or precancerous changes, performed annually post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectovaginal Exam">
      <data key="d0">Rectovaginal Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A rectovaginal exam is an examination technique used to check for abnormalities in the space between the rectum and vagina.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing">
      <data key="d0">Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing encompasses various medical assessments, including biopsy and imaging, critical for diagnosing and staging breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Testing refers to the procedures used to evaluate and diagnose ovarian cancer, including imaging and lab tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominal Exam">
      <data key="d0">Abdominal Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An abdominal exam involves a healthcare provider feeling the abdomen to check for abnormalities or signs of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endometrial Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Endometrial Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An endometrial biopsy involves sampling the uterine lining to determine the presence of uterine cancer or abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormonal Changes">
      <data key="d0">Hormonal Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormonal changes can cause symptoms similar to those of ovarian cancer, complicating the diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Laparoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Laparoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic laparoscopy is a minimally invasive surgical procedure used to view the abdominal organs and is often applied in cases of advanced cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family History and Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Family History and Genetic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family history and genetic testing play a significant role in understanding ovarian cancer risk, including hereditary factors like BRCA mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA1">
      <data key="d0">BRCA1</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA1 is a gene associated with an increased risk of breast, ovarian, pancreatic, and other cancers when mutated.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene that, when mutated, can increase the risk of breast, ovarian, and other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene that, when mutated, can significantly increase the risk of developing ovarian and breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA2">
      <data key="d0">BRCA2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA2 is a gene linked to higher risks of breast cancer, ovarian cancer, and other cancer types when alterations are present.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is another gene associated with hereditary ovarian cancer and impacts the body's ability to repair DNA.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is similar to BRCA1 and its mutations are associated with a higher risk for breast, ovarian, and some other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lynch Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Lynch Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lynch Syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of various cancers, including ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch Syndrome is an inherited cancer syndrome associated with a higher risk of colorectal and other cancers due to mutations in MMR genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a genetic condition that increases the risk of colon cancer and other types of cancer due to inherited MMR gene mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of various cancers, including bladder cancer, due to genetic mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition that raises the risk of various cancers, including pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition that significantly increases the risk of developing various types of cancer, including pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition associated with a higher risk of certain cancers, including colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sedative">
      <data key="d0">Sedative</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sedative is a medication that may be provided to patients to help them relax during imaging tests, particularly if they experience anxiety in confined spaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Sedatives are a type of medication used to help individuals relax, particularly in stressful medical situations such as imaging procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chest X-ray">
      <data key="d0">Chest X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A chest x-ray is an imaging test that helps to show whether cancer has spread to the lungs, often utilized in the initial assessment of suspected cases of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tissue Samples">
      <data key="d0">Tissue Samples</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Tissue samples are collected during biopsies to test and confirm the presence of cancer cells, critical for diagnosing ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologist's Examination">
      <data key="d0">Pathologist's Examination</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A pathologist's examination involves inspecting biopsy samples under a microscope to identify cancerous cells and ascertain the type of cancer present.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paracentesis">
      <data key="d0">Paracentesis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Paracentesis is a procedure that involves inserting a needle into the abdomen to remove fluid for analysis, aiding in cancer diagnosis and management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Family History">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Family History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer family history refers to the record of cancer cases in a patient's family, which can help assess genetic risk factors associated with ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA Genes">
      <data key="d0">BRCA Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA genes are associated with hereditary ovarian cancer, where mutations can increase the risk of developing the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA genes are human genes that produce proteins responsible for repairing damaged DNA, and mutations in these genes can increase the risk of developing ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Registered Dietitian">
      <data key="d0">Registered Dietitian</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A registered dietitian is a healthcare professional who specializes in nutrition and diet planning, often providing guidance to patients regarding dietary habits and health.&lt;SEP&gt;A registered dietitian provides nutritional counseling to cancer patients, helping to manage dietary needs during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CA-125 Test">
      <data key="d0">CA-125 Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The CA-125 test is a blood test that measures the level of the cancer antigen CA-125, which can be an indicator of ovarian cancer but is not definitive for diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;The CA-125 test is a blood test used to measure levels of the cancer antigen 125, which can indicate ovarian cancer presence and treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Chemistry Profile">
      <data key="d0">Blood Chemistry Profile</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood chemistry profile is a set of tests that measures various chemicals in the blood, which can indicate health issues, including possible cancer spread or organ function.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood chemistry profile is a test that measures various chemicals in the blood to assess organ function and health status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colonoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Colonoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonoscopy is a diagnostic procedure used to visualize the interior of the colon and to identify or remove polyps for further examination.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure recommended every 3 to 5 years for monitoring potential colorectal issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure recommended for colorectal cancer screening and monitoring, typically scheduled every 3 years thereafter every 5 years.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the colon for abnormalities, including polyps and cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the inner lining of the colon, typically used to identify and remove polyps.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the interior of the colon and rectum, recommended for cancer surveillance in patients after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the large bowel for issues, which may be part of diagnostic processes in patients with symptoms suggestive of ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a procedure that allows doctors to examine the inner lining of the colon with a flexible scope, often used for polyp removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a procedure used to examine the interior of the colon and rectum, often performed after rectal cancer treatment to check for recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Upper Endoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Upper Endoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Upper endoscopy is a procedure to examine the upper gastrointestinal tract, which may be used in assessing symptoms that could be related to ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker Testing involves analyzing tumor samples to identify DNA mutations or protein levels, guiding personalized treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker Testing is the analysis of tumor samples to identify specific genetic mutations or protein expressions that can guide personalized cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker Testing looks for specific genetic and protein markers in cancer cells to inform personalized treatment strategies and predict outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing analyzes biological markers in the body to determine the presence of certain cancers or diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing analyzes genes or proteins to identify potential mutations affecting treatment options for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing analyzes genetic mutations and proteins present in tumors to guide cancer treatment decisions by identifying targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing examines specific DNA mutations or protein levels in samples to guide cancer treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing identifies specific biological markers in the tumor to determine the best treatment options for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing includes tests that analyze genes or proteins to identify mutations or conditions relevant to treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing a patient's tissue samples for markers that indicate the presence and specifics of cancers like gliomas and CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing biological markers to aid in the diagnosis and treatment of diseases, including cancers like bone cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing cancer cells to identify mutations or proteins that can inform treatment decisions and eligibility for targeted therapies or clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing the tumor for specific biological markers, helping to determine the most effective treatment options for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing tumor features to guide cancer treatment decisions and identify the best therapeutic options for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing tumor tissue or blood to identify specific cancer features which can guide treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing tumor tissue or blood to identify specific features that can inform treatment decisions for ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves examining tumor characteristics to help guide treatment decisions in cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing is a diagnostic process that helps determine specific characteristics of a cancer, guiding treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing is performed to identify specific biological markers related to cancer, aiding in the diagnosis and treatment decision-making process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD)">
      <data key="d0">Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HRD is a condition where cancer cells lack the ability to repair DNA damage effectively, often associated with BRCA mutations, influencing treatment options for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Markers">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Markers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor markers are substances found in blood or tissue that can indicate the presence or progression of cancer, such as CEA and CA 15-3.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor markers are substances found in the blood or body that can indicate the presence of cancer, particularly helpful in monitoring ongoing treatment responses in ovarian cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian cancer symptoms include bloating, pelvic or abdominal pain, difficulty in eating, and feeling full quickly, which can impact dietary habits and overall health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutritional Health">
      <data key="d0">Nutritional Health</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nutritional health refers to the balance of food intake and nutrients that support overall health, significantly impacting treatment outcomes in cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dietitian Consultation">
      <data key="d0">Dietitian Consultation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dietitian consultation involves engaging with a registered dietitian to receive tailored dietary advice and meal planning for health management, especially during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gastrointestinal Tract">
      <data key="d0">Gastrointestinal Tract</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The gastrointestinal tract includes organs like the stomach, small bowel, and large bowel (rectum and colon), which can be evaluated using imaging tests during cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colonoscopy Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Colonoscopy Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonoscopy is a diagnostic procedure that enables the examination of the colon to investigate symptoms and detect abnormalities related to ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Upper Endoscopy Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Upper Endoscopy Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Upper endoscopy is a procedure where a thin tube with a camera is inserted to visualize the upper gastrointestinal tract for diagnostic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Markers Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Markers Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor markers testing helps in monitoring the presence of specific substances in the body that indicate cancer, particularly ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Tissue Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Tissue Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor tissue analysis involves testing specimens of tumor tissue to identify characteristics essential for personalized cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Molecular testing evaluates the genetic makeup of tumor cells to identify mutations and inform targeted therapy strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular testing examines DNA and RNA alterations in tumors to find mutations that could influence treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular testing involves analyzing genetic mutations within tumor samples to provide insights into tumor type and its growth characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Somatic Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Somatic Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Somatic mutations are genetic changes within tumor cells that can help guide treatment decisions and are distinct from inherited mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laparotomy">
      <data key="d0">Laparotomy</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Laparotomy is a surgical procedure involving a large incision in the abdomen, commonly used for staging or cytoreductive surgery in ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IVF">
      <data key="d0">IVF</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">In vitro fertilization (IVF) is a reproductive technology that may be considered for women post-surgery to assist with pregnancy after ovarian cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Serous Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Serous Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade serous carcinoma is one of the most common forms of ovarian cancer, typically associated with aggressive behavior and poor prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor tissue refers to the cellular material extracted from a tumor for diagnostic testing, particularly in the context of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair (MMR)">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair (MMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch repair (MMR) refers to a system for recognizing and repairing erroneous insertion, deletion, and misincorporation of bases during DNA replication, crucial in cancer pathology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 Expression">
      <data key="d0">HER2 Expression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 expression is a characteristic of certain breast cancers that can determine eligibility for targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 expression is a test for the presence of the HER2 protein, which is associated with aggressive breast cancer forms and is relevant in ovarian cancer diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAF V600E Mutation">
      <data key="d0">BRAF V600E Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRAF V600E mutation is a specific genetic alteration that can influence treatment decisions and prognostics in several cancers, including ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Folate Receptor Alpha (FRα) Expression">
      <data key="d0">Folate Receptor Alpha (FRα) Expression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Folate receptor alpha (FRα) expression refers to the presence of a receptor that can be exploited for targeted therapies in certain cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RET Mutations">
      <data key="d0">RET Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RET mutations are genetic alterations that can be detected in various cancers, impacting treatment strategies and patient prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NTRK Gene Fusion">
      <data key="d0">NTRK Gene Fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NTRK gene fusion refers to a chromosomal abnormality that may be targeted in therapies for different cancer types, including ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Before Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Before Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy before surgery is a systemic treatment approach used to shrink tumors prior to surgical intervention in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Total Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Total Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Total hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves removing the uterus and cervix, often performed in the treatment of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bilateral Salpingo-Oophorectomy (BSO)">
      <data key="d0">Bilateral Salpingo-Oophorectomy (BSO)</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy (BSO) involves removing both ovaries and fallopian tubes, a common surgical approach for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unilateral Salpingo-Oophorectomy (USO)">
      <data key="d0">Unilateral Salpingo-Oophorectomy (USO)</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy (USO) is a surgical removal of one ovary and its fallopian tube, indicated in certain early-stage ovarian cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cytoreductive Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Cytoreductive Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Cytoreductive surgery aims to remove as much tumor mass as possible in patients with advanced ovarian cancer to improve outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility-Sparing Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Fertility-Sparing Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility-sparing surgery is a surgical option that avoids the removal of reproductive organs, allowing for potential future pregnancies even after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interval Cytoreductive Surgery (ICS)">
      <data key="d0">Interval Cytoreductive Surgery (ICS)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Interval cytoreductive surgery (ICS) is a surgical procedure performed after chemotherapy to remove as much cancer as possible, particularly in advanced stages of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIGO Staging System">
      <data key="d0">FIGO Staging System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The FIGO staging system is a classification method used to determine the extent of ovarian cancer, based on the growth of the tumor, lymph node involvement, and spread to distant sites.&lt;SEP&gt;The FIGO staging system is used to categorize ovarian cancer into different stages, helping to guide treatment and predict outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC)">
      <data key="d0">American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">AJCC is an organization that standardizes cancer staging systems which help in assessing the extent and prognosis of cancer in patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The AJCC provides a standardized system for staging different types of cancers, including colon cancer, using the TNM classification.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is an organization that has developed a staging system for various types of cancer, including ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is responsible for creating a staging system to classify cancer extent and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Menopause">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Menopause</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical menopause refers to the onset of menopause due to the removal of both ovaries during surgery, resulting in a rapid drop in estrogen levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Side Effects of Ovarian Cancer Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Side Effects of Ovarian Cancer Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Common side effects of ovarian cancer surgery include pain, swelling, leg swelling, and complications such as deep vein thrombosis (DVT).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvis">
      <data key="d0">Pelvis</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The pelvis is a basin-shaped structure in the human body that connects the spine with the lower limbs and houses vital organs within the abdomen.&lt;SEP&gt;The pelvis is a region of the body that may be imaged in cases of rectal cancer to assess the extent of cancer growth.&lt;SEP&gt;The pelvis is the body area located between the abdomen and the legs, housing organs like the prostate and bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;The pelvis is the lower part of the abdomen, surrounding the reproductive organs, including the ovaries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peritoneal Washing">
      <data key="d0">Peritoneal Washing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Peritoneal washing is a procedure where a liquid is circulated in the abdominal cavity during surgery to collect samples for cancer cell testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microscopic Metastases">
      <data key="d0">Microscopic Metastases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microscopic metastases refer to cancer cells that have spread beyond the primary tumor site, often not visible to the naked eye.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is a condition where blood clots form in deep veins, commonly a risk after surgeries including ovarian cancer surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT)">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) is a treatment to alleviate symptoms caused by hormonal imbalances, often considered after surgical menopause.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paclitaxel">
      <data key="d0">Paclitaxel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Paclitaxel is a chemotherapy drug often used in combination with carboplatin for treating ovarian cancer, effective in various stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Paclitaxel is a chemotherapy drug often used to treat ovarian cancer by inhibiting cancer cell growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Paclitaxel is a chemotherapy medication often used in combination with other drugs to treat different forms of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Paclitaxel is a chemotherapy medication often utilized in combination with other agents to treat different types of cancer, including cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Paclitaxel is another taxane chemotherapy drug effective in treating various cancers, including breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bevacizumab">
      <data key="d0">Bevacizumab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bevacizumab (Avastin) is a targeted therapy that works biologically in combination with chemotherapy for treating certain types of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab is a targeted therapy drug that can be used in treating ovarian cancer by inhibiting tumor growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Checking for Cancer Cells">
      <data key="d0">Checking for Cancer Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Checking for cancer cells is a crucial part of diagnosing and staging ovarian cancer to determine appropriate treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bevacizumab (Avastin)">
      <data key="d0">Bevacizumab (Avastin)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Bevacizumab is a targeted therapy that may be added to chemotherapy for patients with recurrent platinum-resistant ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab, commercially known as Avastin, is a medication that inhibits blood vessel growth to tumors and can be added to chemotherapy regimens.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab, marketed as Avastin, is a medication that inhibits the growth of new blood vessels that supply tumors and is used in chemotherapy, particularly for cancers like ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab, marketed as Avastin, is a medication used to inhibit the growth of blood vessels that supply tumors, and is often used in chemotherapy for cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1A">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A is an initial stage of ovarian cancer where cancer is present in one ovary, with intact outer sac and no cancer cells found outside the ovary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1B">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1B ovarian cancer involves cancer in both ovaries while the capsules remain intact and no cancer cells are present outside the ovaries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1C">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1C</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1C signifies cancer presence in one or both ovaries with possible complications such as capsule rupture or presence of cancer cells in bodily fluids.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2A">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2A indicates cancer in one or both ovaries with growth into or spread to the uterus and/or fallopian tubes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2B">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2B reflects cancer spread from one or both ovaries into other organs or tissues in the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3A1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3A1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3A1 shows cancer in one or both ovaries with spread to abdominal lymph nodes, classified further by size of cancer in lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3B">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3B indicates visible cancer on abdominal tissue, with size restrictions defining the stage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3C">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3C</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3C is characterized by visible cancer on abdominal lining and possible spread to the outer surface of the liver or spleen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 bladder cancer represents the most advanced stage where cancer has spread to distant lymph nodes or organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4 is the most advanced stage of ovarian cancer where it spreads to other body parts including organs outside the abdomen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Pleural Effusion">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Pleural Effusion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Malignant pleural effusion refers to the presence of cancer cells in the fluid around the lungs in Stage 4A ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Regimen">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Regimen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A combination of chemotherapy drugs designed to treat specific types of cancer, administered typically alongside radiation for enhanced effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy regimen encompasses the specific combination and schedule of chemotherapy drugs used to treat ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cycles of Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Cycles of Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cycles of treatment refer to the repeated sequence of chemotherapy sessions followed by recovery periods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peripheral Neuropathy">
      <data key="d0">Peripheral Neuropathy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Peripheral neuropathy is a potential side effect of chemotherapy, leading to pain and tingling sensations, notably in hands and feet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravenous Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravenous Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravenous chemotherapy involves administering cancer treatment drugs directly into the bloodstream, commonly used for ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravenous chemotherapy involves delivering chemotherapy drugs directly into the bloodstream, commonly used in the treatment of various cancers, including ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intraperitoneal (IP) Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intraperitoneal (IP) Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IP chemotherapy is a method where chemotherapy drugs are delivered directly into the abdominal cavity, targeting cancer cells more effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Intraperitoneal chemotherapy delivers medication directly into the abdominal cavity, targeting cancer cells in this area for enhanced treatment effectiveness, particularly for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maintenance Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Maintenance Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Maintenance therapy involves ongoing treatments with intravesical BCG to lower the chances of cancer recurrence after initial treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Maintenance therapy is a follow-up treatment aimed at prolonging remission and minimizing the chances of cancer recurrence after initial therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Maintenance therapy refers to systemic treatments given after initial cancer treatment to reduce the risk of recurrence or progression, commonly used for advanced ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARP Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">PARP Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PARP Inhibitors are drugs that block the PARP enzyme, leading to the death of cancer cells, particularly in those with BRCA mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;PARP inhibitors are a class of drugs used in the treatment of ovarian cancer, specifically targeting cancer cells with certain genetic mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;PARP inhibitors are a class of medications used in the treatment of cancers with specific mutations, such as BRCA mutations, to maintain therapy effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;PARP inhibitors are targeted therapies used primarily in cancers with specific genetic mutations, such as BRCA, to enhance the effectiveness of treatment and prolong remission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Olaparib (Lynparza)">
      <data key="d0">Olaparib (Lynparza)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Olaparib, known as Lynparza, is a PARP inhibitor used for maintenance therapy in patients with BRCA-mutated ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Olaparib, known as Lynparza, is a PARP inhibitor used in the maintenance therapy of ovarian cancer, especially in patients with BRCA mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Niraparib (Zejula)">
      <data key="d0">Niraparib (Zejula)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Niraparib, branded as Zejula, is another PARP inhibitor used in maintenance therapy for certain types of ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Niraparib, marketed as Zejula, is another type of PARP inhibitor that is used in the maintenance treatment of ovarian cancer for patients who may not tolerate other therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rucaparib (Rubraca)">
      <data key="d0">Rucaparib (Rubraca)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Rucaparib, referred to as Rubraca, is a PARP inhibitor utilized for maintenance therapy in the treatment of ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Rucaparib, referred to as Rubraca, is a PARP inhibitor utilized in the maintenance therapy for certain types of ovarian cancer, especially in those with BRCA mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myelodysplastic Syndrome (MDS)">
      <data key="d0">Myelodysplastic Syndrome (MDS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myelodysplastic syndrome is a type of cancer that affects blood cell production in the bone marrow and can be a rare but serious side effect of chemotherapy treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Niraparib">
      <data key="d0">Niraparib</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Niraparib is a maintenance therapy drug that can be administered to ovarian cancer patients for up to 3 years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rucaparib">
      <data key="d0">Rucaparib</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Rucaparib is another PARP inhibitor used in the treatment of ovarian cancer, which can be given for up to 2 years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mirvetuximab Soravtansine-gynx (Elahere)">
      <data key="d0">Mirvetuximab Soravtansine-gynx (Elahere)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Mirvetuximab soravtansine-gynx is a targeted therapy specifically for tumors with the folate receptor alpha (FRα) biomarker in recurrent ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker-based treatment">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker-based treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker-based treatment refers to therapies that target specific biological markers associated with cancer, enhancing treatment effectiveness for certain patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypersensitivity Reactions">
      <data key="d0">Hypersensitivity Reactions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hypersensitivity reactions are adverse responses that can occur with repeated chemotherapy use, which can be life-threatening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CA-125 Level">
      <data key="d0">CA-125 Level</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CA-125 level is a tumor marker often monitored in ovarian cancer patients for signs of recurrence or treatment effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2 Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2 Ovarian Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 ovarian cancer indicates the invasive nature of cancer, where it has spread to nearby tissues but not to other organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarkers">
      <data key="d0">Biomarkers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarkers are biological indicators that help in identifying certain characteristics of cancer cells, informing treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are indicators found in the body that signal a condition or disease, used in cancer care to improve diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers refer to biological indicators that can guide treatment decisions in cancer, such as BRCA mutations and others identified for targeted therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Cytoreductive Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Cytoreductive Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondary cytoreductive surgery is a procedure aimed at removing visible tumors and is considered before commencing recurrence treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curriculum Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">Curriculum Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Curriculum guidelines refer to the suggested practices and recommendations for managing ovarian cancer treatment and monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app offers information and guidelines for cancer patients regarding their treatment and side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app provides resources and information for patients, particularly regarding cancer treatments and fertility preservation.&lt;SEP&gt;This app offers cancer care guidelines and support tailored for patients, facilitating access to vital information.&lt;SEP&gt;This app offers patient-friendly information on cancer guidelines and education for patients diagnosed with cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Throat Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Throat cancer refers to cancers affecting the throat area, often staged using the TNM system to determine severity and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer refers to malignant tumors that can occur in the throat, and understanding it is critical for treatment planning and management.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer refers to malignant tumors that develop in the throat, which may require various tests and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer refers to various cancers that can occur in the throat, particularly focusing on SCC oropharynx cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer, particularly oropharyngeal cancer, involves tumors located in the throat and is staged using various systems to guide treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oropharynx">
      <data key="d0">Oropharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Oropharynx is a specific part of the throat that includes structures such as the soft palate, tonsils, and the base of the tongue, and is a common site for throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is a part of the throat behind the mouth, which plays a role in both the respiratory and digestive systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is a part of the throat located behind the mouth, involved in the digestive and respiratory systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is a part of the throat that includes the back of the roof of the mouth, the tonsils, and the base of the tongue, and is commonly affected by cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is the part of the throat located behind the mouth, including the soft palate, tonsils, and the back walls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck cancers are a group of cancers that arise in the head or neck area, closely related to NPC.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers are a group of cancers that arise in the head or neck region, often characterized by specific tumor locations and types, such as SCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers encompass a diverse group of malignancies arising in the anatomical area including the mouth, throat, and nasal cavity, with NPC being one type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharynx">
      <data key="d0">Pharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">anatomy</data>
      <data key="d2">The pharynx is a passage that connects the nasal cavity to the larynx and esophagus and is a critical area where throat cancers can develop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soft Palate">
      <data key="d0">Soft Palate</data>
      <data key="d1">anatomy</data>
      <data key="d2">The soft palate is the back part of the roof of the mouth, involved in swallowing and speech, and is one of the anatomical regions that can develop cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tonsils">
      <data key="d0">Tonsils</data>
      <data key="d1">anatomy</data>
      <data key="d2">Tonsils are lymphoid tissues located at the back of the throat that can serve as sites for cancer, particularly in throat-related cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Larynx">
      <data key="d0">Larynx</data>
      <data key="d1">anatomy</data>
      <data key="d2">The Larynx is a part of the respiratory tract located in the throat, playing a crucial role in sound production and protecting the airway during swallowing.&lt;SEP&gt;The larynx, also known as the voice box, is located in the throat and can be affected by cancers, including throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trachea">
      <data key="d0">Trachea</data>
      <data key="d1">anatomy</data>
      <data key="d2">The trachea, or windpipe, is a tube that connects the throat to the lungs, important in respiration and can be involved in cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Head and Neck">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Head and Neck</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Head and Neck (SCCHN) is the most prevalent form of cancer in the head and neck region, particularly affecting the throat areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasal Cavity">
      <data key="d0">Nasal Cavity</data>
      <data key="d1">anatomy</data>
      <data key="d2">The nasal cavity is involved in breathing and is a region where cancers can develop, playing a role in head and neck cancer classifications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Testing">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility testing is an assessment that may be necessary for cancer patients, as treatments can impact reproductive health.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility testing is part of the assessment process for individuals receiving treatment for ALL, examining how treatment may affect reproductive health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Testing">
      <data key="d0">HPV Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV testing involves checking for the presence of human papillomavirus, which can be related to throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dental Exam">
      <data key="d0">Dental Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A dental exam involves a thorough assessment of oral health and is crucial for patients with head and neck cancer to monitor changes due to disease or treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Dental exams are procedures performed by dentists to assess oral health, particularly important for patients with head and neck cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Dental exams involve checking the health of teeth and gums, particularly important for patients undergoing cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support Services">
      <data key="d0">Support Services</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Support services include counseling and emotional support for patients dealing with distress from a cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Support">
      <data key="d0">Patient Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient support includes various assistance methods for patients undergoing treatment, focusing on emotional, psychological, and logistical needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing Results">
      <data key="d0">Testing Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing results provide critical information about a patient's health status and inform treatment decisions, especially in cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco and Alcohol Use">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco and Alcohol Use</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tobacco and alcohol use are risk factors for developing throat cancer and can impact the effectiveness of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG)">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">ECOG is a performance status scale used to assess a patient's general health and ability to carry out self-care, influencing treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The ECOG is a scale designed to evaluate a patient's performance status in the context of cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is an organization that develops performance scales, such as the ECOG score, to evaluate patient health in clinical settings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mind and Body Therapists">
      <data key="d0">Mind and Body Therapists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mind and Body Therapists are professionals who assist cancer patients in coping with the psychological and physical effects of their illness through therapeutic techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Counseling Services">
      <data key="d0">Counseling Services</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Counseling Services provide emotional support and guidance for individuals facing distress related to cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distress">
      <data key="d0">Distress</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress is an emotional state that cancer patients may experience, characterized by feelings of sadness, fear, helplessness, and anxiety.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress refers to the emotional, mental, physical, or spiritual discomfort experienced by patients diagnosed with cancer, impacting their overall quality of life and treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B and C">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B and C</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B and C are viral liver diseases that can complicate cancer treatment, particularly for patients undergoing certain therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Function Tests">
      <data key="d0">Lung Function Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung Function Tests assess the efficiency of the lungs and are important for cancer patients whose treatments may affect respiratory health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and Neck Tumors can have significant implications for hearing and treatment procedures, impacting quality of life for affected patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment and Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment and Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment can lead to various side effects, influencing physical health, psychological wellbeing, and fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Status Scale">
      <data key="d0">Performance Status Scale</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance Status Scale evaluates a patient's level of functioning, greatly affecting treatment plans for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) PS">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) PS</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ECOG PS is a specific scale for assessing a patient's functional status, which informs treatment decisions in oncology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Papillomavirus (HPV)">
      <data key="d0">Human Papillomavirus (HPV)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV is a common sexually transmitted infection that is a major cause of cervical cancer and is significant for cervical dysplasia.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV is a sexually transmitted virus strongly linked to the development of anal cancer and other related cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV is a widely prevalent sexually transmitted virus that is a significant causative factor in cervical cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Papillomavirus (HPV) is a group of viruses that can infect the skin or mucous membranes, and is known to cause certain types of cancer, including throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16 protein">
      <data key="d0">p16 protein</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">P16 is a protein used as a biomarker in cancer testing, particularly for detecting HPV-related cancers and influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormonal Birth Control">
      <data key="d0">Hormonal Birth Control</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormonal Birth Control consists of medications or devices aimed at preventing pregnancy, which might be influenced by cancer treatment considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormonal Birth Control includes methods used to prevent pregnancy, which may be discussed among patients undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormonal birth control encompasses various methods that use hormones to prevent pregnancy and may be recommended based on the patient's health and treatment circumstances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intrauterine Devices (IUDs)">
      <data key="d0">Intrauterine Devices (IUDs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IUDs are a form of long-term birth control that can be inserted into the uterus to prevent pregnancy and may be discussed with cancer patients before treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrauterine Devices are a form of long-term birth control used to prevent pregnancy, with specific implications during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barrier Methods">
      <data key="d0">Barrier Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Barrier Methods include contraceptive techniques that physically block sperm from reaching the egg, essential in the context of cancer treatment complications.&lt;SEP&gt;Barrier methods include contraception techniques to prevent pregnancy during and after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Barrier methods of contraception, including condoms and diaphragms, provide physical barriers to prevent sperm from fertilizing an egg.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Menstruation">
      <data key="d0">Menstruation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Menstruation refers to the monthly process in which the female body expels the uterine lining, and its interruption may occur due to cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pregnancy Plan">
      <data key="d0">Pregnancy Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pregnancy plan outlines the timing and health considerations for individuals looking to conceive, especially after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy Samples">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy Samples</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsy samples are collected from suspected cancerous tissues to confirm diagnoses and provide insight into cancer characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsy samples are collected to diagnose conditions such as cancer, and are tested for specific indicators like HPV presence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV-related Cancer">
      <data key="d0">HPV-related Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV-related cancers are those that are linked to infections by Human Papillomavirus, most notably throat and cervical cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome 9">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome 9</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosome 9 is one of the human chromosomes that houses genes, including those linked to p16 protein production and cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosome 9 is one of the two chromosomes involved in the BCR::ABL1 gene rearrangement, contributing to the development of CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="open MRI">
      <data key="d0">open MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Open MRI is a type of MRI that features a large opening, making it less confining than traditional closed MRIs, which is beneficial for patients with claustrophobia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="contrast material">
      <data key="d0">contrast material</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast material is a substance used in medical imaging to enhance the clarity of images during scans, aiding in the diagnosis of various conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultrasound is a common imaging technique used to examine the thyroid gland for nodules and other abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses high-frequency sound waves to create images of internal body structures, commonly used in various medical evaluations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="panoramic dental x-ray">
      <data key="d0">panoramic dental x-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A panoramic dental x-ray captures comprehensive images of the mouth, helping dentists plan for treatments like braces and extractions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="endoscopy">
      <data key="d0">endoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endoscopy is a medical procedure that uses an endoscope to internally examine organs and tissues, which may be necessary for biopsies or diagnosing conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Laryngoscopy is a procedure used to examine the larynx (voice box) and adjacent areas for abnormalities, such as tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Open MRI">
      <data key="d0">Open MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Open MRI is a type of MRI that features a magnet with openings on both ends, providing a less claustrophobic experience compared to closed MRIs.&lt;SEP&gt;Open MRI offers a design with a magnetic top and bottom, allowing an opening at each end, making it less claustrophobic compared to closed MRI.&lt;SEP&gt;Open MRI refers to a type of MRI that has a larger opening, reducing the feeling of confinement for patients, particularly beneficial for those with claustrophobia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Closed MRI">
      <data key="d0">Closed MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Closed MRI is a more common form of MRI that has a tube-like structure, which can create feelings of claustrophobia for some patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Closed MRI is a type of MRI that features a capsule-like design with the magnet surrounding the patient, which can be challenging for individuals with claustrophobia.&lt;SEP&gt;Closed MRI is the more common form of MRI, featuring a tube-shaped magnet that can cause discomfort for patients with claustrophobia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endoscopy is a procedure that involves inserting a flexible tube with a camera into the body to examine internal organs and may be used for biopsies or diagnostic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fiberoptic Nasopharyngolaryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Fiberoptic Nasopharyngolaryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fiberoptic Nasopharyngolaryngoscopy is an examination technique used to visualize the larynx and surrounding structures, often performed using a flexible endoscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Laryngoscopy is a procedure that involves examining the larynx and throat structures with a laryngoscope to identify potential abnormalities or tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fiberoptic laryngoscopy is a medical procedure using a flexible scope to examine the throat, typically done rapidly for diagnostic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Direct Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Direct Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Direct laryngoscopy is a thorough examination of the throat performed under general anesthesia, allowing for deeper visual inspection and biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Esophagoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophagoscopy involves guiding a device down the throat to inspect the esophagus for abnormalities, conducted under anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Bronchoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchoscopy is a procedure that examines the inside of the airway including the trachea and bronchi, often used in cancer detection and treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panendoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Panendoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Panendoscopy is a comprehensive inspection procedure combining multiple endoscopies to assess areas from the mouth to the bronchi, undertaken under anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H/dMMR Mutation">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H/dMMR Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H/dMMR mutation refers to cancer cell changes that result in errors in DNA repair, affecting treatment responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite instability (MSI) and deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) mutations are conditions where DNA repair mechanisms are flawed, linked to certain cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1 Testing">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1 Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 testing assesses the expression of a protein that can suppress immune response against cancer cells, guiding treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;PD-L1 testing examines the presence of the PD-L1 protein on cancer cells, impacting treatment plans by signaling whether immune therapy could be effective.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Testing by p16">
      <data key="d0">HPV Testing by p16</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV testing by p16 is recommended for throat cancer diagnosis, assessing the presence of the HPV virus related to cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flexible Scope">
      <data key="d0">Flexible Scope</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">The flexible scope is a medical device used in various endoscopic procedures to visualize internal areas such as the throat and esophagus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laryngoscope">
      <data key="d0">Laryngoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">The laryngoscope is a tool used during direct laryngoscopy to illuminate and access the throat for examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tissue Sample">
      <data key="d0">Tissue Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tissue sample is a medical specimen taken for diagnostic purposes, particularly relevant in cancer diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A tissue sample is a small amount of tissue taken from the body to be examined in a laboratory, typically to check for diseases like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A tissue sample is collected during procedures like biopsies to be examined for abnormalities or diseases such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair (MMR) Proteins">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair (MMR) Proteins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MMR proteins are involved in fixing DNA replication errors, maintaining genomic stability and preventing cancer mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Mismatch repair proteins are responsible for correcting DNA errors; their deficiency can lead to microsatellite instability and contribute to cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Combined Positive Score (CPS)">
      <data key="d0">Combined Positive Score (CPS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CPS is a scoring system used to evaluate PD-L1 expression levels on tumor cells in order to guide immunotherapy treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16 (HPV) Testing">
      <data key="d0">p16 (HPV) Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">p16 (HPV) testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with throat cancer to provide information about the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AJCC Staging System">
      <data key="d0">AJCC Staging System</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The AJCC created a standardized system for staging cancer, incorporating tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB) refers to the number of mutations within cancer cells, and when elevated, it is often targeted by immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden (TMB) quantifies the number of mutations in the DNA of tumor cells, influencing treatment strategies with immune therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden refers to the number of mutations present in a tumor, which can guide treatment options for cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy is a treatment that helps the immune system recognize and attack cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer(">
      <data key="d0">Cancer(</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer refers to a group of diseases characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells that can invade and damage surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Checkpoint Inhibitor">
      <data key="d0">Checkpoint Inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Checkpoint inhibitors are a form of cancer immunotherapy that helps the immune system detect and attack cancer cells by blocking certain proteins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy and Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy and Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This refers to the combination of chemotherapy drugs and immune checkpoint inhibitors as a therapeutic strategy for treating certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor testing encompasses various diagnostic tests conducted on tumor samples to identify mutations, protein expressions, and other characteristics relevant to treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor testing involves analyzing tissue samples from tumors to identify genetic information and potential treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biological Cancer Testing">
      <data key="d0">Biological Cancer Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biological cancer testing includes tests like biopsies and blood tests that assess tumor characteristics and help determine suitable treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oropharyngeal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Oropharyngeal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oropharyngeal cancer is a specific type of head and neck cancer that occurs in the oropharynx, the part of the throat just behind the mouth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is the most common cancer diagnosed in children, characterized by the rapid proliferation of immature lymphocytes or lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing cancer that begins in lymphocytes, leading to an overproduction of immature white blood cells that can disrupt normal blood functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell crucial to the immune response, playing a role in fighting infections and forming antibodies.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell crucial to the immune system, with primary types including B-cells, T-cells, and NK cells, all of which play roles in fighting diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell essential for the immune response, responsible for generating antibodies and fighting infections.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell involved in the immune response, and they include B cells and T cells, which are affected by ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that play a crucial role in the immune system, fighting infections, and are the cells that can become cancerous in lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that play a crucial role in the immune system, involved in fighting infections and diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that plays a crucial role in immune response, and their abnormal growth characterizes lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that plays a key role in the immune system, and their abnormal growth signifies the presence of lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are crucial components of the immune system and are categorized into B-cells and T-cells; they are the cells affected by ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are white blood cells that are integral to the immune system and can become cancerous in lymphomas, including primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM System">
      <data key="d0">TNM System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM staging system is a classification system used to stage cancers, indicating the size of the tumor, involvement of lymph nodes, and presence of metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a classification method used by cancer experts to describe tumors based on size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a classification method used for staging most bone cancers, categorizing them based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a standard staging system used to describe the extent of cancer based on tumor size (T), lymph node involvement (N), and metastasis (M).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV">
      <data key="d0">HPV</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV (human papillomavirus) is a common sexually transmitted infection linked to most cervical cancers, with various risk levels depending on the strain.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV, or Human Papillomavirus, is a sexually transmitted virus linked to an increased risk of anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Papillomavirus (HPV) is a common sexually transmitted virus that is a strong risk factor for developing anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Papillomavirus (HPV) is a virus that can cause abnormal tissue growth and is associated with head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Papillomavirus (HPV) is a virus that can lead to certain types of cancer, including some throat cancers, particularly those classified as p16 positive.&lt;SEP&gt;Human papillomavirus (HPV) is a virus that can cause various types of cancers, including anal cancer, and is classified into more than 100 strains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Stage">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical stage is determined before treatment based on physical exams and tests, guiding initial treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical stage is the cancer stage assessed based on physical exams, biopsies, and imaging tests before any treatment is administered.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical stage refers to the rating given to cancer before any treatment, based on physical exams, biopsies, and imaging tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologic Stage">
      <data key="d0">Pathologic Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologic stage is assessed after surgical removal of tissue to provide a more accurate understanding of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologic stage is determined by examining tissue removed during surgery, providing detailed information on cancer spread and severity.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologic stage refers to the cancer stage determined by examining tissue removed during surgery, providing a precise assessment of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM Scores">
      <data key="d0">TNM Scores</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TNM scores indicate the size of the tumor (T), involvement of lymph nodes (N), and presence of metastasis (M) in the staging of cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resectable Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Resectable Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A resectable tumor is one that can be completely removed with surgery, indicating a more favorable prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unresectable Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Unresectable Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An unresectable tumor is one that cannot be safely removed due to its invasive characteristics or involvement with vital structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locoregional Disease">
      <data key="d0">Locoregional Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locoregional disease refers to cancer that has spread to tissue, organs, or lymph nodes in the vicinity but not to distant parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16- Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d0">p16- Throat Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">p16- throat cancer is a type that begins in the oropharynx and is not caused by human papillomavirus (HPV), affecting treatment and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16-positive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">p16-positive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">p16-positive cancer is associated with HPV and influences the approach to treatment and management of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer affecting lymphocytes, leading to the production of immature lymphoblasts in the bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer characterized by an increase in immature lymphocytes called lymphoblasts, often requiring specific treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer that arises from lymphocytes, leading to the proliferation of immature white blood cells called lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a type of blood cancer characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphoblasts, affecting the patient's ability to produce healthy blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia is the most common cancer diagnosed in children and is a fast-growing cancer that involves lymphocytes, affecting their production in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell ALL (B-ALL)">
      <data key="d0">B-cell ALL (B-ALL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell ALL is a subtype of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia that originates in B-cell lymphocytes and is more common than T-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell ALL (T-ALL)">
      <data key="d0">T-cell ALL (T-ALL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell ALL is another subtype of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia that starts in T-cell lymphocytes and can lead to complications affecting the thymus.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell ALL, also known as T-ALL, is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that originates in T-lymphocytes and can lead to significant health problems including organ enlargement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burkitt Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Burkitt Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Burkitt leukemia is a rare subtype of mature B-cell ALL closely related to Burkitt lymphoma but treated differently from typical B-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Burkitt leukemia is a rare subtype of mature B-cell ALL that is treated differently than B-ALL, having similarities with Burkitt lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a nonprofit alliance of leading cancer centers that publishes guidelines outlining treatment options for cancers, including ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines and recommendations for cancer care, particularly for Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric cancer care refers to the treatment protocols and considerations for younger patients with ALL, specifically those 18 years or younger and certain adolescents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">B-cell Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that are involved in the immune response and are the origin of B-cell ALL, which is more common than T-cell ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">T-cell Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell lymphocytes are another type of white blood cell, the origin of T-cell ALL, which can lead to clinical symptoms such as enlarged thymus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burkitt Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Burkitt Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Burkitt lymphoma is a highly aggressive form of B-cell lymphoma that shares similarities with B-cell ALL but is treated differently.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoblastic Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoblastic lymphoma occurs when lymphoblasts primarily grow in lymph nodes rather than in the bone marrow, related to both B-ALL and T-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric acute lymphoblastic leukemia refers specifically to ALL cases diagnosed in children and young adults, along with special considerations in their treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALL">
      <data key="d0">ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ALL, or Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, is a type of cancer affecting the blood and bone marrow, characterized by an excess of lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a type of cancer that affects lymphoid progenitor cells and can lead to an increased amount of lymphocytes, disrupting normal blood cell formation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-ALL refers to B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, a common subtype of ALL that affects B-lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL) is a subtype of ALL characterized by the proliferation of B-lymphoblasts, which requires specific treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-ALL">
      <data key="d0">T-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-ALL is a subtype of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia that originates in T-cell lymphocytes and is less common than B-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;T-ALL, or T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is a type of cancer that originates in T-cell lymphocytes, potentially causing serious health issues including difficulty in breathing.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL) involves cancer that starts in T-cell lymphocytes and has different treatment options from B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="rectal cancer">
      <data key="d0">rectal cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the rectum and can often be treated effectively, especially when diagnosed early.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is cancer that forms in the rectum, often starting as a non-cancerous polyp called an adenoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenocarcinoma is a less common form of bladder cancer that arises from glandular cells in the bladder tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a rare form of bladder cancer arising from glandular cells in the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer that forms in mucus-secreting glands, commonly associated with rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is the most common type of rectal tumor that originates in the inner lining of the rectum or colon and can become invasive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="blood cells">
      <data key="d0">blood cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood cells include various types such as red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, all of which originate from blood stem cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lymphoid progenitor cells">
      <data key="d0">lymphoid progenitor cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoid progenitor cells are specialized stem cells that develop into lymphocytes and their proliferation is associated with Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell vital for the immune response and are specifically affected in ALL, which arises from lymphoid progenitor cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1-negative B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1-negative B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL1-negative B-ALL is a type of B-ALL characterized by negative results for the BCR::ABL1 gene, guiding treatment options and indicating the need for clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL treatment focuses on inhibiting the activity of the BCR::ABL protein, and typically includes intensive systemic therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="clinical trial">
      <data key="d0">clinical trial</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A clinical trial for cancer treatment is an experiment conducted to test new therapies or medications for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study conducted to evaluate the effectiveness and safety of new treatments or interventions, often involving human participants.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study examining new treatments, intended to develop approved therapies by the FDA.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials provide research opportunities for novel therapies in the treatment of ALL and are pivotal for collecting data on efficacy and safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="patient advocacy groups">
      <data key="d0">patient advocacy groups</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient advocacy groups offer support and resources for families dealing with cancer, including guidance on treatment options and emotional support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="pediatric leukemia center">
      <data key="d0">pediatric leukemia center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric leukemia centers specialize in diagnosing and treating leukemias in children, providing tailored care and expert treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colon Cancer provide specific protocols and recommendations for the diagnosis and treatment of colon cancer, similar to those for ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lynch syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Lynch syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition that increases the risk of developing colorectal and other cancers due to mutations in mismatch repair (MMR) genes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)">
      <data key="d0">Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Familial adenomatous polyposis is a rare inherited syndrome that causes the formation of numerous polyps in the colon and rectum, which can lead to rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA)">
      <data key="d0">circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) testing is a method of analyzing small fragments of DNA shed by tumors into the bloodstream to detect cancer presence or recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Polyps are abnormal growths on the lining of the colon that can potentially develop into colorectal cancer if not monitored or removed.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are abnormal growths on the lining of the rectum, which may become cancerous over time, necessitating monitoring and potential removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are non-cancerous growths in the colon and rectum that can occasionally lead to cancer if not removed, especially adenomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are non-cancerous growths that form on the inner lining of the colon and rectum, with the most common type being adenomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperplastic Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Hyperplastic Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperplastic polyps are a type of colon polyp that is generally considered benign and does not typically lead to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperplastic polyps are another type of polyp that rarely turn into cancer, unlike adenomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory polyps are non-cancerous and generally occur as a result of inflammation in the colon.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory polyps are polyps that arise due to inflammation, often linked to conditions like ulcerative colitis, and are not cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polypectomy">
      <data key="d0">Polypectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure performed during colonoscopy to remove polyps before they can progress to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure to remove polyps from the colon during a colonoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure used to remove polyps from the colon during a colonoscopy to prevent cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a surgical procedure performed during colonoscopy to remove one or more polyps from the colon.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a surgical procedure to remove polyps from the rectum, which can sometimes contain cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Testing">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch Repair Testing is a genetic test conducted on tumors to check for mutations in genes that repair DNA, significant for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Mismatch repair testing is a process to identify deficiencies in DNA repair mechanisms in tumors, critical for treatment planning in colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CEA (Carcinoembryonic Antigen)">
      <data key="d0">CEA (Carcinoembryonic Antigen)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CEA is a protein found in the blood that is typically elevated in individuals with colorectal cancer, used for monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Circulating Tumor DNA (ctDNA)">
      <data key="d0">Circulating Tumor DNA (ctDNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Circulating Tumor DNA (ctDNA) refers to small pieces of DNA from tumor cells that can be detected in the bloodstream, useful for predicting cancer recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) refers to DNA fragments shed by tumors into the bloodstream, used for monitoring cancer and guiding treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectum">
      <data key="d0">Rectum</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The rectum is the final section of the large intestine that stores feces until they are expelled from the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The rectum is the final section of the large intestine, holding stool until it is expelled from the body through the anus.&lt;SEP&gt;The rectum is the terminal part of the intestine that stores feces before they are expelled from the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endorectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Endorectal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An endorectal ultrasound (EUS) uses sound waves to produce images of the rectal area and evaluate cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal cancer is a common type of cancer that begins in the colon or rectum, often originating from polyps.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal cancer is a type of cancer that affects the colon and rectum, often requiring various imaging techniques for diagnosis and staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal cancer predominantly begins in glandular cells within the colon or rectum, differing from anal cancer in cell type and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal cancer refers to cancer that begins in the colon or rectum and is often linked to polyps in the colon lining.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal cancer refers to cancer that starts in the colon or rectum and can spread to nearby lymph nodes or other organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sperm Banking">
      <data key="d0">Sperm Banking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sperm banking involves freezing semen for future use, especially important for men undergoing cancer treatments that may impact fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Sperm banking is a process where semen is preserved for future use, often a consideration during cancer treatment for fertility preservation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Egg Freezing">
      <data key="d0">Egg Freezing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Egg freezing involves extracting and preserving unfertilized eggs for potential future use, important for women undergoing certain cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Egg freezing is a fertility preservation method that allows unfertilized eggs to be removed, frozen, and stored for potential future use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Tissue Banking">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Tissue Banking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian tissue banking is a method to freeze ovarian tissue containing eggs for later use, applicable for women facing fertility risks from cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian tissue banking is a technique for preserving fertility by freezing ovarian tissue containing eggs for later re-implantation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Agent">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Agent</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A contrast agent is a substance used during imaging tests to enhance the clarity of medical images, often injected or ingested to aid diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A contrast agent is a substance used in imaging procedures to enhance the visibility of blood vessels and organs in the images.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steroid">
      <data key="d0">Steroid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Steroids are medications that may be given to patients before imaging tests to reduce the risk of allergic reactions to contrast agents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antihistamine Medication">
      <data key="d0">Antihistamine Medication</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Antihistamines, like Benadryl, are medications used to prevent allergic reactions, often administered before imaging tests involving contrast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (POI)">
      <data key="d0">Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (POI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (POI) is a condition that may result from cancer treatments, affecting fertility in women.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSS">
      <data key="d0">MSS</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSS, or microsatellite stable, refers to a specific classification of rectal cancer that suggests certain characteristics regarding the tumor's genetic stability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection (ESD)">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection (ESD)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">ESD is a minimally invasive procedure for removing small cancers from the rectal wall, allowing for a potential cure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Local Excision">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Local Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Transanal local excision is a surgical technique used to remove rectal tumors located at the end of the rectum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flexible Sigmoidoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Flexible Sigmoidoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Flexible sigmoidoscopy is a medical procedure used to check for recurrence of rectal cancer after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Endoscopic Microsurgery (TEM)">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Endoscopic Microsurgery (TEM)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">TEM is an advanced technique for removing tumors higher in the rectum through the anal canal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Minimally Invasive Surgery (TAMIS)">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Minimally Invasive Surgery (TAMIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">TAMIS is another advanced surgical technique used for the removal of rectal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Rectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Rectal cancer involves tumors in the rectal area, with treatment options dependent on staging and tumor invasion.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a form of cancer affecting the rectum, requiring various treatment protocols depending on its staging and location within the rectal wall.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer refers to the malignancy that occurs in the rectum, often arising from polyps on the lining of the rectal wall and characterized by various stages of advancement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Imaging Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Medical Imaging Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical imaging techniques such as MRI and CT scans are used to diagnose and stage rectal cancer by visualizing the extent of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenomas">
      <data key="d0">Adenomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenomas are benign tumors that can develop into adenocarcinomas, a type of cancer, if left untreated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A malignant polyp is a cancerous growth in the rectum that can lead to rectal cancer if not removed.&lt;SEP&gt;A malignant polyp refers to a polyp in which cancer has initiated, leading to potential invasive growth if not treated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Resection">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Resection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical resection involves the removal of a portion of the rectum and is performed when cancer is found during examinations of polyps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic Ultrasound (EUS)">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic Ultrasound (EUS)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endoscopic ultrasound is a diagnostic technique used to evaluate the depth of rectal cancer and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic Mucosal Resection (EMR)">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic Mucosal Resection (EMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endoscopic mucosal resection is a technique for removing superficial gastrointestinal tumors, including early-stage rectal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surveillance involves monitoring patients after treatment for any signs of recurrence in cases of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Surveillance is the monitoring process that follows treatment to check for signs of cancer recurrence or progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Surveillance refers to the ongoing monitoring of patients after treatment for rectal cancer to detect any recurrence of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Transabdominal Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Transabdominal surgery is a major surgical procedure preferred for tumors that have invaded the muscle layer of the rectal wall, often referred to as T2 tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Transabdominal surgery is a method used for larger tumors that may have advanced beyond early-stage classifications.&lt;SEP&gt;Transabdominal surgery is a preferred treatment for T2 tumors that have invaded the muscle layer of the rectal wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Anterior Resection (LAR)">
      <data key="d0">Low Anterior Resection (LAR)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">LAR is a type of transabdominal surgery used for tumors in the mid to upper rectum, allowing for bowel connection after tumor removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Low Anterior Resection is a surgical method for removing rectal tumors located in the mid to upper rectum, allowing for potential bowel reconnection post-operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colostomy">
      <data key="d0">Colostomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A colostomy is a surgical procedure that creates an opening in the abdominal wall for stool to leave the body, often necessary after anal cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy is a procedure connecting a part of the colon to an opening in the abdomen for stool exit, sometimes needed temporarily or permanently.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy is a surgical procedure that connects the remaining colon to the exterior of the abdomen to divert stool after rectum removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy is a surgical procedure that creates an opening in the abdomen for waste to exit when the bowel cannot be reconnected after surgery, which can be temporary or permanent.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy is a surgical procedure that creates an opening in the abdomen to allow stool to exit the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy is a surgical procedure that creates an opening in the abdomen, allowing stool to exit the body when the anus is removed or needs diversion.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy is a surgical procedure where an opening is created in the abdomen for stool to exit when it is unsafe to reconnect the colon after colectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)">
      <data key="d0">Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">APR is a surgical procedure for lower rectal tumors, requiring permanent colostomy due to the removal of the anus and rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;APR is a surgical procedure recommended for removing cancer from the anal area if it returns, involving significant surgical intervention.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal Resection is a surgery performed on lower rectal tumors involving complete removal of the rectum and anus, resulting in a permanent colostomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a surgical procedure used to remove the anus and rectum to treat anal cancer, especially when it is metastatic.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a surgical procedure used to remove the anus and typically involves removing the rectum and surrounding tissues to treat metastatic anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection is a surgical procedure that removes the anus, rectum, and part of the sigmoid colon, typically used in anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidney cancer refers to cancers that originate in the tissues of the kidneys, primarily including renal cell carcinoma (RCC), the most common type.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidney cancer refers to malignancies affecting the kidneys, which filter blood and produce hormones.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidney cancer refers to malignancies in the kidneys that may arise from various types of kidney cells, impacting their ability to perform filtration and hormone regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T2 Tumor">
      <data key="d0">T2 Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T2 Tumor is a classification indicating that the cancer has invaded into the muscle layer of the rectal wall, pushing the need for more aggressive treatment methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Staging">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical staging involves the surgical assessment of lymph nodes and tissues to determine cancer stage and inform treatment decisions in cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical staging is the process of assessing the extent of cancer based on the analysis of removed tissues and lymph nodes during surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)">
      <data key="d0">Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) is the most common type of kidney cancer that originates in cells lining the renal tubules.&lt;SEP&gt;Renal cell carcinoma (RCC) is the most prevalent form of kidney cancer, comprising about 90% of cases, and begins in the renal tubules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidneys">
      <data key="d0">Kidneys</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The kidneys are a pair of organs that filter blood, remove waste, and produce urine, playing a critical role in the urinary system.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidneys are vital organs responsible for filtering blood, producing urine, and managing electrolyte balance in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidneys are vital organs that filter blood to produce urine and are crucial in imaging assessments for detecting cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary System">
      <data key="d0">Urinary System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The urinary system consists of organs that remove waste from the body in the form of urine, including the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines provide evidence-based recommendations aimed at patients for understanding and managing bladder cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Soft Tissue Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Soft Tissue Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines provide evidence-based treatment recommendations intended for patients dealing with soft tissue sarcoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wilms Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Wilms Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Wilms tumor is a type of kidney cancer that typically occurs in young children, characterized by cancer cells found in the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d0">Non-Clear Cell RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Clear Cell RCC (nccRCC) encompasses various subtypes of renal cell carcinoma, with Papillary RCC being the most prevalent subtype, characterized by long, thin growths.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-clear cell RCC is a category of renal cell carcinoma that encompasses several subtypes, differing from clear cell RCC in cellular characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-clear cell RCC refers to a category of renal cell carcinoma that includes several subtypes distinct from clear cell RCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Irinotecan">
      <data key="d0">Irinotecan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Irinotecan is a chemotherapy drug used for treating certain types of cancer, particularly relevant in regimens that include 'IRI' in their name and is known to cause various side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Capsule">
      <data key="d0">Renal Capsule</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The renal capsule is a thin layer of tissue that surrounds each kidney, providing protection and structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gerota’s Fascia">
      <data key="d0">Gerota’s Fascia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Gerota's fascia is an outer layer of fibrous tissue that surrounds the kidneys and adrenal glands, contributing to their structural support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urine">
      <data key="d0">Urine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urine is a liquid byproduct of metabolism and is excreted by the kidneys, and its collection methods may change after bladder surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Urine is the liquid waste produced by the urinary system, formed by the kidneys filtering blood and excreting waste and excess substances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Artery">
      <data key="d0">Renal Artery</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The renal artery carries blood from the heart to the kidneys, delivering it for filtration and waste removal.&lt;SEP&gt;The renal artery is the blood vessel that supplies blood to the kidney, playing a critical role in its function and health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Vein">
      <data key="d0">Renal Vein</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The renal vein collects clean, filtered blood from the kidneys and returns it to the circulatory system, specifically merging with the vena cava.&lt;SEP&gt;The renal vein is responsible for draining blood from the kidney and is involved in the process of renally-extracted waste removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ureters">
      <data key="d0">Ureters</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The ureters are muscular tubes that transport urine from the kidneys to the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;The ureters are tubes that carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder, and can also be examined during urological procedures to check for tumors or blockages.&lt;SEP&gt;Ureters are tubes that carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder and are part of the urinary tract system, playing a crucial role in urine transport.&lt;SEP&gt;Ureters are tubes that carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder, playing a vital role in the urinary system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder">
      <data key="d0">Bladder</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The bladder is a hollow organ in the human body that stores urine before it is excreted; it is often examined for abnormalities related to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is a muscular sac in the pelvis that stores urine before it is excreted from the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is a muscular sac that stores urine until it is expelled from the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is a muscular sac that temporarily holds urine before it is expelled from the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is a vital organ in the human body that stores urine and is involved in the development of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is an organ that holds urine and plays a critical role in the urinary system, located below the prostate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urethra">
      <data key="d0">Urethra</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The urethra is the duct through which urine is discharged from the bladder in both genders, and in males, it also carries sperm.&lt;SEP&gt;The urethra is the tube that carries urine from the bladder to outside the body, completing the urinary excretion process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinomas are cancers that originate in the cells lining the inner or outer surfaces of the body, significant in the context of kidney cancer as most kidney cancers are of this type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial carcinoma is a cancer that starts in the cells lining the renal pelvis and ureter, previously known as transitional cell cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nephroblastoma">
      <data key="d0">Nephroblastoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nephroblastoma, commonly known as Wilms tumor, is a type of kidney cancer that primarily occurs in children and can be characterized by the presence of cancer cells in the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear Cell RCC (ccRCC) is the most common subtype of renal cell carcinoma, occurring in about 70% of cases, distinguished by its appearance of clear cells under a microscope.&lt;SEP&gt;Clear cell RCC (ccRCC) is the most common subtype of renal cell carcinoma, recognized for the pale or clear appearance of cancer cells under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell RCC (ccRCC)">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell RCC (ccRCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear Cell RCC is the most prevalent subtype of RCC, occurring in approximately 70% of RCC cases, characterized by clear-looking cells under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Clear Cell RCC (nccRCC)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Clear Cell RCC (nccRCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Clear Cell RCC is a classification of RCC with several subtypes, including Papillary RCC, which represents about 10% of RCC cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary RCC (PRCC)">
      <data key="d0">Papillary RCC (PRCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary RCC is a subtype of non-clear cell RCC, characterized by tumor cells that resemble long, thin finger-like growths under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromophobe RCC">
      <data key="d0">Chromophobe RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromophobe RCC is a subtype of RCC where cells are pale and larger than those of clear cell RCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromophobe RCC is a type of renal cell carcinoma where the cancer cells are larger and pale compared to clear cell counterparts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Translocation RCC">
      <data key="d0">Translocation RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Translocation RCC is a rare type of RCC that can occur at any age, often diagnosed in younger adults and children, associated with unique gene fusions.&lt;SEP&gt;Translocation RCC is a rare variant of RCC, typically diagnosed in younger patients, and associated with unique gene fusions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Collecting Duct RCC (cdRCC)">
      <data key="d0">Collecting Duct RCC (cdRCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Collecting Duct RCC is a rare subtype of RCC forming in the cells of the collecting ducts or tubules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomatoid RCC (sRCC)">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomatoid RCC (sRCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomatoid RCC describes an RCC variant where cancer cells exhibit characteristics similar to sarcoma, leading to more aggressive behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastasis is the process by which cancer spreads from its original site to other parts of the body, which is critical in staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the process where cancer cells spread from the primary tumor to other parts of the body, forming new tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the spread of cancer cells from the original tumor site to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the process by which cancer cells spread from the original (primary) site to distant locations in the body, such as other organs or bones.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the process by which cancer cells spread from the original site (the prostate) to other parts of the body, forming new tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the process by which prostate cancer spreads to other parts of the body, complicating treatment and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the spread of cancer cells from the original tumor site to distant organs, commonly the lung and bone in liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the spread of cancer cells from the original tumor to distant organs, marking a critical aspect of breast cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the spread of cancer cells from the primary tumor to other parts of the body, complicating treatment and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the spread of cancer from its primary site to distant areas of the body, such as the liver or lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney">
      <data key="d0">Kidney</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The kidney is a vital organ responsible for filtering blood to remove waste and excess water, often impacted by different types of cancer including RCC.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidney is an essential organ involved in filtering blood, regulating body fluids, and is the site where renal cell carcinoma often originates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary RCC">
      <data key="d0">Papillary RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary RCC (PRCC) is a subtype of non-clear cell RCC found in about 10% of cases, displaying unique cellular structures under microscopy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Collecting Duct RCC">
      <data key="d0">Collecting Duct RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Collecting Duct RCC (cdRCC) is a rare subtype that develops in the collecting ducts of the kidney, often aggressive in nature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Medullary Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Renal Medullary Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal Medullary Carcinoma (RMC) is a rare and aggressive form of RCC seen primarily in young individuals of African descent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor is a specific type of RCC recently defined for its distinct histological features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC">
      <data key="d0">Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC is a subtype of RCC associated with specific genetic mutations affecting fumarate metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomatoid Renal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomatoid Renal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomatoid RCC (sRCC) refers to RCC variants that exhibit sarcomatoid features, leading to a more aggressive disease course.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Cancer Spread">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Cancer Spread</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidney cancer spread involves processes such as local invasion and metastasis, where cancer cells spread beyond the original tumor site.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic tumors are secondary tumors that form in distant organs as a result of cancer spread from the primary tumor, often complicating treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Histology">
      <data key="d0">Histology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Histology is the study of the anatomy of cells and tissues under a microscope, often used to make treatment decisions in cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Histology is the study of the microscopic structure of tissues, which plays a crucial role in assessing cancer grade and type.&lt;SEP&gt;Histology is the study of tissues and cells under a microscope, essential for diagnosing cancers and determining their characteristics and treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Histology refers to the microscopic examination of tissue changes in tumors, crucial for determining cancer type and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Size">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Size</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor size is an important factor in cancer treatment decisions, impacting prognosis and therapy approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphocyte">
      <data key="d0">Lymphocyte</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell integral to the immune system, with subtypes including B cells and T cells, which help fight infections and tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that plays a crucial role in the immune system, primarily consisting of B cells and T cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">T Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T lymphocytes are a subtype of lymphocytes involved in directly killing infected cells and regulating immune responses, also impacted by leukemias.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progenitor cells are early stem cells that differentiate into lymphocytes and other blood cells essential for immune function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL)">
      <data key="d0">B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that originates in B-cell lymphocytes and can have various manifestations, including lymphoblastic lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL)">
      <data key="d0">T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that starts in T-cell lymphocytes and can cause an enlarged thymus, leading to potential respiratory issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA)">
      <data key="d0">Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA) is defined as a person between the ages of 15 to 39 at the time of cancer diagnosis, facing unique treatment circumstances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunophenotype">
      <data key="d0">Immunophenotype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunophenotype refers to the characterization of leukemia cells based on the types of lymphocytes present and their maturity, playing a key role in diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for ALL">
      <data key="d0">Testing for ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for ALL encompasses various medical evaluations, including blood tests and genetic testing, essential for diagnosing and planning treatment for leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ayas">
      <data key="d0">Ayas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adolescents and young adults (AYAs) are a demographic specifically addressed in treatment guidelines for ALL, taking into account their unique healthcare needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthcare Team">
      <data key="d0">Healthcare Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The healthcare team refers to the group of medical professionals involved in diagnosing and treating patients with leukemia, ensuring comprehensive care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gene or Chromosome Changes">
      <data key="d0">Gene or Chromosome Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene or chromosome changes in leukemia cells are genetic alterations that can significantly impact the type of leukemia and its treatment strategy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">APL is a rare subtype of AML characterized by the presence of the abnormal fusion gene PML::RARA, affecting treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;APL is a rare subtype of AML characterized by the presence of the abnormal fusion gene PML::RARA, which influences treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Blood Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood cancer encompasses malignancies that impact the production and function of blood cells; AML is a specific subtype.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oxygen">
      <data key="d0">Oxygen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oxygen is a vital gas transported by red blood cells to tissues throughout the body, necessary for cellular respiration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing Procedures">
      <data key="d0">Testing Procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing procedures include a variety of tests required for diagnosing AML, such as blood tests, bone marrow aspirates, and imaging tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Specialist">
      <data key="d0">AML Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An AML specialist is a healthcare professional who focuses on the diagnosis and treatment of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML), providing expertise in managing patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Patient Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The patient care team comprises healthcare professionals, including doctors and specialists, who collaborate to provide comprehensive care and support for patients with AML.&lt;SEP&gt;The patient care team consists of healthcare professionals who collaborate to develop and implement treatment plans tailored to the individual needs of bladder cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The patient care team includes healthcare professionals responsible for providing support and treatment through diagnosis and therapy for breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Questions to Ask">
      <data key="d0">Questions to Ask</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Questions to Ask is a section in the NCCN Guidelines that equips patients with inquiries to facilitate discussions about their care and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Blood Count (CBC)">
      <data key="d0">Complete Blood Count (CBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A complete blood count (CBC) is a test that evaluates the number of different blood cells to monitor health and detect disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;The CBC measures red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets in the blood to evaluate general health.&lt;SEP&gt;The Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a blood test that evaluates overall health and detects a variety of disorders, including anemia and infection, by measuring different blood components.&lt;SEP&gt;The Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a common blood test that measures various components, essential in diagnosing and monitoring AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CBC Measurement">
      <data key="d0">CBC Measurement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CBC Measurement refers to a test that counts various types of cells in the blood, providing crucial insights into a patient's health, particularly in relation to blood disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hgb">
      <data key="d0">Hgb</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hgb, or Hemoglobin, is a measurement indicating the oxygen-carrying capacity of blood and is used to assess the necessity for red blood cell transfusions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLTs">
      <data key="d0">PLTs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PLTs, or Platelets, are blood cells essential for clotting to prevent bleeding during injuries or surgical procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANC">
      <data key="d0">ANC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ANC, or Absolute Neutrophil Count, refers to the number of neutrophils in the blood which help fight infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Creatinine">
      <data key="d0">Creatinine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Creatinine is a waste product filtered by the kidneys, and its levels indicate kidney function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BUN">
      <data key="d0">BUN</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BUN, or Blood Urea Nitrogen, is a waste product measured to assess kidney function and potential kidney damage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Tests">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver Function Tests (LFTs) evaluate the health and efficiency of the liver based on chemical levels in the blood.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver Function Tests measure chemicals processed by the liver to assess liver health and overall function.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver function tests assess the health of the liver by measuring enzymes and substances produced or processed by the liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS Scale">
      <data key="d0">PS Scale</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance Status (PS) Scale measures a person's general health and ability to perform daily activities, influencing treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;The PS scale evaluates the overall health and functional status of patients, guiding treatment decisions in oncology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Differential Count">
      <data key="d0">Differential Count</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Differential Count measures the number of different types of white blood cells in the blood and assesses their ratios to identify abnormalities in the blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blasts in Blood">
      <data key="d0">Blasts in Blood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blasts in blood refer to immature white blood cells that are typically found in higher numbers in the presence of conditions such as leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Absolute Neutrophil Count">
      <data key="d0">Absolute Neutrophil Count</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Absolute Neutrophil Count measures the number of neutrophils, crucial for fighting infections and protecting against bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Creatinine Levels">
      <data key="d0">Creatinine Levels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Creatinine levels indicate kidney function and are crucial for assessing renal health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Urea Nitrogen">
      <data key="d0">Blood Urea Nitrogen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) levels serve as an indicator of kidney function, with elevated levels pointing to potential malfunction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitamin B12 Levels">
      <data key="d0">Vitamin B12 Levels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitamin B12 levels are assessed to ensure adequate production of red blood cells and proper neurological function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Folic Acid Levels">
      <data key="d0">Folic Acid Levels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Folic Acid levels are critical for DNA synthesis and red blood cell production, monitored in blood tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron Levels">
      <data key="d0">Iron Levels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron levels are crucial for hemoglobin production; deficiencies or excesses can lead to health complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Leukocyte Antigen">
      <data key="d0">Human Leukocyte Antigen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) typing assesses protein markers on cells, important for organ transplantation compatibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bone marrow tests">
      <data key="d0">bone marrow tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow tests, which include bone marrow aspirate and biopsy, are medical procedures used for diagnosing conditions like Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="pathologist">
      <data key="d0">pathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pathologist is a medical doctor who examines tissues and cells to diagnose diseases, particularly cancer, through various analytical methods.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical professional who examines tissue samples and provides diagnostic insights, particularly crucial in confirming cases of AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is responsible for analyzing tissue samples to diagnose cancer, stage it, and provide biomarker testing information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flow Cytoetry">
      <data key="d0">Flow Cytoetry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Flow Cytometry is a laboratory technique used to analyze physical and chemical characteristics of cells, essential in the diagnosis and classification of blood cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML genetic testing">
      <data key="d0">AML genetic testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML genetic testing involves analyzing leukemia cells to identify genetic mutations and abnormalities that influence treatment choices and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="leukemia predisposition syndromes">
      <data key="d0">leukemia predisposition syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia predisposition syndromes are hereditary conditions that increase the risk of developing leukemia, warranting special consideration in treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutations are changes in the genetic code of cells that can contribute to the development of cancer, including specific types seen in AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deletion">
      <data key="d0">Deletion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deletion refers to the loss of a portion of a chromosome, which can lead to genetic abnormalities in leukemia cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insertion">
      <data key="d0">Insertion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Insertion is a genetic alteration where additional DNA is added into a chromosome, possibly contributing to cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inversion">
      <data key="d0">Inversion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inversion is a type of genetic mutation where a segment of DNA is reversed within a chromosome, affecting gene function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amplification">
      <data key="d0">Amplification</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Amplification involves an increase in the number of copies of a gene, often associated with aggressive cancer behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Translocation">
      <data key="d0">Translocation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A translocation is a genetic alteration where segments of chromosomes break and reattach to different chromosomes, often leading to cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Translocation is a genetic phenomenon where parts of two chromosomes swap places, often implicated in leukemogenesis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RUNX1::RUNX1T Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes">
      <data key="d0">RUNX1::RUNX1T Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RUNX1::RUNX1T Leukemia predisposition syndromes are hereditary cancer syndromes that may be passed from parent to child and influence the likelihood of developing leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;RUNX1::RUNX1T leukemia predisposition syndromes are hereditary cancer syndromes that can be inherited from biological parents to children, increasing the likelihood of developing leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood and Saliva Tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood and Saliva Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood and saliva tests are used to evaluate the remission status of leukemia cells and genetic predispositions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Saliva Tests">
      <data key="d0">Saliva Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Saliva tests can be used to assess genetic changes and health conditions, including those related to leukemia and its predisposition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Machine">
      <data key="d0">MRI Machine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI machine uses powerful magnets to capture images of the inside of the body, avoiding radiation exposure associated with X-rays.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrocardiogram">
      <data key="d0">Electrocardiogram</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG) detects electrical changes in the heart, providing insights into heart rate and rhythm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Specialist (Cardiologist)">
      <data key="d0">Heart Specialist (Cardiologist)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A heart specialist, known as a cardiologist, focuses on diagnosing and treating conditions related to the heart and cardiovascular system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prolonged QT Interval (QTc)">
      <data key="d0">Prolonged QT Interval (QTc)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prolonged QT interval (QTc) is a condition where the heart takes longer than normal to recharge between beats, which can lead to dangerous rhythms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)">
      <data key="d0">Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) is a radiotracer used in PET scans to identify cancerous tissues by highlighting their sugar metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Phases">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Phases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment for AML is generally divided into phases with the primary goal of achieving remission through various therapeutic approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antimetabolites">
      <data key="d0">Antimetabolites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Antimetabolites are chemotherapy drugs that interfere with DNA building blocks, used in various regimens for treating AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a type of blood cancer caused by a specific abnormal gene, resulting in overproduction of certain white blood cells due to the fusion of chromosome segments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL1 is a specific abnormal gene created from a rearrangement of chromosomes 9 and 22, associated with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).&lt;SEP&gt;The BCR::ABL1 gene is a fused gene resulting from the translocation of chromosomes 9 and 22, critical in the development of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin">
      <data key="d0">Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin (GO) is a targeted therapy that releases chemotherapy inside cells by binding to the CD33 protein found on leukemic blasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intrathecal Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intrathecal Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrathecal chemotherapy is a method of delivering chemotherapy directly into the spinal fluid, often used for cancers affecting the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Azacitidine (Vidaza)">
      <data key="d0">Azacitidine (Vidaza)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Azacitidine, known commercially as Vidaza, is a hypomethylating agent used in cancer treatment to reverse silencing of genes in leukemic cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Decitabine (Dacogen)">
      <data key="d0">Decitabine (Dacogen)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Decitabine, marketed as Dacogen, is another hypomethylating agent used in treating certain types of leukemia, including CML and AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin (GO)">
      <data key="d0">Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin (GO)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Gemtuzumab ozogamicin (GO) is a targeted therapy linked with a chemotherapy agent, impacting leukemia cells by attaching to specific proteins on their surface.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CD33">
      <data key="d0">CD33</data>
      <data key="d1">protein</data>
      <data key="d2">CD33 is a cell surface protein commonly found on leukemic blasts but not on mature blood cells, making it a target for specific therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLT3 Mutations">
      <data key="d0">FLT3 Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">FLT3 mutations in leukemia can significantly affect treatment approaches and prognosis, particularly in acute myeloid leukemia (AML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blast Phase CML">
      <data key="d0">Blast Phase CML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blast phase CML is an advanced form of chronic myeloid leukemia characterized by a high number of lymphoid or myeloid progenitor cells, affecting treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome 22">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome 22</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosome 22 is involved in the formation of the Philadelphia chromosome, specifically in the translocation event leading to BCR::ABL1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Care Centers">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Care Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer care centers are specialized medical facilities that provide treatment and support for patients with cancers like CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for CML">
      <data key="d0">Testing for CML</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for CML refers to the various medical tests performed to diagnose and monitor chronic myeloid leukemia and its biomarkers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Advocacy">
      <data key="d0">Patient Advocacy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient advocacy involves the role of patients in their care, ensuring they receive appropriate treatment and support through active participation in medical decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burkitt leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Burkitt leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Burkitt leukemia is a rare and aggressive form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) that primarily affects B-cell lymphoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric refers to the branch of medicine that deals with children and the treatment of childhood-related diseases, such as acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AYAs">
      <data key="d0">AYAs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AYAs, or adolescents and young adults, are individuals aged 15 to 39 at the time of cancer diagnosis who may receive tailored treatment options based on their specific needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that primarily affects B-cell lymphoblasts and can occur when these cells grow mostly in the lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)®">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)®</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN is a non-profit organization aimed at improving care for cancer patients through integrated guidelines and resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell ALL">
      <data key="d0">B-cell ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that specifically affects B-lymphocytes, leading to the development of cancerous cells in the blood and bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic and Biomarker Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic and Biomarker Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic and biomarker mutation testing involves evaluating a patient's genetic material to identify mutations that may affect cancer treatment options and outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic and biomarker mutation testing is crucial in diagnosing and tailoring treatments for various types of cancers, including ALL and pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) Testing">
      <data key="d0">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for ALL includes various procedures and tests to diagnose and evaluate the progression of the disease and the effectiveness of treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steroid and Multiagent Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Steroid and Multiagent Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Steroid and multiagent chemotherapy are standard treatment approaches for ALL, designed to reduce cancer cell proliferation and support remission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pancreatic cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the pancreas, often linked to various lifestyle and genetic risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the pancreas, with stages ranging from 0 to 4, and involves various risk factors and hereditary conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the tissues of the pancreas, often diagnosed through a series of imaging and genetic tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic cancer is one of the most common and challenging cancers to treat, originating in the pancreas and affecting digestion and hormone production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 2: Testing for ALL">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 2: Testing for ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 2 of the book discusses the testing procedures and assessments involved in diagnosing Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL), focusing on fertility and genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 3: Types of Treatments">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 3: Types of Treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 3 provides an overview of various treatment options for ALL patients, detailing standard therapies and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 4: Supportive Care">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 4: Supportive Care</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 4 addresses supportive care for ALL patients, including managing side effects and enhancing quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 5: BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 5: BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 5 focuses on treatment approaches for specific subtypes of B-ALL, particularly those negative for the BCR::ABL1 gene mutation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 6: BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 6: BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 6 discusses the treatment strategies for BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL, aimed at inhibiting the BCR::ABL protein.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 7: T-ALL">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 7: T-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 7 explores T-ALL, outlining the treatment options available for this less common form of ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 8: Infant ALL Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 8: Infant ALL Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 8 highlights the unique treatment considerations for infants diagnosed with ALL, under 12 months of age.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 9: Other Resources">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 9: Other Resources</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 9 provides information on additional resources including patient advocacy groups and support systems for families affected by ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HBOC Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">HBOC Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary breast-ovarian cancer syndrome (HBOC) is a genetic condition that increases the risk of developing pancreatic cancer and other types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peutz-Jeghers Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Peutz-Jeghers Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is a genetic disorder that can lead to an increased risk of pancreatic cancer alongside other tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is a hereditary condition that raises the risk of several cancers, including pancreatic cancer, characterized by the presence of polyps in the gastrointestinal tract.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Melanoma Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Familial Melanoma Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Familial melanoma syndrome, also known as melanoma-pancreatic cancer syndrome, increases the risk of melanoma and pancreatic cancer in affected families.&lt;SEP&gt;Familial melanoma syndrome, also referred to as melanoma-pancreatic cancer syndrome, increases susceptibility to melanoma and pancreatic cancer in families.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Tests">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic tests are examinations used to evaluate the risk factors associated with developing prostate cancer based on a person's inherited genetic makeup.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic tests are medical tests that identify hereditary mutations linked to conditions that increase the risk of pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic tests are procedures that analyze DNA to identify mutations or predispositions to certain diseases, including cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic tests are used to identify inherited mutations associated with various cancer risks, playing a critical role in treatment planning for certain patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic tests are utilized to identify mutations linked to hereditary conditions that elevate the risk of pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Duct Wall">
      <data key="d0">Duct Wall</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The duct wall is the innermost layer of the pancreas involved in the development and staging of pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Drug Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Drug Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Drug treatment refers to the use of medications aimed at controlling the growth of pancreatic cancer and alleviating symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Drug treatment refers to the use of medications aimed at managing pancreatic cancer's growth and symptoms, especially when surgery is not viable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lifestyle Factors">
      <data key="d0">Lifestyle Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lifestyle factors increasing the risk of pancreatic cancer include smoking tobacco, heavy alcohol use, and unhealthy diets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Breast-Ovarian Cancer Syndrome (HBOC)">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Breast-Ovarian Cancer Syndrome (HBOC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HBOC is a genetic condition that increases the likelihood of developing both breast and ovarian cancers and is linked to a higher risk of pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stages of Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stages of Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The stages of pancreatic cancer range from 0 to 4, determined by the size of the tumor and the extent of cancer spread in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 indicates pancreatic cancer that is contained only in the innermost layer of the duct wall and has not spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1 Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1 Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1 pancreatic cancer indicates the presence of a tumor that is up to 4 centimeters in size and has not spread outside the pancreas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2 Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2 Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 indicates a larger tumor size (greater than 4 cm) or spread to 1-3 nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3 Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3 Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3 pancreatic cancer means that the cancer has spread to nearby major arteries or at least 4 lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4 Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4 Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 pancreatic cancer refers to metastatic cancer that has spread to distant organs such as the liver or lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate Cancer is a common cancer in men, requiring genetic and molecular testing for risk assessment and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate Cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the prostate, a small walnut-shaped gland that produces seminal fluid.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a common cancer in American males, characterized by uncontrolled growth of cells in the prostate gland.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a common disease affecting the prostate gland, characterized by uncontrolled growth of prostate cells, with various symptoms and risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of cells in the prostate gland, often requiring various tests and treatments for detection and management.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a disease in which cancer cells form in the prostate, often treated with ADT and various hormonal therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a disease where malignant cells form in the prostate gland, detected through various testing methods including PSA levels and digital exams.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a form of cancer that originates in prostate cells, with various risk factors associated with its development, including age, family history, and genetic factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a malignancy that develops from prostate cells, leading to uncontrolled growth and potentially spreading beyond the prostate.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the prostate gland, and is commonly treated with hormone therapy and ADT.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the prostate gland, characterized by the uncontrolled growth of cells and categorized into early-stage and advanced-stage forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the prostate gland, characterized by uncontrolled growth of cells and can be categorized into localized and metastatic forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the prostate gland and may require imaging tests for diagnosis and monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the prostate gland of men and is characterized by the overproduction of prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA).&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the prostate gland, which is affected by hormonal changes and requires detailed staging for treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the prostate, a small walnut-sized gland that produces seminal fluid.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the prostate, affecting primarily males, and can be aggressive or localized based on its stage of progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Providers">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Health care providers are professionals responsible for delivering medical services, including diagnosis, treatment planning, and patient support.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers are professionals responsible for diagnosing and treating patients, particularly in complex conditions like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers are professionals who assist in the diagnosis, treatment, and management of health conditions, including screening and discussions around prostate cancer for individuals at higher risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers are professionals who offer medical advice and services regarding prostate cancer screening and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers include doctors, nurses, and specialists involved in the diagnosis and treatment of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers refer to professionals specializing in diagnosing and treating health conditions, particularly those related to breast health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A multidisciplinary care team comprises various specialists and providers working together to ensure comprehensive treatment for patients, particularly in complex medical cases like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A multidisciplinary care team includes various specialists working together to diagnose and treat complex cancers, including pancreatic and prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A multidisciplinary care team is a group of healthcare professionals from various specialties working in coordination to provide comprehensive care for patients with complex medical conditions, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The multidisciplinary care team consists of various health care providers who collaborate to treat and manage bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The multidisciplinary care team consists of various healthcare providers, including doctors and specialists, who collaborate to manage a patient's treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms may include blood in urine, urinary pain, weight loss, and erectile dysfunction, signaling possible prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of prostate cancer can include blood in urine, painful urination, and unexplained weight loss, often occurring in advanced stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palliative Care">
      <data key="d0">Palliative Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Palliative care focuses on improving the quality of life for patients with serious illnesses by providing relief from symptoms, pain, and stress.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic radiologist interprets imaging tests to identify the presence of diseases, such as cancer, in patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic radiologist interprets medical images such as X-rays and CT scans to diagnose conditions, including cancers in patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic radiologist is a medical doctor who specializes in interpreting medical images, crucial for diagnosing conditions like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gastroenterologist">
      <data key="d0">Gastroenterologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A gastroenterologist is a physician who specializes in the diagnosis and treatment of digestive system disorders, including pancreatic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Gastroenterologists are specialized doctors who may evaluate and treat anal cancer symptoms and conduct relevant examinations.&lt;SEP&gt;Gastroenterologists specialize in diagnosing and treating gastrointestinal disorders, and may conduct procedures relevant to anal cancer assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical oncologist is a doctor who performs operations to remove tumors and cancerous tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geriatrician">
      <data key="d0">Geriatrician</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A geriatrician is a physician who focuses on the health care of elderly patients, often managing complex cases like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mental Health Provider">
      <data key="d0">Mental Health Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A mental health provider offers psychological support to cancer patients, helping them cope with emotional challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate">
      <data key="d0">Prostate</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The prostate is a gland in the male reproductive system that produces seminal fluid and is the area affected by prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a gland in the male reproductive system that surrounds the urethra and is involved in the production of seminal fluid.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a gland in the male reproductive system, essential for producing seminal fluid and play a role in sexual reproduction.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a gland situated deep within the lower body in the male reproductive system, responsible for producing seminal fluid and proteins essential for reproductive functions.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a gland situated in the male reproductive system that plays a crucial role in sexual reproduction and the production of seminal fluid.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a small gland in men that produces seminal fluid and is commonly affected by cancer, particularly prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Black Males">
      <data key="d0">Black Males</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Black males are a demographic known to have a significantly higher risk of developing prostate cancer compared to other ethnicities, often diagnosed at a younger age.&lt;SEP&gt;Black males are a demographic more likely to develop prostate cancer compared to other ethnic groups, facing higher risks and more aggressive forms of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Black males have a higher risk of developing prostate cancer at an earlier age and face more aggressive forms of the disease compared to other demographics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Males">
      <data key="d0">White Males</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">White males are a demographic in the United States that experience prostate cancer at a lower frequency compared to Black males.&lt;SEP&gt;White males have a comparatively lower incidence of prostate cancer than Black males, indicating variations in cancer risk among ethnic groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage prostate cancer is characterized by cancer that has not spread beyond the prostate and generally grows slowly.&lt;SEP&gt;Early-stage prostate cancer refers to cancer that has not spread beyond the prostate gland and is typically slow-growing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced-stage prostate cancer describes cancer that has spread beyond the prostate gland, potentially affecting lymph nodes and other organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced-stage prostate cancer is characterized by cancer cells that have spread outside the prostate to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced-stage prostate cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond the prostate gland, leading to more complex treatment scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced-stage prostate cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond the prostate to other areas of the body, being more challenging to treat.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced-stage prostate cancer refers to the progression of prostate cancer that has spread beyond the prostate gland, often posing significant treatment challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Factors">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic factors include specific hereditary changes that may increase the risk of developing prostate cancer, such as mutations in the BRCA2 gene.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic factors involve hereditary characteristics that may increase susceptibility to prostate cancer, including specific gene mutations such as those in the BRCA2 gene.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diet and Lifestyle">
      <data key="d0">Diet and Lifestyle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diet and lifestyle are influential factors in the risk assessment of prostate cancer, with high-fat diets linked to increased risk and healthy practices possibly reducing it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Localized Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Localized Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Localized prostate cancer is a classification of early-stage prostate cancer that remains confined within the prostate and has not spread to nearby tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Localized prostate cancer is a type of early-stage cancer that remains confined within the prostate gland.&lt;SEP&gt;Localized prostate cancer refers to early-stage prostate cancer that remains confined within the prostate gland and has not spread to other areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Localized prostate cancer refers to the early-stage form of prostate cancer that remains confined within the prostate gland and has not spread to other areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diet">
      <data key="d0">Diet</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diet refers to the types of food consumed, where high-fat diets have been linked to an increased risk of prostate cancer, while a diet rich in fruits and vegetables may lower risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exercise and Weight Loss">
      <data key="d0">Exercise and Weight Loss</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Exercise and weight loss contribute positively to health, potentially decreasing the aggressiveness of prostate cancer and the likelihood of mortality associated with it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ejaculation">
      <data key="d0">Ejaculation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ejaculation is the biological process through which semen is expelled from the male reproductive system, involving muscular contractions facilitated by the prostate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic prostate cancer is an advanced stage where the cancer has spread beyond the prostate to other parts of the body, such as the lymph nodes, bones, liver, or lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic prostate cancer is an advanced stage where the cancer has spread outside the prostate to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes, bones, liver, or lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic prostate cancer is when cancer has spread to distant parts of the body, such as bones and organs, often increasing treatment complexity.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic prostate cancer refers to advanced prostate cancer that has spread beyond the prostate gland to other parts of the body, requiring special treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Active Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Active Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Active Surveillance is a watchful waiting strategy for certain cancers, where treatment is delayed until necessary.&lt;SEP&gt;Active surveillance is a management strategy for early-stage prostate cancer that involves regular monitoring and testing without immediate treatment unless changes necessitate it.&lt;SEP&gt;Active surveillance is a management strategy for low-risk thyroid cancers that involves closely monitoring the condition instead of immediate surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone therapy for prostate cancer includes various methods to lower testosterone, including LHRH agonists, antagonists, and anti-androgens.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy for prostate cancer refers to treatments designed to lower testosterone levels to slow tumor growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy is a method used to slow the growth of prostate cancer by reducing testosterone levels or blocking its effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy is a treatment that reduces or blocks the production of male hormones to slow the growth of prostate cancer cells, commonly used in advanced stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy is a treatment that reduces or blocks the production of male hormones to slow the growth of prostate cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA) Test">
      <data key="d0">Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA) Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The PSA test is a blood test that measures the level of prostate-specific antigen in the bloodstream, often used as a screening tool for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Exam">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Digital Rectal Exam is a medical procedure performed to examine the anal region for abnormalities or signs of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A digital rectal exam is a physical examination used to check for abnormalities in the prostate because of potential prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A digital rectal exam is a simple procedure used to assess prostate health by examining size and texture.&lt;SEP&gt;Digital rectal exam is a physical examination technique used by healthcare providers to check for abnormalities in the prostate gland, used in conjunction with other tests for prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital rectal exam (DRE) is a common procedure used to detect abnormalities in the anal canal, crucial for diagnosing anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital rectal exam is a clinical examination where a doctor checks the prostate's size and texture to identify potential tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital rectal exam is a physical examination method used to check for abnormalities in the prostate gland, typically paired with other diagnostic methods.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital rectal exam is a physical examination used to evaluate the prostate gland and check for abnormalities that may signify cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital rectal exam is a procedure used by doctors to check the prostate for abnormalities or tumors through a physical examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regular Tests">
      <data key="d0">Regular Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regular tests refer to periodic medical evaluations performed on patients to monitor the progression of prostate cancer and the effectiveness of treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer includes multiple modalities such as hormone therapy, chemotherapy, and radiation to manage symptoms and control growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bones">
      <data key="d0">Bones</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Bones can be involved in the metastasis of prostate cancer, where cancer cells spread and inhabit the skeletal system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Testing">
      <data key="d0">PSA Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA testing is a medical procedure used to measure the level of prostate-specific antigen in the blood, which may indicate prostate issues including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic testing refers to procedures, including biopsies and imaging, utilized to confirm the presence of prostate cancer after initial tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA">
      <data key="d0">PSA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA, or Prostate-Specific Antigen, is a protein produced by the prostate gland, used as a marker to detect potential prostate issues, including cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Gland">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The prostate gland is a small gland located below the bladder in men, playing a crucial role in the male reproductive system and producing PSA.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate gland is an important component of the male reproductive system located beneath the bladder and above the urethra.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Test">
      <data key="d0">Blood Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Blood Test is a common medical examination that checks for various health markers, crucial for patients undergoing tests related to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood test involves taking a sample of blood from a patient to measure various components, including PSA levels, to assess health.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood test is a common procedure used to detect and diagnose conditions like CLL, involving the analysis of a blood sample for abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are analytical procedures that may be used to detect cancer markers or genetic information from a blood sample.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4Kscore">
      <data key="d0">4Kscore</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">4Kscore is a blood test that measures four different prostate-specific markers to help assess the risk of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHI">
      <data key="d0">PHI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The PHI test is a diagnostic tool that combines several measurements to determine the risk of prostate cancer more accurately than PSA alone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IsoPSA">
      <data key="d0">IsoPSA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IsoPSA is a more advanced blood test for prostate cancer that offers a better distinction between benign and malignant conditions than traditional PSA tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ExoDx">
      <data key="d0">ExoDx</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ExoDx is a urine test designed to detect prostate cancer biomarkers, aiding in the initial assessment and management of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="miR Sentinel">
      <data key="d0">miR Sentinel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">miR Sentinel is a urine-based assay that tests for prostate cancer-related biomarkers to help in diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCA3">
      <data key="d0">PCA3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PCA3 is a gene that is expressed specifically in prostate cancer cells, and its urinary detection can help determine the need for biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SelectMDx">
      <data key="d0">SelectMDx</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SelectMDx is a urine test that assesses biomarkers to predict the presence of clinically significant prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA/PET Imaging">
      <data key="d0">PSMA/PET Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">PSMA/PET imaging is a specialized procedure targeting prostate-specific membrane antigen to detect prostate cancer spread and monitor treatment efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Scan">
      <data key="d0">Bone Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Bone Scan is a diagnostic imaging test that utilizes a radiotracer to visualize areas of bone damage and assess bone health.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone scan is a diagnostic imaging test used to evaluate the bones for evidence of cancer metastasis or other conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone scan is a diagnostic test used to determine whether cancer has metastasized to the bones, involving a radioactive tracer for imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone scan is a nuclear imaging technique used to evaluate if cancer has spread to the bones.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone scan is an imaging technique used to detect whether prostate cancer has metastasized to the bones, utilizing radioactive tracers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A prostate biopsy is a medical procedure in which samples from the prostate are taken to determine if cancer is present.&lt;SEP&gt;A prostate biopsy is a medical procedure used to extract tissue samples from the prostate for testing to identify cancer or other conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urologist">
      <data key="d0">Urologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A urologist is a medical doctor specialized in diagnosing and treating diseases of the urinary system and the male reproductive organs.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologist is a medical doctor specializing in the diagnosis and treatment of urinary tract and male reproductive system disorders, including bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologist is a medical doctor specializing in the diagnosis and treatment of urinary tract conditions and male reproductive organs, playing a critical role in bladder cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologist is a specialized doctor who treats urinary system diseases and male reproductive organ conditions, including performing prostate biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologist specializes in the urinary system and male reproductive organs, playing a crucial role in diagnosing and treating bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA1, BRCA2, ATM, CHEK2">
      <data key="d0">BRCA1, BRCA2, ATM, CHEK2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA1, BRCA2, ATM, CHEK2 are genes that, when mutated, can increase the risk of certain cancers, including prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic lymph nodes are structures in the lower abdomen that filter lymph fluid and are crucial in assessing the spread of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Level">
      <data key="d0">PSA Level</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA (Prostate-Specific Antigen) Level is a blood measurement used to screen for and monitor prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;PSA level refers to the amount of prostate-specific antigen in the bloodstream, which can indicate a risk for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transperineal Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Transperineal Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A transperineal biopsy is a specialized type of biopsy where a needle is inserted through the perineum to obtain prostate tissue samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Sample">
      <data key="d0">Core Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core sample is a small cylinder of tissue removed during a biopsy for examination, used to assess the presence and extent of prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A core sample is a small cylindrical tissue sample taken from the prostate during a biopsy for cancer testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Tests">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive tests are medical procedures that involve entering the body to obtain information, often related to cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Infection">
      <data key="d0">Infection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Infection is a potential complication from invasive medical procedures, including biopsies for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bleeding">
      <data key="d0">Bleeding</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bleeding is a possible complication following biopsy procedures, particularly in sensitive areas like the prostate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family History of Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Family History of Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family history of cancer refers to the prevalence of cancer within a family, influencing genetic testing and risk assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A mutation is a change in a gene that may lead to diseases, including an increased risk of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A mutation is an abnormal change in DNA that can disrupt cell behavior and lead to diseases like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inherited Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Inherited Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inherited mutations are genetic alterations passed down from parents that may increase the risk of certain cancers, including prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATM">
      <data key="d0">ATM</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">ATM is a gene involved in DNA repair, and mutations can lead to an increased risk for various cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;ATM is a gene that plays a role in cellular response to DNA damage and is involved in cancer risk assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEK2">
      <data key="d0">CHEK2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">CHEK2 is a gene that, if mutated, increases the risk of certain cancers, including breast and prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;CHEK2 is a gene that, when mutated, can increase the risk for various cancers including breast and prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MLH1">
      <data key="d0">MLH1</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">MLH1 is a gene related to DNA mismatch repair, mutations of which are associated with several cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSH2">
      <data key="d0">MSH2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">MSH2 is involved in DNA repair and is associated with increased risks for colorectal and uterine cancers when mutated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSH6">
      <data key="d0">MSH6</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">MSH6 is a gene linked to DNA repair that can contribute to cancer susceptibility when mutated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PALB2">
      <data key="d0">PALB2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">PALB2 is a gene that helps in repairing DNA damage, and mutations can lead to increased cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PMS2">
      <data key="d0">PMS2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">PMS2 is a gene associated with DNA repair, and its mutations can increase cancer risk, particularly colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic counseling involves assessing the risk of inherited cancers and providing guidance on genetic testing and implications for family health.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic counseling is a process to evaluate family history and genetic factors influencing the risk of cancer, providing guidance for testing and risk management.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic counseling is a service provided by trained experts helping individuals understand genetic risks and testing options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Biomarker Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">service</data>
      <data key="d2">Molecular biomarker testing analyzes biopsy samples to assess cancer risks and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germline Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Germline Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Germline mutations are inherited genetic changes passed from parents to offspring, which may predispose individuals to certain cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Germline mutations are inherited genetic changes that occur in every cell in the body, impacting health and disease risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Germline Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Germline Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic germline testing is a medical procedure that analyzes genetic material from blood, urine, or saliva to identify mutations that may increase cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Cancer Risk Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Cancer Risk Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate cancer risk assessment involves evaluating genetic risks and family history to determine the likelihood of developing prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Higher Risk Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Higher Risk Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Higher risk prostate cancer refers to cases where the disease has a greater likelihood of progression and may require aggressive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ashkenazi Jewish Ancestry">
      <data key="d0">Ashkenazi Jewish Ancestry</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry is a specific ethnic background associated with higher rates of certain genetic mutations, including BRCA1 and BRCA2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Planning">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Planning</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment planning incorporates various diagnostic outcomes to decide the best approach for managing anal squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment planning involves devising a strategy for managing cancer based on patient-specific factors, including test results and risk assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Test">
      <data key="d0">PSA Test</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">A PSA test measures prostate-specific antigen levels in the blood to screen for prostate cancer or monitor its progression.&lt;SEP&gt;The PSA test measures the level of prostate-specific antigen in the blood, serving as a common method for detecting prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The PSA test measures the prostate-specific antigen levels in the blood to help identify potential cases of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gleason Score">
      <data key="d0">Gleason Score</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gleason score is a grading system used to evaluate the aggressiveness of prostate cancer based on the microscopic appearance of cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The Gleason Score is a grading system for prostate cancer based on the patterns observed in biopsy samples, ranging from 6 to 10, determining the cancer's aggressiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;The Gleason score is a grading system that classifies prostate cancer based on the appearance of cancer cells, helping predict the aggressiveness of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group categorizes Gleason scores into five levels to assess the aggressiveness of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Group">
      <data key="d0">Risk Group</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk group categorizes patients based on the prognosis and likely outcome of their prostate cancer, influencing treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Density">
      <data key="d0">PSA Density</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA density is a calculation used to evaluate prostate cancer risk by comparing the PSA level to the size of the prostate.&lt;SEP&gt;PSA density is a measurement used to assess the risk of prostate cancer based on levels of prostate-specific antigen in the blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy Results">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsy results provide essential information about the presence and characteristics of cancer cells in prostate tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Stage">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor stage describes the extent of prostate cancer in the body based on the size and spread of the tumor, which impacts treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Age Factors">
      <data key="d0">Age Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Age factors refer to how PSA levels change with age, influencing the likelihood of prostate cancer risks in different age groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Race Factors">
      <data key="d0">Race Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Race factors account for variations in normal PSA levels between different racial groups, affecting cancer risk assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Issues">
      <data key="d0">Health Issues</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health issues related to the prostate may influence PSA levels and could complicate cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group System">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Grade Group system categorizes prostate cancer based on Gleason scores, indicating the aggressiveness of the cancer from low to high risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Risk Factors">
      <data key="d0">Familial Risk Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Familial risk factors refer to the hereditary patterns that increase the likelihood of developing prostate cancer among individuals with a family history of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 1">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 1 represents a Gleason score of 6, indicating low risk for cancer aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 2">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 2 is associated with a Gleason score of 7, indicating intermediate risk for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 3">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 3 corresponds to a Gleason score of 7-8, indicating more serious cancer compared to Grade Group 2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 4">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 4</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 4 is linked with a Gleason score of 8-9, predicting high risk cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 5">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 5</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 5 represents a Gleason score of 9-10, indicating very high risk for cancer aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BPH">
      <data key="d0">BPH</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH) is a common condition in older men that presents many of the same symptoms as prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Tract">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Tract</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The urinary tract includes the prostate, bladder, and urethra, essential for urine excretion and associated with prostate cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testosterone">
      <data key="d0">Testosterone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testosterone influences the growth and function of prostate tissues, playing a role in the development of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seminal Vesicles">
      <data key="d0">Seminal Vesicles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The seminal vesicles are two glands located above the prostate that produce a significant component of semen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA2 Gene">
      <data key="d0">BRCA2 Gene</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The BRCA2 gene is a gene linked to increased risk of prostate and other cancers when genetic mutations occur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Race">
      <data key="d0">Race</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Race refers to the categorization of individuals that impacts the likelihood of developing prostate cancer, indicating disparities in cancer incidence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early Detection Screening">
      <data key="d0">Early Detection Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early detection screening refers to medical tests aimed at identifying prostate cancer at its initial stages to improve treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Abnormalities">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Abnormalities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic abnormalities are mutations or changes in DNA that can lead to an increased risk of developing prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Obesity">
      <data key="d0">Obesity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Obesity is a risk factor associated with an increased likelihood of developing and dying from prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Options">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer treatment options include various therapies such as chemotherapy, radiation, immunotherapy, and surgical interventions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment options include various therapies such as hormone therapy, chemotherapy, and radiation, aimed at managing prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Local Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Localized prostate cancer refers to cancer that has not spread beyond the prostate gland and can often be treated effectively in its early stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regional Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Regional Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regional prostate cancer is a form of prostate cancer that has spread to nearby areas but not to distant organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Centers">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer centers are specialized healthcare institutions dedicated to the research, diagnosis, and treatment of cancer, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer centers are specialized medical institutions focusing on the research, diagnosis, and treatment of various cancers, including prostate cancer, and implementing best practices for patient care.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer centers provide specialized care and treatment for patients diagnosed with cancer, including access to social workers and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Detection Methods">
      <data key="d0">Detection Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Detection methods for prostate cancer are advanced techniques that allow for early identification and monitoring of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Choices">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Choices</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment choices for prostate cancer patients have increased significantly, offering various therapies to combat the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Best Practices">
      <data key="d0">Best Practices</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Best practices in treating prostate cancer summarize the latest research and methodologies employed by top cancer centers to improve patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Team Approach">
      <data key="d0">Team Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The team approach refers to the collaborative effort by health care professionals to design and implement an individualized treatment plan for prostate cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Tests">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic tests are medical procedures performed to detect the presence of prostate cancer and assess its severity.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic tests are medical procedures used to confirm the presence of illness, assess its stage or localization, and plan treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic tests are used to determine the presence, type, and appropriate treatment of gliomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic tests, including imaging and biopsies, are essential for identifying the presence and type of glioma and planning treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancers are a group of diseases characterized by uncontrolled cell growth, which can run in families, affecting the health history of relatives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Disease">
      <data key="d0">Heart Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Heart disease encompasses various heart conditions that can also run in families and affect overall health history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stroke">
      <data key="d0">Stroke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stroke refers to a medical condition that can have a genetic component, impacting family health histories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diabetes">
      <data key="d0">Diabetes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diabetes is a chronic disease that may have hereditary links, influencing family health histories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">Diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnosis is the process of identifying an illness based on medical tests and evaluations.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnosis refers to the process of identifying a disease or condition, such as primary CNS lymphoma, based on symptoms and tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnosis refers to the process of identifying a disease or condition, which is crucial in determining appropriate treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prognosis">
      <data key="d0">Prognosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prognosis involves predicting the likely outcome or course of a disease based on diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Enlarged Glands">
      <data key="d0">Enlarged Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Enlarged glands, or lymph nodes, are often signs of certain health conditions, including cancers, and can be felt in areas such as the groin, underarms, and neck.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Relatives">
      <data key="d0">Blood Relatives</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood relatives are family members related by genetics, important for gathering health history, especially concerning hereditary diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood relatives are family members who share genetic material and may also carry inherited mutations that affect cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quality of Life">
      <data key="d0">Quality of Life</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Quality of life encompasses a person's overall well-being and ability to engage in regular activities, significantly impacted by health conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Quality of life refers to the overall well-being of patients, including physical, emotional, and social health, especially important during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsies">
      <data key="d0">Biopsies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsies are medical procedures involving the extraction of tissue samples for examination to confirm cancer presence or characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsies are medical tests involving the removal of tissue for examination to diagnose cancer and other diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Primary Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary tumor is defined as the original cancerous growth that can determine the treatment and prognosis for the patient.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary tumor is the initial mass of cancer cells that forms at the site of origin, in this case, the breast tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary Tumor refers to the original tumor site from which cancer may spread, and its size and growth pattern are significant in staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiologist's Results">
      <data key="d0">Radiologist's Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiologist's results are the findings reported by a radiologist after analyzing imaging tests, crucial for the patient’s diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurrence indicates a return of cancer after treatment, necessitating careful monitoring and possibly further intervention.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence is the return of cancer after a period of improvement or NED, requiring further assessment and intervention.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to the return of cancer after treatment, which may be persistent (non-responsive to treatment) or newly developed cancer in the same or another location.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to the return of cancer after treatment, which necessitates surveillance measures and potential further interventions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA-PET">
      <data key="d0">PSMA-PET</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">PSMA-PET is an advanced imaging technique targeting prostate-specific membrane antigens for enhanced detection of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA">
      <data key="d0">PSMA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSMA, or prostate-specific membrane antigen, is a protein expressed on the surface of prostate cancer cells, which is targeted by certain medical tracers and scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA-PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">PSMA-PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PSMA-PET scan is a specific imaging test used to diagnose and monitor prostate cancer by targeting PSMA on cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transrectal Ultrasound (TRUS)">
      <data key="d0">Transrectal Ultrasound (TRUS)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">TRUS is an ultrasound technique that assists in guiding prostate biopsies, providing images from inside the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Cancer Cells">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Cancer Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate cancer cells are abnormal cells derived from the prostate gland that exhibit characteristics of cancer and can produce high levels of prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA-Tracers">
      <data key="d0">PSMA-Tracers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSMA-tracers are specialized imaging agents developed to target the PSMA protein on prostate cancer cells for diagnostic purposes, particularly in PSMA-PET scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Bone Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone metastasis occurs when prostate cancer spreads to the bones, which can be diagnosed using imaging techniques like bone scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI-TRUS Fusion">
      <data key="d0">MRI-TRUS Fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI-TRUS fusion is a technique that combines MRI images with real-time ultrasound to improve the accuracy of prostate biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urological Procedures">
      <data key="d0">Urological Procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urological procedures encompass various diagnostic and therapeutic techniques focused on the urinary tract and male reproductive organs, including biopsies and imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Chicago Medicine">
      <data key="d0">University of Chicago Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">University of Chicago Medicine is a healthcare institution that provides expertise in treating diseases of the urinary system and offers various diagnostic services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Test">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A genetic test is a diagnostic procedure that identifies mutations or changes in genes which may increase the risk of developing cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germline Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Germline Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Germline mutations are hereditary genetic changes that can be passed from parents to offspring, increasing cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Sample">
      <data key="d0">Blood Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Blood Sample is a specimen taken from a patient to conduct various tests that provide insights into their health status.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood sample is a specimen collected from a patient for testing that can provide information about various health conditions, including the presence of cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complications">
      <data key="d0">Complications</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Complications refer to unwanted and unforeseen issues that arise from a medical procedure, such as infection or bleeding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inherited Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Inherited Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An inherited mutation is a genetic change that is passed down from parents to children and can increase the risk of certain diseases, including cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Somatic Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Somatic Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A somatic mutation is a genetic alteration that occurs in non-germline tissues and cannot be inherited but can lead to cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Somatic mutation refers to a permanent change in the DNA sequence within a cell that can lead to various forms of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA Repair Genes">
      <data key="d0">DNA Repair Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA repair genes are crucial for fixing mutations in the DNA, and their failure can lead to various types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Node Metastasis (TNM) System">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Node Metastasis (TNM) System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM system is a cancer staging system that classifies the extent of cancer spread in an individual, based on tumor size and presence in lymph nodes or metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Testing Methods">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Testing Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor testing methods include various analyses conducted on tumor tissues to determine the genetic and molecular characteristics of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Early-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Early-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines provide patients with comprehensive information on cancer treatment options and management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Castration">
      <data key="d0">Castration</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Castration is a permanent method of hormone therapy for prostate cancer, which involves the surgical removal of the testicles or the use of medication to lower testosterone levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumors represent abnormal growths of tissue, which can vary in shape and size, and are categorized based on their characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Experts">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Experts</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer experts are professionals specialized in diagnosing and treating cancer, playing a key role in developing treatment protocols like the TNM system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage of Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage of Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The stage of cancer indicates how much disease is present in the body, including growth and spread, essential for treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tests">
      <data key="d0">Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests refer to various medical assessments such as biopsies and imaging used in diagnosing and planning treatment for conditions like NPC.&lt;SEP&gt;Tests such as imaging scans and biopsies are essential for diagnosing cancer and determining its stage and appropriate treatment pathways.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Groups">
      <data key="d0">Risk Groups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk groups classify patients based on the likelihood of cancer progression and response to treatment, guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Androgen Deprivation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Androgen Deprivation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Androgen deprivation therapy (ADT) is a treatment aimed at reducing testosterone levels to combat prostate cancer, utilizing various hormonal approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Androgen deprivation therapy (ADT) is a treatment for prostate cancer that lowers testosterone levels through various hormonal approaches and medications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LHRH Agonist">
      <data key="d0">LHRH Agonist</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LHRH Agonists are medications used in ADT to reduce testosterone levels; common examples include goserelin, leuprolide, and triptorelin.&lt;SEP&gt;LHRH Agonists such as goserelin, leuprolide, and triptorelin are medications used in ADT to lower testosterone production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LHRH Antagonist">
      <data key="d0">LHRH Antagonist</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LHRH Antagonists are a class of medications that block testosterone production and are used in the treatment of prostate cancer, with examples including degarelix and relugolix.&lt;SEP&gt;LHRH Antagonists like degarelix and relugolix are used in ADT and effectively block testosterone production by acting differently than agonists.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Castration">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Castration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical castration is a procedure that permanently reduces testosterone levels, often used for hormone therapy in prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical castration is a procedure that permanently reduces testosterone production and is often utilized as a hormonal therapy for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast Cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of breast cells, and its diagnosis and treatment often involve tumor size, lymph node status, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Cancer is a type of cancer that develops in breast tissue and can impact fertility and overall health, requiring careful medical management.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Cancer is a type of cancer that develops in breast tissue, commonly evaluated through Mammograms, MRIs, and other imaging techniques to assess its presence and severity.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a condition that occurs when malignant cells form in the tissues of the breast, with varying types including ductal and lobular carcinomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a disease that begins in the cells of the breast, commonly categorized as carcinomas, which start in the lining cells of organs or surfaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a disease where cancerous cells develop in the breast tissue, which can be invasive or noninvasive and affects individuals regardless of gender.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a malignancy that arises from the cells in the breast tissue, with significant implications for treatment depending on type and stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a malignant condition originating in breast cells, commonly affecting individuals assigned female at birth, characterized by various subtypes, including IDC and lobular carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the cells of the breast, predominantly manifesting as carcinomas that start in either the ductal or lobular regions.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that develops from breast tissue, and its staging is crucial for determining treatment options and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the breast tissue, often beginning in the ducts or lobules, and can present in various forms, including inflammatory breast cancer (IBC).&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the breast tissue, predominantly in the ducts or lobules, and can affect individuals of all genders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive breast cancer is a stage of breast cancer where the cancer cells have spread from the ducts or lobules into nearby breast tissue or lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer is cancer that has grown outside the ducts or lobules into surrounding tissue, allowing it to potentially spread to nearby lymph nodes or other body parts.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer refers to cancer that has spread from the ducts or lobules into surrounding breast tissue or nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer refers to cancer that has spread from the original site in the breast ducts or lobules to surrounding tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guidelines for Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guidelines for Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN provides patient guidelines to help individuals understand their cancer treatment options, including metastatic breast cancer information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Axillary lymph nodes are located in the armpit and are significant in breast cancer treatment as they are common sites for the spread of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are located near the armpit and are critical in the spread and treatment monitoring of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located near the armpit that can be involved in the spread of breast cancer and are assessed during diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN.org/patientguidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN.org/patientguidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN provides comprehensive patient guidelines and resources for understanding and managing various cancers, including metastatic breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph vessels are part of the lymphatic system that help transport lymph, and in inflammatory breast cancer, they can be blocked by cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph vessels transport lymph, a fluid that helps to provide nutrients to cells and plays a role in immune function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC)">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC) describes breast cancer that has spread to distant parts of the body, markedly affecting treatment and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic breast cancer is a stage of breast cancer where the disease has spread beyond the breast and lymph nodes to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ducts">
      <data key="d0">Ducts</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Ducts are the tubes in the breast that carry milk from the lobules to the nipple, and are the initial site where breast cancer may develop.&lt;SEP&gt;Ducts are thin tubes in the breast that carry milk from lobules to the nipple and are often where certain types of breast cancer begin.&lt;SEP&gt;Ducts are tubular structures in the breast that transport breast milk from the lobules to the nipple, and are a common site for the initiation of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobules">
      <data key="d0">Lobules</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobules are glands in the breast that produce milk and are also potential sites for the development of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobules are glandular structures in the breast that produce breast milk, important in lactation and potential sites for cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobules are structures within the breast that produce milk and can be sites where breast cancer develops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptor Status">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptor Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone Receptor Status involves testing for receptors that hormones attach to on cells, influencing treatment decisions in breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone Receptor Status refers to the presence or absence of hormone receptors on cancer cells, influencing treatment options especially in breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone receptor status is a test result that indicates whether breast cancer cells have receptors for hormones like estrogen and progesterone, influencing treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Cancer Risk Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Cancer Risk Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic cancer risk testing assesses an individual's genetic predisposition to various types of cancer, including those affecting bones.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing identifies inherited mutations that may increase a person's risk of developing breast cancer, guiding prevention and treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing involves analyzing DNA to identify inherited mutations that may increase the risk of developing cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing involves analyzing a person's genetic material to identify mutations that may increase cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Nodes (ALNs)">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Nodes (ALNs)</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located in the armpit that can be affected by breast cancer as it spreads from the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="breast cancer">
      <data key="d0">breast cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast cancer is a common type of cancer that arises in breast tissue and can develop in anyone, with particular frequency in individuals assigned female at birth.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a common type of cancer that can develop in individuals assigned male or female at birth, generally arising from the epithelial cells of the breast.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the breast tissue and can be categorized by stages and grades of abnormality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="male and female at birth">
      <data key="d0">male and female at birth</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to individuals classified as male or female upon birth, relevant in discussions about breast cancer susceptibility and treatment differences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="primary tumor">
      <data key="d0">primary tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary tumor is the original mass of cancer cells from where the cancer originates before it potentially spreads to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary tumor refers to the original mass of cancer cells that form before they spread to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="metastasis">
      <data key="d0">metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastasis is the process by which cancer cells spread from the original (primary) tumor to form secondary tumors in distant parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the process by which cancer cells spread from the primary tumor to other parts of the body, forming secondary tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="local metastasis">
      <data key="d0">local metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local metastasis refers to cancer spreading to nearby tissues or lymph nodes from the primary site of the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Local metastasis refers to the spread of cancer to nearby tissues or lymph nodes from the primary cancer site.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="distant metastasis">
      <data key="d0">distant metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distant metastasis occurs when cancer cells spread far from the primary tumor site, commonly affecting organs like bones, lungs, liver, and brain.&lt;SEP&gt;Distant metastasis occurs when cancer cells spread far from the primary tumor site, commonly affecting organs such as bone, lungs, liver, and brain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="testing for MBC">
      <data key="d0">testing for MBC</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC) involves various health evaluations, including blood tests and imaging to determine the extent of cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="biopsy">
      <data key="d0">biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy is a medical procedure in which samples of tissue are taken from the body for examination to diagnose diseases like lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy is a medical procedure in which tissue samples are taken for further analysis to confirm diagnoses like CBCL.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy is a medical procedure used to extract tissue samples for diagnostic evaluation, particularly relevant for detecting cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy is a medical test that involves taking a sample of tissue to examine for the presence of cancer cells and to help guide treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy is a medical test that involves taking a small sample of tissue from the body to examine it for cancer cells and determine the presence of disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ductal carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">ductal carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ductal carcinoma is a type of breast cancer that starts in the milk ducts of the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lobular carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">lobular carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobular carcinoma is a type of breast cancer that begins in the lobules, which are the glands that produce milk in the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cancer cells">
      <data key="d0">cancer cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer cells are abnormal cells that grow uncontrollably and differ from normal cells in structure and function, particularly in how they spread and form tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="invasive breast cancer">
      <data key="d0">invasive breast cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive breast cancer describes cancer that has spread from the milk ducts or lobules into nearby breast tissue or lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="blood tests">
      <data key="d0">blood tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood tests are performed to provide additional information regarding a patient's health and to help in planning treatment for conditions like lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are procedures that analyze blood samples to check for substances that indicate the presence of cancer or its effects on the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="hormone receptor status">
      <data key="d0">hormone receptor status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone receptor status refers to whether certain hormones are present in cancer cells, which can influence treatment options and outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 status">
      <data key="d0">HER2 status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 status indicates the presence of the human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 protein, which can affect the behavior of breast cancers and treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Carcinoma In Situ">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Carcinoma In Situ</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) is a noninvasive breast cancer found in the cells that line the ducts of the breast and is treated to prevent invasive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A mammogram is an X-ray procedure used to screen for breast cancer, which can be categorized as screening or diagnostic based on symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast">
      <data key="d0">Breast</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The breast is an anatomical structure and gland in the human body responsible for milk production and is made of various tissues including ducts, lobules, and connective tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;The breast is an organ and gland on the chest, composed of milk ducts, fat, nerves, lymph and blood vessels, and connective tissue, playing a crucial role in lactation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DCIS Test Results">
      <data key="d0">DCIS Test Results</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">DCIS test results provide critical information regarding the presence of ductal carcinoma in situ and are essential for treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nipple-Areola Complex">
      <data key="d0">Nipple-Areola Complex</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The nipple-areola complex (NAC) includes the nipple and the surrounding areola, significant in breast structure and health assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mammograms">
      <data key="d0">Mammograms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mammograms are imaging tests using low dose X-rays to examine breast tissue for cancer detection, available in both 2D and 3D formats.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients offers comprehensive recommendations and resources for breast cancer management, including various types and stages of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Diagnostic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Breast Diagnostic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A breast diagnostic radiologist interprets mammograms, MRIs, and performs necessary biopsy procedures for breast health.&lt;SEP&gt;A breast diagnostic radiologist specializes in interpreting imaging tests, such as mammograms and MRIs, to diagnose breast conditions, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast diagnostic radiologists are healthcare professionals specializing in interpreting mammograms, MRIs, and conducting necessary biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthcare Providers">
      <data key="d0">Healthcare Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Healthcare providers are professionals involved in diagnosing and treating medical conditions related to breast health, including specialists such as gynecologists and lactation consultants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ductal carcinoma is the most common type of breast cancer, originating in the cells that line the milk ducts of the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Lobular Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobular carcinoma is a type of breast cancer that begins in the lobules, the milk-producing glands of the breast.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobular carcinoma is a type of breast cancer that starts in the lobules, the milk-producing glands of the breast.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobular carcinoma starts in the lobules, the milk glands of the breast, and is another significant type of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DCIS">
      <data key="d0">DCIS</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DCIS, or Ductal Carcinoma In Situ, is a non-invasive form of breast cancer that remains in the ducts of the breast and is often detected through screening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Breast Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A breast specialist is a medical professional who focuses on diagnosing and treating diseases related to breast health, particularly breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A breast specialist is an expert focused on breast health and diseases, particularly in the diagnosis and treatment of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast specialists are healthcare professionals with expertise in diagnosing and treating conditions related to the breast, including breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologist">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A gynecologist is a physician who specializes in women's reproductive health, including the diagnosis and treatment of conditions affecting the breasts.&lt;SEP&gt;A gynecologist specializes in diagnosing and treating diseases related to the female reproductive organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Gynecologists diagnose and treat diseases of the female reproductive organs and are often involved in breast health and cancer screenings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lactation Consultant">
      <data key="d0">Lactation Consultant</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A lactation consultant assists mothers with breastfeeding, providing guidance and support, particularly for those affected by breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A lactation consultant specializes in breastfeeding, helping mothers successfully breastfeed.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactation consultants are professionals who specialize in breastfeeding support and education, crucial during the postpartum period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen Receptor Status">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen Receptor Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen receptor status is an important factor in breast cancer, determining how the cancer will respond to hormonal therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Technologists">
      <data key="d0">Technologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mammogram, MRI, and ultrasound technologists operate specific imaging equipment to assist in diagnosing breast conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Practice Providers">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Practice Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced practice providers like nurse practitioners and physician assistants support patient care and management within the multidisciplinary team.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced practice providers, including nurse practitioners and physician assistants, offer comprehensive care, evaluations, and treatment plans for breast health issues, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced practice providers, including nurse practitioners and physician assistants, play an essential role in monitoring patient health and providing care.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced practice providers, including nurse practitioners and physician assistants, provide comprehensive care including evaluation and treatment of diseases like breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Obstetrician">
      <data key="d0">Obstetrician</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An obstetrician specializes in managing pregnancies and childbirth but also addresses related breast health issues that may arise during these times.&lt;SEP&gt;An obstetrician specializes in pregnancy and childbirth, focusing on maternal and fetal health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC) is a more aggressive form of breast cancer that causes redness, swelling, and can be difficult to diagnose due to the absence of a palpable lump.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory breast cancer (IBC) is a rare type of breast cancer characterized by the blockage of lymph vessels under the skin of the breast, causing visible swelling.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory breast cancer is a rare and aggressive form of breast cancer that causes swelling and changes in the skin of the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Ductal Carcinoma (IDC)">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Ductal Carcinoma (IDC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive ductal carcinoma is the most common type of breast cancer, originating in the ducts and spreading to surrounding tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive ductal carcinoma is the most prevalent type of breast cancer that begins in the milk ducts and invades surrounding tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Screening Tests">
      <data key="d0">Breast Screening Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast screening tests are medical procedures such as mammograms and ultrasounds aimed at early detection of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mammogram Technologist">
      <data key="d0">Mammogram Technologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A mammogram technologist is responsible for performing mammograms and assisting in the diagnostic process of breast health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Technologist">
      <data key="d0">MRI Technologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI technologist operates MRI machines to obtain images that help diagnose breast conditions and cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound Technologist">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound Technologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An ultrasound technologist uses ultrasound technology to assist in the diagnosis of breast abnormalities and conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Provider (PCP)">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Provider (PCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary care provider gives broad healthcare services, including preventive care and management of breast health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Screening">
      <data key="d0">Breast Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast screening involves various tests such as mammograms, MRIs, and ultrasounds aimed at early detection of breast cancer before symptoms appear.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptor Testing">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptor Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone receptor testing is performed to determine the presence of hormone receptors in breast cancer cells, which influences treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 Testing">
      <data key="d0">HER2 Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 testing assesses the level of HER2 protein in cancer cells, helping to identify patients who may benefit from targeted therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nipple">
      <data key="d0">Nipple</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The nipple is the protruding structure on the breast through which milk is delivered to infants, and can be affected in cases of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bra">
      <data key="d0">Bra</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bra is a form-fitting garment worn by women that supports the breasts; discomfort can be an indicator of underlying health issues like breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pain">
      <data key="d0">Pain</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pain, particularly sharp and shooting pains, can be a symptom experienced by individuals with breast cancer, signaling the need for medical evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgeon is a medical doctor specialized in performing surgeries, often involved in the treatment and diagnosis of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgeon is a medical professional who performs operations to remove cancerous tissues or organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Online Patient Portals">
      <data key="d0">Online Patient Portals</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Online patient portals are digital platforms that allow patients to access their health records and test results, enhancing patient engagement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Concerns">
      <data key="d0">Health Concerns</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health concerns cover a range of symptoms and issues related to medical conditions, including those prompting breast cancer tests and treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Records">
      <data key="d0">Medical Records</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical records are comprehensive documents containing a patient's health history, treatments, and diagnostic tests, crucial for ongoing care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App serves as a mobile resource for patients to access important cancer treatment and care information.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app offers information and guidance to cancer patients regarding their care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contraceptive Sponge">
      <data key="d0">Contraceptive Sponge</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Contraceptive Sponge is a barrier contraceptive device that prevents pregnancy through physical and chemical means.&lt;SEP&gt;The Contraceptive Sponge is a barrier method that individuals can use to prevent pregnancy during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pregnancy Test">
      <data key="d0">Pregnancy Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pregnancy Test determines whether an individual is pregnant, an important consideration before starting cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pregnancy Test is a diagnostic tool used to determine if a person is pregnant, which is crucial before starting cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-ray">
      <data key="d0">X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An X-ray is a diagnostic imaging test that uses low-dose radiation to create images of bones and assess for abnormalities or tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Diagnostic Mammogram is an imaging test that uses x-rays to create detailed pictures of the breast for assessing abnormalities or tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic mammogram is a detailed imaging test used to detect abnormalities in breast tissue, often performed when initial screenings reveal potential issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast MRI">
      <data key="d0">Breast MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Breast MRI is a specialized MRI scan that provides detailed images of the breast for assessing potential cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sodium Fluoride PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">Sodium Fluoride PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Sodium Fluoride PET-CT is a specific type of PET scan used to evaluate bone conditions, particularly as an alternative to traditional bone scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiotracer">
      <data key="d0">Radiotracer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Radiotracer is a substance used in diagnostic imaging tests like Bone Scans and PET Scans that releases small amounts of radiation to help visualize internal structures, particularly cancerous areas in the bones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Damage">
      <data key="d0">Bone Damage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Damage refers to any deterioration or injury to the bone structure that may be visible on imaging tests such as Bone Scans, often associated with conditions like cancer, injury, or other health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast MRI">
      <data key="d0">Contrast MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast MRI is a specific type of Magnetic Resonance Imaging that uses a contrast agent to enhance the visibility of internal structures in the images produced.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Screening Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Screening Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Screening Mammogram is a routine imaging test performed to detect breast cancer in individuals without any symptoms, typically conducted on a regular basis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET-CT scan">
      <data key="d0">PET-CT scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET-CT scan is a medical imaging technique that combines positron emission tomography (PET) with computed tomography (CT) to give detailed images of tissues and organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="sodium fluoride PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">sodium fluoride PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A sodium fluoride PET-CT is a specific type of PET-CT scan that uses sodium fluoride as a radiotracer primarily for imaging bone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">FDG PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An FDG PET-CT scan employs F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose as a radiotracer, useful for detecting cancer in lymph nodes and other distant sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FES PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">FES PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An FES PET-CT uses a radioactive form of estrogen to identify estrogen receptor-positive cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathology Report">
      <data key="d0">Pathology Report</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pathology report contains detailed information about test results, crucial for making cancer diagnoses and treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathology report contains the results from examining biopsy samples, crucial for determining the presence and extent of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathology report provides detailed information about biopsy samples, indicating whether nodules are cancerous and assisting in determining suitable treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="positive PET scan">
      <data key="d0">positive PET scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A positive PET scan indicates an abnormal finding, potentially showing cancerous activity in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cancer center">
      <data key="d0">cancer center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A cancer center is a specialized medical facility focused on the diagnosis and treatment of cancer, offering various imaging and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="radiotracer">
      <data key="d0">radiotracer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiotracers are substances used in PET scans that emit positrons; they help visualize functions of the body's tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="inflamed skin">
      <data key="d0">inflamed skin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflamed skin is a potential area of concern in breast cancer diagnostics, relevant in various biopsy methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="fine-needle aspiration (FNA)">
      <data key="d0">fine-needle aspiration (FNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fine-needle aspiration is a biopsy technique that uses a thin needle to extract tissue or fluid from a suspicious area for testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="core biopsy (CB)">
      <data key="d0">core biopsy (CB)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Core biopsy is a technique that uses larger needles to obtain larger tissue samples for more comprehensive analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="vacuum-assisted core biopsy (VACB)">
      <data key="d0">vacuum-assisted core biopsy (VACB)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">VACB is a method that utilizes suction to remove tissue samples with a specialized device during a biopsy procedure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="incisional biopsy">
      <data key="d0">incisional biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An incisional biopsy involves surgically removing a portion of a tumor for analysis, frequently used when other methods are unfeasible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="excisional biopsy">
      <data key="d0">excisional biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Excisional biopsy is a procedure to remove an entire abnormal area for evaluation, although it is less preferred due to invasiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="axillary lymph node needle biopsy">
      <data key="d0">axillary lymph node needle biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An axillary lymph node needle biopsy tests nearby lymph nodes to determine if cancer has spread from the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="sentinel lymph node biopsy (SLNB)">
      <data key="d0">sentinel lymph node biopsy (SLNB)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">SLNB is a procedure to remove and examine the sentinel lymph nodes to check for the presence of cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mammogram">
      <data key="d0">mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A mammogram is a specific type of breast imaging that plays a critical role in breast cancer screening and diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentinel Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Sentinel Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sentinel Lymph Node (SLN) is the first lymph node that cancer cells are likely to spread to from a primary tumor, which is crucial for assessing the spread of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Sentinel Lymph Node (SLN) is the initial lymph node that cancer cells are most likely to spread to from a primary tumor, making it crucial for cancer staging and treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Breast Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast Tumor refers to abnormal cell growth in the breast, which can be indicative of cancer and is examined through various surgical and diagnostic methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen is a hormone that plays a role in breast development and can promote the proliferation of cancerous cells when receptors are present.&lt;SEP&gt;Estrogen is a hormone that promotes breast development and can fuel the growth of hormone receptor-positive breast cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progesterone">
      <data key="d0">Progesterone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progesterone is a hormone involved in the menstrual cycle and pregnancy, which also interacts with receptors on some breast cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Progesterone is a hormone involved in the menstrual cycle and pregnancy, which can also interact with breast cancer cells if hormone receptors are present.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocrine Therapy involves treatment methods aimed at blocking hormones to slow or shrink hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine Therapy is a treatment that blocks hormone receptor signaling in breast cancer, helping manage hormone receptor-positive cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine Therapy is a treatment that blocks hormone receptor signaling in hormone receptor-positive breast cancer, potentially stopping the growth of cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 Status">
      <data key="d0">HER2 Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 Status refers to the measurement of the HER2 protein, which can affect the growth of breast cancer cells and informs treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunohistochemistry">
      <data key="d0">Immunohistochemistry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a lab technique used to detect specific antigens in cancer cells, aiding in classification and treatment of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a processing technique to visualize proteins in cells, aiding in the diagnosis and characterization of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mastectomy">
      <data key="d0">Mastectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Mastectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of breast tissue to treat or prevent breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mastectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of one or both breasts, often performed in breast cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lumpectomy">
      <data key="d0">Lumpectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Lumpectomy is a surgical procedure where only the tumor and a small margin of surrounding tissue are removed, utilized as a breast cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2">
      <data key="d0">HER2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 (Human Epidermal growth factor Receptor 2) is a protein that, when overexpressed, can lead to aggressive breast cancer and informs targeted treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 is a gene that can influence cancer cell growth and is often tested in breast cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA">
      <data key="d0">BRCA</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA is a gene that plays a crucial role in tumor suppression and DNA repair, with mutations increasing the risk for various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CEA">
      <data key="d0">CEA</data>
      <data key="d1">tumor_marker</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) is a protein that can be elevated in certain cancers, including breast cancer, and used as a tumor marker.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CA 15-3">
      <data key="d0">CA 15-3</data>
      <data key="d1">tumor_marker</data>
      <data key="d2">CA 15-3 is a biomarker used to monitor treatment responses in breast cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB-H)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB-H)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TMB-H refers to a high number of mutations in tumor DNA, which can help predict responses to immune checkpoint inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-1">
      <data key="d0">PD-1</data>
      <data key="d1">protein</data>
      <data key="d2">Programmed death protein 1 (PD-1) is an immune receptor that influences immune responses against tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FISH">
      <data key="d0">FISH</data>
      <data key="d1">testing_method</data>
      <data key="d2">Fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) is a method used to visualize specific DNA sequences, often used for cancer diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IHC">
      <data key="d0">IHC</data>
      <data key="d1">testing_method</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a technique that uses antibodies to detect specific proteins in cancer or immune cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCR">
      <data key="d0">PCR</data>
      <data key="d1">testing_method</data>
      <data key="d2">Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is a lab technique that amplifies DNA, making it easier to study mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Risk Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Risk Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic risk testing assesses hereditary mutations that may increase cancer risk and can guide treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune checkpoint inhibitors are a class of drugs designed to inhibit proteins like PD-1 and PD-L1 to enhance the immune response against tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Immune checkpoint inhibitors such as Nivolumab (Opdivo) and Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) enhance immune response against cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Instability">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Instability</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite instability (MSI) refers to the retention of repeated DNA sequences, which can indicate a specific type of genetic error common in certain cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite instability is a condition of genetic hypermutability that indicates a deficiency in the DNA mismatch repair system, significant for certain cancer diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Proteins">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Proteins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch repair proteins are involved in correcting errors in DNA replication, crucial for maintaining genetic stability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOG">
      <data key="d0">ECOG</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ECOG, or Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group, is a scale used to measure a person’s performance status and ability to carry out daily activities in relation to cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Genetic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary genetic testing is a process to determine if a cancer is inherited, which may influence treatment decisions and family health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group)">
      <data key="d0">ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ECOG is a system used to assess the performance status of cancer patients, providing a standardized way to evaluate a patient's ability to perform daily activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor 2 (HER2)">
      <data key="d0">Human Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor 2 (HER2)</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 is a protein that can promote the growth of cancer cells, and its status is assessed during breast cancer diagnosis to guide treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen Receptor (ER)">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen Receptor (ER)</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen Receptor (ER) is a protein found in some breast cancer cells that, if present, can influence treatment decisions, like hormone therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progesterone Receptor (PR)">
      <data key="d0">Progesterone Receptor (PR)</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">Progesterone Receptor (PR) is another protein that can affect breast cancer treatment, and its status is important for determining the most effective therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Biomarker Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer biomarker testing involves checking for specific genes, proteins, and markers in cancer cells to help decide treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Size (T)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Size (T)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Size (T) refers to the measurement of the primary tumor's dimensions, which is a critical factor in determining the stage of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Status (N)">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Status (N)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph Node Status (N) evaluates whether cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes and how many lymph nodes are affected, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distant Metastasis (M)">
      <data key="d0">Distant Metastasis (M)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distant Metastasis (M) indicates whether cancer has spread to distant organs, such as the lungs or liver, which is crucial for staging and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prognostic Stage">
      <data key="d0">Prognostic Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prognostic Stage encompasses factors like TNM classification, tumor grade, and biomarker status to assess the cancer's likely progression and response to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Staging">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical Staging includes evaluation methods such as physical exams and imaging tests applied before treatment to assess cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologic Staging">
      <data key="d0">Pathologic Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologic Staging examines cancerous tissue removed surgically to provide a more accurate stage of the disease after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard-of-Care Approaches">
      <data key="d0">Standard-of-Care Approaches</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard-of-Care Approaches refer to established treatment protocols that breast cancer patients typically undergo, affecting staging outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Recurrence Risk">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Recurrence Risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Recurrence Risk indicates the likelihood that a cancer will return after treatment, which is affected by factors like tumor grade and metastasis status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Isolated Tumor Cells (ITCs)">
      <data key="d0">Isolated Tumor Cells (ITCs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Isolated Tumor Cells (ITCs) are small clusters of cancer cells found in lymph nodes that may indicate an early spread of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Micrometastases">
      <data key="d0">Micrometastases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Micrometastases are tiny metastases that may not be detected by traditional imaging but can signal more advanced disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade G1">
      <data key="d0">Grade G1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade G1 indicates a low-grade tumor associated with a low risk of cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade G2">
      <data key="d0">Grade G2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade G2 signifies an intermediate-grade tumor that has a moderate risk of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade G3">
      <data key="d0">Grade G3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade G3 represents a high-grade tumor with a higher risk of recurrence compared to lower grades.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 indicates that the cancer has not invaded deeper layers of the bladder and is confined to the inner lining.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 0 is a noninvasive stage of breast cancer, often classified as Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage I">
      <data key="d0">Stage I</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage I breast cancer is classified as invasive, indicating that cancer has begun to spread outside the ducts or lobules.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage I denotes the earliest stage of cancer where the disease is still localized, making treatment more effective.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage II">
      <data key="d0">Stage II</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage II indicates a progression in cancer, where it may be larger or have spread to nearby lymph nodes, but not further.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage II is an invasive stage of breast cancer, showing increased tumor size or spread to nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage III">
      <data key="d0">Stage III</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage III breast cancer is advanced and can involve extensive local spread or lymph node involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage III reflects more advanced cancer, which has spread to nearby tissues or lymph nodes, requiring more intensive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage IV">
      <data key="d0">Stage IV</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage IV indicates metastatic breast cancer, where cancer has spread to distant sites.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage IV is the most advanced stage of cancer, indicating widespread metastasis, significantly impacting treatment options and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="multidisciplinary team (MDT)">
      <data key="d0">multidisciplinary team (MDT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MDT refers to a team of health care professionals from various specialties working together to plan and implement cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="diagnostic radiologist">
      <data key="d0">diagnostic radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic radiologist interprets medical imaging tests like mammograms to help in breast cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="interventional radiologist">
      <data key="d0">interventional radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An interventional radiologist performs procedures like needle biopsies and places IV ports for patient treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="surgical oncologist">
      <data key="d0">surgical oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical oncologist is a physician specializing in the surgical removal of cancerous tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="reconstructive surgeon">
      <data key="d0">reconstructive surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A reconstructive surgeon performs procedures to restore appearance after mastectomy if desired by the patient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="medical oncologist">
      <data key="d0">medical oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical oncologist treats cancer using systemic therapies such as chemotherapy or targeted therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="radiation oncologist">
      <data key="d0">radiation oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A radiation oncologist administers radiation therapy as part of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="anesthesiologist">
      <data key="d0">anesthesiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An anesthesiologist provides anesthesia during surgical procedures to ensure patients do not feel pain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="nurse practitioner (NP)">
      <data key="d0">nurse practitioner (NP)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nurse practitioner is a healthcare provider who can offer many of the same services as a physician.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="oncology nurse">
      <data key="d0">oncology nurse</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An oncology nurse provides direct patient care and manages treatments for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="oncology pharmacist">
      <data key="d0">oncology pharmacist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An oncology pharmacist specializes in managing the medications used to treat cancer and their side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="clinical trials">
      <data key="d0">clinical trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical trials are research studies that test new treatments and therapies for cancer, often involving patient participation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Infraclavicular Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Infraclavicular Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Infraclavicular nodes are lymph nodes located below the clavicle, which can be involved in breast cancer staging and assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supraclavicular Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Supraclavicular Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supraclavicular nodes are lymph nodes located above the clavicle, significant in determining the extent of breast cancer spread and staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GX">
      <data key="d0">GX</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">GX indicates that the grade of the cancer cannot be determined, used in grading breast tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G1">
      <data key="d0">G1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">G1 refers to low-grade cancer, indicating that the tumor cells appear closer to normal and have a lower risk of spread or recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G2">
      <data key="d0">G2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">G2 refers to intermediate-grade cancer with characteristics that are more abnormal than G1, showing a moderate risk of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G3">
      <data key="d0">G3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">G3 represents high-grade cancer, with highly abnormal cells that tend to grow and spread faster, indicating a higher risk of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS)">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ductal Carcinoma In Situ is a non-invasive breast cancer that is confined to the ducts and has not spread to surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathology Reports">
      <data key="d0">Pathology Reports</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathology reports provide crucial information about the diagnosis, staging, and characteristics of cancer tumors based on tissue analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hospitals">
      <data key="d0">Hospitals</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Hospitals provide medical care and treatment for cancer patients, hosting various specialties involved in treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology">
      <data key="d0">Oncology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncology is the branch of medicine that specializes in the diagnosis and treatment of cancer, encompassing various treatment modalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Workers">
      <data key="d0">Social Workers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Social workers assist individuals in navigating emotional and practical challenges, providing support for mental health issues and care coordination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory breast cancer is a rare and aggressive form of breast cancer that requires specific treatments, including systemic therapy and surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptor Tests">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptor Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone receptor tests determine the presence of hormone receptors in cancer cells, guiding treatment options for breast cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Research Team">
      <data key="d0">Research Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The research team is a group of experts that manages and conducts the clinical trial, guiding participants throughout the process.&lt;SEP&gt;The research team is responsible for collecting data and coordinating care for patients involved in clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emotional Wellbeing">
      <data key="d0">Emotional Wellbeing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Emotional wellbeing is a crucial aspect of a patient's health, particularly for those coping with serious illnesses like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen Receptor">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen Receptor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen receptors are proteins that indicate sensitivity of breast cancer to hormonal treatment, influencing therapeutic approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progesterone Receptor">
      <data key="d0">Progesterone Receptor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progesterone receptors are involved in hormone-regulated processes in breast cancer and affect treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic therapies are treatments that affect the entire body, including chemotherapy and hormone therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Doxorubicin">
      <data key="d0">Doxorubicin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Doxorubicin, also known as Adriamycin, is an anthracycline chemotherapy drug used in various cancer treatments, particularly breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Docetaxel">
      <data key="d0">Docetaxel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Docetaxel is a chemotherapeutic agent categorized as a taxane, used to treat several cancers by inhibiting cell division.&lt;SEP&gt;Docetaxel is a taxane chemotherapy drug used for the treatment of several types of cancer, including breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2-positive breast cancer">
      <data key="d0">HER2-positive breast cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2-positive breast cancer is a type of breast cancer characterized by higher levels of the HER2 protein, which can affect treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trastuzumab">
      <data key="d0">Trastuzumab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Trastuzumab is an antibody drug used to treat HER2-positive breast cancer by targeting the HER2 protein.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDK4/6 Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">CDK4/6 Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CDK4/6 inhibitors are a class of drugs that inhibit certain proteins involved in cell cycle regulation, used in hormone receptor-positive breast cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;CDK4/6 inhibitors are drugs that help control the growth of hormone receptor-positive, HER2-negative cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Everolimus">
      <data key="d0">Everolimus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Everolimus is an mTOR inhibitor used to enhance the effectiveness of endocrine therapy in breast cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Everolimus is another name for Afinitor, a targeted therapy for treating certain cancers by inhibiting the mTOR pathway.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neoadjuvant therapy">
      <data key="d0">Neoadjuvant therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neoadjuvant therapy refers to systemic (drug) treatment given before surgery to shrink tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adjuvant therapy">
      <data key="d0">Adjuvant therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adjuvant therapy refers to systemic (drug) treatment given after surgery to eliminate remaining cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified Radical Mastectomy">
      <data key="d0">Modified Radical Mastectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Modified radical mastectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of an entire breast and some lymph nodes, often performed to treat breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT)">
      <data key="d0">External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">EBRT is a form of radiation therapy that delivers high-energy rays directly to a cancerous area, commonly used in treating various types of cancer, including cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT is a form of radiation treatment that directs high-energy rays at cancerous tissue to kill or inhibit the growth of cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT is a form of radiation treatment used after surgery for cervical cancer, aimed at targeting residual cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT is a method of delivering radiation to the cancer site from outside the body, recommended for various stages of cervical cancer based on the presence of risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT is a method of delivering targeted radiation from outside the body, commonly recommended for cervical cancer patients based on their staging and associated risks.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a method of delivering high-energy radiation from outside the body to treat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a type of radiation treatment that uses a machine to direct radiation at tumors from outside the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neoadjuvant Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Neoadjuvant Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neoadjuvant therapy is a systemic treatment given before surgery aimed at shrinking the tumor to facilitate surgical removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Herbal Supplements">
      <data key="d0">Herbal Supplements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Herbal supplements can affect the efficacy of systemic therapies and are often assessed during cancer treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Doxorubicin (Adriamycin)">
      <data key="d0">Doxorubicin (Adriamycin)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Doxorubicin is an anthracycline chemotherapy drug used in various cancer treatments, known for its effectiveness and potential side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epirubicin (Ellence)">
      <data key="d0">Epirubicin (Ellence)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Epirubicin is an anthracycline chemotherapy drug similar to Doxorubicin, used in the treatment of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Capecitabine (Xeloda)">
      <data key="d0">Capecitabine (Xeloda)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy medication that is used as part of treatment regimens for breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid Growth Factors (MGFs)">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid Growth Factors (MGFs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloid growth factors are substances that stimulate the production of white blood cells to prevent infection during chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2-targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d0">HER2-targeted Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2-targeted therapy specifically targets the HER2 protein in breast cancer, commonly used alongside chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Everolimus (Afinitor)">
      <data key="d0">Everolimus (Afinitor)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Everolimus is an mTOR inhibitor used in combination with other therapies to enhance the treatment of cancers that have become resistant to endocrine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tamoxifen">
      <data key="d0">Tamoxifen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tamoxifen is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) used to block estrogen receptors in certain types of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Tamoxifen is a selective estrogen receptor modulator used in treating hormone receptor-positive breast cancer, often in conjunction with other therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ibrance">
      <data key="d0">Ibrance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ibrance is a medication used in breast cancer treatment, classified as a CDK4/6 inhibitor that targets cellular growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kisqali">
      <data key="d0">Kisqali</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kisqali is a medication used to treat breast cancer, functioning as a CDK4/6 inhibitor, aiming to control cancer cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Afinitor">
      <data key="d0">Afinitor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Afinitor is a medication that serves as an mTOR inhibitor, which is employed in cancer therapy to inhibit cell growth and division.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lynparza">
      <data key="d0">Lynparza</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lynparza is a PARP inhibitor used in cancer treatment, especially targeting cancer cells with BRCA mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Talzenna">
      <data key="d0">Talzenna</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Talzenna is a PARP inhibitor similar to Lynparza, designed to interfere with the repair mechanisms of cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Piqray">
      <data key="d0">Piqray</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Piqray is a medication classified as a PIK3CA inhibitor, used for treating breast cancer linked to mutations in the PIK3CA gene.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Keytruda">
      <data key="d0">Keytruda</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Keytruda is a checkpoint inhibitor immunotherapy drug used to treat bladder cancer by enhancing immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;Keytruda is an immunotherapy drug that works by targeting the PD-1 pathway, helping to activate the immune system against tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GnRH Agonists">
      <data key="d0">GnRH Agonists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">GnRH Agonists are drugs that suppress hormone production from the ovaries and are utilized in endocrine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Letrozole">
      <data key="d0">Letrozole</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Letrozole is a non-steroidal aromatase inhibitor that prevents the conversion of androgens to estrogens, used in breast cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Megestrol Acetate">
      <data key="d0">Megestrol Acetate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Megestrol Acetate is a synthetic progestin used to treat breast cancer and manage weight loss in cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary CNS Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of cancer that affects the central nervous system, often requiring specialized care and various testing methods for diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer that originates in the central nervous system, affecting the brain and spinal cord.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a rare and aggressive type of cancer that originates in the lymphocytes of the central nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of brain cancer that arises from lymphatic tissue in the central nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of cancer that affects the central nervous system, particularly the brain and spinal cord.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of cancer that affects the central nervous system, requiring a specific treatment protocol that involves chemotherapy and immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer primarily affecting the central nervous system, including the brain and spinal cord. It is characterized by uncontrolled growth of lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that affects the central nervous system, and treatments include chemotherapy, radiation, and stem cell rescue.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the central nervous system, characterized by tumors developing in the brain's lymphatic tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the central nervous system, often involving the brain and potentially the eyes, requiring specialized diagnostic procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the central nervous system, often requiring specialized treatment methods including the consideration of clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mTOR Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">mTOR Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">mTOR Inhibitors are a class of drugs that inhibit the mTOR protein, playing a critical role in cell division and cancer growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exemestane">
      <data key="d0">Exemestane</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Exemestane is a steroidal aromatase inhibitor used in endocrine therapy to treat hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fulvestrant">
      <data key="d0">Fulvestrant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fulvestrant is a selective estrogen receptor degrader (SERD) used as part of endocrine therapy for breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormonal Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Hormonal Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormonal Therapy is a treatment method aimed at interfering with hormone actions, often used to treat hormone-sensitive cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Ablation">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Ablation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian Ablation involves the removal or radiation of the ovaries to reduce hormone production for cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Suppression">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Suppression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian Suppression refers to using drugs to temporarily stop the ovaries from producing hormones for cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goserelin">
      <data key="d0">Goserelin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Goserelin is a GnRH agonist used to suppress hormone production in ovarian cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leuprolide">
      <data key="d0">Leuprolide</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leuprolide is a medication that is a GnRH agonist helping to control hormone levels in cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA1 Mutation">
      <data key="d0">BRCA1 Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA1 Mutation is a genetic mutation that increases the risk for breast and ovarian cancer, often targeted in treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA2 Mutation">
      <data key="d0">BRCA2 Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA2 Mutation is similar to BRCA1 but involves different genetic risk factors for breast and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine System">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Endocrine System comprises glands that produce hormones, which can impact cancer growth in hormone-sensitive cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Central Nervous System (CNS)">
      <data key="d0">Central Nervous System (CNS)</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Central Nervous System (CNS) consists of the brain and spinal cord and is essential for processing sensory information and controlling bodily functions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Central Nervous System (CNS) consists of the brain and spinal cord, and is responsible for processing sensory information and controlling bodily responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV">
      <data key="d0">HIV</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV infection is a risk factor for anal cancer and can affect the immune system's ability to combat the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system and is associated with increased risk of developing certain cancers, including primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is a virus that can increase the risk of developing anal cancer, making its status important for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is the virus that causes AIDS and is frequently linked to an increased risk of developing primary CNS lymphoma due to its impact on the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is a virus that weakens the immune system and increases the risk of developing anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epstein-Barr Virus">
      <data key="d0">Epstein-Barr Virus</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) is a common virus associated with infectious mononucleosis and can cause EBV-related cancers, particularly nasopharyngeal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Epstein-Barr Virus is a common virus that can lead to various diseases and is notably a risk factor for developing primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The Epstein-Barr virus is a common virus associated with mononucleosis and other health issues, and is one of the risk factors for primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Older Adults">
      <data key="d0">Older Adults</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Older adults are individuals generally in their 60s or 70s, and they have a higher statistical chance of being diagnosed with primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Older adults are individuals typically in their 60s and 70s who statistically have a higher incidence of certain types of cancers, including primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunodeficiency Disorder">
      <data key="d0">Immunodeficiency Disorder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An immunodeficiency disorder weakens the immune system's ability to combat infections and diseases, increasing susceptibility to various conditions, including lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;An immunodeficiency disorder weakens the immune system's ability to fight infections and can increase the risk of developing primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Autoimmune Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Autoimmune Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Autoimmune diseases occur when the immune system mistakenly attacks the body's own tissues, which can be linked to increased risk of various lymphomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Autoimmune diseases, such as lupus, occur when the immune system attacks healthy tissues and are linked to an increased risk of primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Certain autoimmune diseases can weaken the immune system, making it harder for the body to clear HPV infections, thereby increasing cervical cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Premenopause">
      <data key="d0">Premenopause</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Premenopause is a transitional phase in a woman's life characterized by changes in hormone levels, specifically estrogen and progesterone, leading to the potential cessation of menstrual periods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amenorrhea">
      <data key="d0">Amenorrhea</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Amenorrhea refers to the absence of menstrual periods, which can occur during treatment for various conditions, including certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Brain Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A brain tumor is an abnormal growth of cells within the brain, which can be benign or malignant. Primary CNS Lymphoma is a specific type of brain tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;A brain tumor is an abnormal growth of tissue in the brain that can cause a variety of symptoms including nausea, confusion, and weakness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF)">
      <data key="d0">Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF)</data>
      <data key="d1">organs</data>
      <data key="d2">Cerebrospinal fluid is a clear fluid found in the brain and spine that can be involved in the spread of cancer, such as primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerebrospinal fluid is the liquid that surrounds and protects the brain and spinal cord, crucial for maintaining CNS health and function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurological Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Neurological Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neurological symptoms are changes in function related to the nervous system, including issues with coordination, movement, and cognitive functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Neurological symptoms associated with primary CNS lymphoma may include weakness, coordination issues, behavioral changes, and memory problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Abnormality">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Abnormality</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic abnormality refers to mutations in genes that can lead to uncontrolled cell growth, such as that seen in cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="primary CNS lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary CNS lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer that primarily affects the brain and can also occur in the spinal cord and eyes. It is characterized by the growth of abnormal lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that affects the central nervous system, including the brain and spinal cord. It can require treatments such as chemotherapy and radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNS">
      <data key="d0">CNS</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">CNS, or central nervous system, includes the brain and spinal cord and is the primary site affected by primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;CNS, or the central nervous system, includes the brain and spinal cord, being the primary location for primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The central nervous system (CNS) comprises the brain and spinal cord, and is the primary site affected by primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cell Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cell Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">A stem cell transplant may be utilized in treating primary CNS lymphoma to restore healthy blood cells after intensive treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell transplant is a procedure in which healthy cells are used to replace damaged cells following intense treatment for lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnosis Tests">
      <data key="d0">Diagnosis Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnosis tests for primary CNS lymphoma include neurological exams, MRIs, CT scans, and biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Plans">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Plans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment plans for primary CNS lymphoma consist of tailored strategies involving chemotherapy, radiation therapy, and possible clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune System">
      <data key="d0">Immune System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The immune system is the body's defense mechanism against infections and diseases, and an impaired immune system is a significant risk factor for developing primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support for Caregivers">
      <data key="d0">Support for Caregivers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Support for caregivers of patients with primary CNS lymphoma involves providing physical, emotional, and informational assistance throughout the treatment process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A stereotactic biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure used to obtain a tissue sample from the brain to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma with precision.&lt;SEP&gt;A stereotactic biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure used to obtain tissue samples from brain tumors located in challenging areas.&lt;SEP&gt;A stereotactic biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure used to obtain tissue samples from the brain for the diagnosis of tumors, including lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A stereotactic biopsy is a minimally invasive surgical procedure performed by a neurosurgeon to obtain a tissue sample from a brain tumor using precision techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic biopsy is a medical procedure used to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma by obtaining a tissue sample from the brain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurosurgeon">
      <data key="d0">Neurosurgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neurosurgeon is a medical doctor specializing in performing surgeries on the brain and nervous system, including biopsies to diagnose tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurosurgeon is a medical professional specialized in surgeries involving the brain and nervous system, playing a key role in performing biopsies for CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurosurgeon is a physician specializing in surgical procedures on the brain, spine, and nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurosurgeon specializes in surgery of the brain and nervous system, including procedures like biopsy and resection of tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stephanie E. Weiss, MD, FASTRO">
      <data key="d0">Stephanie E. Weiss, MD, FASTRO</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Stephanie E. Weiss is a medical doctor specializing in the analysis and treatment of cancer, particularly lymphoma, contributing to the diagnostic process as a pathologist.&lt;SEP&gt;Stephanie E. Weiss is a medical doctor with expertise in radiation oncology, contributing to the understanding of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Tissue Sample">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Tissue Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tumor tissue sample is a small piece of tumor removed for diagnostic testing to confirm conditions such as primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Scans">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging scans are tests like MRI and CT scans that visualize the inside of the body to help diagnose diseases such as primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Planning Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Planning Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Planning treatment involves determining the best therapeutic strategies for a patient diagnosed with primary CNS lymphoma based on diagnostic tests and evaluations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET/CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET/CT scan combines positron emission tomography and computed tomography to visualize cancer cells in the body, useful for assessing spread.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET/CT scan is a combined imaging technique that uses radioactive substances and x-rays to detect cancerous cells and assess their spread in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical imaging procedure that combines Positron Emission Tomography (PET) and Computed Tomography (CT) to visualize metabolic processes in the body, specifically to detect cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="vitreous">
      <data key="d0">vitreous</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The vitreous is a clear gel-like substance filling the eye, and its involvement is significant when diagnosing conditions like vitreoretinal lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="retina">
      <data key="d0">retina</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The retina is the light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye; lymphoma may affect it, especially in cases of vitreoretinal lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="eye doctor">
      <data key="d0">eye doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An eye doctor specializes in diagnosing and treating eye-related conditions, essential for evaluating lymphoma's impact on the eyes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="biopsy of the vitreous">
      <data key="d0">biopsy of the vitreous</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy of the vitreous involves extracting a small tissue sample from the eye to test for lymphoma or other diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chest">
      <data key="d0">chest</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The chest is a body area that may be assessed through imaging to check for spread of lymphoma from the CNS.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="abdomen">
      <data key="d0">abdomen</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The abdomen is the region used to look for potential cancer spread in lymphoma patients via imaging techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="pelvis">
      <data key="d0">pelvis</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The pelvis is an area that may be examined during lymphoma diagnosis to check for metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="specialized center">
      <data key="d0">specialized center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialized center refers to medical facilities that focus on specific technologies or treatments, such as a center for stereotactic biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound of the Testicles">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound of the Testicles</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An imaging test used to assess the testicular area for abnormalities, often conducted after initial cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Options for Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Options for Primary CNS Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Various methods and therapies available for treating primary CNS lymphoma, including clinical trials, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, and immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP)">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP)</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">The CMP provides information on kidney and liver function, and checks for components like calcium that might be affected by cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) is a blood test that provides important information about the body’s metabolism, including blood sugar levels and liver function, through the measurement of various substances in the blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH)">
      <data key="d0">Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH)</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme found in many body tissues, and elevated levels in the blood can indicate tissue damage, including damage caused by cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV Blood Test">
      <data key="d0">HIV Blood Test</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">The HIV blood test is essential for individuals diagnosed with primary CNS lymphoma to determine the presence of the virus, which impacts treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B Testing">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B testing is necessary for patients undergoing immunotherapy treatment to ensure that the virus does not interfere with cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cell Rescue">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cell Rescue</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stem cell rescue includes procedures to restore stem cells following high doses of chemotherapy or radiation, used to recover the blood and immune systems post-treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue is a medical procedure involving the harvesting, chemotherapy treatment, and transfusion of stem cells to restore healthy blood-forming cells in patients with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue is a procedure that involves harvesting, chemotherapy, and infusion of stem cells to restore blood cell production in bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANCER Information Service">
      <data key="d0">CANCER Information Service</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The CANCER Information Service is a part of the National Cancer Institute that helps individuals find clinical trials and provides cancer-related information.&lt;SEP&gt;The CANCER Information Service is a resource from the NCI that helps individuals find clinical trials and understand cancer-related information.&lt;SEP&gt;The CANCER Information Service is a resource provided by NCI, offering information on cancer treatment and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steroids">
      <data key="d0">Steroids</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Steroids are medications that can help manage symptoms of lymphoma by decreasing swelling in the brain but have potential side effects and are used in limited duration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Team">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The treatment team consists of medical professionals who collaborate to provide care, discuss treatment options, and assist patients in navigating clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Informed Consent">
      <data key="d0">Informed Consent</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Informed consent is a process ensuring that trial participants understand the risks and benefits before joining a study.&lt;SEP&gt;Informed consent is a process to ensure participants are aware of the risks and benefits before joining clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Informed consent is the agreement given by a patient to participate in a clinical trial, acknowledging that they have been informed about the study's purpose, risks, and benefits.&lt;SEP&gt;Informed consent is the process of providing potential clinical trial participants with comprehensive information about the study, including risks and benefits, ensuring they understand before agreeing to participate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Side Effects of Steroids">
      <data key="d0">Side Effects of Steroids</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Side effects of steroids can include increased hunger, mood changes, and potential spikes in blood sugar levels, impacting patients undergoing treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transport and Childcare Costs">
      <data key="d0">Transport and Childcare Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transport and childcare costs are indirect expenses that participants in clinical trials may incur, necessitating financial consideration in trial participation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="methotrexate">
      <data key="d0">methotrexate</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug commonly used in high doses for treating primary CNS lymphoma, known for its effectiveness but potential toxicity to organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="intrathecal chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">intrathecal chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrathecal chemotherapy is a method of administering chemotherapy directly into the cerebrospinal fluid, used for treating cancer that has spread to the CNS.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="intraocular chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">intraocular chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Intraocular chemotherapy is used when primary CNS lymphoma occurs in the eye, involving the infusion of chemotherapy directly into the eye.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="radiation therapy">
      <data key="d0">radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation therapy is a treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma that uses high-energy beams to kill cancer cells, often administered in small doses over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="whole-brain radiation therapy (WBRT)">
      <data key="d0">whole-brain radiation therapy (WBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Whole-brain radiation therapy (WBRT) involves treating the entire brain to eliminate multiple tumor spots simultaneously.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Nausea and Vomiting">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Nausea and Vomiting</data>
      <data key="d1">resource</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide information on managing side effects such as nausea and vomiting during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Dose Methotrexate">
      <data key="d0">High-Dose Methotrexate</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">High-dose methotrexate is a potent chemotherapy drug specifically used to treat primary CNS lymphoma and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Failure">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Failure</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidney failure is a serious potential side effect of high-dose methotrexate chemotherapy, resulting from damage to the kidneys.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fluids">
      <data key="d0">Fluids</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fluids are administered during high-dose methotrexate treatment to help reduce toxicity and prevent kidney damage during chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">CNS Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CNS Lymphoma is a type of cancer that develops in the central nervous system, affecting the brain and spinal cord.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rituximab">
      <data key="d0">Rituximab</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Rituximab is an immunotherapy drug often used in conjunction with chemotherapy for treating cancers like primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is an immunotherapy drug used to help the immune system fight primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab, marketed as Rituxan, is an immunotherapy drug used to treat primary CNS lymphoma by targeting cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Team">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The treatment team consists of various healthcare professionals working together to provide care for patients undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The treatment team is composed of healthcare professionals involved in the patient's cancer management and care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Dose Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">High-Dose Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">High-Dose Chemotherapy involves administering intensive chemotherapy to maximize the destruction of cancer cells, crucial in treating primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;High-dose chemotherapy involves administering large amounts of chemotherapy drugs to kill cancer cells effectively but also impacts fast-dividing normal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;High-dose chemotherapy is an intensive treatment designed to eliminate cancer cells but also affects healthy cells in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eyes">
      <data key="d0">Eyes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The eyes are sensitive organs that may be treated with radiation therapy, particularly for conditions like primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cataracts">
      <data key="d0">Cataracts</data>
      <data key="d1">condition</data>
      <data key="d2">Cataracts are a potential side effect of radiation therapy that can cloud the lens of the eye and may require surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CD20 Proteins">
      <data key="d0">CD20 Proteins</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">CD20 proteins are found on the surface of certain blood cells and are targeted by rituximab to aid in the destruction of cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms and Side Effects Tracking">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms and Side Effects Tracking</data>
      <data key="d1">process</data>
      <data key="d2">Tracking symptoms and side effects involves monitoring reactions to treatments in a systematic way to inform healthcare providers about the patient's condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy (WBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy (WBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">WBRT is the most commonly used radiation technique for treating primary CNS lymphoma, targeting the entire brain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Care">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Care</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Multidisciplinary care involves a team of healthcare providers working collaboratively to manage and improve cancer patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient">
      <data key="d0">Patient</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A patient is an individual who undergoes medical treatment for conditions such as primary CNS lymphoma, receiving various therapies including stem cell rescue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nausea">
      <data key="d0">Nausea</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Nausea is a common side effect of chemotherapy and other treatments, causing discomfort and a feeling of sickness.&lt;SEP&gt;Nausea is a symptom that can occur due to a brain tumor exerting pressure on the brain, impacting the digestive system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vomiting">
      <data key="d0">Vomiting</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Vomiting is a side effect frequently experienced by patients undergoing chemotherapy, often accompanying nausea.&lt;SEP&gt;Vomiting is a symptom associated with brain tumors, specifically caused by increased pressure on the brain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diarrhea">
      <data key="d0">Diarrhea</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Diarrhea is another side effect of chemotherapy that can occur as the treatment alters normal digestive processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuro-Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Neuro-Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neuro-oncologist is a specialist focusing on the diagnosis and treatment of brain tumors and other neurological cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Advocate">
      <data key="d0">Patient Advocate</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A patient advocate is an individual who supports and helps patients navigate their treatment plans, ensuring their needs are met.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colorectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colorectal Cancer is a resource that provides information on the treatment of colorectal cancer, including early detection and prevention strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colon cancer is a common and treatable cancer that forms in the colon or rectum, and its early detection significantly improves treatment outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Colon cancer is a malignant growth that originates from cells in the colon and can develop from benign polyps such as adenomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Colon cancer is a type of cancer that originates from the inner lining of the colon and can develop from adenomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colonoscopic Polypectomy">
      <data key="d0">Colonoscopic Polypectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonoscopic Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure to remove polyps from the colon, which can prevent cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNS Lymphoma Treatment">
      <data key="d0">CNS Lymphoma Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CNS Lymphoma treatment encompasses various approaches including chemotherapy and immunotherapy aimed at managing primary CNS lymphoma effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Induction Phase">
      <data key="d0">Induction Phase</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Induction Phase is the initial stage of treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, focusing on eradicating lymphoma cells through chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Consolidation Phase">
      <data key="d0">Consolidation Phase</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Consolidation Phase follows the Induction Phase, aiming to eliminate any remaining cancer cells and prevent recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ibrutinib">
      <data key="d0">Ibrutinib</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Ibrutinib is a medication used for treating certain cancers, including a potential alternative for patients unable to tolerate standard chemotherapy options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temozolomide">
      <data key="d0">Temozolomide</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Temozolomide is an oral chemotherapy drug that may be used in combination with other treatments for CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vincristine">
      <data key="d0">Vincristine</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Vincristine is a chemotherapy drug used in various cancer protocols including those for lymphomas, working by inhibiting cancer cell division.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Procarbazine">
      <data key="d0">Procarbazine</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Procarbazine is a chemotherapy agent that can be part of the treatment regimen for certain types of lymphoma, including primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lenalidomide">
      <data key="d0">Lenalidomide</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Lenalidomide is an immunomodulatory drug that may be utilized in the treatment of multiple myeloma and certain lymphomas, including CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colorectal Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colorectal Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines provide evidence-based recommendations for the prevention, detection, and treatment of colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoembryonic antigen is a protein whose levels can indicate the presence and progression of colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polyp Removal">
      <data key="d0">Polyp Removal</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Polyp removal is a preventative measure against colon cancer, typically conducted during a colonoscopy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non-Metastatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has not spread beyond its original site, making it generally more treatable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colectomy">
      <data key="d0">Colectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Colectomy is a surgical operation involving the removal of a portion of the colon, utilized primarily for treating colorectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Colectomy is a surgical procedure for cancer that involves removing a portion of the colon.&lt;SEP&gt;Colectomy is a surgical procedure to remove all or part of the colon, leading to various potential side effects and recovery processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Colectomy is a surgical procedure to remove part or all of the colon and is a standard treatment for non-metastatic colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Local Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local therapies are treatments targeting specific tumor sites, such as liver and lung tumors, in cases of metastatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Survivorship">
      <data key="d0">Survivorship</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Survivorship encompasses the various aspects of care and quality of life for individuals who have undergone cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Survivorship refers to the ongoing care and support required for individuals after cancer treatment to manage long-term health challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploration of Options">
      <data key="d0">Exploration of Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Exploration of options refers to the process of investigating various treatment paths available for cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mental Health">
      <data key="d0">Mental Health</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mental health refers to the emotional and psychological well-being of an individual, which is essential during cancer treatment and survivorship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ctDNA Testing">
      <data key="d0">ctDNA Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ctDNA testing, or circulating tumor DNA testing, is an emerging method for detecting early-stage colon cancer through a liquid biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Young Adults">
      <data key="d0">Young Adults</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Young adults are increasingly being diagnosed with colorectal cancer and may have unique fertility considerations related to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diminished Mismatch Repair">
      <data key="d0">Diminished Mismatch Repair</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diminished mismatch repair (dMMR) is a key biomarker that should be tested for in all colon cancers as it indicates potential treatment pathways.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Self-Care">
      <data key="d0">Self-Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Self-care refers to the practice of taking action to preserve or improve one's own health, particularly emphasized for those facing health challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colonoscopy Screening">
      <data key="d0">Colonoscopy Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonoscopy screening is a procedure used to detect changes or abnormalities in the colon and rectum, vital for early detection of colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Cancer Screening Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Cancer Screening Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The colorectal cancer screening guidelines provide recommendations for health checks and screenings to detect colorectal cancer early, enhancing treatment success.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A polyp is an abnormal growth of tissue on the inner lining of the colon, which can be benign or malignant.&lt;SEP&gt;A polyp is an abnormal growth on the inner lining of the colon wall, which can be classified into various types, including adenomas, pedunculated, and sessile polyps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pedunculated Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Pedunculated Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pedunculated polyps are a type of polyp characterized by a stalk and a round top, resembling a mushroom.&lt;SEP&gt;Pedunculated polyps are characterized by a stalk and rounded top, resembling mushrooms, and are one type of polyp found in the colon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sessile Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Sessile Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sessile polyps are flat polyps that do not have a stalk, lying directly against the colon wall, which can pose a higher risk of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Sessile polyps are flatter polyps without a stalk that lie directly against the colon wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colon">
      <data key="d0">Colon</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The colon is a part of the digestive system where the removal of water from waste occurs and where polyps can form.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon, also known as the large intestine, is responsible for absorbing water and forming waste products to be excreted.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon, or large intestine, is responsible for water absorption and the formation of feces in the digestive system.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon, part of the digestive system, is where the removal of water from waste occurs, and it is the site for the development of polyps and cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colon Wall">
      <data key="d0">Colon Wall</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The colon wall has several layers and provides structure to the colon; cancer begins in the inner layer, also known as the mucosa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nivolumab">
      <data key="d0">Nivolumab</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Nivolumab is an immunotherapy checkpoint inhibitor used as a treatment for certain types of cancers, including in patients with dMMR/MSI-H colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAPEOX">
      <data key="d0">CAPEOX</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">CAPEOX is a chemotherapy regimen that includes capecitabine and oxaliplatin, recommended for the treatment of stage 3 colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOLFOX">
      <data key="d0">FOLFOX</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">FOLFOX is a chemotherapy regimen used to treat colorectal cancer involving folinic acid, fluorouracil, and oxaliplatin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM Staging System">
      <data key="d0">TNM Staging System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TNM staging system is a classification method used to describe the size, location, and spread of tumors in cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM staging system is used by the AJCC to classify cancer based on Tumor size, Node involvement, and Metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM staging system is used to describe the size and extent of cancer spread, including the primary tumor (T), regional lymph nodes (N), and distant metastasis (M).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anastomotic Leak">
      <data key="d0">Anastomotic Leak</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An anastomotic leak is a complication where there is leakage from the site where the colon has been reconnected during colectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer recurrence refers to the return of cancer after treatment, which can occur in patients previously diagnosed and treated for colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal cancer (NPC) is a rare type of cancer affecting the nasopharynx, often occurring in specific populations, defined by particular symptoms and treatment requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the nasopharynx and requires staging to determine its extent and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of cancer that primarily affects the nasopharynx and is often associated with Epstein-Barr Virus. It requires various diagnostic tests and treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Patient Guidelines provide comprehensive resources and recommendations for cancer patients regarding treatment options and care standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Pharynx">
      <data key="d0">The Pharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The pharynx is a muscular tube in the neck that connects the nasal cavity and the mouth, divided into three parts: nasopharynx, oropharynx, and hypopharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance Follow-up Testing">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance Follow-up Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surveillance Follow-up Testing refers to ongoing medical assessments performed after cancer treatment to monitor for any signs of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Exams">
      <data key="d0">Physical Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical Exams are routine examinations conducted to assess the health status of patients, including those recovering from cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical exams are assessments conducted by oncologists as part of cancer surveillance and follow-up to monitor for symptoms or changes in health after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT Scans">
      <data key="d0">CT Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CT Scans are imaging tests that help detect potential cancer recurrence and evaluate its spread in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA) Test">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA) Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA) Test is a blood test used as a tumor marker for certain cancers, including surveillance in colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stages 1, 2, and 3 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stages 1, 2, and 3 Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages 1, 2, and 3 Colon Cancer refer to classifications based on the extent and progression of colorectal cancer, influencing treatment and monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Survivorship">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Survivorship</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Survivorship encompasses the ongoing care and support provided to individuals after cancer treatment, essential for quality of life and health management.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer survivorship refers to the health and quality of life of individuals who have been diagnosed with cancer, focusing on their long-term well-being after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer survivorship refers to the ongoing health and wellness considerations faced by individuals who have completed cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC)">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC) is a rare form of cancer that originates in the nasopharynx, often diagnosed in specific demographics.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) is a type of cancer that occurs in the nasopharynx, and is staged to determine treatment options and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Stage Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Stage Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced Stage Cancer refers to a progression level where the cancer has spread significantly, making symptoms harder to detect and requiring specialized treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NPC">
      <data key="d0">NPC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NPC (nasopharyngeal carcinoma) is a type of cancer affecting the nasopharynx which connects the nasal cavity to the pharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;NPC (nasopharyngeal carcinoma) is a type of cancer that affects the nasopharynx, a region connecting the nasal cavity to the mouth, primarily impacting respiratory and digestive functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Comprehensive Cancer Center is a specialized healthcare facility that provides a full spectrum of cancer care, including diagnosis, treatment, and research for cancers like NPC.&lt;SEP&gt;A comprehensive cancer center specializes in the evaluation and treatment of various cancers, including NPC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility counseling is a supportive service for cancer patients that provides information on preserving fertility options during and after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eye Exam">
      <data key="d0">Eye Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An eye exam is a procedure that assesses vision health and may be necessary for individuals with head and neck cancer to monitor potential side effects of treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Eye exams assess vision and eye health, and may be particularly recommended for patients with nasopharyngeal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speech and Swallowing Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Speech and Swallowing Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Speech and swallowing assessments determine a patient's ability to communicate and ingest food, crucial in cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;Speech and swallowing assessments evaluate a patient's ability to speak and swallow, which may be impacted by head and neck cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy of Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy of Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy of metastasis involves examining tissue samples from areas where cancer has spread to confirm its presence and extent.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy of metastasis involves taking a sample from cancer spread in other areas of the body, like lymph nodes or lungs, to confirm the presence of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsy of metastasis involves sampling cancer spread to confirm the disease's presence in secondary locations such as lymph nodes or lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ontario Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Ontario Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Ontario Cancer Care is a healthcare organization providing resources and support for cancer patients, including guidance on imaging tests and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDC.gov/tobacco">
      <data key="d0">CDC.gov/tobacco</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">CDC.gov/tobacco is the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention's website providing information and resources about tobacco-related issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor HPV Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor HPV Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor HPV testing identifies whether human papillomavirus (HPV) is associated with specific types of cancers, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA">
      <data key="d0">DNA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is the hereditary material in humans, containing genetic instructions crucial for the functioning of cells and development of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden-High">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden-High</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Mutational Burden-High (TMB-H) indicates a significant number of mutations in cancer cells, often correlating with better responses to immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)">
      <data key="d0">Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) is a virus that can be tested in cancer patients and is particularly relevant in the context of nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 to Stage IV">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 to Stage IV</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages range from Stage 0 (no cancer) to Stage IV (advanced cancer) to classify the severity of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Stage">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical stage is a classification based on findings from surgery, which helps in accurate cancer staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Prognosis">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Prognosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer prognosis refers to the likely course and outcome of cancer, important for patient treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharynx">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The nasopharynx is the anatomical region where the nasal cavity connects to the throat and is commonly associated with certain types of cancers, including squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The nasopharynx is the upper part of the throat behind the nose, where squamous cell carcinoma can start.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ectocervix">
      <data key="d0">Ectocervix</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The ectocervix is the outer part of the cervix lined with squamous cells and is a common site for changes leading to cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The ectocervix is the outer portion of the cervix, exposed to the vaginal canal, and often affected by dysplastic changes leading to cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocervix">
      <data key="d0">Endocervix</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The endocervix is the inner canal of the cervix that communicates with the uterine cavity, lined with mucus-secreting cells, and significant for reproductive processes.&lt;SEP&gt;The endocervix is the inner part of the cervix that connects the vagina to the uterus and is lined with columnar cells, significant for reproductive processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Dysplasia">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Dysplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical dysplasia refers to abnormal cell changes on the cervix that can potentially lead to cervical cancer if untreated.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical dysplasia refers to abnormal cellular changes in the cervix that can potentially progress to cervical cancer if not appropriately treated.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical dysplasia refers to the presence of abnormal cells on the cervix, which can progress to cervical cancer if not treated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (LSIL)">
      <data key="d0">Low-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (LSIL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LSIL indicates mild dysplasia in cervical cells, often associated with a lower risk of progressing to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;LSIL indicates mild dysplastic changes in cervical cells that typically have a lower likelihood of progressing to invasive cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (HSIL)">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (HSIL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HSIL denotes moderate to severe dysplasia in cervical cells and is considered a pre-cancerous condition that may lead to cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HSIL represents moderate to severe dysplasia in cervical cells and is considered a precursor that may lead to cervical cancer without intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pap Smear">
      <data key="d0">Pap Smear</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pap smear is a medical screening procedure that involves collecting cells from the cervix to detect precancerous changes or cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transformation Zone">
      <data key="d0">Transformation Zone</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The transformation zone is the area at the cervix where the ectocervix meets the endocervix and is a common site for abnormal cellular changes leading to dysplasia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Stroma">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Stroma</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical stroma is the connective tissue underlying the cervical lining and provides structural support; it is essential for cervical function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parametrium">
      <data key="d0">Parametrium</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The parametrium is the connective tissue surrounding the uterus and cervix, serving as a structural component that supports reproductive organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix">
      <data key="d0">Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuroendocrine carcinoma of the cervix (NECC) is a rare and aggressive form of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A cervical biopsy is a medical procedure to remove tissue samples from the cervix to diagnose cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A cervical biopsy is a procedure in which samples of cervical tissue are taken to detect abnormalities or cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia 1">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia 1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia 1 (CIN 1) is classified as low-grade dysplasia and is often associated with mild cellular abnormalities on the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia 2">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia 2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia 2 (CIN 2) is considered moderate dysplasia and indicates a higher risk of progression to cancer compared to CIN 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia 3">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia 3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia 3 (CIN 3) is classified as severe dysplasia and is considered a pre-cancerous condition that requires careful monitoring and intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Vaccines">
      <data key="d0">HPV Vaccines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV vaccines are medical products designed to protect against various strains of the human papillomavirus, reducing the risk of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs)">
      <data key="d0">Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Certain sexually transmitted infections can increase the risk of cervical cancer by facilitating HPV infection and affecting the immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cone Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cone Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A cone biopsy is a procedure that both tests and treats early cervical cancers by removing a cone-shaped section of the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;A cone biopsy is a surgical procedure used to remove a cone-shaped section of cervical tissue for diagnosis or treatment of early cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone biopsy is a surgical procedure used to diagnose and treat early-stage cervical cancers and may be followed by further treatment depending on results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Loop Electrosurgical Excision Procedure (LEEP)">
      <data key="d0">Loop Electrosurgical Excision Procedure (LEEP)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">LEEP is a surgical procedure that uses a thin electrified wire loop to remove cervical tissue, which may be used as a treatment for cervical pre-cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocervical Curettage">
      <data key="d0">Endocervical Curettage</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocervical curettage involves scraping the cervical canal to collect tissue samples for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health History and Physical Exam">
      <data key="d0">Health History and Physical Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Health history and physical exams are essential steps in planning cancer treatment, assessing a patient's past and current health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Scalpel">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Scalpel</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical scalpel is a small knife used by surgeons for incisions, particularly in procedures like cone biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Medium">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Medium</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast medium is a substance used in imaging tests that enhances the visibility of internal structures in scans like CT.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vital Signs">
      <data key="d0">Vital Signs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vital signs include blood pressure, heart rate, breathing rate, and body temperature, which are assessed during physical exams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tissues">
      <data key="d0">Tissues</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tissues are groups of cells that work together to perform a specific function; analysis of tissue samples is crucial for diagnosing cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Positron Emission Tomography (PET)">
      <data key="d0">Positron Emission Tomography (PET)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">PET is an imaging test that helps reveal the presence of cancer in the body by using a radioactive tracer.&lt;SEP&gt;PET is an imaging test that uses radiotracers to observe metabolic processes in the body, especially in identifying cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC)">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NECC is a rare type of cervical cancer that may require specific imaging tests, such as MRI of the brain.&lt;SEP&gt;NECC is a rare type of cervical cancer that may require specific imaging tests, such as an MRI of the brain for assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Transposition">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Transposition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian transposition is a fertility preservation technique that moves the ovaries out of the radiation beam's path during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian transposition is a surgical procedure that relocates ovaries during cancer treatment to reduce the risk of damaging them with radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian transposition is a surgical procedure that relocates the ovaries to protect them from radiation exposure during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian transposition is a surgical procedure that relocates the ovaries to protect them from radiation exposure during cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Cystoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure that allows a doctor to view the inside of the bladder using a camera to diagnose abnormalities, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a medical procedure that allows a doctor to look inside the bladder and urethra using a thin tube with a camera.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the urethra and bladder for signs of cancer, allowing for tissue samples to be taken.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure that allows a doctor to look inside the bladder using a cystoscope, essential for diagnosing bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure that allows doctors to view the bladder and collect tissue samples for diagnosis of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure to examine the bladder using a cystoscope and may be performed if cancer is suspected.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure to visually examine the bladder and urinary tract for abnormalities, which can be relevant for cancer staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure used to examine the bladder for signs of cancer by inserting a camera to view the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Proctoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Proctoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Proctoscopy is a medical examination method to visualize the anus and rectum, which may be necessary for assessing cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Proctoscopy is a medical procedure used to visually inspect the rectum and lower part of the colon for abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiotracers">
      <data key="d0">Radiotracers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiotracers are small amounts of radioactive materials used in PET scans to enhance imaging of cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transvaginal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Transvaginal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Transvaginal ultrasound is a procedure that uses sound waves to create images of the cervix and surrounding structures for diagnostic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reproductive Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d0">Reproductive Endocrinologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A reproductive endocrinologist is a specialist in fertility treatment and may be consulted for fertility preservation options in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A reproductive endocrinologist specializes in fertility issues and may be consulted for patients considering fertility-sparing treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO)">
      <data key="d0">International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The FIGO is an international organization that standardizes the staging of gynecological cancers, including cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nicotine Withdrawal">
      <data key="d0">Nicotine Withdrawal</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nicotine withdrawal refers to the symptoms that occur when a person stops using nicotine, making the cessation process difficult.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support Resources">
      <data key="d0">Support Resources</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Support resources include various programs and online services aimed at helping individuals quit smoking, such as SmokeFree.gov and BeTobaccoFree.gov.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1A Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1A Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A cervical cancer is characterized by a tumor that is 5 mm or smaller, indicating an early stage of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1B Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1B Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1B cervical cancer indicates that the cancer is larger than 5 mm but has not spread beyond the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT, MRI, PET, and/or transvaginal ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">CT, MRI, PET, and/or transvaginal ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">These imaging techniques are utilized to determine the extent and staging of cervical cancer prior to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d0">Trachelectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Trachelectomy is a surgical procedure aimed at removing the cervix while preserving the uterus and ovaries, allowing for potential future pregnancies.&lt;SEP&gt;Trachelectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the cervix, and is performed as a fertility-sparing option for patients with early-stage cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A hysterectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the uterus, often performed on patients with advanced cervical cancer or those who do not wish to preserve fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the uterus, which may also include the cervix and other reproductive tissues, often used in the treatment of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hysterectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the uterus, which can include the cervix, performed as a treatment for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hysterectomy is the surgical removal of the uterus, which may involve considerations for preserving the ovaries based on individual health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Initial Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Initial Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Initial imaging methods include CT, MRI, and PET scans, which help gauge the extent of cervical cancer prior to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility-Sparing Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Fertility-Sparing Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility-sparing treatment options are available for early-stage cervical cancer, allowing women the possibility of natural pregnancy post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIGO System">
      <data key="d0">FIGO System</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The FIGO system is an international standard for staging cervical cancer, determining treatment options based on the extent of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="atrophy">
      <data key="d0">atrophy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Atrophy is a medical condition characterized by the thinning, drying, and inflammation of the vaginal lining, often linked to estrogen deficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the uterus and may include the possibility of preserving ovaries based on medical advice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lymph node dissection">
      <data key="d0">lymph node dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node dissection is a surgical procedure involving the removal of lymph nodes for cancer testing to determine the extent of disease spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="sentinel lymph node biopsy">
      <data key="d0">sentinel lymph node biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Sentinel lymph node biopsy is a focused procedure to identify and test the first lymph nodes likely affected by cancer through the use of dyes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External beam radiation therapy (EBRT)">
      <data key="d0">External beam radiation therapy (EBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">EBRT is a form of radiation therapy that targets cancerous tissues using high-energy waves, aimed at treating tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT)">
      <data key="d0">intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IMRT is an advanced form of EBRT that utilizes varying beam intensities to precisely target tumors while minimizing damage to adjacent healthy tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT is an advanced form of radiation therapy that targets cancers with precision, improving treatment for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">SBRT is a specialist EBRT used for treating metastatic cancers with high doses of radiation delivered in few sessions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ovarian transposition">
      <data key="d0">ovarian transposition</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian transposition is a surgical procedure to relocate ovaries outside the radiation treatment area to help preserve ovarian function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="menopausal hormone therapy (MHT)">
      <data key="d0">menopausal hormone therapy (MHT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Menopausal hormone therapy (previously known as hormone replacement therapy or HRT) helps alleviate symptoms associated with menopause.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Atrophy">
      <data key="d0">Atrophy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Atrophy is a medical condition where the vaginal lining becomes thin and dry, often associated with reduced estrogen levels, leading to inflammation and discomfort.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cardiovascular Disease">
      <data key="d0">Cardiovascular Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cardiovascular disease refers to a range of conditions that affect the heart and blood vessels, and long-term estrogen deficiency can increase the risk of these conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Loss (osteoporosis)">
      <data key="d0">Bone Loss (osteoporosis)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone loss, known as osteoporosis, is a condition characterized by decreased bone density, which can also be a consequence of low estrogen levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT)">
      <data key="d0">Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">IMRT is a type of radiation therapy that allows maximum radiation delivery to the tumor while minimizing exposure to surrounding healthy tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT is an advanced technique of EBRT that allows for precise targeting of tumors while minimizing exposure to surrounding healthy tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT is an advanced technique of radiation therapy that targets tumors while sparing surrounding healthy tissue, specifically used for anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SBRT is a specialized form of radiation therapy that delivers high doses of radiation to small tumors with high precision.&lt;SEP&gt;SBRT is a specialized form of radiation therapy used for limited small metastases in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT)">
      <data key="d0">Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MHT, formerly known as hormone replacement therapy (HRT), involves administering hormones to alleviate symptoms of menopause, often following treatments like radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EBRT">
      <data key="d0">EBRT</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a common form of radiation used to target tumor tissues in cervical cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a common method of radiation therapy used for treating locally advanced cervical cancer, involving multiple sessions.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a treatment that uses targeted radiation to treat locally advanced cervical cancer, typically conducted over a series of sessions.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a type of radiation therapy that targets tumor tissues from outside the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intracavitary Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intracavitary Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Intracavitary brachytherapy involves placing radioactive sources inside the body via instruments, allowing precise delivery of high doses of radiation to cervical cancer tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interstitial Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Interstitial Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Interstitial brachytherapy is a specialized technique used for more advanced cases of cervical cancer, placing radioactive sources directly into the tumor area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Dose-Rate (HDR) Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">High Dose-Rate (HDR) Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">High Dose-Rate Brachytherapy is a method of brachytherapy that delivers high doses of radiation in a short time frame, often preferred for cervical cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Dose-Rate (LDR) Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Low Dose-Rate (LDR) Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Low Dose-Rate Brachytherapy is a method of brachytherapy that administers radiation slowly over time, used for specific cases of cervical cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA)">
      <data key="d0">U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The FDA is a U.S. government agency that oversees the approval and regulation of drugs and medical treatments, ensuring they are safe and effective for public use.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA is the U.S. government agency responsible for approving drugs, including those tested in clinical trials for cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Cancer Institute (NCI)">
      <data key="d0">National Cancer Institute (NCI)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCI is a part of the U.S. National Institutes of Health and provides extensive information and resources related to cancer treatment including clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCI is a part of the U.S. government and plays a significant role in cancer research and clinical trials, providing resources for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCI is part of the U.S. National Institutes of Health, providing information on cancer treatments and supporting clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute is a component of the U.S. National Institutes of Health (NIH) and is dedicated to cancer research and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute is part of the U.S. National Institutes of Health and leads the national effort to eliminate cancer through research.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EGRT (External Beam Radiation Therapy)">
      <data key="d0">EGRT (External Beam Radiation Therapy)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">EGRT is a type of radiation therapy that uses high-energy x-rays directed at cancer sites to kill cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical cancer treatment encompasses various methods such as surgery, chemoradiation, and observation, depending on the cancer stage and other medical factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-stage Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-stage Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage cancer refers to cancers that have not spread beyond the organ of origin, allowing for more effective treatment options like surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphovascular Space Invasion (LVSI)">
      <data key="d0">Lymphovascular Space Invasion (LVSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LVSI indicates the presence of cancer cells in the lymph or blood vessels, affecting treatment decisions and prognostic assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility-sparing Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Fertility-sparing Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility-sparing treatment allows for the option of preserving fertility in early-stage cervical cancer patients through surgical methods that don't involve hysterectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Lymph Node Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Lymph Node Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic lymph node evaluation is conducted to assess the spread of cervical cancer and guide treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Trachelectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radical trachelectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the cervix and part of the vagina, preserving the uterus and allowing for future pregnancies in selected cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaginal Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Vaginal Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaginal brachytherapy is a localized form of radiation treatment used in conjunction with EBRT in cervical cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-stage Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-stage Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage cervical cancer indicates cancers that are contained within the cervix, generally more treatable than later stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locally Advanced Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Locally Advanced Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locally advanced cervical cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond the cervix but not to distant organs, requiring aggressive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic Cervical Cancer refers to cervical cancer that has spread beyond the cervix to other parts of the body and typically requires systemic therapy for treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cervical cancer is an advanced stage where cancer cells have spread to distant parts of the body, presenting a complex treatment challenge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenosquamous Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenosquamous Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenosquamous carcinoma is a subtype of cervical cancer that contains both adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma, influencing treatment protocol.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Staging of Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Staging of Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Staging is the process of determining the extent of cancer spread in the body, crucial for determining appropriate treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Influence of LVSI on Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Influence of LVSI on Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The presence of lymphovascular space invasion (LVSI) significantly impacts treatment decisions, indicating a higher risk of cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extrafascial Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Extrafascial Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extrafascial hysterectomy is a surgical procedure for treating certain stages of cervical cancer and is typically recommended under specific criteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Extrafascial hysterectomy is a surgical procedure performed to treat certain stages of cervical cancer, typically recommended under specific conditions related to cancer staging and biopsy results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified Radical Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Modified Radical Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Modified radical hysterectomy is a surgical option for treating cervical cancer, particularly for cases where cancer spreading is detected at certain stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Modified radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure used to treat cervical cancer when cancer spread is detected, offering a more extensive removal of tissue compared to extrafascial hysterectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Lymph Node Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Lymph Node Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic lymph node dissection involves the surgical removal of lymph nodes in the pelvic region, used to assess cancer spread in cervical cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Para-aortic Lymph Node Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Para-aortic Lymph Node Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Para-aortic lymph node dissection is a procedure involving the removal of lymph nodes located near the aorta to evaluate for cancer spread during cervical cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LVSI (Lymphovascular Space Invasion)">
      <data key="d0">LVSI (Lymphovascular Space Invasion)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LVSI refers to the presence of cancer in lymphatic and blood vessel spaces, serving as a critical factor in staging cervical cancer and guiding treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell neuroendocrine carcinoma of the cervix (NECC) is a rare and aggressive form of cervical carcinoma usually characterized by small cells and high-grade neuroendocrine features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET/CT scan">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET/CT scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fluorodeoxyglucose positron emission tomography/computed tomography (FDG-PET/CT) scan is an imaging technique used to detect disease, including cancer recurrence, by highlighting areas of high metabolic activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic MRI">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic MRI is a non-invasive imaging procedure utilized to evaluate pelvic structures after cervical cancer treatment, particularly when detecting recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelvic MRI is an imaging procedure used to evaluate the structures of the pelvis, often utilized for patients after cervical cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-Up Visits">
      <data key="d0">Follow-Up Visits</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up visits are scheduled appointments with an oncologist to assess a patient's ongoing health after cancer treatment, typically occurring at specified intervals post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET/CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fluorodeoxyglucose positron emission tomography/computed tomography (FDG-PET/CT) scan is an imaging technique used to identify potential cancer recurrence and assess metabolic activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Survivorship Care">
      <data key="d0">Survivorship Care</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Survivorship care entails ongoing health management for cancer survivors, focusing on monitoring for recurrence and managing long-term effects of cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploratory Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Exploratory Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Exploratory surgery is a procedure aimed at examining the body for cancer spread or recurrence, aiding in treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Exenteration">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Exenteration</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic exenteration is a radical surgical procedure involving the removal of multiple organs from the pelvis, performed to treat advanced cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelvic exenteration is a radical surgical procedure that involves the removal of multiple organs from the pelvis to treat advanced cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that entails the removal of the uterus, along with surrounding tissues, and is considered for certain cases of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that removes the uterus along with surrounding tissues, considered for some patients with cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-Based Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-Based Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-based chemotherapy is a common treatment regimen for advanced cervical cancer to manage and control disease progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is a treatment regimen commonly used for various cancers, including cervical cancer, to control disease progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stoma">
      <data key="d0">Stoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A stoma is a surgical opening created on the abdomen that allows for the passage of urine from the bladder to the outside of the body, often used in conjunction with urinary diversion techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;A stoma is a surgically created opening on the body surface for the passage of bodily waste after certain surgeries, such as colostomy.&lt;SEP&gt;A stoma is an artificial opening created on the body for the purpose of diverting bodily waste, often created after certain cancer surgeries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ileal Conduit">
      <data key="d0">Ileal Conduit</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Ileal conduit is a specific type of urinary diversion performed after bladder removal, using a piece of intestine to form a new passage for urine.&lt;SEP&gt;Ileal conduit is a type of urinary diversion performed after bladder removal, allowing urine to exit the body through the abdominal stoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectum Removal">
      <data key="d0">Rectum Removal</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Rectum removal refers to a surgical procedure where the rectum is excised, often resulting in a necessary colostomy for waste elimination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Double-Barreled Wet Colostomy (DBWC)">
      <data key="d0">Double-Barreled Wet Colostomy (DBWC)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">DBWC is a technique for urinary and fecal diversion where both waste types exit through a single stoma, improving patient comfort.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Disease">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic disease refers to cancer that has spread from its original site to distant parts of the body, often making treatment more complex.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma (MGZL) is a type of lymphoma that exhibits overlapping features of primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma and Hodgkin lymphoma, generally treated through chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Mediastinal B-cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary Mediastinal B-cell Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary Mediastinal B-cell Lymphoma (PMBL) is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by large cells and typically requires specialized treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients for Immunotherapy Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients for Immunotherapy Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN provides guidelines for cancer patients regarding immunotherapy side effects and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tisotumab vedotin-tftv">
      <data key="d0">Tisotumab vedotin-tftv</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tisotumab vedotin-tftv (Tivdak) is a targeted therapy option for certain metastatic cancers, often used when standard treatments are ineffective.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First-Line Therapy Regimen">
      <data key="d0">First-Line Therapy Regimen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">First-line therapy regimens are the initial treatment protocols used for cancer patients based on their specific biomarkers and cancer type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1 Biomarker">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1 Biomarker</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The PD-L1 biomarker indicates the presence of a specific protein on cancer cells that can be targeted by certain immunotherapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topotecan">
      <data key="d0">Topotecan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Topotecan is a chemotherapy drug utilized for treating certain types of cancer, including cervical cancer, usually when other treatments have failed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biologic Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Biologic Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biologic therapy involves the use of living organisms or their products to treat diseases, commonly utilized in conjunction with chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Combination Regimen">
      <data key="d0">Combination Regimen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Combination regimens involve the use of two or more treatments, such as chemotherapy and immunotherapy, to improve treatment efficacy in cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advance Care Planning">
      <data key="d0">Advance Care Planning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advance care planning involves discussions about future medical care preferences and decisions, particularly in the event of serious illness or end-of-life considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="thyroid cancer">
      <data key="d0">thyroid cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the thyroid gland, which is responsible for producing hormones that regulate various bodily functions. It is the most common cancer in adults aged 18 to 33 years and often diagnosed earlier in adulthood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="thyroid">
      <data key="d0">thyroid</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped gland located in the front of the neck that creates hormones essential for the body's proper functioning, including metabolism and temperature regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)">
      <data key="d0">familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Familial adenomatous polyposis is a hereditary syndrome that increases the risk of developing certain cancers, including thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carney complex">
      <data key="d0">Carney complex</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carney complex is a genetic syndrome associated with an increased risk of developing several tumors, including thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cowden syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Cowden syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cowden syndrome, also known as PTEN hamartoma tumor syndrome, is an inherited condition that increases the risk of multiple cancers, including follicular thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="multiple endocrine neoplasia (MEN)">
      <data key="d0">multiple endocrine neoplasia (MEN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiple endocrine neoplasia refers to a group of hereditary conditions that can lead to tumors in various endocrine glands, including an increased risk for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="radiation exposure">
      <data key="d0">radiation exposure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation exposure is known to increase the risk of developing thyroid cancer, especially among those who received radiation treatment to the head or neck in the past.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="older persons">
      <data key="d0">older persons</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Older persons are individuals who have a higher rate of bone marrow involvement and more advanced-stage diseases, including various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DLBCL">
      <data key="d0">DLBCL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma (DLBCL) is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by large B cells that grow rapidly, often requiring specific treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="extranodal disease">
      <data key="d0">extranodal disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extranodal disease refers to the spread of lymphoma or other cancers outside the lymph nodes, affecting other organ systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MGZL">
      <data key="d0">MGZL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma (MGZL) has features of both classical Hodgkin lymphoma and non-Hodgkin PMBL, presenting treatment challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chemoimmunotherapy">
      <data key="d0">chemoimmunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemoimmunotherapy is a treatment approach that combines chemotherapy with immunotherapy to enhance treatment effectiveness for certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISRT">
      <data key="d0">ISRT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Involved-Site Radiation Therapy (ISRT) is a method of radiation treatment targeted at specific localized disease areas in patients with lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="thyroid hormone">
      <data key="d0">thyroid hormone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid hormones, primarily thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), are critical for regulating metabolism and overall body function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="iodine">
      <data key="d0">iodine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Iodine is a mineral required by the thyroid to produce hormones, found in certain foods and iodized salt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="thyroid nodules">
      <data key="d0">thyroid nodules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid nodules are abnormal growths within the thyroid gland, most of which are benign and often asymptomatic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TSH">
      <data key="d0">TSH</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid Stimulating Hormone (TSH) is produced by the pituitary gland and regulates the production of thyroid hormones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="female at birth">
      <data key="d0">female at birth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Individuals assigned female at birth have a higher likelihood of being diagnosed with thyroid cancer compared to those assigned male at birth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="risk factors">
      <data key="d0">risk factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk factors are characteristics or conditions that increase the likelihood of developing a disease, including thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="personal history of thyroid cancer">
      <data key="d0">personal history of thyroid cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A personal history of thyroid cancer can increase the risk of subsequent occurrences or related malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Nodule">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Nodule</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid nodules are lumps in the thyroid gland that can produce hormones and are commonly assessed for cancer risk through various tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Hormone">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Hormone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid hormones are critical hormones produced by the thyroid gland, which regulate metabolism and are vital for an individual's overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid hormones are hormones produced by the thyroid gland that regulate various metabolic processes in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TSH Level">
      <data key="d0">TSH Level</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) levels indicate the functionality of the thyroid gland, with levels influencing the diagnosis of conditions like hyperthyroidism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Papillary Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary Carcinoma is a common form of thyroid cancer known for its generally favorable prognosis and treatment outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Papillary carcinoma is the most common type of thyroid cancer, typically growing slowly and having a favorable prognosis if caught early.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular Carcinoma is a type of thyroid cancer that may behave more aggressively than Papillary Carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Follicular carcinoma is the second most common type of thyroid cancer, also characterized by differentiated growth patterns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medullary Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Medullary Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medullary thyroid cancer can be inherited and requires distinct management due to its unique etiology and familial tendencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anaplastic thyroid cancer is a rare, aggressive form of thyroid cancer that primarily affects older adults and has a poor prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Levothyroxine">
      <data key="d0">Levothyroxine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Levothyroxine is a commonly prescribed medication for thyroid hormone replacement therapy, essential for patients post-thyroidectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Levothyroxine is a commonly used medication for thyroid hormone replacement therapy, typically taken once daily on an empty stomach.&lt;SEP&gt;Levothyroxine is a medication used to manage thyroid hormone levels, particularly in patients with thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Levothyroxine is a synthetic thyroid hormone used to treat hypothyroidism and is recommended for patients after thyroid surgery to maintain normal TSH levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Total Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d0">Total Thyroidectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Total Thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure involving the complete removal of the thyroid gland, often used in cases of thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy involves the complete removal of the thyroid gland and is indicated for invasive follicular or oncocytic tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure involving the complete removal of the thyroid gland, often performed on patients with thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the entire thyroid gland, often required for cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobectomy">
      <data key="d0">Lobectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobectomy is a surgical procedure that involves removing a portion of the thyroid and may be suitable for less invasive thyroid cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure that removes one lobe of the thyroid gland and may be sufficient for certain low-risk thyroid cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure that removes part of the thyroid gland containing cancerous tissue, applicable in certain cases of thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure where a portion of the thyroid is removed, generally utilized in the treatment of thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperthyroidism">
      <data key="d0">Hyperthyroidism</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperthyroidism is a condition characterized by excessive thyroid hormone production, leading to various symptoms and health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine Uptake Test">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine Uptake Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A radioactive iodine uptake test helps assess thyroid function and can aid in diagnosing thyroid disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid cancer includes several subtypes and requires various treatment options such as surgery and targeted therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is a cancer that affects the thyroid gland and may require treatments such as radioactive iodine therapy for high-risk cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is a type of cancer that affects the thyroid gland and can sometimes be treated through various targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the thyroid gland, with several subtypes and treatment approaches based on diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer refers to cancer that begins in the thyroid gland, which is responsible for producing hormones that regulate metabolism and other functions in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neck Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Neck Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Neck dissection is a surgical procedure to remove lymph nodes in the neck that may be affected by thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive iodine therapy utilizes iodine-131 to target and kill thyroid cancer cells with the goal of preserving healthy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d0">Endocrinologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An endocrinologist is a medical doctor who specializes in the treatment of hormonal issues, often part of a cancer treatment team.&lt;SEP&gt;An endocrinologist is a medical specialist involved in treating hormone-related disorders, including thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-Body Radioiodine Scan">
      <data key="d0">Whole-Body Radioiodine Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A whole-body radioiodine scan is performed to detect residual thyroid tissue and hidden thyroid cancer after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH)">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TSH is a hormone produced by the pituitary gland, which stimulates the thyroid; its levels are essential in monitoring thyroid health after surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH) is a hormone produced by the pituitary gland that regulates the thyroid gland's metabolic activity and is closely monitored during thyroid hormone replacement therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d0">Thyroidectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure to remove all or part of the thyroid gland, commonly performed for thyroid cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyrotoxicosis">
      <data key="d0">Thyrotoxicosis</data>
      <data key="d1">condition</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyrotoxicosis is a condition caused by excessive thyroid hormones in the body, resulting in various health issues and requiring careful management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Calcium and Vitamin D Supplements">
      <data key="d0">Calcium and Vitamin D Supplements</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Calcium and vitamin D supplements are often recommended to strengthen bones, particularly during thyroid hormone replacement therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nuclear Medicine Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Nuclear Medicine Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nuclear medicine specialist is a physician who specializes in using radioactive materials for diagnosis and treatment of diseases like thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyrotropin Alfa (Thyrogen)">
      <data key="d0">Thyrotropin Alfa (Thyrogen)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyrotropin alfa (Thyrogen) is a synthetic form of TSH used to stimulate iodine uptake for patients undergoing radioactive iodine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Adolescent and Young Adult Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Adolescent and Young Adult Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Guidelines for Patients is an organization that provides resources and guidelines for cancer patients, specifically addressing the needs of adolescents and young adults.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. FDA">
      <data key="d0">U.S. FDA</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. FDA is a government agency responsible for regulating food, drugs, and medical devices, including the approval of new cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) oversees the approval and regulation of new cancer treatments, ensuring their safety and efficacy before they are made available to the public.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic therapy">
      <data key="d0">Systemic therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic therapy involves treatments that affect the entire body, including chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation therapy is a treatment that employs high-energy radiation such as x-rays or gamma rays to eliminate cancer cells and reduce tumors, often delivered in sessions.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy uses high-energy radiation to target and kill cancer cells and reduce tumors, often involving multiple treatment sessions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical trials">
      <data key="d0">Clinical trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical trials are structured research studies designed to evaluate new cancer treatments for safety and effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Targeted therapy">
      <data key="d0">Targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Targeted therapy involves treatments that specifically attack cancer cells without affecting normal cells, often used for thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External radiation">
      <data key="d0">External radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">External radiation refers to delivering radiation from outside the body to the cancerous area, often discussed with patients regarding their treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Side effects of therapy">
      <data key="d0">Side effects of therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Side effects of therapy include various health responses to cancer treatments, such as fatigue, nausea, and changes in taste, that can occur both during and long after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility preservation">
      <data key="d0">Fertility preservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility preservation involves strategies discussed with patients regarding options to maintain reproductive capabilities after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical trial">
      <data key="d0">Clinical trial</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical trials are medical research studies designed to evaluate new treatments and compare them against standard therapy options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation therapy side effects">
      <data key="d0">Radiation therapy side effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation therapy side effects can result in symptoms like skin rash, dry mouth, and fatigue, which may occur shortly after or long after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 1 trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 1 trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 1 trials are the initial stage of clinical trials that assess the safety and side effects of a new drug or treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 2 trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 2 trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 2 trials focus on investigating how well a new treatment works against a specific type of cancer after safety is established in Phase 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 3 trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 3 trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 3 trials compare the new treatment against standard treatments to determine its effectiveness across larger patient populations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 4 trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 4 trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 4 trials study the long-term effects and benefits of a drug or treatment once it has already been approved by the FDA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM)">
      <data key="d0">U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NLM offers extensive resources, including information on clinical trials and cancer research to the public.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine provides access to clinical trials through its database clinicaltrials.gov.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine provides access to health information and clinical trials through its database.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Information Service (CIS)">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Information Service (CIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The CIS offers support and information regarding cancer and clinical trials for patients and families.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Information Service offers support and information related to cancer treatment, including clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma (PTC)">
      <data key="d0">Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma (PTC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma is the most common type of thyroid cancer, known for its relatively slow growth and good treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI)">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive Iodine Therapy is a treatment used after thyroid surgery to eliminate remaining cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive iodine therapy is a treatment option used to eliminate remaining thyroid cancer cells post-surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transportation Costs">
      <data key="d0">Transportation Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transportation Costs refer to any expenses incurred by a patient traveling to participate in a clinical trial.&lt;SEP&gt;Transportation costs refer to expenses incurred by participants traveling to and from trial locations, often not covered by sponsors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Childcare Costs">
      <data key="d0">Childcare Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Childcare Costs are expenses that may arise due to additional appointments required for participation in a clinical trial.&lt;SEP&gt;Childcare costs refer to expenses related to taking care of children while participants attend clinical trial appointments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nuclear Medicine Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Nuclear Medicine Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nuclear medicine doctor is a physician specializing in the use of radioactive materials to diagnose and treat diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAI Therapy">
      <data key="d0">RAI Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RAI Therapy (Radioactive Iodine Therapy) is a treatment option for thyroid cancer to eliminate remaining cancer cells after surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy is a treatment for thyroid cancer that involves taking radioactive iodine to destroy thyroid tissue and cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy is necessary for patients who have had their thyroid gland removed to regulate hormone levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid hormone replacement therapy is a treatment option for patients who have had their thyroid gland removed or partially removed, aimed at maintaining normal hormone levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Oncocytic Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncocytic Carcinoma is a rare form of thyroid cancer that may have different treatment and prognosis considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncocytic carcinoma, formerly known as Hürthle cell carcinoma, is an uncommon type of thyroid cancer that is also noted for its aggressive behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular thyroid carcinoma is the second most common type of thyroid cancer, characterized by its potential to invade blood vessels and spread to lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Completion Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d0">Completion Thyroidectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Completion thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure recommended to remove the remainder of the thyroid gland if high-risk features are detected after an initial surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIFTP">
      <data key="d0">NIFTP</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NIFTP refers to noninvasive follicular thyroid neoplasm with papillary-like nuclear features, considered a low-risk tumor that requires no further treatment after surgical removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neck Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Neck Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Neck ultrasound is a diagnostic imaging procedure used to monitor for recurrence of thyroid cancer after treatment or surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lenvatinib">
      <data key="d0">Lenvatinib</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Lenvatinib (Lenvima) is a targeted therapy medication recommended for cancer that cannot be removed with surgery and is progressing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sorafenib">
      <data key="d0">Sorafenib</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Sorafenib (Nexavar) is a targeted therapy medication for thyroid cancer that cannot be surgically removed and is worsening over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cabozantinib">
      <data key="d0">Cabozantinib</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Cabozantinib (Cabometyx) is a treatment option recommended for thyroid cancer that has progressed after prior therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade carcinoma is a type of cancer that is aggressive and shows a high likelihood of spreading and recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The thyroid is a gland in the neck that produces hormones regulating various biological functions, and it is critical in the development of thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-Up Care">
      <data key="d0">Follow-Up Care</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up care is the ongoing management and support given to patients after initial treatment to monitor recovery and detect any recurrence of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Follow-up care refers to the ongoing medical supervision of patients after treatment to monitor for cancer recurrence or complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing and Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Testing and Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing and surveillance are ongoing assessments conducted to ensure that any signs of cancer recurrence are detected early.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Short-Term Follow-Up">
      <data key="d0">Short-Term Follow-Up</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Short-term follow-up involves monitoring patients closely after significant treatments like total thyroidectomy to detect any immediate recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Long-Term Monitoring">
      <data key="d0">Long-Term Monitoring</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Long-term monitoring consists of regular evaluations to ensure there are no signs of cancer returning years after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Procedures">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging procedures like CT or MRI are employed to visualize structures within the body for assessing the cancer's status or detect recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroglobulin (Tg)">
      <data key="d0">Thyroglobulin (Tg)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroglobulin is a protein produced by the thyroid gland; its levels in the blood are used to monitor for thyroid cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anti-Thyroglobulin Antibodies (Tg ab)">
      <data key="d0">Anti-Thyroglobulin Antibodies (Tg ab)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Anti-thyroglobulin antibodies are produced by the immune system that may interfere with thyroglobulin testing in thyroid cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NED (No Evidence of Disease)">
      <data key="d0">NED (No Evidence of Disease)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NED refers to the state in which no signs of cancer disease are present during follow-up examinations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT Imaging">
      <data key="d0">CT Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">CT imaging is a diagnostic imaging tool used to visualize the internal structures of the body, assisting neurosurgeons in targeting tumors for biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;CT imaging is a diagnostic tool that uses X-rays to create detailed images of organs and structures inside the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Imaging">
      <data key="d0">MRI Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI imaging is a medical imaging technique that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed images of organs and tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI imaging provides high-resolution images of brain structures and is utilized in conjunction with biopsies to accurately locate tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients®">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients®</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) provides guidelines and resources for cancer patients, including information on fertility preservation and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® provide trusted cancer care recommendations based on the latest research and practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bone cancer">
      <data key="d0">bone cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone cancer includes a group of cancers starting in the bone or cartilage, with primary bone cancers classified as sarcomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="sarcomas">
      <data key="d0">sarcomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomas are a rare group of cancers that originate from connective tissues, including bone and cartilage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="osteosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">osteosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteosarcoma is a type of bone sarcoma that begins in the cells forming bone, commonly occurring in the areas near growth plates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ewing sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Ewing sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ewing sarcoma typically arises in the bone marrow and is most frequently found in long bones or flat bones like the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chondrosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">chondrosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chondrosarcoma originates in cartilage cells and is recognized as a type of primary bone cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chordoma">
      <data key="d0">chordoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chordoma is a type of bone cancer that develops from notochordal cells, primarily found in the spine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="biomarker testing">
      <data key="d0">biomarker testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker testing is recommended to determine eligibility for targeted therapies or immunotherapies in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Bone Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone tumors are abnormal growths that can be either malignant (cancerous) or benign (noncancerous) and can occur within the bone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Primary Bone Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary bone cancer originates within the bone itself, as opposed to metastatic cancers that spread to the bone from other locations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Bone Malignancies">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Bone Malignancies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondary bone malignancies are cancers that start elsewhere in the body and metastasize to the bone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bone Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone cancer is a rare form of cancer that affects the bones and has various types, including osteosarcoma, chondrosarcoma, and Ewing sarcoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone cancer refers to any malignancy that originates in the bone tissue and can manifest in various forms, with specific staging criteria based on tumor, node, and metastasis (TNM) classifications.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone cancer refers to malignant tumors that arise in bone tissue and require specific treatment strategies and diagnostic approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone cancer refers to malignant tumors that develop in the bone tissue, requiring specialized testing and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chondrosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Chondrosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chondrosarcoma is a type of bone cancer that arises from cartilage cells and may be associated with genetic mutations such as IDH1.&lt;SEP&gt;Chondrosarcoma is a type of bone cancer that typically requires surgical treatment to remove the tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chordoma">
      <data key="d0">Chordoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chordoma is a type of bone cancer that may involve treatment options such as surgery and radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ewing Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Ewing Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ewing sarcoma is a bone cancer that often begins with systemic therapy as part of its treatment plan.&lt;SEP&gt;Ewing sarcoma is a type of cancer that occurs in bones or soft tissues and is associated with specific chromosomal translocations.&lt;SEP&gt;Ewing sarcoma is a type of cancer that primarily affects bones or soft tissues and is characterized by specific chromosomal translocations during cell division.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Giant Cell Tumor of Bone">
      <data key="d0">Giant Cell Tumor of Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Giant Cell Tumor of Bone (GCTB) is generally not cancerous but requires aggressive treatment to prevent bone damage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Osteosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Osteosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteosarcoma is a common type of bone cancer that typically involves multiple treatment therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone">
      <data key="d0">Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone is a critical part of the human skeletal system and can be a site for metastasis in liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone is a vital structure in the human body, known for being light and strong, with the ability to regrow after injury.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pregnancy">
      <data key="d0">Pregnancy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pregnancy is a significant concern during cancer treatment as it can affect both maternal health and the effectiveness of the therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP)">
      <data key="d0">Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ALP is an enzyme whose high levels in the blood can indicate issues such as bone tumors or liver disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Type(s)">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Type(s)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment Type(s) involves various modalities such as surgery, chemotherapy, or radiation that are utilized based on the specific diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Dose">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Dose</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment Dose refers to the specific amount of medication or therapy administered to a patient, essential to ensure effective treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Length">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Length</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment Length describes the duration of treatment, which is important for both efficacy and patient management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H/dMMR mutation">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H/dMMR mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H/dMMR mutation refers to a genetic change that results in microsatellite instability, often associated with certain types of cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite instability (MSI) or deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) mutations are related to alterations that affect DNA repair mechanisms in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Claustrophobia">
      <data key="d0">Claustrophobia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Claustrophobia is an anxiety disorder characterized by an irrational fear of enclosed spaces, which may affect patients undergoing an MRI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tracer">
      <data key="d0">Tracer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tracer is a radioactive substance used in PET scans to help visualize areas of high metabolic activity, often indicating cancerous growths.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Core Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core needle biopsy is a medical procedure that uses a hollow needle to extract tissue from a tumor for diagnostic evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Core Needle Biopsy is a procedure used to obtain a sample of bone tissue to diagnose conditions like bone cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Core needle biopsy is a specific type of biopsy that uses a hollow needle to collect tissue samples for testing, often guided by imaging techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical biopsy is an operation to remove tissue from a tumor for further analysis and is typically performed in an operating room setting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Test">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow test is a procedure to examine the bone marrow's health and function, often used to diagnose blood disorders and certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IDH Genes">
      <data key="d0">IDH Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IDH genes are a group of genes involved in cellular energy metabolism, and mutations in these genes can be associated with certain types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)">
      <data key="d0">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Pembrolizumab, commonly known as Keytruda, is an immunotherapy drug used to treat specific cancers, including those with MSI-H/dMMR mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nivolumab (Opdivo)">
      <data key="d0">Nivolumab (Opdivo)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Nivolumab, branded as Opdivo, is an immunotherapy drug utilized for treating various cancers, particularly those with high tumor mutational burden (TMB-H).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ipilimumab (Yervoy)">
      <data key="d0">Ipilimumab (Yervoy)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Ipilimumab, also known as Yervoy, is an immunotherapy drug used in the treatment of cancers with specific mutations, including TMB-H.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IDH1 mutation">
      <data key="d0">IDH1 mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IDH1 mutation refers to a specific genetic alteration that can be targeted with therapies like ivosidenib for specific cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ivosidenib (Tibsovo)">
      <data key="d0">Ivosidenib (Tibsovo)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Ivosidenib, known as Tibsovo, is a targeted therapy used for patients with IDH1 mutations, particularly in chondrosarcoma cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next-generation sequencing (NGS)">
      <data key="d0">Next-generation sequencing (NGS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Next-generation sequencing is a modern DNA sequencing technology that enables detailed analysis of genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polymerase chain reaction (PCR)">
      <data key="d0">Polymerase chain reaction (PCR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Polymerase chain reaction is a laboratory technique used to amplify DNA sequences, making it easier to analyze genetic information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deficient Mismatch Repair (dMMR)">
      <data key="d0">Deficient Mismatch Repair (dMMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) refers to the failure of the DNA mismatch repair system, often associated with certain cancers like colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next-generation Sequencing (NGS)">
      <data key="d0">Next-generation Sequencing (NGS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Next-generation sequencing (NGS) is an advanced technology for rapidly sequencing DNA to detect mutations in a patient's tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IDH1 Mutation">
      <data key="d0">IDH1 Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IDH1 Mutation is a specific alteration in the IDH1 gene that can be targeted with specific therapies like ivosidenib in certain cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spine">
      <data key="d0">Spine</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The spine is a vital structure of the human body composed of a flexible column of bones known as vertebrae, allowing for mobility and protection of the spinal cord.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skeletal Maturity">
      <data key="d0">Skeletal Maturity</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Skeletal maturity refers to the stage of growth where bones mature and stop elongating, which can result in visible changes in bone structure such as physeal scars.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diaphysis">
      <data key="d0">Diaphysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diaphysis refers to the middle region of a long bone, primarily responsible for the bone's length and structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physis">
      <data key="d0">Physis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Physis is also known as the growth plate, which is composed of cartilage and plays a crucial role in bone growth and development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Node Metastasis (TNM) Scores">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Node Metastasis (TNM) Scores</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TNM scores are used in oncology to describe the anatomical extent of cancers, detailing the size of the tumor, involvement of lymph nodes, and presence of metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Structure">
      <data key="d0">Bone Structure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone structure refers to the physical composition and organization of bone tissues, including its different regions and types of cells, which are vital for overall skeletal health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skeletal Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d0">Skeletal Cancer Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skeletal cancer staging is a method used to assess the extent of bone cancer and involves determining the size and spread of tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Liver Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic Liver Cancer refers to cancer that has spread to the liver from other parts of the body and is discussed in terms of liver cancer's metastasis patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic liver cancer refers to liver cancer that originates elsewhere in the body and spreads to the liver, commonly from cancers like colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatocellular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Hepatocellular Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC) is a type of liver cancer that is targeted for screening and diagnosis in at-risk populations.&lt;SEP&gt;Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is the most common type of primary liver cancer in adults, originating in the liver cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fibrolamellar Hepatocellular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Fibrolamellar Hepatocellular Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fibrolamellar hepatocellular carcinoma (FLHC) is a rare subtype of HCC that typically affects younger individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma is the second most common type of primary liver cancer, affecting the bile ducts within the liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gallbladder">
      <data key="d0">Gallbladder</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The gallbladder is a small organ located under the liver that stores bile, aiding in the digestion of fats.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatobiliary System">
      <data key="d0">Hepatobiliary System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The hepatobiliary system consists of the liver, gallbladder, and bile ducts, collectively forming part of the digestive system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spinal Column">
      <data key="d0">Spinal Column</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The spinal column provides support for the body and protects the spinal cord, and is also susceptible to metastasis from various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Grade">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer grade is a classification system that describes how abnormal tumor cells appear under a microscope, impacting the prognosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Liver Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver cancer can either originate in the liver or spread to the liver from other locations, significantly affecting liver function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatic Artery">
      <data key="d0">Hepatic Artery</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The hepatic artery supplies oxygen-rich blood to the liver, playing a vital role in its function and health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Portal Vein">
      <data key="d0">Portal Vein</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The portal vein carries blood from the digestive organs to the liver, essential for nutrient processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatic Veins">
      <data key="d0">Hepatic Veins</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatic veins are responsible for draining blood from the liver, returning it to the heart for circulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inferior Vena Cava">
      <data key="d0">Inferior Vena Cava</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The inferior vena cava is a large vein that carries deoxygenated blood from the lower body to the heart, receiving blood from the hepatic veins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis refers to inflammation of the liver, which can be a significant risk factor for developing liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)">
      <data key="d0">Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC) is the most common type of liver cancer, which this text discusses extensively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Hepatitis B">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Hepatitis B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Hepatitis B is a viral infection that can increase the risk of developing liver cancer, making screening essential.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Hepatitis B is identified as a significant risk factor for developing liver cancer, and it requires screening for early detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Hepatitis C">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Hepatitis C</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Hepatitis C is another important risk factor for liver cancer, associated with liver damage and cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cirrhosis">
      <data key="d0">Cirrhosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cirrhosis is a chronic liver disease characterized by the replacement of healthy liver tissue with scar tissue, increasing the risk of liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cirrhosis is a late-stage scarring of the liver often resulting from chronic liver diseases, significantly raising the risk of liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cirrhosis is a late-stage scarring of the liver resulting from chronic liver diseases, significantly raising the risk of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) test">
      <data key="d0">Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) test is used in screening for primary liver cancer, indicating elevated levels may signal cancer presence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound (US)">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound (US)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultrasound (US) is a diagnostic tool employed in the screening process for liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Cancer Screening Program">
      <data key="d0">Liver Cancer Screening Program</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A liver cancer screening program is designed for individuals with risk factors like cirrhosis or chronic hepatitis B to detect liver cancer early.&lt;SEP&gt;The Liver Cancer Screening Program is designed for individuals at higher risk for liver cancer, helping with early detection through regular tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) Test">
      <data key="d0">Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) Test is used in screening for primary liver cancer, indicating elevated levels may signal cancer presence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Keywords for Support">
      <data key="d0">Keywords for Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Keywords for support include asking questions, bringing someone to appointments, and keeping copies of test results to enhance patient engagement and understanding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach">
      <data key="d0">Stomach</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The stomach is a muscular organ that mixes food with digestive juices and is essential for the initial stages of digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;The stomach is an organ within the digestive system that mixes food with enzymes and acids to break it down into chyme.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Intestine">
      <data key="d0">Small Intestine</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The small intestine is a long, coiled tube where most digestion and nutrient absorption occur after chyme exits the stomach.&lt;SEP&gt;The small intestine is the section of the gastrointestinal tract where the majority of digestion and nutrient absorption takes place.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chyme">
      <data key="d0">Chyme</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chyme is a semi-fluid mass of partially digested food that passes from the stomach to the small intestine for further digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Chyme is the semi-fluid mass of partially digested food produced in the stomach that moves into the small intestine for further digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pylorus">
      <data key="d0">Pylorus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The pylorus is the opening from the stomach into the small intestine, regulated by a valve that controls the passage of chyme.&lt;SEP&gt;The pylorus is the valve at the lower end of the stomach that controls the passage of chyme into the small intestine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cardia">
      <data key="d0">Cardia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The cardia is the region of the stomach that connects to the esophagus, facilitating the entry of food into the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fundus">
      <data key="d0">Fundus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The fundus is the upper portion of the stomach that stores undigested food and gas released during digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antrum">
      <data key="d0">Antrum</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The antrum is the lower part of the stomach where food is mixed with gastric juices before entering the small intestine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mucosa">
      <data key="d0">Mucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The mucosa is the innermost layer of the stomach wall, responsible for secreting gastric juices that aid in digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Submucosa">
      <data key="d0">Submucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The submucosa is a layer of connective tissue containing blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves that supports the mucosa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscularis">
      <data key="d0">Muscularis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The muscularis is a layer of muscle in the stomach wall that aids in churning food to mix it with digestive juices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Serosa">
      <data key="d0">Serosa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The serosa is the outermost layer of the stomach that provides structure and support, allowing for smooth movement against other organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stomach cancer refers to cancers that form in the lining of the stomach, often developing slowly over many years, with various stages based on how far they have spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-stage Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-stage Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage stomach cancer has not grown beyond the first layer of the stomach wall and is characterized by small tumors that are often undetected.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locoregional Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Locoregional Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locoregional stomach cancer has invaded other layers of the stomach wall and/or spread to nearby lymph nodes or organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic stomach cancer refers to cancers that have spread to distant parts of the body, such as the liver and lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H. pylori">
      <data key="d0">H. pylori</data>
      <data key="d1">organism</data>
      <data key="d2">H. pylori is a bacterium linked to stomach issues, including the development of stomach cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family Medical History">
      <data key="d0">Family Medical History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family medical history compiles information on health issues, helping to identify potential genetic risks for diseases such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach Mucosa">
      <data key="d0">Stomach Mucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The stomach mucosa is the innermost layer of the stomach wall where stomach cancers often begin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutritional Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Nutritional Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Nutritional assessment evaluates a patient's dietary intake to ensure proper nutrition, especially important during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Glioma is a type of brain tumor that develops from glial cells and can disrupt brain function, often requiring complex treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma is a type of brain tumor that develops from glial cells, affecting brain function and requiring complex treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma is a type of brain tumor that originates from glial cells and is the most common malignant brain tumor in adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma is a type of tumor that occurs in the brain and spinal cord and is classified into various types based on cell characteristics and growth rates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Astrocytomas">
      <data key="d0">Astrocytomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Astrocytomas are a type of glioma that resemble star-shaped glial cells called astrocytes, which maintain chemical communications between nerve cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oligodendrogliomas">
      <data key="d0">Oligodendrogliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oligodendrogliomas are a type of glioma made from oligodendrocytes that protect electrical signaling in nerve cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glioblastomas">
      <data key="d0">Glioblastomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Glioblastomas are the most aggressive and deadly form of glioma, developed from precursor cells similar to astrocytes and oligodendrocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor treatment includes options like surgery, radiation therapy, and chemotherapy to manage gliomas and slow their progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain">
      <data key="d0">Brain</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The brain is the central organ where gliomas develop, affecting its functions and leading to various symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glial Cells">
      <data key="d0">Glial Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Glial cells are a type of support cell in the brain that can develop into gliomas, playing a vital role in maintaining the environment for neurons.&lt;SEP&gt;Glial cells are supportive cells in the brain involved in the formation of gliomas when they grow abnormally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Central Nervous System">
      <data key="d0">Central Nervous System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The central nervous system consists of the brain and spinal cord, where gliomas can originate and disrupt normal functioning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Brain Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Brain Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Malignant brain tumors, like gliomas, are cancerous growths that can invade surrounding brain tissue and disrupt normal functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of gliomas include headaches, seizures, nausea, and changes in personality or mood, which help in diagnosing the condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Cells">
      <data key="d0">Brain Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain cells, specifically glial cells, support nerve function in the brain and spinal cord and are the source of gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor symptoms include headaches, seizures, fatigue, and cognitive issues, which arise as gliomas affect brain function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Headaches">
      <data key="d0">Headaches</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Headaches are a common symptom associated with gliomas, often being the first indication of a tumor presence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seizures">
      <data key="d0">Seizures</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Seizures can be an early sign of gliomas, particularly in low-grade tumors, indicating a disruption in normal brain activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fatigue">
      <data key="d0">Fatigue</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Fatigue is another frequent symptom of gliomas, affecting the patient's overall energy levels and quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nausea and Vomiting">
      <data key="d0">Nausea and Vomiting</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Nausea and vomiting can occur as a result of increased pressure in the brain from a glioma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cognitive Problems">
      <data key="d0">Cognitive Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Cognitive problems can manifest as confusion, memory loss, and difficulties in thinking and speech, related to glioma growth in the brain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gliomas are a type of brain tumor that can lead to specific neurological symptoms such as vision problems and changes in personality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Memory Loss">
      <data key="d0">Memory Loss</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Memory loss refers to cognitive dysfunction that can happen in individuals with brain tumors affecting their thinking ability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Personality Changes">
      <data key="d0">Personality Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Personality changes may occur in patients with brain tumors located in areas of the brain that regulate mood and behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle Weakness">
      <data key="d0">Muscle Weakness</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle weakness is a potential symptom experienced by individuals with brain tumors, often manifesting on one side of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vision Problems">
      <data key="d0">Vision Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vision problems can include blurred or double vision, which may indicate the presence of a brain tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure refers to medical interventions, such as biopsies or resections, performed to treat brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Open Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Open Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An open biopsy is a surgical approach used to remove tissue for diagnosis, typically used when tumors are accessible.&lt;SEP&gt;An open biopsy, or craniotomy, is a surgical procedure where a neurosurgeon removes a section of the skull to access and extract tumor tissue for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuropathologist">
      <data key="d0">Neuropathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neuropathologist is a specialist who analyzes tissue samples under a microscope to determine the nature and characteristics of brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Astrocytoma IDH-mutant grade 2">
      <data key="d0">Astrocytoma IDH-mutant grade 2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Astrocytoma IDH-mutant grade 2 is a specific classification of glioma characterized by its cell type (astrocytoma), genetic mutation (IDH-mutant), and a lower growth rate (grade 2).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Frame or Markers">
      <data key="d0">Frame or Markers</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">Frames or tiny markers are used during stereotactic biopsy to ensure precision in targeting the tumor within the brain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laboratory Testing">
      <data key="d0">Laboratory Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Laboratory testing involves analyzing tissue samples to determine the presence of cancer cells and classifying the tumor type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A malignant tumor is a cancerous growth that has the potential to invade surrounding tissues and spread to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Benign Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Benign Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A benign tumor is a non-cancerous growth that typically does not spread and is often less aggressive in nature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glioma Classification">
      <data key="d0">Glioma Classification</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Glioma classification refers to the categorization of gliomas based on their histology, grade, and biomarkers, guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 1 Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Grade 1 Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 1 gliomas are slow-growing tumors that are usually benign, more common in children, and can often be treated with surgery alone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 2 Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Grade 2 Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 2 gliomas consist of abnormal cells that can invade normal tissue but grow slowly and may not recur for many years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 3 Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Grade 3 Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 3 gliomas contain cells that increase in number rapidly and invade nearby tissue, requiring additional therapies after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 4 Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Grade 4 Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 4 gliomas, also known as glioblastomas, are highly aggressive tumors that spread quickly and require intensive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a slow-growing type of blood cancer affecting lymphocytes and may require treatment only as the disease progresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a slow-growing type of blood cancer affecting lymphocytes that may require treatment only as the disease progresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the accumulation of abnormal lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Lymphocytic Lymphoma (SLL)">
      <data key="d0">Small Lymphocytic Lymphoma (SLL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small Lymphocytic Lymphoma (SLL) is another name for CLL when the cancerous cells are primarily found in lymph nodes rather than in blood and bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph System">
      <data key="d0">Lymph System</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The lymph system is a network of tissues and organs that help rid the body of toxins, waste, and other unwanted materials, playing a key role in the immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymph system, consisting of lymph nodes, vessels, spleen, and thymus, is critical in immune function and fighting infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Richter Transformation">
      <data key="d0">Richter Transformation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Richter Transformation is a rare complication of CLL that can lead to a more aggressive type of cancer, specifically diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL).&lt;SEP&gt;Richter Transformation is a rare progression of CLL that leads to the development of a more aggressive type of lymphoma, specifically diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL).&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation is a condition discussed in the context of lymphoma, providing insights into its implications for patients diagnosed with it.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation is a condition that indicates a specific type of progression in lymphoma, often requiring further reading for those affected.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation refers to a rare progression of CLL to a more aggressive form of lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopathologist">
      <data key="d0">Hematopathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A hematopathologist is a medical professional specializing in the diagnosis of blood disorders through laboratory analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A hematopathologist is a medical specialist trained to diagnose diseases related to blood and immune cells, including various forms of leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A hematopathologist is a specialist in diagnosing blood-related diseases, including different types of leukemia and lymphoma, by examining blood and bone marrow samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Cell Proteins">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Cell Proteins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer cell proteins, such as CD20 and CD200, are markers that can help in identifying and classifying lymphomas and leukemias.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anemia">
      <data key="d0">Anemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anemia is a condition often associated with CLL, characterized by a deficiency in the number of red blood cells, leading to fatigue and weakness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cell Count">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cell Count</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A white blood cell count measures the number of white blood cells in the blood, which is a crucial parameter in diagnosing and managing CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IV Infusions">
      <data key="d0">IV Infusions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IV infusions are a method of delivering medication directly into the bloodstream, commonly used in the treatment of CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Cell Surface">
      <data key="d0">B Cell Surface</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The B Cell Surface is a biological structure where various tests related to blood cells are conducted to diagnose diseases like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Smear">
      <data key="d0">Blood Smear</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood smear is a diagnostic laboratory procedure that involves examining a drop of blood under a microscope to identify abnormal cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLL">
      <data key="d0">CLL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CLL, or Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia, is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the overproduction of lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer characterized by the accumulation of abnormal white blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Tests for CLL">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Tests for CLL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical tests for diagnosing CLL include various blood tests, biopsies, and imaging studies to assess disease presence and progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CD20">
      <data key="d0">CD20</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CD20 is a protein that is typically expressed on the surface of B cells and is often targeted in treatments for B cell malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopathology">
      <data key="d0">Hematopathology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopathology is the branch of pathology that involves the study and diagnosis of blood diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microscope">
      <data key="d0">Microscope</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A microscope is a tool used in laboratories to examine blood samples for signs of disease and abnormalities in cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lab Tests">
      <data key="d0">Lab Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lab tests are various diagnostic procedures conducted to analyze blood and tissue samples to identify diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Status Score">
      <data key="d0">Performance Status Score</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance status score measures a patient's functional status and ability to perform daily activities, impacting treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neck, Armpit, Belly, Groin">
      <data key="d0">Neck, Armpit, Belly, Groin</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Body areas including the neck, armpit, belly, and groin are examined by oncologists for signs of lymph node swelling in cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beta-2 Microglobulin">
      <data key="d0">Beta-2 Microglobulin</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">Beta-2 microglobulin is a small protein found on most cells that can indicate CLL progression when present in high levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic CT">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Diagnostic CT scan is a medical imaging procedure that provides detailed images of the body's tissues and is often used to evaluate the extent of diseases, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Watch and Wait">
      <data key="d0">Watch and Wait</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Watch and wait is an approach in cancer management where treatment is deferred until symptoms develop or become severe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Kidney Disease">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Kidney Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic kidney disease is a long-term condition in which the kidneys do not work effectively, impacting overall health and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Cells">
      <data key="d0">B Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">B cells are a type of lymphocyte responsible for producing antibodies as part of the immune response and are involved in the pathophysiology of CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker Tests">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker tests analyze specific biological markers used to inform treatment plans for conditions such as CLL, often involving blood or bone marrow samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fluorescence in situ Hybridization (FISH)">
      <data key="d0">Fluorescence in situ Hybridization (FISH)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">FISH is a lab test that looks for specific chromosomal abnormalities in cells, providing information about conditions like CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA Sequencing">
      <data key="d0">DNA Sequencing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA sequencing examines genetic material to identify mutations in specific genes associated with cancer, which can affect treatment decisions in CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Consultation with a CLL Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Consultation with a CLL Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A consultation with a CLL specialist includes discussing treatment options, diagnostics, and management strategies for CLL patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Counselor">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Counselor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A fertility counselor advises cancer patients on options for preserving fertility prior to undergoing treatments that may affect reproductive health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">Patient Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient guidelines refer to resources provided for patients regarding treatment options and supportive care concerning cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthy Babies">
      <data key="d0">Healthy Babies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Healthy babies refer to newborns that are born without complications despite the mother undergoing cancer treatment, highlighting the advances in supportive care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Survival Rates">
      <data key="d0">Survival Rates</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Survival rates are statistics indicating the percentage of patients who survive a certain cancer type for specific periods post-diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous B-cell Lymphoma (CBCL)">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous B-cell Lymphoma (CBCL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CBCL is a form of lymphoma that originates in B cells and appears on the skin as palpable nodules, often confused with benign skin conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;CBCL is a type of lymphoma that arises from B cells and appears as nodules under the skin, often mistaken for small pimples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Cutaneous Lymphoma (PCL)">
      <data key="d0">Primary Cutaneous Lymphoma (PCL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PCL is a category of lymphomas that primarily affect the skin and are not classified as traditional skin cancers, distinguished by their unique characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;PCL is a rare group of NHLs located in the skin that is distinct from traditional skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Cutaneous Marginal Zone Lymphoma (PCMZL)">
      <data key="d0">Primary Cutaneous Marginal Zone Lymphoma (PCMZL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PCMZL is a subtype of PCL, also known as PCMZ lymphoproliferative disorder, characterized by slow growth.&lt;SEP&gt;PCMZL is a subtype of primary cutaneous lymphoma that typically features slow-growing, indolent behavior and is associated with marginal zones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Cutaneous Follicle Center Lymphoma (PCFCL)">
      <data key="d0">Primary Cutaneous Follicle Center Lymphoma (PCFCL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PCFCL is another subtype of PCL that arises from follicle center cells and is characterized by specific growth patterns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Cutaneous Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary Cutaneous Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This lymphoma type is a more aggressive form of PCL and is often referred to by its specific subtype classification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for CBCL">
      <data key="d0">Testing for CBCL</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for CBCL encompasses various diagnostic methods, including blood tests, imaging, and biopsies, to accurately diagnose the condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cutaneous B-cell lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">cutaneous B-cell lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) is a type of cancer that affects the skin, requiring accurate testing for diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that primarily affects the skin, characterized by the malignant growth of B cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="blood and tissue tests">
      <data key="d0">blood and tissue tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood and tissue tests are diagnostic procedures used to detect the presence of diseases, including cancers like cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood and tissue tests are essential diagnostics used to assess the presence and specifics of diseases like CBCL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="imaging studies">
      <data key="d0">imaging studies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging studies include techniques such as CT scans and MRIs that provide visual evidence of cancer spread and assist in diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging studies provide visual representations of the body and assist in diagnosing conditions such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="primary care physician">
      <data key="d0">primary care physician</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary care physician (PCP) coordinates patient care and manages overall health, being a crucial part of the healthcare team.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary care physician (PCP) is a healthcare provider responsible for coordinating overall patient care and health management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bladder cancer">
      <data key="d0">bladder cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bladder cancer is a common cancer in adults, where abnormal cells grow in the bladder lining and can spread if not monitored.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer occurs when abnormal cells grow uncontrollably in the bladder, resulting in tumors and requiring ongoing management and follow-ups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="urothelium">
      <data key="d0">urothelium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The urothelium is the inner lining of the bladder and urinary tract, where most bladder cancers originate.&lt;SEP&gt;Urothelium is the tissue lining the bladder and urinary tract which is the origin of most bladder cancers, including transitional cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="squamous cell carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">squamous cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell carcinoma is a less common type of bladder cancer that can occur if urothelial cells become cancerous.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a rare type of bladder cancer that originates from squamous cells, representing a different pathophysiology compared to urothelial carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="small cell carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">small cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell carcinoma is a rare type of aggressive bladder cancer that comes from neuroendocrine cells in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Small cell carcinoma is one of the less frequent types of bladder cancer, originating from small round cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="online patient portals">
      <data key="d0">online patient portals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Online patient portals allow patients to access their medical records, lab results, and communicate with their healthcare team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="friends and family support system">
      <data key="d0">friends and family support system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Having a support system of friends and family is vital for patients during diagnosis and treatment to provide emotional and practical assistance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="health history">
      <data key="d0">health history</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health history is a comprehensive account of a patient's past medical issues and treatments that informs current medical decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="physical exam">
      <data key="d0">physical exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A physical exam is an assessment performed by healthcare providers to evaluate a patient's physical condition, often crucial for diagnosing conditions like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bladder cancer is a disease characterized by abnormal cell growth in the bladder, which can be detected and monitored through various tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer affecting the urinary bladder, requiring collaborative diagnosis and treatment from a multidisciplinary team of specialists.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that affects the bladder and is characterized by the presence of malignant cells in the bladder lining.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that affects the bladder and may require various treatment approaches, including surgery and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that affects the bladder, necessitating various treatment approaches including chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that affects the cells of the bladder and includes various forms such as urothelial carcinoma and muscle-invasive bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that affects the urinary bladder, requiring a multidisciplinary approach for diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the bladder lining and can require various treatments, including surgery and therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the bladder's lining and requires specific treatment approaches such as intravesical therapy and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that occurs when abnormal cells grow uncontrollably in the inner layer of the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the bladder, with various stages that indicate how deeply the cancer has invaded surrounding tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is characterized by the presence of cancerous cells in the bladder, with varying stages and grades impacting growth and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelium">
      <data key="d0">Urothelium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The urothelium is the inner lining of the bladder and other parts of the urinary tract, where bladder cancer can originate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of bladder cancer include hematuria (blood in urine), frequent urination, urgency to urinate, and painful urination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnosis of Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Diagnosis of Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnosis of bladder cancer involves tests such as cystoscopy, urinalysis, and biopsy to confirm the presence of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Factors for Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Risk Factors for Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk factors for bladder cancer include smoking, age, family history, and exposure to certain chemicals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment of Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Treatment of Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment options for bladder cancer vary based on the stage of the disease and can include surgery, chemotherapy, and immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell carcinoma is a rare and aggressive type of bladder cancer that originates from neuroendocrine cells in the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematuria">
      <data key="d0">Hematuria</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematuria, or blood in urine, is a key symptom of bladder cancer that can lead to further diagnostic testing for the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinalysis">
      <data key="d0">Urinalysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinalysis is a laboratory test of urine that helps detect abnormalities such as blood, which can indicate bladder cancer or other conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a form of bladder cancer that has not spread into the muscle layer of the bladder wall.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer refers to the initial stages of bladder cancer where the cancer hasn't yet infiltrated muscle layers, often treated with less aggressive therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle-invasive bladder cancer indicates a more severe form that penetrates the muscle wall of the bladder, typically requiring aggressive treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a more advanced form of the disease that has penetrated the bladder's muscle layer.&lt;SEP&gt;Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a severe form of bladder cancer that may necessitate radical surgical intervention like cystectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic bladder cancer is the stage where cancer cells have spread to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes or organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tests for Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Tests for Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Various tests are employed to diagnose bladder cancer and monitor its progression, including blood tests, urine tests, and imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urine Tests">
      <data key="d0">Urine Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">tests</data>
      <data key="d2">Urine tests help in diagnosing bladder cancer by detecting cancerous cells or markers in the urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary care doctor oversees the general medical needs and health of the patient while helping facilitate communication with cancer specialists during treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care doctor provides general medical care and supports patients in communicating their treatment preferences to their cancer care team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urologic Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Urologic Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A urologic oncologist is a surgeon who specializes in diagnosing and treating urinary tract cancers, typically using surgical methods and intravesical therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologic oncologist specializes in the surgical and therapeutic management of cancers related to the urinary system and male reproductive organs, including bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharmacist">
      <data key="d0">Pharmacist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The pharmacist prepares and dispenses medications, including chemotherapy agents, and provides medication management for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The pharmacist prepares medications, including chemotherapy drugs, and provides treatment advice to ensure patient safety and efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anesthesiologist">
      <data key="d0">Anesthesiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An anesthesiologist administers anesthesia and manages pain for patients undergoing medical procedures related to cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;An anesthesiologist administers anesthesia during procedures to ensure patient comfort and pain management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutritionists">
      <data key="d0">Nutritionists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Nutritionists assist cancer patients in understanding dietary needs and make recommendations for nutrition during treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutritionists guide patients on dietary choices that are most beneficial for their specific medical conditions, such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Psychologists and Psychiatrists">
      <data key="d0">Psychologists and Psychiatrists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mental health experts who assist patients with emotional and psychiatric issues related to cancer treatment, including anxiety and depression.&lt;SEP&gt;Mental health professionals who provide support for psychological well-being during cancer treatment and assist with coping strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counselors">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counselors</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic counselors offer advice and information regarding hereditary cancer risks and genetic testing related to cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic counselors provide insights on how family history may influence cancer treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Test">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A laboratory test that measures liver enzymes to evaluate liver health and function, included in various medical assessments including CMPs.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver function test assesses enzymes produced by the liver to determine its health status, often included in a CMP.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Function Test">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Function Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A test that assesses waste products in the blood to determine if the kidneys are functioning normally, often included in the CMP.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidney function test evaluates waste products in the blood to ensure the kidneys are functioning properly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urine Cytology">
      <data key="d0">Urine Cytology</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic test that analyzes urine samples to check for cancer or precancerous cells in the urinary tract.&lt;SEP&gt;Urine cytology is a laboratory test analyzing urine for cancer or precancerous cells, often conducted if blood is present in urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Residents and Fellows">
      <data key="d0">Residents and Fellows</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Residents and fellows are doctors in training who may be specializing in oncology or urology and provide care under supervision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ureteroscopy">
      <data key="d0">Ureteroscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Ureteroscopy is a similar procedure to cystoscopy but is conducted to examine the upper parts of the urinary tract, including the ureters and inner kidneys.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging encompasses various medical visualization techniques, such as CT scans and MRIs, used to monitor and evaluate cancer progression or recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging refers to the recommended scanning methods such as CT scans and MRIs to monitor for recurrence of anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging refers to various techniques like x-rays and MRIs used to create detailed pictures of the body's internal structures, helping to visualize tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Solution">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Solution</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A contrast solution is a substance used in imaging procedures to improve the visibility of internal structures, enhancing the clarity of diagnostic images.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urogram">
      <data key="d0">Urogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A urogram is a medical imaging procedure that helps visualize the lining of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder to diagnose issues such as blockages or blood in the urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Renal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal ultrasound is a non-invasive test that displays real-time images of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder, commonly used for examination during pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pyelogram">
      <data key="d0">Pyelogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A pyelogram is an x-ray procedure using a contrast solution to diagnose obstructions in the urinary system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDKN2A">
      <data key="d0">CDKN2A</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">CDKN2A is an important gene associated with an increased risk for bladder cancer when mutated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FGFR3">
      <data key="d0">FGFR3</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">FGFR3 is another gene linked to an elevated risk of bladder cancer due to genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Studies">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Studies</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging studies include techniques like CT scans and MRIs used to visualize internal body structures for diagnostic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inherited Cancer Risk">
      <data key="d0">Inherited Cancer Risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inherited cancer risk refers to the likelihood of developing cancer due to genetic mutations passed down from parents to children.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking Cessation">
      <data key="d0">Smoking Cessation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking cessation refers to the process of quitting smoking and the various supports available to individuals trying to quit.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-Ray">
      <data key="d0">X-Ray</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An X-ray is a form of radiation used in medical imaging to visualize the inside of the body, crucial for diagnosing various conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 bladder cancer is the earliest stage, affecting only the inner lining of the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1 bladder cancer has grown through the inner lining into the connective tissue but has not invaded the muscle layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 bladder cancer has invaded the muscle layer of the bladder wall without spreading beyond this layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3 bladder cancer shows invasion through the bladder wall into the fatty layer and may spread to nearby lymph nodes or organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 bladder cancer has spread to lymph nodes or organs far from the bladder, indicating advanced cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Grade Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Low Grade Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low grade cancer refers to cancer cells that grow slowly and are less likely to spread quickly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Grade Cancer">
      <data key="d0">High Grade Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High grade cancer signifies aggressive cancer cells that are fast-growing and more likely to spread rapidly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1 bladder cancer has penetrated the inner lining and reached the connective tissue but has not invaded the muscle yet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 bladder cancer has invaded the muscle layer of the bladder wall, indicating a progression in cancer severity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3 bladder cancer has grown through the bladder wall and may have spread into the fatty tissue around the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoma in Situ (CIS)">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoma in Situ (CIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoma in Situ represents a flat patch of cancer cells that has not invaded deeper layers, classified as part of Stage 0.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Grading">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Grading</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer grading is a system used to evaluate the aggressiveness of the cancer cells based on their appearance compared to normal cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Grade (LG)">
      <data key="d0">Low Grade (LG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low Grade cancer refers to cancer cells that appear more like normal cells and tend to grow slowly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Grade (HG)">
      <data key="d0">High Grade (HG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High Grade cancer consists of more abnormal-looking cells that are aggressive and likely to spread rapidly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial cancer is a type of bladder cancer that originates in the urothelial cells of the bladder's inner lining, which can vary in growth rate and subtype.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor (TURBT)">
      <data key="d0">Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor (TURBT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">TURBT is a surgical procedure aimed at removing bladder tumors and providing diagnostic information regarding bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;TURBT is a surgical procedure used to examine and remove tumors from the bladder wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravesical Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravesical Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravesical therapy involves administering a liquid medication directly into the bladder to target and destroy cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy involves the direct administration of cancer medicine into the bladder through a catheter for treating bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy is a method of treating bladder cancer by directly delivering medication into the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy is a treatment typically performed in conjunction with TURBT to target bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy refers to the administration of medication directly into the bladder, primarily used to treat bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder Cancer Treatment Options">
      <data key="d0">Bladder Cancer Treatment Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bladder cancer treatment options include surgery, intravesical therapy, systemic therapy, radiation therapy, and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder Cancer Stages">
      <data key="d0">Bladder Cancer Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bladder cancer stages describe the extent of cancer spread and depth of invasion into the bladder wall, influencing treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Grade Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Low Grade Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low grade bladder cancer is characterized by slow growth rates, indicating a less aggressive form of the disease that typically requires less intensive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Grade Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">High Grade Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High grade bladder cancer is identified by faster growth rates and requires more aggressive treatment options due to its potential to spread rapidly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder Cancer Subtypes">
      <data key="d0">Bladder Cancer Subtypes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bladder cancer subtypes refer to the different classifications of bladder cancer based on cellular characteristics, which can affect treatment and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glandular Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Glandular Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The glandular subtype of bladder cancer arises from glandular cells in the bladder and may have distinct treatment needs compared to typical urothelial carcinomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The squamous cell subtype of bladder cancer develops from squamous cells and is less common but can exhibit different behavioral patterns than other subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Micropapillary Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Micropapillary Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The micropapillary subtype is a rare variant of bladder cancer known for its aggressive nature and complex behaviors, impacting treatment choices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plasmacytoid Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Plasmacytoid Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The plasmacytoid subtype of bladder cancer is associated with distinctive cellular characteristics and may require specialty treatment approaches due to its aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomatoid Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomatoid Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The sarcomatoid subtype is a rare form of bladder cancer that can behave aggressively and is important to identify for proper treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nested Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Nested Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The nested subtype of bladder cancer has unique histological features and may influence treatment strategies based on its specific characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoepithelioma-like Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoepithelioma-like Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This rare subtype is characterized by a particular cellular structure and may carry different treatment implications compared to more common forms of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microcystic and Tubular Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Microcystic and Tubular Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The microcystic and tubular subtype of bladder cancer is noted for its unique appearance under the microscope, influencing diagnosis and treatment decision-making.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TURBT">
      <data key="d0">TURBT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">TURBT, or Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor, is a surgical procedure used to remove visible tumors from the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT) is a procedure performed to examine and remove tumors present on the bladder wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cystectomy">
      <data key="d0">Cystectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cystectomy refers to surgical procedures involving the removal of the bladder, which can be either radical or partial, depending on the extent of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Cystectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Cystectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radical cystectomy is a common surgical treatment for muscle-invasive bladder cancer involving the removal of the bladder and nearby structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Radical cystectomy is the most common procedure for muscle-invasive bladder cancer, entailing the removal of the entire bladder and possibly nearby organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Diversion">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Diversion</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinary diversion involves surgical procedures that create a new passage for urine to exit the body after bladder removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Urinary diversion is a surgical procedure that provides an alternative way for urine to exit the body after removal of the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neobladder">
      <data key="d0">Neobladder</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A neobladder is a type of urinary diversion that replaces the bladder with a segment of the small intestine, allowing urine to exit through the urethra.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Continent Cutaneous Pouch">
      <data key="d0">Continent Cutaneous Pouch</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">The continent cutaneous pouch (Indiana pouch) is a type of urinary diversion that creates a pouch to store urine using a section of the intestine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ostomy Bag">
      <data key="d0">Ostomy Bag</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">An ostomy bag is a medical device used to collect urine after surgical procedures like urinary diversion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgeons are medical professionals who perform surgical operations, including procedures necessary for treating bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indiana University">
      <data key="d0">Indiana University</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Indiana University is a prestigious academic institution where surgeons developed the Indiana pouch for urinary diversion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravesical BCG Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravesical BCG Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravesical BCG Therapy is a treatment involving the instillation of a weakened bacterium into the bladder to stimulate the immune response against bladder cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravesical Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravesical Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravesical Chemotherapy is a method of delivering chemotherapy directly into the bladder to kill cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical chemotherapy targets the bladder specifically, treating local cancer cells without systemic effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCG">
      <data key="d0">BCG</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BCG is a vaccine derived from a weakened strain of Mycobacterium bovis, used in the treatment of bladder cancer to prevent recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indiana Pouch">
      <data key="d0">Indiana Pouch</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Indiana Pouch is a type of urinary diversion solution developed by surgeons, involving the creation of a pouch from a segment of the small intestine that connects to a stoma on the abdomen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Catheter">
      <data key="d0">Catheter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A catheter is a flexible tube inserted into the stoma or urethra to allow for the drainage of urine from the Indiana Pouch.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG)">
      <data key="d0">Bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BCG is a type of intravesical therapy that utilizes a weakened strain of bacteria to stimulate the immune system to combat bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TURBT (Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor)">
      <data key="d0">TURBT (Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">TURBT is a surgical procedure used to remove tumors from the bladder and is often the first step in bladder cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic chemotherapy treats the entire body by injecting drugs into the bloodstream, impacting all cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dose-Dense Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Dose-Dense Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dose-dense chemotherapy is a method that reduces the time intervals between chemotherapy doses to expedite treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trimodal Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Trimodal Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Trimodal therapy combines TURBT, radiation, and chemotherapy as a bladder-preserving treatment strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;Trimodal therapy refers to combining TURBT, radiation, and chemotherapy to treat bladder cancer while preserving the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Opdivo">
      <data key="d0">Opdivo</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Opdivo is another checkpoint inhibitor used in immunotherapy for treating bladder cancer, aiming to improve immune effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bavencio">
      <data key="d0">Bavencio</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bavencio is an immunotherapy drug that targets specific proteins to enhance the immune response against bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tecentriq">
      <data key="d0">Tecentriq</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tecentriq is an immunotherapy drug used for bladder cancer treatment that helps the immune system recognize and attack cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Padcev">
      <data key="d0">Padcev</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Padcev is a targeted therapy used for specific bladder cancer types that targets the Nectin-4 protein on cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Balversa">
      <data key="d0">Balversa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Balversa is a targeted therapy for bladder cancer patients with specific FGFR3 gene mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trodelvy">
      <data key="d0">Trodelvy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Trodelvy is a targeted treatment that attaches to Trop-2 proteins on cancer cells, used in bladder cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDA">
      <data key="d0">FDA</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The FDA (Food and Drug Administration) is a federal agency responsible for approving drugs and treatments based on trial results.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA (Food and Drug Administration) is a government agency responsible for approving new medications and treatments based on clinical trial results.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA (U.S. Food and Drug Administration) is responsible for approving drugs and treatments based on clinical trial outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 2 Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 2 Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 2 trials study the effectiveness of a drug or treatment against a specific type of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 3 Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 3 Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 3 trials test a drug against a standard treatment to evaluate its effectiveness before approval.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 4 Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 4 Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 4 trials study the long-term safety and benefits of a treatment that has already been approved by the FDA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eligibility Criteria">
      <data key="d0">Eligibility Criteria</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Eligibility criteria are the rules defining who can participate in a clinical trial, focusing on factors like age and health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Placebos">
      <data key="d0">Placebos</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Placebos are inactive treatments used in clinical trials to compare the effects of a new drug against an inactive version.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Side Effects of Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Side Effects of Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Side effects of treatment encompass various negative reactions patients may experience as a consequence of their cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder Removal Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Bladder Removal Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Bladder removal surgery is a surgical procedure which may involve various side effects to be considered during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Incontinence">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Incontinence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinary incontinence is a condition characterized by the involuntary loss of urine, often a concern for individuals post-surgery or during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Floor Exercises">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Floor Exercises</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic floor exercises are physical activities aimed at strengthening the pelvic muscles, which can help in regaining control over urinary function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Short- and Long-Term Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Short- and Long-Term Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Short- and long-term side effects refer to the various complications that can arise from treatments for conditions like bladder cancer, necessitating management strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage of Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage of Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The stage of bladder cancer indicates the extent of the disease, which is crucial for determining treatment options and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the tissues of the anus and can be caused by human papillomavirus (HPV) infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the tissues of the anus, often linked to human papillomavirus (HPV) infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the tissues of the anus, often requiring various treatment approaches such as surgery and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the anal canal or the perianal region, with squamous cell carcinoma being the most common form.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the anal region, with varying stages and treatment approaches depending on the differentiation and progression of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that starts in the anal canal or the perianal region, with common characteristics and treatment protocols depending on its stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is defined as a rare type of cancer occurring in the anal canal or surrounding skin, primarily affecting squamous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digestive Health Experts">
      <data key="d0">Digestive Health Experts</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Digestive health experts are specialized medical professionals who focus on conditions affecting the digestive system, including cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Resolution Anoscopy">
      <data key="d0">High-Resolution Anoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">High-resolution anoscopy (HRA) is a procedure that uses magnification to examine the anal canal for signs of abnormal cells and anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SISCCA">
      <data key="d0">SISCCA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SISCCA refers to a specific classification of anal cancer which may not require aggressive treatment compared to more advanced stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Superficially invasive squamous cell carcinoma (SISCCA) is an early form of anal cancer identified through biopsies or incidental findings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal surgeons are medical specialists who diagnose and treat conditions affecting the colon, rectum, and anus, including anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal surgeons are specialists trained to evaluate and treat anal cancer and its precursors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human papillomavirus">
      <data key="d0">Human papillomavirus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Human papillomavirus (HPV) is a virus with more than 100 strains, some of which are high-risk and associated with various cancers including anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV16">
      <data key="d0">HPV16</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV16 is one of the high-risk strains of human papillomavirus that is notably linked to the development of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Dysplasia">
      <data key="d0">Anal Dysplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal dysplasia refers to the presence of abnormal cells in the anal area, which may precede the development of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (LSIL)">
      <data key="d0">Low-grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (LSIL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LSIL is a form of anal dysplasia characterized by mild abnormalities in the cells of the anus and may indicate risk for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (HSIL)">
      <data key="d0">High-grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (HSIL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HSIL is a more severe form of anal dysplasia indicating a higher risk of progression to anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Intraepithelial Neoplasia (AIN)">
      <data key="d0">Anal Intraepithelial Neoplasia (AIN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AIN represents abnormal growth of cells in the anal region that could lead to anal cancer if untreated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Vaccine">
      <data key="d0">HPV Vaccine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The HPV vaccine is designed to protect against the most harmful strains of HPV, potentially reducing the risk of anal cancer among vaccinated individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectal Bleeding">
      <data key="d0">Rectal Bleeding</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Rectal bleeding is a common symptom of anal cancer and is characterized by blood in stool or on toilet paper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Itching">
      <data key="d0">Anal Itching</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal itching is another symptom that may be associated with anal cancer, often leading individuals to seek medical evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Exam (DRE)">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Exam (DRE)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The digital rectal exam (DRE) is a physical examination used to detect abnormalities in the anal and rectal areas, important for diagnosing anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Resolution Anoscopy (HRA)">
      <data key="d0">High-Resolution Anoscopy (HRA)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">High-resolution anoscopy (HRA) is a detailed examination of the anal canal with magnification to identify abnormal tissues indicative of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) is a type of cancer affecting the anal canal, identified through biopsy and often requiring comprehensive testing and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologic Exam">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologic Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Gynecologic Exam includes procedures to screen for cervical cancer and HPV, which are important for women at risk of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor, Node, Metastasis (TNM) system">
      <data key="d0">Tumor, Node, Metastasis (TNM) system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM system is a standardized method used for staging cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HRA">
      <data key="d0">HRA</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">HRA refers to health risk assessments that may be pertinent in evaluating the health status of individuals, particularly regarding cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excisional Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Excisional Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An excisional biopsy is a surgical procedure where a lymph node is completely removed to test for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computed Tomography (CT)">
      <data key="d0">Computed Tomography (CT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">CT is an imaging technique that uses X-rays to create detailed pictures of the body, especially for staging cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Michael">
      <data key="d0">Michael</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Michael is a survivor of anal cancer who offers support and mentorship to others undergoing similar experiences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 anal cancer includes abnormal cells that may develop into cancer and is also known as carcinoma in situ.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage I Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage I Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage I anal cancer is characterized by tumors that are 2 cm or smaller, with no spread to lymph nodes or distant sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage II Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage II Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage II anal cancer is separated into stages IIA and IIB, defined by tumor size, where tumors are larger than 2 cm but have not metastasized.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage III Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage III Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage III anal cancer has invaded nearby lymph nodes or organs and is divided into substages based on invasion and size.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage IV Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage IV Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage IV anal cancer refers to the metastatic stage where the cancer has spread beyond the pelvic area to distant organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrocautery">
      <data key="d0">Electrocautery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrocautery is a medical procedure utilized in the treatment of anal cancer where electrical currents are used to destroy abnormal tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oocyte Cryopreservation">
      <data key="d0">Oocyte Cryopreservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oocyte cryopreservation is a method of preserving female fertility by freezing unfertilized eggs for future use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Semen Cryopreservation">
      <data key="d0">Semen Cryopreservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Semen cryopreservation involves freezing sperm to preserve male fertility for potential future use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMRT">
      <data key="d0">IMRT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IMRT is an advanced radiation therapy technique that limits radiation exposure to healthy tissues surrounding a tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT) is an advanced radiation therapy technique preferred for treating anal cancer, allowing targeted radiation to the tumor while minimizing damage to surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic anal cancer refers to anal cancer that has spread beyond the pelvis, requiring specialized treatment options including surgery and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic anal cancer refers to anal cancer that has spread beyond the pelvis, requiring systemic therapy for treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Metastatic Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non-Metastatic Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-metastatic anal cancer covers stages I, II, and III anal cancer where the disease has not spread beyond the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV Infection">
      <data key="d0">HIV Infection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV infection is a significant risk factor for developing anal cancer, particularly in individuals with compromised immune systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunosuppressant Use">
      <data key="d0">Immunosuppressant Use</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The use of immunosuppressants is linked to an increased risk of anal cancer, as they weaken the immune response to infections like HPV.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Autoimmune Disorders">
      <data key="d0">Autoimmune Disorders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Certain autoimmune disorders are considered risk factors for the development of anal cancer due to their effects on the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Sex">
      <data key="d0">Anal Sex</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A history of anal sex is identified as a risk factor for anal cancer, potentially due to increased exposure to HPV and other infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sexually Transmitted Infection">
      <data key="d0">Sexually Transmitted Infection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Past infections with various sexually transmitted infections can increase the risk of anal cancer by affecting the anal region's tissue health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DRE">
      <data key="d0">DRE</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Digital rectal examination (DRE) is a recommended testing procedure for evaluating anal health after a cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Anoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anoscopy is a medical procedure used for examining the anal canal, often used in the diagnosis and management of anal concerns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Screening for cervical cancer is advised in individuals at risk of anal cancer, especially those with high-risk HPV.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mitomycin">
      <data key="d0">Mitomycin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mitomycin is a chemotherapy drug often combined with radiation therapy to enhance treatment efficacy in anal cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Capecitabine">
      <data key="d0">Capecitabine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy drug taken during radiation therapy for anal cancer treatment, aimed at inhibiting cancer cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-FU">
      <data key="d0">5-FU</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">5-FU (5-fluorouracil) is a chemotherapeutic agent that is used in treatment regimens for anal cancer alongside radiation or surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;5-FU (Fluorouracil) is a chemotherapy drug used in various combinations to treat anal cancer and is administered via infusion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Perianal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Perianal cancer refers to tumors located around the anus, primarily treated through chemoradiation for stages I to III, which are nonmetastatic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Excision">
      <data key="d0">Local Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local excision is a surgical procedure that involves removing a tumor along with a small margin of adjacent tissue, recommended for early-stage perianal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravenous Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravenous Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A method of delivering medication directly into the bloodstream via a vein, commonly used for chemotherapy and hydration in patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Oral Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A method of administering medication by mouth, allowing patients to take certain chemotherapy drugs at home.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-FU Infusion">
      <data key="d0">5-FU Infusion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The delivery of the chemotherapy drug 5-FU through a continuous infusion system, allowing sustained exposure to the medication during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neutropenia">
      <data key="d0">Neutropenia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A condition characterized by low white blood cell counts, increasing the risk of infections, often a side effect of chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hand-Foot Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Hand-Foot Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A common side effect of certain chemotherapy drugs, including capecitabine, causing redness, swelling, and pain on palms and soles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Margins">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Margins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The area of healthy tissue surrounding a tumor that is removed during local excision to ensure complete cancer removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Upper Chest Port">
      <data key="d0">Upper Chest Port</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical device implanted to allow easy access to veins for administering chemotherapy treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PICC Line">
      <data key="d0">PICC Line</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Peripherally Inserted Central Catheter used for long-term intravenous therapy, allowing continuous access to the bloodstream.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Janet">
      <data key="d0">Janet</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Janet is an anal cancer survivor who advocates for acceptance and awareness regarding anal cancer, emphasizing that it can affect anyone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Examination (DRE)">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Examination (DRE)</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">DRE is a follow-up procedure recommended to check for any recurrence of anal cancer after treatment, typically conducted every 3 to 6 months for 5 years post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perianal Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Perianal Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Perianal cancers refer to malignancies that occur in the tissue surrounding the anus, categorized by their differentiation levels and treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Margin">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Margin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical margin indicates the normal tissue that is removed along with a tumor during surgery, which is crucial for determining further treatment based on its condition post-surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Re-excision">
      <data key="d0">Re-excision</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Re-excision is a preferred treatment for cases where the surgical margins are less than 1 cm or contain cancer cells, indicating a need for additional surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Regimens">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Regimens</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy regimens such as Mitomycin and capecitabine or 5-FU and cisplatin are used alongside radiation therapy for treating anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Remission">
      <data key="d0">Complete Remission</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Complete remission represents a state in which no signs of cancer are detectable in the body after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persistent Disease">
      <data key="d0">Persistent Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Persistent disease occurs when cancer remains in the body but shows no signs of growth or spread after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progressive Disease">
      <data key="d0">Progressive Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progressive disease refers to a situation where cancer continues to grow or spread post-treatment, necessitating further intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Exams">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node exams check for cancer spread and are recommended as part of the follow-up process for anal cancer survivors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Groin Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Groin Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Groin dissection involves removing lymph nodes in the groin area during cancer surgery if cancer is present in those nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Groin dissection is a surgical procedure to remove lymph nodes in the groin, often performed when cancer metastasizes to these nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-based Chemotherapy Regimens">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-based Chemotherapy Regimens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-based chemotherapy regimens are standard treatment options for metastatic anal cancer, often employing drugs like carboplatin and paclitaxel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Side Effects of Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Side Effects of Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Common side effects of chemotherapy include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, hair loss, and mouth sores.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects of chemotherapy include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, hair loss, and sores, which can impact patient quality of life during treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects of chemotherapy include various adverse reactions such as nausea, fatigue, and hair loss, affecting patients during and after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle Flap Reconstruction">
      <data key="d0">Muscle Flap Reconstruction</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle flap reconstruction is a surgical technique used to close wounds after cancer surgery, utilizing tissue from another part of the body to aid healing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified DCF Regimen">
      <data key="d0">Modified DCF Regimen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Modified DCF Regimen consists of docetaxel, cisplatin, and fluorouracil for chemotherapy treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palliative Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Palliative Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Palliative radiation therapy is used to alleviate symptoms of cancer and can help limit tumor size.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thom">
      <data key="d0">Thom</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Thom is an anal cancer survivor who offers personal insights and advice regarding patient advocacy and treatment experiences.&lt;SEP&gt;Thom is an anal cancer survivor who shares personal experiences and motivations for coping and moving forward after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Routine IV Hydration">
      <data key="d0">Routine IV Hydration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Routine IV hydration is part of the care protocol to help reduce the nephrotoxic effects of platinum-based chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melissa">
      <data key="d0">Melissa</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Melissa is an anal cancer survivor who shares her experience and emphasizes the importance of finding a knowledgeable oncology team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="anal cancer">
      <data key="d0">anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal cancer is a rare type of cancer that can occur in the anal canal, requiring specialized treatment and care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chemoradiation">
      <data key="d0">chemoradiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemoradiation is a standard treatment involving chemotherapy and radiation recommended for most non-metastatic anal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a treatment that combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy to combat cancer, commonly used for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy utilizes medications to treat cancer by targeting and killing cancer cells throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DRE (Digital Rectal Exam)">
      <data key="d0">DRE (Digital Rectal Exam)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DRE is a diagnostic procedure used to examine the anal and rectal area for abnormalities, part of routine follow-up care for anal cancer survivors.&lt;SEP&gt;DRE is a medical examination used to check for abnormalities in the anal area and is part of routine follow-up care for anal cancer survivors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="anal cancer survivor">
      <data key="d0">anal cancer survivor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An anal cancer survivor is a person who has successfully undergone treatment for anal cancer and continues with follow-up care and survivorship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="oncology team">
      <data key="d0">oncology team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An oncology team consists of medical professionals specialized in cancer treatment, providing care and support for cancer patients during and after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mitomycin">
      <data key="d0">mitomycin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mitomycin is a chemotherapy drug used in conjunction with other treatments for anal cancer, playing a critical role in patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="capecitabine">
      <data key="d0">capecitabine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy medication used to treat various cancers, including anal cancer, often combined with radiation.</data>
