    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Medical Imaging Techniques">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Medical imaging techniques are critical for diagnosing and staging rectal cancer, enabling appropriate treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnosis, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Endoscopic Ultrasound (EUS)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endoscopic ultrasound is utilized to determine the staging and spread of rectal cancer, guiding surgical decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic assessment, treatment guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Endoscopic Mucosal Resection (EMR)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">EMR is a preferred technique for treating early-stage rectal cancers identified during screening procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment, minimally invasive</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="T2 Tumor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Rectal cancer staging includes classifications like T2 tumors, indicating the level of invasion, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is a standard treatment option for rectal cancer post-surgery to eliminate any lingering malignant cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, post-operative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adenomas" target="Malignant Polyp">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adenomas can progress to malignant polyps if not monitored and managed correctly, indicating the potential for cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor development, cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Malignant Polyp" target="Polyp">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Malignant polyps indicate that cancer has begun to develop within a polyp, necessitating immediate removal and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer identification, urgency</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Resection" target="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical resection may sometimes lead to the need for transabdominal surgery if the tumor is found to be larger or more invasive during procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surveillance" target="Early-stage Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surveillance is essential for early-stage cervical cancer patients to monitor for recurrence after treatment has been completed.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-treatment care, monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surveillance" target="Complete Remission">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surveillance is conducted to ensure complete remission is maintained and to catch any potential recurrence early.</data>
      <data key="d6">monitoring recovery, cancer follow-up</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surveillance" target="Persistent Disease">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surveillance is essential after treatment to monitor for persistent disease, assessing whether cancer is stable or needs action.</data>
      <data key="d6">monitoring treatment efficacy, follow-up care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surveillance" target="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surveillance is a key part of post-treatment care for anal cancer patients, focusing on early detection of any return or progression of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-treatment monitoring, cancer care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transabdominal Surgery" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transabdominal surgery involves removing lymph nodes for comprehensive cancer staging and treatment evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, cancer assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Low Anterior Resection (LAR)" target="Colostomy">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">After LAR, a colostomy may be required if reconnection of the bowel is impossible or delayed for healing.&lt;SEP&gt;LAR can lead to a colostomy if the bowel does not heal correctly or cannot be reconnected.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical outcome, bowel management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colostomy" target="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">APR always results in a permanent colostomy due to the removal of the anus and rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;APR necessitates a colostomy as the procedure involves a complete removal of the anus and rectum, leading to permanent changes in waste elimination.&lt;SEP&gt;APR usually necessitates a colostomy for stool diversion after the removal of the anus, a significant aspect of post-surgical care.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical outcome, patient care&lt;SEP&gt;surgical outcome, permanent intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colostomy" target="Colectomy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colostomy may be necessary after a colectomy if it is unsafe to reconnect the colon's healthy ends.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy may be performed following a colectomy when reattaching the colon is not feasible.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, follow-up treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colostomy" target="Rectum Removal">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">After rectum removal surgery, a colostomy is often required to redirect stool to the exterior of the body through a stoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical follow-up, waste management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colostomy" target="anal cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A colostomy may be necessary for patients whose anal cancer does not respond to initial treatments, establishing a treatment pathway.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgery, treatment option</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)" target="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">APR is a surgical intervention employed when anal cancer recurs, illustrating a more radical approach to treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection is a treatment option for anal cancer, particularly for cases that are metastatic or do not respond to other treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgery treatment, cancer management&lt;SEP&gt;surgical intervention, cancer recurrence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)" target="Metastatic Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abdominoperineal resection is a surgical approach specifically used to treat metastatic anal cancer when it does not respond to other initial treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgery treatment, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy often precedes surgery like APR, influencing healing and treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment sequence, surgical impact</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidney Cancer" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">In kidney cancer, assessment of lymph nodes is crucial for determining the stage and appropriate treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer spread, treatment importance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidney Cancer" target="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Renal cell carcinoma (RCC) is a specific subtype of kidney cancer, representing the predominant form of this disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, main category</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidney Cancer" target="Wilms Tumor">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Wilms tumor is a specific pediatric form of kidney cancer that is often discussed within the broader spectrum of kidney cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Wilms tumor is a specific type of kidney cancer that occurs predominantly in children, making it a subset of kidney cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d6">pediatric oncology, cancer subtype&lt;SEP&gt;pediatric oncology, cancer type</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidney Cancer" target="Carcinomas">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The majority of kidney cancers are classified as carcinomas, originating from the cells that line the kidney surfaces.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidney Cancer" target="Urothelial Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urothelial carcinoma can be confused with kidney cancers but starts in the ureter and renal pelvis, differentiating it from RCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, differentiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidney Cancer" target="Irinotecan">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Irinotecan is used in treatment regimens for kidney cancer, highlighting its relevance in cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy, treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Staging" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The surgical staging process includes analyzing lymph nodes removed during transabdominal surgery to determine cancer spread and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer assessment, staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Staging" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical staging is performed to accurately assess the extent of cervical cancer and tailor treatment recommendations accordingly.</data>
      <data key="d6">evaluation process, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-clear cell RCC is a subtype of renal cell carcinoma, reflecting its classification within kidney cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-clear cell RCC is classified under renal cell carcinoma (RCC), representing a different subtype within the RCC category.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype classification, cancer types&lt;SEP&gt;subtype classification, kidney cancer</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Clear Cell RCC (ccRCC)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clear Cell RCC is a subtype of Renal Cell Carcinoma, indicating it is a specific type of this broader cancer category.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC (nccRCC)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-Clear Cell RCC represents another category within the Renal Cell Carcinoma umbrella, encompassing multiple subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Sarcamoid RCC (sRCC)">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sarcomatoid features can appear in any type of RCC, including Clear Cell and Non-Clear Cell varieties, complicating treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, cancer behavior</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Metastasis">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The process of metastasis describes how RCC can spread from the kidneys to distant sites in the body.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer spread, progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Kidney">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">RCC specifically originates in the cells lining the renal tubules of the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ, cancer source</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Urinary System">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The kidneys are central to the urinary system, responsible for filtering blood and producing urine as waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidneys are integral parts of the urinary system, responsible for filtering blood and producing urine.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ function, system role&lt;SEP&gt;organ system, function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Renal Capsule">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The renal capsule surrounds the kidneys, providing protection and structural support to these vital organs.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical structure, support</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Renal Artery">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The renal artery supplies blood to the kidneys for filtration, playing a crucial role in the urinary system.</data>
      <data key="d6">blood supply, kidney function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Renal Vein">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The renal vein takes filtered blood away from the kidneys, returning it to the heart for circulation.</data>
      <data key="d6">blood circulation, excretion process</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Gerota’s Fascia">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Gerota’s fascia provides protective structural support around the kidneys, contributing to their anatomical integrity.</data>
      <data key="d6">support, anatomical structure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Ureteroscopy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ureteroscopy may also involve examining the area around the kidneys for any signs of tumors or other abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ examination, medical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Renal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Renal ultrasound examines the kidneys and provides real-time images that aid in diagnosing kidney-related ailments.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic tool, organ assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Bladder Cancer" target="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Soft Tissue Sarcoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both NCCN guidelines provide treatment recommendations for different types of cancers to assist patients in managing their health.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer management, patient guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Clear Cell RCC" target="Renal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-Clear Cell RCC is a major category of renal cancer, reflecting the diversity within tumor subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Clear Cell RCC" target="Papillary RCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Papillary RCC represents a significant subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC, showcasing specific tumor morphology.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Clear Cell RCC" target="Translocation RCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Translocation RCC is classified as a rare variant within the Non-Clear Cell RCC group, typically showing aggressive behavior.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urine" target="Renal Tubules">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Renal tubules are responsible for processing blood and producing urine by filtering out waste products.</data>
      <data key="d6">filtration process, waste removal</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urine" target="Bladder">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urine collected in the renal pelvis is transported to the bladder for temporary storage before excretion.</data>
      <data key="d6">storage, excretion process</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Artery" target="Kidney">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The renal artery provides blood supply to the kidney, critical for maintaining its filtration and excretory functions.</data>
      <data key="d6">vascular supply, organ function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Vein" target="Kidney">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The renal vein is essential for the drainage of blood from the kidney, directly impacting the organ's health and function.</data>
      <data key="d6">vascular supply, organ function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ureters" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder cancer can affect the ureters as it impacts the urinary tract, potentially causing symptoms and necessitating treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">impact on urinary tract, cancer progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ureters" target="Ureteroscopy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ureteroscopy involves examining the ureters, making them a direct focus of the procedure for detecting abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ examination, medical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ureters" target="Pyelogram">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pyelogram is used to identify obstructions in the ureters, making it essential for diagnosing urinary system issues.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical imaging, obstruction detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder" target="Urethra">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The urethra carries urine from the bladder to outside the body, completing the waste elimination process.</data>
      <data key="d6">excretion, urinary pathway</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder" target="Prostate">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The prostate is located just beneath the bladder, structurally related and part of the male urinary and reproductive systems.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical relationship, male anatomy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder" target="Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cystoscopy allows for direct visualization of the bladder, enabling diagnosis of abnormalities, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is specifically designed to examine the bladder for abnormalities, making the bladder the primary focus of the procedure.</data>
      <data key="d6">direct observation, disease confirmation&lt;SEP&gt;organ examination, medical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder" target="Urogram">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A urogram helps visualize the bladder and diagnose conditions that may affect it, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, cancer assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder" target="Stage 0">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 0 bladder cancer is confined to the inner lining of the bladder, indicating no muscle invasion."|&gt;"cancer stage, location</data>
      <data key="d6">9</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder" target="Cancer Grading">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The stage of bladder cancer influences grading, as different stages reflect varying levels of tissue invasion."|&gt;"cancer assessment, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder" target="Stage 1">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1 bladder cancer is characterized by its growth into the connective tissue of the bladder."|&gt;"cancer stage, anatomical involvement</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team is crucial in managing and treating bladder cancer based on its stage and grade."|&gt;"patient care, treatment approach</data>
      <data key="d6">9</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urethra" target="Bladder Cancer">
