    <edge source="Locally Advanced Cervical Cancer" target="Metastatic Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Locally advanced cervical cancer can progress to metastatic cancer if not adequately treated, highlighting the progression of disease stages.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer progression, treatment urgency</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Cervical Cancer" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastatic cervical cancer is generally treated using systemic therapy methods, including chemotherapy and immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment approach, cancer therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Influence of LVSI on Treatment" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The presence of LVSI influences the treatment approach for cervical cancer due to its impact on prognosis and spread risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment impact, risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Extrafascial Hysterectomy" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Extrafascial hysterectomy is a recommended surgical treatment for certain early stages of cervical cancer, particularly when specific criteria are met.&lt;SEP&gt;Extrafascial hysterectomy is a recommended treatment for early stages of cervical cancer when specific criteria are met for the procedure.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, cancer staging&lt;SEP&gt;treatment option, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Modified Radical Hysterectomy" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Modified radical hysterectomy is employed for treating cervical cancer, especially when there is a risk of spread detected at the margins.&lt;SEP&gt;Modified radical hysterectomy is suggested for cervical cancer when certain stages or conditions, such as cancer found at biopsy margins, are present.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, cancer staging&lt;SEP&gt;treatment option, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Lymph Node Dissection" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic lymph node dissection is critical in cervical cancer treatment to assess the spread of cancer and direct further treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical assessment, cancer evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Para-aortic Lymph Node Dissection" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Para-aortic lymph node dissection is performed to detect the presence of cancer beyond pelvic nodes in cervical cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical assessment, cancer evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LVSI (Lymphovascular Space Invasion)" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The presence of LVSI is a significant factor in determining treatment and prognosis for patients with cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer characteristic, treatment decision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic MRI" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic MRI is employed to assess the extent of cervical cancer, particularly for those diagnosed with recurrent disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging, cancer assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDG-PET/CT Scan" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FDG-PET/CT scans are essential in detecting recurrence of cervical cancer by highlighting areas of abnormal metabolic activity in the body.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Survivorship Care" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Survivorship care framework focuses on long-term management and monitoring for cervical cancer survivors to prevent recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-treatment care, health management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Exenteration" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic exenteration is a surgical treatment option for advanced cases of cervical cancer, aimed at removing cancerous tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelvic exenteration is a surgical treatment option for advanced cervical cancer, aimed at removing cancerous tissues and potentially curing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgery treatment, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radical Hysterectomy" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radical hysterectomy is a surgical approach utilized for treating cervical cancer by removing affected tissues to control the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Radical hysterectomy is another surgical approach utilized to remove cancerous tissue associated with cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgery, cancer removal</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Platinum-Based Chemotherapy" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Platinum-based chemotherapy is often a form of systemic therapy used for treating metastatic cervical cancer after initial procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stoma" target="Ileal Conduit">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An ileal conduit creates a stoma for urine to exit the body after the bladder has been removed, providing a method for waste elimination.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, waste diversion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stoma" target="Indiana Pouch">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Indiana Pouch utilizes a stoma as an external opening for urine drainage, highlighting a direct functional relationship.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical function, urinary diversion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Disease" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cervical cancer can lead to metastatic disease, complicating treatment outcomes and options for patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, cancer spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma" target="Primary Mediastinal B-cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma has characteristics that overlap with Primary Mediastinal B-cell Lymphoma, necessitating careful diagnosis and treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical classification, overlapping features</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First-Line Therapy Regimen" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The first-line therapy regimen typically involves systemic therapies tailored to the patient's specific cancer type and biomarkers.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment protocol, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First-Line Therapy Regimen" target="PD-L1 Biomarker">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The PD-L1 biomarker plays a crucial role in determining the appropriate first-line therapy regimen for patients with certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarkers, treatment selection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid cancer" target="thyroid">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid cancer originates in the thyroid gland, which is responsible for hormone production that regulates bodily functions.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer origin, gland function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid cancer" target="NCCN Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines provide crucial information on the treatments and management of thyroid cancer for patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient resources, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid cancer" target="radiation exposure">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Prior radiation exposure is a well-established risk factor that can contribute to the development of thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factors, disease causality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid cancer" target="familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Familial adenomatous polyposis is a genetic condition that increases the likelihood of developing thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk, cancer association</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid cancer" target="Carney complex">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Carney complex is associated with an increased risk of thyroid cancer along with other tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic syndrome, cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid cancer" target="Cowden syndrome">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cowden syndrome increases the risk of developing follicular thyroid cancer due to genetic factors.</data>
      <data key="d6">inherited cancer risk, syndrome</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid cancer" target="multiple endocrine neoplasia (MEN)">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Multiple endocrine neoplasia conditions can lead to thyroid cancer among other endocrine tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">endocrine tumors, genetic predisposition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid cancer" target="female at birth">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Individuals assigned female at birth are at a higher risk for developing thyroid cancer compared to those assigned male.</data>
      <data key="d6">gender prevalence, cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid cancer" target="risk factors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Known risk factors, such as radiation exposure and family history, can increase the likelihood of developing thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer causation, risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid" target="thyroid hormone">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid hormones produced by the thyroid gland are essential for numerous bodily functions, influencing metabolism and growth.</data>
      <data key="d6">gland function, metabolic regulation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid" target="thyroid nodules">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid nodules are abnormal growths that can occur within the thyroid gland, often requiring monitoring or biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d6">growth abnormalities, gland health</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="older persons" target="DLBCL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Older persons are more likely to be diagnosed with DLBCL due to their higher rate of more advanced-stage diseases.</data>
      <data key="d6">age-related risk, cancer incidence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DLBCL" target="extranodal disease">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">DLBCL can present with extranodal disease, which complicates diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer progression, diagnosis challenges</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DLBCL" target="MGZL">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MGZL is related to DLBCL in that it presents features that challenge treatment typically associated with DLBCL.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment complexity, lymphoma overlap</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MGZL" target="chemoimmunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemoimmunotherapy may be utilized in treating MGZL, reflecting its complex treatment needs.</data>
      <data key="d6">advanced treatment, lymphoma therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MGZL" target="ISRT">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ISRT could be used for localized MGZL, emphasizing targeted treatment approaches for this variant of lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">localized treatment, radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid hormone" target="iodine">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Iodine is necessary for the production of thyroid hormones, linking dietary intake to thyroid health.</data>
      <data key="d6">nutrition, hormone production</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="thyroid hormone" target="TSH">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">TSH regulates the secretion of thyroid hormones, thus directly influencing metabolism and physiological processes.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormonal regulation, pituitary function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="risk factors" target="personal history of thyroid cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A personal history of thyroid cancer serves as a risk factor for future occurrences or related health challenges.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer history, risk linkage</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Nodule" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A biopsy is performed on thyroid nodules to determine if they are cancerous, significantly impacting treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsy is performed on thyroid nodules to ascertain if they are cancerous, influencing treatment courses.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnosis, treatment determination&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic procedure, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Nodule" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans can be utilized to visualize thyroid nodules and their relation to surrounding tissues for diagnosis purposes.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging technique, nodule assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Hormone" target="Radioactive Iodine Uptake Test">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The radioactive iodine uptake test helps assess the thyroid's ability to produce hormones, influencing hyperthyroidism evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, thyroid function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Papillary Carcinoma" target="RAI Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">RAI Therapy may be used after treatment for Papillary Carcinoma to ensure complete removal of cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment protocol, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer" target="Total Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Total thyroidectomy may be required for treating anaplastic thyroid cancer due to its aggressive nature.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgery is often necessary in cases of anaplastic thyroid cancer to remove malignant tissue and affected nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment necessity, cancer intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Levothyroxine" target="Total Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Post-surgery, patients often require levothyroxine for hormone replacement to manage thyroid hormone levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Post-total thyroidectomy, patients typically require levothyroxine for hormone replacement therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-treatment therapy, hormone management&lt;SEP&gt;treatment regimen, hormone therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Levothyroxine" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Levothyroxine is prescribed to manage thyroid hormone levels in patients diagnosed with thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Levothyroxine is used as a treatment to replace the hormones in patients with thyroid cancer after surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone replacement, cancer treatment&lt;SEP&gt;treatment, hormone management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Levothyroxine" target="Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Levothyroxine aims to maintain optimal levels of TSH in patients who have undergone thyroid hormone replacement therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone regulation, therapy monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Levothyroxine" target="NCCN Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients recommend levothyroxine for maintaining normal TSH levels in patients post-thyroid surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical guidelines, treatment recommendations</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">29.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Total Thyroidectomy is a common surgical approach for patients with aggressive or larger thyroid cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Total Thyroidectomy is a standard treatment option for various types of thyroid cancer, especially when the cancer has spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy is a common treatment procedure for thyroid cancer to remove the affected tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI)">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radioactive Iodine Therapy is often used after Total Thyroidectomy to ensure that any remaining cancer cells are destroyed.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-surgery treatment, cancer care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="RAI Therapy">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">RAI Therapy is often administered following Total Thyroidectomy to eradicate any remaining thyroid cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-surgical treatment, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A total thyroidectomy is often recommended for follicular thyroid carcinoma that has spread beyond the thyroid.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgery indication, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Total thyroidectomy is indicated for patients with oncocytic carcinoma that shows signs of aggressive spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, cancer type</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Follow-Up Care">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Follow-up care is necessary after total thyroidectomy to monitor for recurrence and complications of thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-surgery care, monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Short-Term Follow-Up">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Short-term follow-up is critical post-total thyroidectomy to ensure there is no immediate recurrence of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical follow-up, early detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lobectomy" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lobectomy is also used in the treatment of thyroid cancer, particularly for less extensive disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy may be considered as a treatment option for selected patients with low-risk thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, cancer management&lt;SEP&gt;surgical treatment, risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lobectomy" target="Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lobectomy may be performed for less invasive follicular thyroid carcinoma, depending on its growth characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical options, tumor classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radioactive iodine therapy is specifically used to treat differentiated thyroid cancers, particularly after total thyroidectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d4">23.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An endocrinologist may be part of a treatment team managing a patient diagnosed with thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrinologists are key members of the treatment team managing thyroid cancer cases and related therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrinologists manage hormone therapies and monitor patients with thyroid cancer for effective treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical expertise, cancer treatment&lt;SEP&gt;medical team, specialty care&lt;SEP&gt;specialized care, treatment management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">13.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is employed for treating thyroid cancer cases that are inoperable or have spread to other organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy is utilized for cases of thyroid cancer that are not amenable to surgery or have metastasized.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer therapy, treatment option&lt;SEP&gt;cancer treatment, palliative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Nuclear Medicine Specialist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nuclear medicine specialists play a key role in diagnosing and treating thyroid cancer using radioactive materials.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical expertise, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Calcium and Vitamin D Supplements">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Calcium and vitamin D supplements help mitigate the risk of bone issues resulting from thyroid hormone treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">bone health, supportive treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="RAI Therapy">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">RAI therapy is a therapeutic approach for thyroid cancer that specifically targets thyroid cells and cancerous tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">radioactive treatment, cancer therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Neck Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Neck ultrasound is a key imaging method used in the surveillance of thyroid cancer recurrence after surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Neck ultrasound is utilized to monitor thyroid cancer recurrence post-treatment, enhancing surveillance efforts.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic monitoring, surveillance&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic tool, monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Blood Tests">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blood tests are used to track a patient's hormonal levels and detect signs of recurrence in thyroid cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">monitoring, diagnostics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Lenvatinib">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lenvatinib is recommended as a targeted therapy for certain cases of advanced thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lenvatinib is recommended as a treatment for progressive thyroid cancer that has not been manageable via surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Sorafenib">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sorafenib is one of the treatment options considered for advanced cases of thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Sorafenib serves as another potential treatment for patients with advanced thyroid cancer unmanageable by surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, cancer management&lt;SEP&gt;targeted therapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Cabozantinib">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cabozantinib may be used for patients whose thyroid cancer worsens after treatment with other therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, progression treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients with thyroid cancer are encouraged to consider participation in clinical trials for new experimental therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">research, treatment exploration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Imaging Procedures">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging procedures like CT and MRI are crucial in assessing thyroid cancer status for recurrence or metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic assessment, cancer evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine Therapy" target="Whole-Body Radioiodine Scan">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The scan is conducted post-radioactive iodine therapy to monitor the effectiveness of the treatment and check for remaining cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">monitoring treatment, cancer surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine Therapy" target="Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radioactive iodine therapy is often used after thyroidectomy to eliminate remaining thyroid cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-surgery treatment, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine Therapy" target="Thyrotropin Alfa (Thyrogen)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyrotropin Alfa (Thyrogen) is used to increase the TSH level for effective uptake of radioactive iodine during therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment facilitation, hormone management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Adolescent and Young Adult Cancer" target="U.S. FDA">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN Guidelines provide guidance for treatments that must be approved by the U.S. FDA before use.</data>
      <data key="d6">guidance, treatment approval</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="Clinical trial">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The U.S. FDA is responsible for overseeing the approval processes that clinical trials must undergo before any new cancer treatment can be offered to patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">regulation, treatment approval</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="Targeted therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Targeted therapies must undergo rigorous evaluation and receive approval from the U.S. FDA before being available for patient use.</data>
      <data key="d6">regulation, treatment approval</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Systemic therapy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is a type of systemic therapy used to treat cancers throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment classification, cancer therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation therapy" target="Clinical trials">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials often evaluate the effectiveness and safety of radiation therapy for treating certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment evaluation, research</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation therapy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is often used in conjunction with radiation therapy to treat certain aggressive types of cancer for better outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d6">combined treatment, cancer therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation therapy" target="Radiation therapy side effects">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is important to understand regarding potential side effects that can affect quality of life during and after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment awareness, patient education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical trials" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials assess the effectiveness of chemotherapy for cancers that are resistant to standard treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment assessment, research</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Side effects of therapy" target="Fertility preservation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Discussing fertility preservation is crucial for patients undergoing therapies that might affect their reproductive capabilities as a side effect.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient care, reproductive health</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical trial" target="Phase 1 trials">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 1 trials are the first step in clinical trials used to test new treatments for safety and side effects.</data>
      <data key="d6">research phases, safety assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 1 trials" target="Phase 2 trials">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Following Phase 1 trials, Phase 2 trials evaluate the treatment's effectiveness against a specific type of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment effectiveness, research progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 2 trials" target="Phase 3 trials">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 3 trials assess the new treatment against standard therapies to evaluate its overall effectiveness and benefits.</data>
      <data key="d6">comparative effectiveness, research validation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM)" target="NCI">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The National Cancer Institute works with the U.S. National Library of Medicine to provide resources for finding clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d6">resource sharing, clinical trials</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Information Service (CIS)" target="NCI">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The National Cancer Institute partners with the Cancer Information Service to provide educational resources about cancer treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d6">educational support, patient resources</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI)" target="Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">RAI therapy may be used after total thyroidectomy for patients with follicular thyroid carcinoma to eliminate residual cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-surgery treatment, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI)" target="Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">RAI therapy may be beneficial for patients with oncocytic carcinoma after surgery, particularly if the cancer has invaded blood vessels.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapeutic strategy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transportation Costs" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Participants in clinical trials may incur transportation costs when traveling to trial locations for appointments.</data>
      <data key="d6">cost considerations, participant burden</data>
